(2295, 1, 3, 11, 'Extend Counting Sequence to 120', 356, 1, 'What number comes right after 58?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '57', '59', '68', '60', 'B', 'active', 'When counting forward sequentially, 58 is followed by 59.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student counted backward instead of forward.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice forward number sequence on a number line.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student incremented the tens digit instead of the ones digit.\", \"remediation\": \"Review sequential counting within the same decade.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student confused sequence with rounding up to the next decade.\", \"remediation\": \"Reinforce that 58 is followed by 59 before reaching 60.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2296, 1, 3, 11, 'Extend Counting Sequence to 120', 356, 1, 'What number comes immediately after 109?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '100', '110', '1010', '200', 'B', 'active', 'After 109, counting forward by ones reaches 110.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student reverted to the century base instead of counting forward.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice crossing decade transitions past 100.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Place-value syntax error: concatenated \'10\' next to \'100\'.\", \"remediation\": \"Use 3-digit place value cards to illustrate 110.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Century boundary misconception: skipped to next hundred (200).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice oral counting from 100 to 120.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2297, 1, 3, 11, 'Extend Counting Sequence to 120', 356, 1, 'Ali is counting: 114, 115, ___, 117, 118. Which number is missing?', NULL, 1, 'High', '116', '126', '119', '161', 'A', 'active', 'The number between 115 and 117 in sequential counting is 116.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student changed the tens digit (126) instead of keeping decade 110.\", \"remediation\": \"Reinforce maintaining the hundreds and tens place while counting ones.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student placed the terminal number instead of the intermediate missing value.\", \"remediation\": \"Use missing number strips on a 120-chart.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Digit reversal / spatial inversion error (161 instead of 116).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice number writing and numeral recognition drills.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2298, 1, 3, 11, 'Understand Place Value - Tens & Ones', 357, 1, 'A teacher ties 10 pencils in one bundle. If she has 3 bundles, how many pencils does she have in total?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '3', '13', '30', '300', 'C', 'active', 'Each bundle is 1 ten. 3 bundles of 10 equal 30 pencils.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the number of bundles rather than total units.\", \"remediation\": \"Use base-10 ten-rods to demonstrate each bundle equals 10 individual units.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 10 and 3 together rather than grouping three tens.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice skip-counting by 10s: 10, 20, 30.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Magnitude exaggeration: assigned hundreds value to tens.\", \"remediation\": \"Differentiate between tens and hundreds bundles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2299, 1, 3, 11, 'Understand Place Value - Tens & Ones', 357, 1, 'In the number 72, what does the digit 7 represent?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '7 ones', '7 tens (70)', '2 tens', '700', 'B', 'active', 'In a 2-digit number, the left digit is the tens place. 7 represents 7 tens or 70.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Position blindness: treated the left digit as ones.\", \"remediation\": \"Introduce a two-column Tens & Ones T-chart.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Interchanged the identity of tens and ones digits.\", \"remediation\": \"Reinforce left-to-right reading of two-digit numbers (Tens first, Ones second).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Assigned hundreds place value to a 2-digit number.\", \"remediation\": \"Limit place-value column identification to two digits.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2300, 1, 3, 11, 'Understand Place Value - Tens & Ones', 357, 1, 'Which equation correctly breaks down the number 16?', NULL, 1, 'High', '1 + 6', '10 + 6', '60 + 1', '10 + 60', 'B', 'active', 'The number 16 is composed of 1 ten (10) and 6 ones (6), so 16 = 10 + 6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Face-value misconception: treated the digit 1 as single unit instead of a ten.\", \"remediation\": \"Demonstrate teen numbers as one group of ten plus extra ones using double ten-frames.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Reversed place value decomposition (60 + 1).\", \"remediation\": \"Use expanded form decomposition cards.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Excessive place-value expansion.\", \"remediation\": \"Review that 16 is less than 20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2301, 1, 3, 11, 'Compare Two-Digit Numbers', 358, 1, 'Which statement is TRUE?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '4 tens = 40', '4 tens = 4', '4 tens = 14', '4 tens = 400', 'A', 'active', 'Four tens means 4 groups of 10, which equals 40.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Ignored unit word \'tens\' and focused solely on digit 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice verbalizing unit values: 1 ten = 10, 2 tens = 20, 4 tens = 40.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Confused decade value with teen number 14.\", \"remediation\": \"Contrast teen numbers (14) with decade multiples (40).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Appended two zeros instead of one for tens.\", \"remediation\": \"Review zero-padding rules for base-10 multiples.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2302, 1, 3, 11, 'Compare Two-Digit Numbers', 358, 1, 'Which symbol correctly compares these numbers?  62 ___ 49', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '<', '>', '=', '+', 'B', 'active', 'Compare the tens digits first: 6 tens is greater than 4 tens, so 62 > 49.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Looked only at the ones digits (9 vs 2) and erroneously concluded 49 is larger.\", \"remediation\": \"Teach rule: \'Always compare the tens place first when comparing two-digit numbers.\'\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Equal sign misuse: failed to distinguish distinct numerical magnitudes.\", \"remediation\": \"Review the definition and balance meaning of the equal sign.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Selected an arithmetic operator instead of a comparison symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"Clarify difference between operations (+, -) and comparisons (<, >, =).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2303, 1, 3, 11, 'Compare Two-Digit Numbers', 358, 1, 'Sara has 83 stickers and Bilal has 87 stickers. Which statement is correct?', NULL, 1, 'High', '83 > 87', '87 > 83', '83 = 87', '87 < 83', 'B', 'active', 'Both have 8 tens, but 87 has 7 ones while 83 has 3 ones. 87 is greater than 83 (87 > 83).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Comparison symbol direction inverted (alligator mouth pointed to smaller number).\", \"remediation\": \"Use the visual alligator mnemonic: the open mouth always faces the larger value.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Determined equality based on identical tens without comparing ones digits.\", \"remediation\": \"Teach tie-breaker rule: when tens digits match, compare the ones digits.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Selected \'less than\' symbol for a greater quantity.\", \"remediation\": \"Reinforce left-to-right reading of comparison statements.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2304, 1, 3, 11, 'Mental Math - 10 More & 10 Less', 359, 1, 'What is 10 more than 40?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '41', '50', '30', '60', 'B', 'active', '40 has 4 tens. 10 more adds 1 ten, making 5 tens or 50.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Added 1 to the ones place instead of adding 10 to the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"Contrast adding 1 (one unit) versus adding 10 (one ten-rod).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Confused \'more\' with \'less\' and performed subtraction.\", \"remediation\": \"Reinforce directional math keywords: \'more\' means increase, \'less\' means decrease.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Added 20 instead of 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice single-step skip counting by tens.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2305, 1, 3, 11, 'Mental Math - 10 More & 10 Less', 359, 1, 'What is 10 more than 64?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '65', '74', '54', '84', 'B', 'active', 'Adding 10 increases only the tens digit from 6 to 7, while the ones digit stays 4 (74).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 1 to the ones column instead of 10 to the tens column.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice vertical movement on a 100-chart where one row down equals +10.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Subtracted 10 instead of adding 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Reinforce that \'10 more\' increases the tens digit by 1.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Incremented tens digit by 2 instead of 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice single-decade jumps from arbitrary bases.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2306, 1, 3, 11, 'Mental Math - 10 More & 10 Less', 359, 1, 'I am thinking of a number. It is 10 less than 92. What is my number?', NULL, 1, 'High', '91', '82', '102', '72', 'B', 'active', '10 less than 92 decreases the tens digit from 9 to 8, resulting in 82.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Subtracted 1 from ones place rather than subtracting 10 from tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"Use 100s chart vertical upward jump (one row up = -10).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Added 10 instead of taking away 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Clarify that \'less\' means going backward on the number track.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Subtracted 20 instead of 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Check mental subtraction step-by-step: 9 tens minus 1 ten = 8 tens.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2307, 1, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 1, 'What is 70 – 30?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '40', '50', '4', '100', 'A', 'active', '7 tens minus 3 tens equals 4 tens (40).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Calculation error in subtracting single-digit decade heads (did 7 - 2 instead of 7 - 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Use fingers or counters to confirm 7 minus 3 equals 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Omitted the tens place value (wrote 4 instead of 40).\", \"remediation\": \"Remind student that 4 tens equals 40.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Added 70 and 30 instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"Focus on identifying minus sign (-) as take away.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2308, 1, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 1, 'What is 34 + 5?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '39', '84', '49', '38', 'A', 'active', 'Add ones to ones: 4 + 5 = 9. The tens remain 3. Total is 39.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Added single-digit 5 to the tens place (3 + 5 = 8) instead of the ones place.\", \"remediation\": \"Teach vertical alignment: ones must always be added to ones.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Added 5 to ones but also erroneously incremented the tens digit.\", \"remediation\": \"Emphasize that when ones sum is less than 10, the tens digit stays unchanged.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Counting error by 1 during single-digit addition.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice counting on from 34: 35, 36, 37, 38, 39.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2309, 1, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 1, 'A shop has 43 red balls and 30 blue balls. How many balls are there in total?', NULL, 1, 'High', '46', '73', '70', '83', 'B', 'active', 'Add tens to tens: 4 tens + 3 tens = 7 tens. 3 ones + 0 ones = 3 ones. Total is 73.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Added the digit 3 of 30 to the ones column instead of tens column.\", \"remediation\": \"Break down 30 into 3 tens, and add to the 4 tens of 43.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Added tens correctly (40 + 30 = 70) but forgot to include the 3 ones.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice recombining decomposed numbers: 70 tens + 3 ones = 73.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Miscalculated decade addition (4 + 3 = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"Review single-digit basic addition facts: 4 + 3 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2310, 1, 3, 11, 'Understand Place Value - Tens & Ones', 357, 1, 'Look at the base-10 blocks: There are 2 rods of ten and 6 small unit cubes. What number is shown?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '8', '62', '26', '206', 'C', 'active', '2 rods of ten = 20, and 6 unit cubes = 6. 20 + 6 = 26.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Place-value blindness: counted total items (2 + 6 = 8) ignoring that rods represent ten each.\", \"remediation\": \"Physically count individual squares on a ten-rod to demonstrate it equals 10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Spatial/digit inversion: placed ones count in the tens position (62 instead of 26).\", \"remediation\": \"Use two-pocket place value mats (Tens on left, Ones on right).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Literal concatenation: wrote 20 followed by 6 creating 206.\", \"remediation\": \"Use overlay cards to demonstrate how 6 ones replaces the zero in 20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2311, 1, 3, 11, 'Mental Math - 10 More & 10 Less', 359, 1, 'A farmer has 53 sheep. He sells 10 sheep. How many sheep are left?', NULL, 1, 'High', '52', '43', '63', '35', 'B', 'active', 'Selling 10 sheep from 53 means subtracting 10: 53 - 10 = 43.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Subtracted 1 from the ones column (53 - 1 = 52) instead of subtracting 10 from tens column.\", \"remediation\": \"Highlight that selling \'10\' reduces the tens digit by one.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Added 10 instead of subtracting, misinterpreting the keyword \'sells\'.\", \"remediation\": \"Clarify that selling or losing items means subtraction.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Digit reversal: inverted the digits of 53 to 35.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice writing numbers from left to right.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2312, 1, 3, 11, 'Unknown Addends & Equations', 361, 1, 'Which number makes this equation true?  6 + ___ = 10', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '16', '4', '5', '60', 'B', 'active', '6 + 4 = 10, so the missing number is 4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Operation impulse: saw plus sign and added both visible numbers (6 + 10 = 16) instead of finding the missing addend.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a balance beam or ten-frame to show that 6 plus something must equal 10.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Inaccurate counting on: miscounted by 1 while counting up from 6 to 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice counting on fingers: 6 in head, 7, 8, 9, 10 (4 fingers).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Appended zero to create 60 without understanding equality.\", \"remediation\": \"Reinforce that the equal sign means both sides have the same value.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 11:33:25', '2026-09-11 11:42:22'),
(2313, 6, 3, 11, 'Mental Addition & Subtraction', 152, 2, 'What is 8 + 7?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '14', '15', '16', '13', 'B', 'active', 'Use doubles strategy: 7 + 7 = 14, so 8 + 7 = 14 + 1 = 15. Or make 10: 8 + 2 = 10, and 10 + 5 = 15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a doubling error (7 + 7 = 14 without adding the extra 1).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice \\\"doubles plus one\\\" mental addition facts within 20.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an off-by-one counting error while counting up.\", \"remediation\": \"Use number lines or ten-frames to verify mental sums.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused addition with subtraction or miscounted backwards.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice basic addition combinations that make 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2314, 6, 3, 11, 'Counting & Number Representation', 149, 2, 'What is the missing number in this pattern: 35, 40, 45, __, 55?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '46', '48', '50', '52', 'C', 'active', 'The numbers increase by 5 each step (35 + 5 = 40, 40 + 5 = 45). Adding 5 to 45 gives 50.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student counted by 1s instead of recognizing the skip-count pattern of 5s.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice skip-counting out loud by 5s using a 100-chart.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed a number between 45 and 55 without checking the constant rule (+5).\", \"remediation\": \"Find the rule of a pattern by calculating the difference between consecutive numbers.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the wrong step value (+7 instead of +5).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice counting sequences ending in 0 and 5 up to 100.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2315, 6, 3, 11, 'Counting & Number Representation', 149, 2, 'Which group of numbers are ALL even numbers?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '2, 5, 8', '4, 8, 12', '3, 7, 9', '6, 9, 14', 'B', 'active', 'Even numbers can be paired equally and end in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8. The numbers 4, 8, and 12 are all even.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student overlooked the odd number 5 because 2 and 8 are even.\", \"remediation\": \"Check every number individually in a list to verify if it ends in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused the definition of even numbers with odd numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Use counters in pairs of two to visually see the difference between odd and even.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student ignored that 9 cannot be split into two equal whole numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice pairing objects to test for remainders.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2316, 6, 3, 11, 'Mental Addition & Subtraction', 152, 2, 'What is 16 - 9?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '7', '8', '9', '6', 'A', 'active', 'Think of addition: 9 + 7 = 16, so 16 - 9 = 7. Or subtract to 10: 16 - 6 = 10, then 10 - 3 = 7.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused with half of 16 (16 - 8 = 8) or made an off-by-one counting error.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice subtracting 9 by taking away 10 and adding back 1.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student misunderstood subtraction as finding doubles (9 + 9 = 18).\", \"remediation\": \"Use fact families (7 + 9 = 16, 16 - 9 = 7) to relate subtraction to addition.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted only the ones place (16 - 10 = 6) without adjusting for 9.\", \"remediation\": \"Break numbers down using ten-frames when subtracting across 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2317, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value', 150, 2, 'In the number 348, what is the value of the digit 4?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '4', '40', '400', '48', 'B', 'active', 'In 348, the digit 3 is in the hundreds place (300), the digit 4 is in the tens place (4 tens = 40), and 8 is in the ones place (8).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student stated the face value of the digit instead of its place value in the tens column.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice naming the value of digits based on their position (Hundreds, Tens, Ones).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused the tens place with the hundreds place.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a place value chart to align 3-digit numbers into H, T, and O columns.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student combined the tens and ones digits together rather than identifying digit 4 alone.\", \"remediation\": \"Isolate individual column values using base-ten blocks.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2318, 6, 3, 11, 'Mental Math - 10 More & 10 Less', 359, 2, 'What number is 10 more than 53?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '54', '63', '43', '73', 'B', 'active', 'Adding 10 increases only the tens digit by 1: 5 tens + 1 ten = 6 tens. The ones digit stays 3. So, 53 + 10 = 63.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 1 instead of adding 10 (changed the ones digit instead of tens).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice distinguishing between \\\"+1\\\" (next number) and \\\"+10\\\" (next row on a 100-chart).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 10 instead of adding 10 (confused \\\"10 more\\\" with \\\"10 less\\\").\", \"remediation\": \"Highlight keywords: \\\"more\\\" means increase (+), \\\"less\\\" means decrease (-).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 20 instead of 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice jumping one row down (+10) on a hundred chart.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2319, 6, 3, 11, 'Equal Groups & Rectangular Arrays', 156, 2, 'A baker puts cookies in 3 rows with 4 cookies in each row. Which addition sentence shows the total number of cookies?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '3 + 4 = 7', '4 + 4 + 4 = 12', '3 + 3 + 3 = 9', '4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16', 'B', 'active', 'There are 3 groups of 4 cookies. Repeated addition is 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added the number of rows to the number in each row instead of using repeated addition.\", \"remediation\": \"Draw an array with rows and columns to show that arrays represent repeated addition.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student repeated the number of rows (3) three times instead of the cookies in each row (4).\", \"remediation\": \"Identify the group size first, then repeat it by the number of rows.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added an extra fourth row (used 4 groups of 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Count rows carefully before writing repeated addition sentences.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2320, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 2, 'What is 43 + 25?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '68', '58', '78', '67', 'A', 'active', 'Add the ones: 3 + 5 = 8. Add the tens: 4 + 2 = 6. Total = 68.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the tens column (4 + 2 = 5 instead of 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice adding multiples of 10 separately from ones.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student unnecessarily carried a ten into the tens column when ones did not exceed 9.\", \"remediation\": \"Check if the ones sum is 10 or greater before regrouping.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an off-by-one calculation error in the ones column (3 + 5 = 7).\", \"remediation\": \"Use touch points or fingers to verify single-digit sums.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2321, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value', 150, 2, 'Sana has 14 tens blocks. How can 14 tens be written as hundreds and tens?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '1 hundred and 4 tens (140)', '14 hundreds (1400)', '1 hundred and 4 ones (104)', '4 hundreds and 1 ten (410)', 'A', 'active', '10 tens make 1 hundred (100). The remaining 4 tens make 40. So 14 tens = 1 hundred and 4 tens = 140.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused the unit \\\"tens\\\" with \\\"hundreds\\\".\", \"remediation\": \"Trade 10 ten-rods for 1 hundred-flat using base-ten manipulatives.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student treated leftover tens as ones (confused 40 with 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice regrouping tens: remember each remaining ten rod has a value of 10.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed the digits (put 4 in hundreds place and 1 in tens place).\", \"remediation\": \"Draw a place value table to place regrouped hundreds on the left.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2322, 6, 3, 11, 'Compare Two-Digit Numbers', 358, 2, 'Which symbol makes this comparison true: 562 __ 526?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '>', '<', '=', '+', 'A', 'active', 'Both numbers have 5 hundreds. Comparing the tens place: 562 has 6 tens and 526 has 2 tens. Since 6 > 2, 562 > 526.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student looked at the ones digits (2 < 6) instead of comparing from left to right (tens digit).\", \"remediation\": \"Always compare digits starting from the highest place value (hundreds, then tens, then ones).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student saw that both numbers contain the digits 5, 6, and 2 and assumed they are equal regardless of order.\", \"remediation\": \"Review how digit order affects place value and total number size.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student selected an operation symbol instead of an inequality comparison symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"Review the comparison symbols (>, <, =) and what each represents.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2323, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 2, 'What is 57 + 26?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '73', '83', '713', '84', 'B', 'active', 'Add ones: 7 + 6 = 13 (write 3, regroup 1 ten). Add tens: 5 + 2 + 1 = 8 tens. Total = 83.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add the regrouped 1 ten to the tens column (5 + 2 = 7).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice writing the carried ten above the tens column and crossing it off after adding.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student wrote the full 13 in the answer instead of regrouping 1 ten to the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"Reinforce place value rules: only one digit can sit in each place value column.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated ones place (7 + 6 = 14 instead of 13).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice addition facts that cross 10 using the \\\"make a ten\\\" strategy.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2324, 6, 3, 11, 'Mental Math - 10 More & 10 Less', 359, 2, 'What number is 100 less than 745?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '735', '645', '845', '635', 'B', 'active', 'Subtracting 100 changes only the hundreds place: 7 hundreds - 1 hundred = 6 hundreds. Tens and ones stay 45. Answer = 645.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 10 instead of 100 (reduced the tens column instead of hundreds).\", \"remediation\": \"Verify which place value column changes when dealing with 10 vs 100.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 100 instead of subtracting (confused \\\"less\\\" with \\\"more\\\").\", \"remediation\": \"Practice matching \\\"less\\\" to subtraction (moving left/down).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted from both hundreds and tens columns at the same time.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice isolating single place values during mental arithmetic.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2325, 6, 3, 11, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'A fruit shop had 64 oranges. By afternoon, 28 oranges were sold. How many oranges are left in the shop?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '44', '92', '36', '46', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract oranges sold: 64 - 28. Regroup 1 ten into 14 ones: 14 - 8 = 6. Tens: 5 - 2 = 3. Answer is 36.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted smaller digit from larger digit in ones place (8 - 4 = 4) instead of regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"If the top digit is smaller than the bottom digit, remember to borrow/regroup from the tens.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 64 + 28 instead of subtracting (misread the action \\\"were sold / left\\\").\", \"remediation\": \"Identify word problem clues: \\\"sold\\\" or \\\"left\\\" signifies taking away (subtraction).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted ones with regrouping (14 - 8 = 6) but forgot to reduce the tens digit from 6 to 5 (6 - 2 = 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Cross out the tens digit when borrowing so you remember its new value.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2326, 6, 3, 11, 'Counting & Number Representation', 149, 2, 'Count by 100s starting from 320: 320, 420, 520, __, 720. What is the missing number?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '530', '620', '600', '521', 'B', 'active', 'Counting by 100s means adding 1 to the hundreds place each time while tens and ones stay unchanged: 520 + 100 = 620.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student counted by 10s instead of 100s (520 + 10 = 530).\", \"remediation\": \"Pay attention to the specified skip step (100 vs 10).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to nearest hundred instead of continuing the non-zero tens pattern.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the tens and ones digits intact when adding hundreds.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student counted by 1s instead of 100s.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice skip-counting by 100s starting from off-decade numbers (e.g., 115, 215, 315).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2327, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 2, 'What is 72 - 38?', NULL, 2, 'High', '46', '34', '44', '36', 'B', 'active', 'Since 2 < 8, borrow 1 ten from 7: 12 - 8 = 4. The 7 tens become 6 tens: 6 - 3 = 3. Result = 34.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed subtraction in both columns (8 - 2 = 6 and 7 - 3 = 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Use base-ten blocks to model vertical subtraction where bottom cannot subtract from top without borrowing.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student regrouped ones (12 - 8 = 4) but forgot to decrement the tens digit from 7 to 6 (7 - 3 = 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice two-digit subtraction with explicit regrouping boxes above the numbers.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 12 - 8 = 6 in the ones column.\", \"remediation\": \"Review subtraction facts from 12 to build automaticity.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2328, 6, 3, 11, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'Ali had 25 stickers. His brother gave him 15 more stickers. Then Ali gave 12 stickers to his friend. How many stickers does Ali have now?', NULL, 2, 'High', '40', '52', '28', '22', 'C', 'active', 'Step 1: Add stickers received: 25 + 15 = 40. Step 2: Subtract stickers given away: 40 - 12 = 28.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student completed only Step 1 (25 + 15 = 40) and stopped before subtracting the 12 stickers given away.\", \"remediation\": \"Read the entire word problem and underline all actions to complete all steps.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added all three numbers (25 + 15 + 12 = 52) without noticing \\\"gave away\\\" means subtraction.\", \"remediation\": \"Break multi-step word problems into two distinct timeline events (+ then -).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted both numbers from 25 (25 - 15 = 10, 10 + 12 = 22) or made a regrouping mistake in 40 - 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Check subtraction across zeros (40 - 12 = 28).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2329, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 2, 'What is 348 + 275?', NULL, 2, 'High', '623', '613', '523', '513', 'A', 'active', 'Ones: 8 + 5 = 13 (write 3, carry 1). Tens: 4 + 7 + 1 = 12 (write 2, carry 1). Hundreds: 3 + 2 + 1 = 6. Total = 623.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added ones (8 + 5 = 13, carried 1) but forgot to add the carried ten in the tens column (4 + 7 = 11).\", \"remediation\": \"Always write carried numbers at the top of the columns to include them in the addition.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to carry 1 hundred to the hundreds column (3 + 2 = 5).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice double-regrouping problems (regrouping ones to tens AND tens to hundreds).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot both regroupings in tens and hundreds.\", \"remediation\": \"Use expanded addition: (300+200) + (40+70) + (8+5).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2330, 6, 3, 11, 'Equal Groups & Rectangular Arrays', 156, 2, 'An array has 4 rows and 5 columns of stars. Which two addition sentences can be used to find the total number of stars?', NULL, 2, 'High', '5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20 and 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 20', '4 + 5 = 9 and 5 + 4 = 9', '4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16 and 5 + 5 + 5 = 15', '20 - 4 = 16 and 20 - 5 = 15', 'A', 'active', 'Count by rows: 4 rows of 5 = 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20. Count by columns: 5 columns of 4 = 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 20.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added row count and column count together instead of repeated addition.\", \"remediation\": \"Draw the grid of dots to visualize how rows and columns create a total area.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student used only 4 groups of 4 instead of representing 5 columns.\", \"remediation\": \"Count rows horizontally and columns vertically before writing expressions.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student used subtraction operations instead of repeated addition sentences.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that arrays represent equal groups combined (addition/multiplication).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2331, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 2, 'What is 500 - 236?', NULL, 2, 'High', '336', '274', '264', '374', 'C', 'active', 'Regroup 1 hundred into 9 tens and 10 ones. Ones: 10 - 6 = 4. Tens: 9 - 3 = 6. Hundreds: 4 - 2 = 2. Result = 264.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 0 from bottom numbers (6 - 0 = 6, 3 - 0 = 3) without regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"When subtracting from zeros on top, you must regroup from the hundreds place first.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made a subtraction error in the tens place (10 - 3 = 7 instead of 9 - 3 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the tens digit becomes 9 when regrouping across a zero to the ones place.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student did not decrease the hundreds digit from 5 to 4 after regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice decomposing 500 into 4 hundreds + 9 tens + 10 ones before subtracting.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2332, 6, 3, 11, 'Mental Addition & Subtraction', 152, 2, 'Zain wants to solve 63 - 19 mentally. Which strategy gives the correct answer?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'Subtract 20, then add 1 back: (63 - 20) + 1 = 43 + 1 = 44', 'Subtract 20, then subtract 1: (63 - 20) - 1 = 43 - 1 = 42', 'Subtract 10, then subtract 9: 63 - 10 = 53, then 53 - 9 = 45', 'Add 19 to 63: 63 + 19 = 82', 'A', 'active', 'Since 19 = 20 - 1, subtracting 19 is the same as subtracting 20 (63 - 20 = 43) and then adding back the extra 1 that was subtracted: 43 + 1 = 44.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted the compensation adjustment instead of adding it back (subtracted 21 instead of 19).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that if you take away too much (-20 instead of -19), you must put 1 back (+1).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an off-by-one arithmetic error when subtracting 9 from 53 (53 - 9 = 44, not 45).\", \"remediation\": \"Use number bonds to break 9 into 3 and 6 when subtracting from 53.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused subtraction operation with addition.\", \"remediation\": \"Read the operation symbol carefully before choosing a mental math pathway.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 13:59:31', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2333, 6, 3, 11, 'Rounding & Place Value', 150, 3, 'What is 74 rounded to the nearest ten?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '70', '80', '75', '100', 'A', 'active', 'In 74, the ones digit is 4. Since 4 is less than 5, round down to 70.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded up instead of down (thought 4 rounds up to the next ten).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the rounding rule: if the ones digit is 0, 1, 2, 3, or 4, round down to the current ten.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student identified the midpoint number instead of rounding to a multiple of ten.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiples of ten always end in 0 (such as 60, 70, 80).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to the nearest hundred instead of the nearest ten.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the place value asked in the question: nearest ten means looking at the ones digit.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2334, 6, 3, 11, '3-Digit Addition & Subtraction', 360, 3, 'What is 342 + 235?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '577', '587', '677', '576', 'A', 'active', 'Add column by column: Ones: 2 + 5 = 7. Tens: 4 + 3 = 7. Hundreds: 3 + 2 = 5. Total = 577.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the tens column (4 + 3 = 8 instead of 7).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice single-digit addition facts using a number line or counters.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an addition error in the hundreds column (3 + 2 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Review basic addition facts within 10 to ensure accurate column sums.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an off-by-one error in the ones column (2 + 5 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Touch-count or use fingers to double check 2 + 5 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2335, 6, 3, 11, 'Multiply by Multiples of Ten', 157, 3, 'What is 6 × 40?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '24', '240', '260', '204', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply the non-zero digits first: 6 × 4 = 24. Since 40 is 4 tens, 6 × 4 tens = 24 tens = 240.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 6 × 4 but forgot to append the zero for the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that multiplying by a multiple of 10 means the answer must end in zero.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a basic multiplication fact error (thought 6 × 4 = 26).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice the 6 times table and 4 times table to master single-digit facts.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student inserted a zero between the digits instead of placing it at the end.\", \"remediation\": \"Place-value multiplication appends zeros to the right of the product.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2336, 6, 3, 11, 'Understanding Fractions as Equal Parts', 159, 3, 'A pizza is cut into 4 equal slices. Sarah eats 1 slice. What fraction of the pizza did Sarah eat?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '1/4', '1/3', '4/1', '3/4', 'A', 'active', 'Sarah ate 1 slice out of 4 total equal slices. The fraction is 1/4.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student compared the 1 eaten slice to the 3 remaining slices instead of total slices.\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator (bottom number) is always the total number of equal parts in the whole.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the numerator and denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: top number is the parts chosen, bottom number is the total parts.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student found the fraction of pizza remaining instead of the fraction eaten.\", \"remediation\": \"Reread the question carefully to identify whether it asks for parts eaten or parts left.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2337, 6, 3, 11, 'Understanding Fractions as Equal Parts', 159, 3, 'A rectangle is divided into 8 equal parts. 3 parts are shaded blue. What fraction of the rectangle is shaded?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '3/8', '5/8', '3/5', '8/3', 'A', 'active', 'The number of shaded parts is 3 (numerator). The total number of equal parts is 8 (denominator). Fraction = 3/8.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted the unshaded parts instead of the shaded parts.\", \"remediation\": \"Count only the parts specified in the question (shaded parts = 3).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student compared shaded parts to unshaded parts (ratio) instead of part-to-whole.\", \"remediation\": \"The bottom number of a fraction must always represent ALL parts combined (3 + 5 = 8).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student swapped the numerator and denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"The numerator (top) is the count of parts, and denominator (bottom) is the total.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2338, 6, 3, 11, 'Equivalent Fractions & Whole Numbers', 159, 3, 'Which fraction is equivalent to 1/2?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '2/4', '1/4', '2/3', '3/5', 'A', 'active', 'Multiplying both numerator and denominator of 1/2 by 2 gives 2/4. Both represent half of a whole.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student kept the numerator the same and doubled only the denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"To make an equivalent fraction, you must multiply BOTH top and bottom by the same number.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 1 to both top and bottom (1+1)/(2+1) instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Adding the same number to numerator and denominator does NOT create an equivalent fraction.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed a fraction with numbers close to half.\", \"remediation\": \"Draw two fraction strips of equal length to test equivalence visually.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2339, 6, 3, 11, 'Multiplication & Division Operations', 157, 3, '35 candies are shared equally among 5 children. How many candies does each child get?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '6', '7', '8', '30', 'B', 'active', 'Equal sharing division: 35 ÷ 5 = 7 because 5 × 7 = 35.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated division by 5 (thought 5 × 6 = 35 instead of 30).\", \"remediation\": \"Count by 5s up to 35 (5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35) to find that 7 steps are needed.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student overestimated the quotient (thought 5 × 8 = 35 instead of 40).\", \"remediation\": \"Review 5s multiplication facts to find the exact factor.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 35 - 5 instead of dividing into 5 equal groups.\", \"remediation\": \"Look for keywords: \\\"shared equally among\\\" signifies division, not subtraction.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2340, 6, 3, 11, 'Rounding & Place Value', 150, 3, 'What is 384 rounded to the nearest hundred?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '380', '400', '300', '500', 'B', 'active', 'To round to the nearest hundred, look at the tens digit. In 384, the tens digit is 8. Since 8 >= 5, round up to 400.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to the nearest ten instead of the nearest hundred.\", \"remediation\": \"When rounding to the nearest hundred, look at the tens digit and end with two zeros.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded down to 300 even though the tens digit (8) is 5 or greater.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice using a number line between 300 and 400 to see that 384 is much closer to 400.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student jumped two hundreds ahead instead of rounding to the next hundred (400).\", \"remediation\": \"Identify the two boundary hundreds (300 and 400) before deciding which is closer.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2341, 6, 3, 11, '3-Digit Addition & Subtraction', 360, 3, 'What is 468 + 275?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '743', '643', '733', '7143', 'A', 'active', 'Ones: 8 + 5 = 13 (write 3, carry 1 ten). Tens: 6 + 7 + 1 = 14 (write 4, carry 1 hundred). Hundreds: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7. Total = 743.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to carry the hundred into the hundreds column (4 + 2 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Always write the regrouped 1 above the hundreds column and add it to the total.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add the carried ten in the tens column (6 + 7 = 13).\", \"remediation\": \"Cross off carried digits after adding them so no regrouped values are missed.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student wrote 14 directly in the tens column instead of regrouping 1 to the hundreds place.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that only one digit fits in each place value column; regroup any group of 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2342, 6, 3, 11, '3-Digit Addition & Subtraction', 360, 3, 'What is 752 - 384?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '432', '368', '378', '468', 'B', 'active', 'Regroup from tens: 12 - 4 = 8. Regroup from hundreds: 14 - 8 = 6. Hundreds: 6 - 3 = 3. Result = 368.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted smaller digits from larger digits in each column without regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"When the top digit is smaller than the bottom digit, borrow 1 from the next place value.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a subtraction error in the tens column (14 - 8 = 7 instead of 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice subtraction facts from 14 using the think-addition strategy (8 + 6 = 14).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to reduce the hundreds digit from 7 to 6 after borrowing.\", \"remediation\": \"Slash and rewrite the decreased digit at the top before completing the subtraction.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2343, 6, 3, 11, 'Multiply by Multiples of Ten', 354, 3, 'A school ordered 8 boxes of markers. Each box contains 30 markers. How many markers did the school order in all?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '240', '38', '210', '2400', 'A', 'active', '8 groups of 30: 8 × 30 = 8 × 3 tens = 24 tens = 240 markers.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 30 + 8 instead of multiplying 8 groups of 30.\", \"remediation\": \"Identify word problem phrasing: \\\"boxes each containing\\\" means equal groups, so multiply.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 8 × 3 as 21 instead of 24.\", \"remediation\": \"Review multiplication facts for 8 (8, 16, 24, 32).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added two zeros instead of one (treated 30 as 300).\", \"remediation\": \"Count the number of zeros in the factors: 30 has exactly one zero, so add one zero to 24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2344, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions on a Number Line', 159, 3, 'A number line from 0 to 1 is divided into 6 equal spaces. Starting from 0, you jump forward 4 spaces. Which fraction represents this point?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '4/6', '2/6', '4/5', '6/4', 'A', 'active', 'Each equal space is 1/6. Jumping 4 spaces from 0 lands on 4/6.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted the remaining spaces to 1 instead of the distance from 0.\", \"remediation\": \"Always measure fractions on a number line starting from 0 moving to the right.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student counted tick marks instead of equal intervals, misidentifying the denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the spaces (intervals) between 0 and 1 to find the denominator.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the fraction on the number line.\", \"remediation\": \"Since the point is between 0 and 1, the numerator must be smaller than the denominator.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2345, 6, 3, 11, 'Equivalent Fractions & Whole Numbers', 159, 3, 'Which fraction is equal to the whole number 4?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '1/4', '4/4', '4/1', '4/2', 'C', 'active', 'Any whole number n can be written as n/1 because 4 whole units divided into 1 part each equals 4 (4 ÷ 1 = 4).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused a whole number (4) with a unit fraction (one-fourth).\", \"remediation\": \"1/4 is less than 1, whereas 4 wholes is represented by 4/1.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student selected 4/4, which equals 1 whole, not 4 wholes.\", \"remediation\": \"When the numerator and denominator are equal, the fraction equals 1 (e.g., 4/4 = 1).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 4/2, which simplifies to 2 wholes (4 ÷ 2 = 2).\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the top number by the bottom number to check: 4 ÷ 1 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2346, 6, 3, 11, 'Comparing Fractions', 159, 3, 'Which statement correctly compares the fractions 3/8 and 5/8?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '3/8 > 5/8', '3/8 < 5/8', '3/8 = 5/8', '5/8 < 3/8', 'B', 'active', 'When fractions have the same denominator (8), compare numerators directly. Since 3 < 5, 3/8 < 5/8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed the inequality symbol, thinking > means less than.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: the wide opening of the symbol always faces the larger quantity.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed the fractions are equal because both denominators are 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the numerators: 5 parts is more than 3 parts of the same size.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student stated that 5 eighths is less than 3 eighths.\", \"remediation\": \"Model both fractions using fraction bars to see that 5 eighths covers more area.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2347, 6, 3, 11, 'Rounding & Place Value', 150, 3, 'A bookstore has 248 science books and 361 math books. About how many books are there in total, rounded to the nearest hundred?', NULL, 3, 'High', '500', '600', '700', '610', 'B', 'active', 'Round each number to the nearest hundred: 248 rounds to 200, and 361 rounds to 400. Estimated sum = 200 + 400 = 600 books.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded both numbers down (200 + 300 = 500) ignoring that 361 is closer to 400.\", \"remediation\": \"Check each number individually: since the tens digit in 361 is 6, it rounds up to 400.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded both numbers up (300 + 400 = 700).\", \"remediation\": \"Look at the tens digit of 248 (4); since 4 is less than 5, 248 rounds down to 200.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to the nearest ten instead of the nearest hundred.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that rounding to the nearest hundred results in a number ending in two zeros (e.g., 600).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2348, 6, 3, 11, '3-Digit Addition & Subtraction', 360, 3, 'What is 600 - 247?', NULL, 3, 'High', '447', '363', '353', '453', 'C', 'active', 'Regroup 1 hundred from 6 to make 9 tens and 10 ones. Ones: 10 - 7 = 3. Tens: 9 - 4 = 5. Hundreds: 5 - 2 = 3. Total = 353.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student brought down the bottom digits 4 and 7 without regrouping from the hundreds place.\", \"remediation\": \"You cannot subtract from 0 without borrowing from the left first.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error in the tens column (10 - 4 = 6 instead of 9 - 4 = 5).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that when borrowing across zeros, the middle tens column becomes 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to decrease the hundreds digit from 6 to 5 after borrowing.\", \"remediation\": \"Decompose 600 into 5 hundreds, 9 tens, and 10 ones before subtracting each column.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2349, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions on a Number Line', 159, 3, 'A number line starts at 0. Each whole unit (from 0 to 1, and 1 to 2) is divided into 3 equal parts (thirds). Which fraction is located at 5 tick marks past 0?', NULL, 3, 'High', '3/5', '5/3', '5/6', '2/3', 'B', 'active', 'Each space represents 1/3. Five jumps of 1/3 equals 5/3 (which is equal to 1 whole and 2/3).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the fraction, confusing the interval size (thirds) with the count.\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator is the number of parts in ONE whole (3), so 5 parts of size 1/3 is 5/3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student counted all intervals across both whole units (3 + 3 = 6) as the denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"Denominator is defined by the number of equal parts in a SINGLE whole, not across multiple wholes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student looked only at the fractional part beyond 1 and omitted the whole unit.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that 5 thirds is 1 whole (3/3) plus 2 more thirds (2/3), giving 5/3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2350, 6, 3, 11, 'Comparing Fractions', 159, 3, 'Which comparison is true, and why?', NULL, 3, 'High', '1/3 > 1/6 because third-parts are larger than sixth-parts', '1/6 > 1/3 because 6 is greater than 3', '1/3 = 1/6 because both have a numerator of 1', '1/6 > 1/3 because a circle cut into 6 slices has more slices', 'A', 'active', 'When dividing a whole into fewer pieces (3), each piece is larger than when dividing into more pieces (6). Thus 1/3 > 1/6.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student applied whole-number rules to denominators, assuming a bigger denominator means a bigger fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: the more pieces you cut a whole into, the SMALLER each individual piece becomes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought fractions with the same numerator are always equal regardless of denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator determines the size of each piece; 1 big third is not equal to 1 small sixth.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused having more pieces with having a larger portion.\", \"remediation\": \"More slices means smaller slices when sharing one whole.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2351, 6, 3, 11, 'Properties of Operations', 157, 3, 'Which expression shows how to break down 7 × 8 using the Distributive Property?', NULL, 3, 'High', '(7 × 5) + (7 × 3)', '(7 + 5) × (7 + 3)', '(7 × 8) + 1', '7 + 8', 'A', 'active', 'Break 8 into 5 + 3. Distribute 7 to both parts: 7 × 8 = 7 × (5 + 3) = (7 × 5) + (7 × 3) = 35 + 21 = 56.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added inside parentheses instead of multiplying the distributed factor.\", \"remediation\": \"In the distributive property, multiply the outside factor by each addend inside the parentheses.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 1 to the product instead of breaking down a factor.\", \"remediation\": \"Distributive property breaks down difficult multiplication into two simpler known multiplication facts.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student replaced multiplication with addition.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that multiplication represents repeated addition of groups, not simple addition of factors.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2352, 6, 3, 11, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 191, 3, 'Maya bought 4 packs of juice boxes. Each pack has 6 juice boxes. Her friends drank 9 juice boxes at a party. How many juice boxes are left?', NULL, 3, 'High', '24', '15', '33', '19', 'B', 'active', 'Step 1: Total juice boxes = 4 × 6 = 24. Step 2: Subtract juice boxes drank: 24 - 9 = 15 juice boxes left.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated only Step 1 (4 × 6 = 24) and forgot to subtract the 9 juice boxes drank.\", \"remediation\": \"Complete both steps of the word problem: first find the starting total, then subtract what was used.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 24 + 9 instead of subtracting what was drank.\", \"remediation\": \"Keywords: \\\"drank\\\" or \\\"left\\\" means items were taken away, which requires subtraction.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made a subtraction calculation error in 24 - 9 (subtracted 5 instead of 9).\", \"remediation\": \"Break down 9 into 4 and 5: 24 - 4 = 20, then 20 - 5 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:17:02', '2026-09-11 18:44:02'),
(2353, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value & 10x Relationships', 161, 4, 'In the number 550, how does the value of the 5 in the hundreds place compare to the value of the 5 in the tens place?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'It is 100 times greater', 'It is 10 less', 'It is 10 times greater', 'They have the exact same value', 'C', 'active', 'The 5 in the hundreds place represents 500. The 5 in the tens place represents 50. Since 500 = 50 × 10, it is 10 times greater.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused the 10x adjacent step with a 100x jump (confused tens place with ones place).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: each step to the left on a place value chart multiplies the value by 10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted instead of recognizing the multiplicative 10x relationship.\", \"remediation\": \"Place value positions grow by multiplying by 10, not by addition or subtraction.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student looked only at the face value (both are the digit 5) ignoring their positions.\", \"remediation\": \"Always consider the column position: 5 hundreds (500) is much larger than 5 tens (50).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2354, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Representation & Expanded Form', 161, 4, 'What is the standard form of 60,000 + 4,000 + 300 + 20 + 8?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '64,328', '604,328', '640,328', '64,238', 'A', 'active', 'Combine the place values: 6 ten thousands, 4 thousands, 3 hundreds, 2 tens, and 8 ones = 64,328.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student inserted an extra zero, shifting 60,000 into the hundred-thousands place.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the digits: 60,000 has 5 digits, so the standard number must be a 5-digit number.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student inserted an extra zero in the thousands place.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a place-value grid to place each digit into its matching column (T-Th, Th, H, T, O).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed the tens and ones digits (wrote 38 instead of 28).\", \"remediation\": \"Match each part carefully: 20 is 2 tens, and 8 is 8 ones.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2355, 6, 3, 11, 'Rounding Multi-Digit Numbers', 161, 4, 'What is 43,628 rounded to the nearest thousand?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '43,000', '43,600', '50,000', '44,000', 'D', 'active', 'To round to the nearest thousand, look at the hundreds digit (6). Since 6 >= 5, round up: 43 thousands becomes 44,000.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded down to 43,000 even though the hundreds digit (6) is 5 or greater.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow the rounding rule: if the digit to the right is 5, 6, 7, 8, or 9, round UP to the next thousand.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to the nearest hundred instead of the nearest thousand.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the question target: rounding to the nearest thousand means replacing hundreds, tens, and ones with zeros.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to the nearest ten thousand instead of nearest thousand.\", \"remediation\": \"Underline the thousands place digit (3) and circle the hundreds digit (6) before rounding.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2356, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Addition & Subtraction', 167, 4, 'What is 34,512 + 23,169?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '57,671', '57,681', '57,680', '58,681', 'B', 'active', 'Add column by column: Ones: 2 + 9 = 11 (write 1, carry 1). Tens: 1 + 6 + 1 = 8. Hundreds: 5 + 1 = 6. Thousands: 4 + 3 = 7. Ten thousands: 3 + 2 = 5. Total = 57,681.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add the carried 1 in the tens column (1 + 6 = 7).\", \"remediation\": \"Always write carried digits at the top of the next column and cross them off after adding.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an addition error in the ones column (2 + 9 = 10 instead of 11).\", \"remediation\": \"Practice single-digit sums that cross 10 using mental math or finger counting.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student carried an extra 1 into the thousands column when no carrying was required.\", \"remediation\": \"Only carry when the column sum is 10 or greater.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2357, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Multiplication', 167, 4, 'What is 423 × 4?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '1,682', '1,612', '1,692', '16,92', 'C', 'active', '4 × 3 ones = 12 (write 2, carry 1 ten). 4 × 2 tens = 8 + 1 = 9 tens. 4 × 4 hundreds = 16 hundreds. Total = 1,692.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add the carried 1 ten to the tens place (4 × 2 = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to multiply first, then add the carried amount: (4 × 2) + 1 = 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student did not carry to the tens place and wrote 1 directly.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice vertical multiplication with clear regrouping boxes at the top.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student misplaced the thousands comma.\", \"remediation\": \"Count three digits from the right to place the comma (1,692).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2358, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Multiplication', 167, 4, 'What is 20 × 30?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '60', '600', '6,000', '500', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply the non-zero digits: 2 × 3 = 6. Count the zeros in both factors: two zeros. Append two zeros: 600.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student appended only one zero instead of two (20 × 3 = 60).\", \"remediation\": \"Count all ending zeros in both factors: 20 has one zero and 30 has one zero, so total 2 zeros.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added too many zeros (thought 20 × 30 = 6,000).\", \"remediation\": \"Match the zero count: 1 zero + 1 zero = 2 zeros in the product.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 20 + 30 = 50 and appended a zero.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that multiplication scales factors; 2 tens × 3 tens = 6 hundreds (600).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2359, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Division & Remainders', 167, 4, 'What is 428 ÷ 4?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '17', '170', '102', '107', 'D', 'active', '4 ÷ 4 = 1 hundred. 2 tens ÷ 4 = 0 tens (remainder 2). 28 ones ÷ 4 = 7 ones. Result = 107.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted the zero in the tens place (wrote 17 instead of 107).\", \"remediation\": \"When a divisor does not go into a digit (2 < 4), you MUST put a 0 in the quotient before bringing down the next digit.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student placed the zero at the end instead of in the tens column.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep quotient digits strictly aligned above the dividend column being divided.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a division fact error with 28 ÷ 4 (thought it was 2).\", \"remediation\": \"Review the 4 times table: 4 × 7 = 28.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2360, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value & 10x Relationships', 161, 4, 'How many times larger is 7,000 than 700?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '10 times', '100 times', '7 times', '1,000 times', 'A', 'active', '7,000 ÷ 700 = 10. Since thousands is exactly one place to the left of hundreds, it is 10 times larger.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought the difference of 2 zeros vs 3 zeros meant 100 times.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare the number of zeros: 7,000 has only one more zero than 700, which means 10 times.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student used the leading digit (7) as the ratio.\", \"remediation\": \"The ratio depends on place value position (powers of 10), not the face value digit.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student named the place value of the larger number instead of the comparison ratio.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the larger number by the smaller number: 7,000 ÷ 700 = 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2361, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Representation & Expanded Form', 161, 4, 'Which number represents \"Five hundred four thousand, twenty-six\"?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '540,026', '504,260', '504,026', '5,426', 'C', 'active', 'Thousands period: \"Five hundred four thousand\" = 504. Units period: \"twenty-six\" = 026. Combined = 504,026.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused 504 thousand with 540 thousand (put 4 in ten-thousands instead of thousands).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Five hundred four\\\" means 5 hundreds and 4 ones in the thousands period (504).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student wrote twenty-six as 260 (confused tens with ones).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Twenty-six\\\" has 2 tens and 6 ones (26), not 26 tens.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student dropped internal placeholder zeros for hundreds and ten-thousands.\", \"remediation\": \"Every multi-digit standard number requires 3 digits in each period (use 0 for empty places).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2362, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Representation & Expanded Form', 161, 4, 'Which symbol makes this comparison true: 348,219 __ 348,291?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '>', '<', '=', '+', 'B', 'active', 'Compare from left to right: Hundred-thousands (3=3), Ten-thousands (4=4), Thousands (8=8), Hundreds (2=2). In the tens place, 1 < 9. Therefore, 348,219 < 348,291.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student looked at the ones place (9 > 1) instead of comparing higher place value (tens: 1 < 9).\", \"remediation\": \"Always compare digits starting from left to right; stop at the first place where digits differ.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student saw that both numbers have the same set of digits and assumed they are equal.\", \"remediation\": \"The position of each digit changes its value; 1 ten is not equal to 9 tens.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student selected an operation symbol instead of a comparison symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"Use < (less than) when the number on the left is smaller.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 11:54:45'),
(2363, 6, 3, 11, 'Rounding Multi-Digit Numbers', 161, 4, 'What is 284,519 rounded to the nearest ten thousand?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '290,000', '280,000', '285,000', '300,000', 'B', 'active', 'The ten-thousands digit is 8. The digit to its right (thousands digit) is 4. Since 4 < 5, round down to 280,000.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded up to 290,000 because of the 5 in hundreds place instead of checking the thousands place (4).\", \"remediation\": \"Only look at the single digit immediately to the right of your target place (thousands digit 4).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to the nearest thousand instead of ten thousand.\", \"remediation\": \"Rounding to nearest ten thousand means replacing thousands, hundreds, tens, and ones with four zeros.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to the nearest hundred thousand.\", \"remediation\": \"Identify the target column: ten-thousands is the second digit from the left in a 6-digit number.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2364, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Addition & Subtraction', 167, 4, 'What is 248,395 + 176,824?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '425,119', '415,219', '424,119', '425,219', 'D', 'active', 'Ones: 5+4=9. Tens: 9+2=11 (write 1, carry 1). Hundreds: 3+8+1=12 (write 2, carry 1). Thousands: 8+6+1=15 (write 5, carry 1). Ten-thousands: 4+7+1=12 (write 2, carry 1). Hundred-thousands: 2+1+1=4. Total = 425,219.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a calculation error in the hundreds column (3 + 8 + 1 = 11 instead of 12).\", \"remediation\": \"Double-check sums greater than 10 using the make-ten strategy: 8 + 3 = 11, + 1 = 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student missed carrying into the ten-thousands place (4 + 7 = 11).\", \"remediation\": \"Track each carried 1 carefully above each column in multi-regrouping problems.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to carry into the thousands place.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice multi-digit addition on lined grid paper to keep columns organized.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2365, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Addition & Subtraction', 167, 4, 'What is 62,415 - 28,173?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '34,242', '44,242', '34,342', '33,242', 'A', 'active', 'Ones: 5-3=2. Tens: 11-7=4 (borrow from 4 hundreds). Hundreds: 3-1=2. Thousands: 12-8=4 (borrow from 6 ten-thousands). Ten-thousands: 5-2=3. Total = 34,242.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to decrease 6 to 5 after borrowing for the thousands column (6 - 2 = 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Always cross out and decrease the borrowed column before doing the subtraction.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot that 4 hundreds was reduced to 3 after borrowing for tens (calculated 4 - 1 = 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Rewrite the new values above the columns immediately when borrowing.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made a subtraction error in the thousands column (12 - 8 = 3 instead of 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Use addition to check: 8 + 4 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2366, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Division & Remainders', 167, 4, 'What is 539 ÷ 5?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '109 R 4', '17 R 4', '107 R 4', '107 R 0', 'C', 'active', '5 ÷ 5 = 1. 3 ÷ 5 = 0 (carry 3). 39 ÷ 5 = 7 with remainder 4 (5 × 7 = 35, 39 - 35 = 4). Quotient = 107 R 4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a division fact error (thought 5 × 9 = 35).\", \"remediation\": \"Review 5s multiplication facts: 5 × 7 = 35 and 5 × 8 = 40.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student missed the 0 in the tens place of the quotient.\", \"remediation\": \"Since 5 goes into 3 zero times, write 0 in the quotient before bringing down 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to write the remainder.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the product from the dividend part: 39 - 35 = 4 remainder.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2367, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value & 10x Relationships', 161, 4, 'How does the value of the 4 in 40,000 compare to the value of the 4 in 400?', NULL, 4, 'High', 'It is 10 times greater', 'It is 1,000 times greater', 'It is 40 times greater', 'It is 100 times greater', 'D', 'active', '40,000 ÷ 400 = 100. Moving two places to the left on a place value chart (hundreds -> thousands -> ten-thousands) multiplies the value by 10 × 10 = 100.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed all place value comparisons are 10 times, not noticing a two-column jump.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the number of columns shifted: 1 jump = 10x, 2 jumps = 100x, 3 jumps = 1,000x.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted 3 extra zeros by mistake.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide: 40,000 ÷ 400 = 100 (remove two zeros from both).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused the face value (4) with the base-ten power.\", \"remediation\": \"Powers of ten are always 10, 100, 1,000, etc., never multiples of the digit.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2368, 6, 3, 11, 'Rounding Multi-Digit Numbers', 161, 4, 'What is the SMALLEST whole number that rounds to 50,000 when rounded to the nearest ten thousand?', NULL, 4, 'High', '54,999', '45,000', '49,500', '44,999', 'B', 'active', 'To round up to 50,000 to the nearest ten thousand, the thousands digit must be at least 5. The smallest number is 45,000.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student selected the LARGEST number that rounds to 50,000 instead of the smallest.\", \"remediation\": \"Read the question carefully: it asks for the SMALLEST whole number, which is 45,000.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 49,500 which is rounded to nearest thousand, not the minimum for ten-thousand.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the cutoff: any number from 45,000 to 54,999 rounds to 50,000; the smallest is 45,000.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 44,999, which rounds down to 40,000 because the thousands digit is 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: 4 rounds down; you need a 5 in the thousands place to round up to 50,000.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2369, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Addition & Subtraction', 167, 4, 'What is 500,000 - 184,327?', NULL, 4, 'High', '325,673', '415,673', '315,673', '316,673', 'C', 'active', 'Borrow 1 hundred-thousand from 5, leaving 4. Middle zeros become 9s: 9 ten-thousands, 9 thousands, 9 hundreds, 9 tens, and 10 ones. Subtracting gives 315,673.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error in the ten-thousands column (calculated 10 - 8 instead of 9 - 8).\", \"remediation\": \"When borrowing across multiple zeros, all intermediate zeros become 9, and only the final ones digit becomes 10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to decrement the leading 5 to 4 after borrowing.\", \"remediation\": \"Always decrease the original source column from which you borrowed.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an off-by-one subtraction error in the thousands column (9 - 4 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Check each column subtraction: 9 - 4 = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2370, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Multiplication', 167, 4, 'What is 36 × 24?', NULL, 4, 'High', '864', '216', '724', '844', 'A', 'active', 'Using partial products: (30 × 20) + (30 × 4) + (6 × 20) + (6 × 4) = 600 + 120 + 120 + 24 = 864.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student completed only the first partial product (36 × 6) or (36 × 4 = 144) without finishing.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by both the ones digit and the tens digit, then add both partial products together.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot the placeholder zero when multiplying by the tens digit (20).\", \"remediation\": \"When multiplying by 2 tens, the partial product must end in 0 (36 × 20 = 720).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made a regrouping addition error in the tens column.\", \"remediation\": \"Align partial products vertically: 144 + 720 = 864.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2371, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Multiplication', 167, 4, 'A factory packs 3,245 boxes of toys every day. How many boxes of toys does the factory pack in 6 days?', NULL, 4, 'High', '18,270', '19,270', '19,470', '19,460', 'C', 'active', 'Multiply: 6 × 5 = 30 (write 0, carry 3). 6 × 4 = 24 + 3 = 27 (write 7, carry 2). 6 × 2 = 12 + 2 = 14 (write 4, carry 1). 6 × 3 = 18 + 1 = 19. Total = 19,470.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot all carried digits into the hundreds and thousands columns.\", \"remediation\": \"Write carried digits above each column and add them after multiplying.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to carry 2 into the hundreds column (6 × 2 = 12, forgot +2 = 14).\", \"remediation\": \"Check each column step: (6 × 2) + 2 = 14.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student misadded carried digits in the tens column (24 + 3 = 26 instead of 27).\", \"remediation\": \"Double-check simple addition of carried amounts.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2372, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Division & Remainders', 167, 4, 'A farmer collected 3,124 eggs. He packs them into cartons that hold 6 eggs each. How many FULL cartons can he pack, and how many eggs will be left over?', NULL, 4, 'High', '520 full cartons, 0 eggs left over', '52 full cartons, 4 eggs left over', '524 full cartons, 0 eggs left over', '520 full cartons, 4 eggs left over', 'D', 'active', 'Divide: 3,124 ÷ 6. 31 ÷ 6 = 5 (remainder 1). 12 ÷ 6 = 2 (remainder 0). 4 ÷ 6 = 0 (remainder 4). Result: 520 full cartons with 4 eggs left over.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student ignored the remainder of 4 eggs completely.\", \"remediation\": \"Always report leftover units: 3,124 = (520 × 6) + 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted the zero in the quotient ones place (wrote 52 instead of 520).\", \"remediation\": \"Since 6 goes into 4 zero times, you MUST record 0 in the quotient before stating the remainder.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forced 4 into the quotient without dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"A remainder must be smaller than the divisor (4 < 6) and cannot be added as a quotient digit.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 14:25:58', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2373, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value & Powers of 10', 161, 5, 'What is 4.5 × 10?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '4.50', '45', '0.45', '450', 'B', 'active', 'Multiplying by 10 moves the decimal point 1 place to the right: 4.5 × 10 = 45.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added a zero at the end without moving the decimal point.\", \"remediation\": \"When multiplying by 10, move the decimal point 1 place to the right.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student moved the decimal point to the left instead of the right.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplying makes a number larger, so move the decimal point to the right.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student moved the decimal point 2 places instead of 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the zeros in 10: there is 1 zero, so move the decimal 1 place to the right.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2374, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimals to Thousandths', 161, 5, 'Which symbol makes this statement true: 0.6 __ 0.09?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '>', '+', '<', '=', 'A', 'active', 'Compare the tenths place: 0.6 has 6 tenths, while 0.09 has 0 tenths. Since 6 tenths > 0 tenths, 0.6 > 0.09.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student selected an operation symbol instead of a comparison symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the > symbol when the number on the left is larger.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought 0.09 is larger because 9 is greater than 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare the tenths place first: 6 tenths is much larger than 0 tenths.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed the numbers are equal.\", \"remediation\": \"Line up the decimal points to compare place values column by column.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2375, 6, 3, 11, 'Rounding Decimals', 161, 5, 'What is 3.74 rounded to the nearest tenth?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '3.8', '3.7', '4.0', '3.70', 'B', 'active', 'The tenths digit is 7. The hundredths digit is 4. Since 4 < 5, round down: 3.7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded up even though the hundredths digit is 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: if the next digit is 4 or less, keep the target digit the same (round down).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to the nearest whole number instead of the nearest tenth.\", \"remediation\": \"Nearest tenth means keeping 1 digit after the decimal point.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student kept the extra zero instead of rounding to tenths.\", \"remediation\": \"Rounding to tenths gives a single decimal place (3.7).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2376, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit & Decimal Operations', 162, 5, 'What is 2.4 + 1.35?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '3.9', '1.59', '3.39', '3.75', 'D', 'active', 'Line up decimals: 2.40 + 1.35 = 3.75.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student misadded the decimal parts.\", \"remediation\": \"Add hundredths first (0 + 5 = 5), then tenths (4 + 3 = 7).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student lined up digits from the right without aligning decimal points.\", \"remediation\": \"Always line up the decimal points before adding: 2.40 + 1.35 = 3.75.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added ones to tenths without aligning place values.\", \"remediation\": \"Put a placeholder 0 at the end of 2.4 to make it 2.40.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2377, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Addition & Subtraction', 163, 5, 'What is 2/5 + 1/5?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '3/5', '3/10', '2/10', '3/25', 'A', 'active', 'When denominators are the same, add numerators: 2 + 1 = 3, keeping denominator 5. Answer = 3/5.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added both numerators and denominators across.\", \"remediation\": \"When denominators are the same, add only the top numbers (numerators) and keep the denominator.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the numerators and added denominators.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the numerators: 2 + 1 = 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student squared the denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the common denominator unchanged (5).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2378, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Multiplication & Division', 165, 5, 'What is 1/2 × 3/4?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '2/8', '4/6', '3/8', '3/6', 'C', 'active', 'Multiply top numbers (1 × 3 = 3) and bottom numbers (2 × 4 = 8) = 3/8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a multiplication fact error.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 1 × 3 = 3 for the numerator.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added across instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply top numbers together (1 × 3 = 3) and bottom numbers together (2 × 4 = 8).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied only the numerators and added denominators.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply both the top numbers and the bottom numbers.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2379, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Multiplication & Division', 165, 5, 'What is 3 ÷ (1/4)?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '3/4', '1/12', '7', '12', 'D', 'active', 'There are 4 fourths in each whole. In 3 wholes, there are 3 × 4 = 12 fourths.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 3 by 1/4 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"Think: how many 1/4 pieces are in 3 wholes? There are 4 pieces per whole, so 3 × 4 = 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the answer (confused 3 ÷ 1/4 with 1/4 ÷ 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Dividing a whole number by a fraction smaller than 1 gives a larger answer.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 4 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the whole number by the denominator: 3 × 4 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2380, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value & Powers of 10', 161, 5, 'What is the value of 5.2 × 10³?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '0.0052', '5,200', '520', '52,000', 'B', 'active', '10³ = 1,000. Multiplying 5.2 by 1,000 moves the decimal point 3 places to the right: 5,200.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 10^3 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplication by positive powers of 10 makes the number larger.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student moved the decimal point 2 places instead of 3.\", \"remediation\": \"The exponent 3 means multiply by 1,000 (move the decimal point 3 places to the right).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student moved the decimal point 4 places.\", \"remediation\": \"Move the decimal exactly 3 places to the right: 5.2 -> 52 -> 520 -> 5,200.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2381, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimals to Thousandths', 161, 5, 'Which decimal represents (7 × 10) + (3 × 1) + (4 × 1/10) + (8 × 1/1000)?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '73.48', '73.480', '73.408', '73.048', 'C', 'active', 'Tens = 7, Ones = 3, Tenths = 4, Hundredths = 0, Thousandths = 8. Decimal is 73.408.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted the placeholder zero in the hundredths place.\", \"remediation\": \"There are no hundredths (1/100), so put a 0 in the hundredths place: 73.408.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student put the zero in the thousandths place instead of hundredths.\", \"remediation\": \"8 is multiplied by 1/1000, so 8 belongs in the third decimal place.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student placed the zero in the tenths place.\", \"remediation\": \"4 is multiplied by 1/10, so 4 is in the tenths place.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2382, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit & Decimal Operations', 162, 5, 'What is 245 × 18?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '4,410', '4,400', '4,310', '2,410', 'A', 'active', '245 × 8 = 1,960. 245 × 10 = 2,450. Adding partial products: 1,960 + 2,450 = 4,410.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 5 × 8 in the ones place.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 245 × 8 = 1,960 carefully before adding the second row.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a carrying addition error in the hundreds column.\", \"remediation\": \"Check each partial product: 245 × 8 = 1,960 and 245 × 10 = 2,450. Then add: 1,960 + 2,450 = 4,410.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot the first partial product.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by both digits (ones and tens), then add both rows together.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2383, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit & Decimal Operations', 162, 5, 'What is 1,536 ÷ 24?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '54', '604', '64', '74', 'C', 'active', 'Divide: 153 ÷ 24 = 6 (remainder 9). Bring down 6: 96 ÷ 24 = 4. Result = 64.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student underestimated the first quotient digit (used 5 instead of 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Estimate: 24 × 6 = 144, which fits into 153 with a remainder of 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student inserted an unnecessary zero in the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"The quotient has only two digits: 6 tens and 4 ones (64).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student overestimated the quotient.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply your answer to check: 64 × 24 = 1,536.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2384, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit & Decimal Operations', 162, 5, 'What is 0.6 × 0.4?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '24', '2.4', '0.024', '0.24', 'D', 'active', '6 × 4 = 24. There is 1 decimal place in 0.6 and 1 in 0.4, total 2 decimal places: 0.24.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot the decimal point completely.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplying decimals less than 1 results in an answer less than 1.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student placed only 1 decimal digit in the answer.\", \"remediation\": \"Count total decimal places in both numbers: 1 place + 1 place = 2 decimal places (0.24).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added an extra zero before 24.\", \"remediation\": \"6 × 4 = 24. Two decimal places gives exactly 0.24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2385, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Addition & Subtraction', 163, 5, 'What is 1/3 + 2/5?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '3/8', '11/15', '3/15', '7/15', 'B', 'active', 'Common denominator is 15: 1/3 = 5/15 and 2/5 = 6/15. Sum = 5/15 + 6/15 = 11/15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added the numerators and denominators across directly (1+2)/(3+5).\", \"remediation\": \"Find a common denominator first: 1/3 = 5/15 and 2/5 = 6/15. Then add: 5/15 + 6/15 = 11/15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added the numerators without converting to equivalent fractions.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply numerator and denominator by the same factor before adding.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the equivalent fraction numerators.\", \"remediation\": \"Check equivalent numerators: 1 × 5 = 5 and 2 × 3 = 6; 5 + 6 = 11.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2386, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Multiplication & Division', 165, 5, 'What is (1/5) ÷ 3?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '1/15', '5/3', '15', '3/5', 'A', 'active', 'Dividing 1/5 into 3 equal shares: (1/5) ÷ 3 = (1/5) × (1/3) = 1/15.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted both numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the numerator 1 and multiply bottom: 5 × 3 = 15, so 1/15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied instead of dividing (confused with 3 ÷ 1/5).\", \"remediation\": \"When dividing a fraction into 3 equal pieces, each piece becomes smaller: (1/5) ÷ 3 = 1/15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the numerator by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"To divide a fraction by a whole number, multiply the denominator by the whole number.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2387, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit & Decimal Operations', 162, 5, 'What is 4.8 ÷ 0.12?', NULL, 5, 'High', '4', '40', '0.4', '400', 'B', 'active', 'Move decimal point 2 places to the right in both numbers: 4.8 becomes 480, and 0.12 becomes 12. 480 ÷ 12 = 40.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student shifted the decimal in the dividend only 1 place.\", \"remediation\": \"Move the decimal 2 places in both numbers: 0.12 becomes 12, and 4.8 becomes 480. Then 480 ÷ 12 = 40.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student placed the decimal point incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Dividing by a small decimal (0.12) makes the quotient larger than 4.8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student shifted the decimal 3 places instead of 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Count decimal places in the divisor: 0.12 has 2 places, so move 2 places in 4.8 to get 480.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2388, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Addition & Subtraction', 163, 5, 'What is 4 1/4 - 1 2/3?', NULL, 5, 'High', '3 7/12', '3 1/12', '2 1/12', '2 7/12', 'D', 'active', 'Common denominator is 12: 4 3/12 - 1 8/12. Borrow 1 from 4: 3 15/12 - 1 8/12 = 2 7/12.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to decrease the whole number 4 to 3 after borrowing.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to decrease the whole number by 1 when borrowing for the fraction.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted smaller fraction from larger fraction (2/3 - 1/4) without borrowing from 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Borrow 1 whole from 4: 4 3/12 becomes 3 15/12. Then 3 15/12 - 1 8/12 = 2 7/12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a subtraction error after finding a common denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert to common denominator 12 and regroup: 15/12 - 8/12 = 7/12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2389, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Multiplication & Division', 165, 5, 'Without calculating, which statement about 4/5 × 7 is true?', NULL, 5, 'High', 'The product is less than 7 because 4/5 is less than 1', 'The product is greater than 7 because multiplication always increases', 'The product is equal to 7', 'The product is less than 4/5', 'A', 'active', 'Multiplying any positive number by a fraction less than 1 results in a product smaller than the original number.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed multiplying always results in a larger number.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplying by a fraction less than 1 scales the number DOWN (makes it smaller).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought multiplying by fractions does not change the whole number.\", \"remediation\": \"Only multiplying by 1 keeps a number equal.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused the scaling factor: multiplying 7 by 4/5 gives a value much larger than 4/5.\", \"remediation\": \"7 groups of 4/5 is 28/5, which is greater than 4/5 but less than 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:23', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2390, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Addition & Subtraction', 163, 5, 'Liam had 3/4 gallon of paint. He used 1/3 gallon to paint a chair and 1/6 gallon to paint a shelf. How much paint does Liam have left?', NULL, 5, 'High', '3/12 gallon', '1/2 gallon', '1/4 gallon', '1/12 gallon', 'C', 'active', 'Total paint used: 1/3 + 1/6 = 2/6 + 1/6 = 3/6 = 1/2 gallon. Left over: 3/4 - 1/2 = 3/4 - 2/4 = 1/4 gallon.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student found 3/12 but did not simplify to simplest form.\", \"remediation\": \"Simplify 3/12 by dividing top and bottom by 3 to get 1/4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the total paint used (1/3 + 1/6 = 1/2) but forgot to subtract it from 3/4.\", \"remediation\": \"Complete the final step: subtract total paint used (1/2 or 2/4) from starting paint (3/4): 3/4 - 2/4 = 1/4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error when finding common denominator 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert all to 12ths: 9/12 - 4/12 - 2/12 = 3/12 = 1/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:24', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2391, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit & Decimal Operations', 162, 5, 'A bookstore sells 318 calendars each month. How many calendars will the bookstore sell in 24 months?', NULL, 5, 'High', '7,532', '6,632', '7,622', '7,632', 'D', 'active', '318 × 4 = 1,272. 318 × 20 = 6,360. Sum = 1,272 + 6,360 = 7,632.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add the carried ten in the hundreds column.\", \"remediation\": \"Check partial products: 318 × 4 = 1,272 and 318 × 20 = 6,360. Sum: 1,272 + 6,360 = 7,632.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student missed carrying into the thousands place.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice vertical column addition of the two partial products.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an off-by-ten subtraction/addition error in the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"Double-check 7 tens + 6 tens = 13 tens (carry 1 hundred).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:24', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2392, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Multiplication & Division', 165, 5, 'A recipe calls for 2 1/2 cups of flour. Chef Leo wants to make 3 1/2 batches of the recipe. How many cups of flour does Chef Leo need?', NULL, 5, 'High', '8 1/2 cups', '8 3/4 cups', '6 1/4 cups', '7 1/2 cups', 'B', 'active', 'Convert to improper fractions: 2 1/2 = 5/2, and 3 1/2 = 7/2. Multiply: (5/2) × (7/2) = 35/4 = 8 3/4 cups.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a division error when converting 35/4 to a mixed number.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 35 by 4: 4 × 8 = 32, with a remainder of 3, giving 8 3/4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the whole numbers (2 × 3 = 6) and fractions (1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4) separately.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert mixed numbers to improper fractions first: 5/2 × 7/2 = 35/4 = 8 3/4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the mixed numbers instead of multiplying for batches.\", \"remediation\": \"Finding the amount for 3 1/2 batches requires multiplication, not addition.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:24:24', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2393, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Division', 166, 6, 'What is 3/4 ÷ 1/8?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '3/32', '1/6', '6', '8/12', 'C', 'active', 'Multiply by the reciprocal: 3/4 ÷ 1/8 = 3/4 × 8/1 = 24/4 = 6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the fractions directly without flipping the second fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to multiply by the reciprocal (flip the second fraction): 3/4 × 8/1.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the answer (confused 3/4 ÷ 1/8 with 1/8 ÷ 3/4).\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the first fraction as it is, and flip only the divisor (second fraction).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student cross-multiplied incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply straight across after flipping: (3 × 8) / (4 × 1) = 24/4 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2394, 6, 3, 11, 'Factors & Multiples', 168, 6, 'What is the Greatest Common Factor (GCF) of 18 and 24?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '6', '72', '3', '12', 'A', 'active', 'Factors of 18: 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18. Factors of 24: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24. The largest common factor is 6.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the Least Common Multiple (LCM) instead of the GCF.\", \"remediation\": \"GCF divides into both numbers (smaller value), while LCM is a multiple (larger value).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student found a common factor (3) but not the GREATEST common factor.\", \"remediation\": \"List all common factors (1, 2, 3, 6) and pick the largest one (6).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 12, which is a factor of 24 but NOT a factor of 18.\", \"remediation\": \"Check that the factor divides both numbers evenly without a remainder.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2395, 6, 3, 11, 'Factors & Multiples', 168, 6, 'What is the Least Common Multiple (LCM) of 4 and 6?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '24', '2', '12', '18', 'C', 'active', 'Multiples of 4: 4, 8, 12, 16... Multiples of 6: 6, 12, 18... The smallest shared multiple is 12.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4 × 6 = 24, which is a common multiple but not the LEAST.\", \"remediation\": \"List multiples in order to find the smallest number that appears in both lists (12).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student found the GCF (2) instead of the LCM.\", \"remediation\": \"LCM must be greater than or equal to both numbers (at least 6).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 18, which is a multiple of 6 but not of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Ensure the number is divisible by both 4 and 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2396, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 169, 6, 'What is the opposite of -7?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '1/7', '-7', '0', '7', 'D', 'active', 'The opposite of a negative number is positive: -(-7) = 7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused opposite (additive inverse) with reciprocal (multiplicative inverse).\", \"remediation\": \"Opposite means changing the sign (+ to - or - to +), not flipping the fraction.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student selected the same number instead of its opposite.\", \"remediation\": \"The opposite of a number has the same distance from 0 but with the opposite sign.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 0 (the reference point), not the opposite.\", \"remediation\": \"Opposite of -7 is positive 7 because both are 7 units away from 0.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2397, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 169, 6, 'What is the absolute value of -15, written as |-15|?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '-15', '15', '0', '-1/15', 'B', 'active', 'Absolute value represents distance from 0 on a number line, which is always non-negative: |-15| = 15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought absolute value preserves negative signs.\", \"remediation\": \"Absolute value measures distance from 0, and distance is always positive: |-15| = 15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 0.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the units from -15 to 0 on a number line: there are 15 units.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused absolute value with negative reciprocal.\", \"remediation\": \"Remove the negative sign to find the absolute value.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2398, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Computation', 167, 6, 'What is 12.5 + 3.84?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '16.34', '5.09', '15.34', '16.89', 'A', 'active', 'Line up decimals: 12.50 + 3.84 = 16.34.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student lined numbers up from the right without looking at the decimal point.\", \"remediation\": \"Always align the decimal points vertically before adding.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to carry the 1 from 5 + 8 = 13 tenths.\", \"remediation\": \"When tenths add up to 10 or more, carry 1 whole to the ones place.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student misaligned the decimal places (added 5 to 4 and 8 to 2).\", \"remediation\": \"Pad 12.5 with a zero to make it 12.50 so all columns align correctly.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2399, 6, 3, 11, 'Coordinate Plane', 170, 6, 'In which quadrant of the coordinate plane is the point (-3, 5) located?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'Quadrant I', 'Quadrant II', 'Quadrant III', 'Quadrant IV', 'B', 'active', 'Points with negative x-coordinate and positive y-coordinate (-x, +y) are located in Quadrant II.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose Quadrant I, where both x and y are positive (+, +).\", \"remediation\": \"Quadrant I has positive x and positive y; since x is -3, it moves left into Quadrant II.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose Quadrant III, where both coordinates are negative (-, -).\", \"remediation\": \"Quadrant III has negative x and negative y; here y is positive 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed coordinates and chose Quadrant IV (+, -).\", \"remediation\": \"Follow the order: x is first (-3 = left) and y is second (5 = up), which lands in Quadrant II.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2400, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Division', 166, 6, 'What is 5/6 ÷ 2/3 in simplest form?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '1 1/2', '5/9', '10/18', '1 1/4', 'D', 'active', 'Multiply by reciprocal: 5/6 × 3/2 = 15/12 = 5/4 = 1 1/4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic reduction error (thought 15/12 simplifies to 3/2).\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 15 and 12 by their GCF 3 to get 5/4, which is 1 1/4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied directly without flipping 2/3: (5×2)/(6×3) = 10/18 = 5/9.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: Keep the first fraction, Change division to multiplication, Flip the second fraction (KCF).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied without flipping and did not simplify.\", \"remediation\": \"Flip the divisor: 5/6 × 3/2 = 15/12, then simplify to 1 1/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2401, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Computation', 167, 6, 'What is 2,808 ÷ 36?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '78', '68', '708', '88', 'A', 'active', 'Divide: 280 ÷ 36 = 7 (36 × 7 = 252). 280 - 252 = 28. Bring down 8: 288 ÷ 36 = 8 (36 × 8 = 288). Quotient = 78.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student underestimated the first quotient digit (used 6 instead of 7).\", \"remediation\": \"Estimate: 36 × 7 = 252, leaving 28 which is less than 36.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student inserted an unnecessary zero in the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"The quotient has 2 digits: 7 tens and 8 ones (78).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student overestimated the first quotient digit.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply: 78 × 36 = 2,808 to verify your answer.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2402, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Computation', 167, 6, 'What is 4.25 × 0.6?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '2.45', '25.5', '2.55', '0.255', 'C', 'active', 'Multiply as whole numbers: 425 × 6 = 2,550. Count decimal places: 2 in 4.25 and 1 in 0.6 = 3 places. 2.550 = 2.55.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a multiplication fact error (425 × 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate: 6 × 5 = 30, 6 × 20 = 120, 6 × 400 = 2400. Sum = 2550.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student placed only 1 decimal digit in the answer.\", \"remediation\": \"Count all decimal places in both factors: 2 places + 1 place = 3 decimal places (2.550 = 2.55).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student shifted the decimal 4 places instead of 3.\", \"remediation\": \"425 × 6 = 2550. Move the decimal point 3 places to the left: 2.550.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2403, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 169, 6, 'Which inequality correctly compares -8 and -3?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '-8 > -3', '-8 = -3', '-3 < -8', '-8 < -3', 'D', 'active', 'On a number line, numbers further to the left are smaller. -8 is further to the left than -3, so -8 < -3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student applied positive number rules (8 > 3) to negative numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"With negative numbers, larger absolute value means smaller quantity (-8 is colder than -3).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought both are equal because both are negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative numbers differ in value based on their distance from 0.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student stated that -3 is less than -8.\", \"remediation\": \"-3 is closer to 0 and to the right of -8, so -3 is GREATER than -8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2404, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 169, 6, 'Order these numbers from least to greatest: -4, 2.5, -1.5, 0', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '2.5, 0, -1.5, -4', '-4, -1.5, 0, 2.5', '-1.5, -4, 0, 2.5', '0, -1.5, 2.5, -4', 'B', 'active', 'Negative numbers are smallest: -4 is less than -1.5. Then 0, then positive 2.5: -4 < -1.5 < 0 < 2.5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student ordered from greatest to least instead of least to greatest.\", \"remediation\": \"Read the order requested: least to greatest means starting with the most negative number.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought -1.5 is less than -4.\", \"remediation\": \"-4 is further to the left on the number line, so -4 is the least.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student placed 0 first.\", \"remediation\": \"All negative numbers are less than 0.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2405, 6, 3, 11, 'Factors & Multiples', 168, 6, 'Which expression uses the Distributive Property and the GCF to rewrite 36 + 48?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '6(6 + 8)', '4(9 + 12)', '12(3 + 4)', '12(3 × 4)', 'C', 'active', 'The GCF of 36 and 48 is 12. Factoring out 12: 36 ÷ 12 = 3 and 48 ÷ 12 = 4. Expression is 12(3 + 4).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student factored out 6, which is a common factor but NOT the greatest common factor (12).\", \"remediation\": \"Always factor out the GREATEST common factor: 3 and 4 have no common factor except 1.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student factored out 4 instead of the GCF 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Inside the parentheses, the two terms must share no common factor (relatively prime).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied inside the parentheses instead of adding.\", \"remediation\": \"Preserve the addition operation inside parentheses: 12(3 + 4).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2406, 6, 3, 11, 'Coordinate Plane', 170, 6, 'What is the distance between point A(-2, 4) and point B(5, 4) on the coordinate plane?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '7 units', '8 units', '3 units', '9 units', 'A', 'active', 'Since the y-coordinates are the same (4), subtract the x-coordinates: 5 - (-2) = 5 + 2 = 7 units.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added the y-coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"Since y-coordinates are identical, distance is measured only along the x-axis.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 5 - 2 = 3 instead of subtracting the negative coordinate: 5 - (-2).\", \"remediation\": \"When points are on opposite sides of the y-axis, add their absolute distances from the axis: |-2| + |5| = 2 + 5 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"Count horizontal units from -2 to 5: -2 to 0 is 2 units, and 0 to 5 is 5 units. Total = 7 units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2407, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Division', 166, 6, 'A ribbon is 2 1/4 feet long. Maya cuts it into pieces that are each 3/8 foot long. How many complete pieces can Maya cut?', NULL, 6, 'High', '5 pieces', '7 pieces', '6 pieces', '8 pieces', 'C', 'active', 'Convert to improper fraction: 2 1/4 = 9/4. Divide: 9/4 ÷ 3/8 = 9/4 × 8/3 = 72/12 = 6 pieces.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a multiplication error in 9/4 × 8/3.\", \"remediation\": \"Simplify before multiplying: (9÷3) × (8÷4) = 3 × 2 = 6 pieces.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded up an incorrect estimate.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate exactly: 9/4 × 8/3 = 72/12 = 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the wrong fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"Flip only the divisor (3/8 becomes 8/3).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2408, 6, 3, 11, 'Factors & Multiples', 168, 6, 'Hot dog buns come in packs of 8, and hot dog sausages come in packs of 12. What is the SMALLEST number of hot dogs and buns you can buy to have an equal number of each with none left over?', NULL, 6, 'High', '4', '48', '96', '24', 'D', 'active', 'This problem requires finding the Least Common Multiple (LCM) of 8 and 12. Multiples of 8: 8, 16, 24... Multiples of 12: 12, 24... The LCM is 24.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the GCF (4) instead of the LCM.\", \"remediation\": \"When matching package sizes to equal quantities, find the LCM, which must be larger than 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student found a common multiple (48) but not the SMALLEST (LCM).\", \"remediation\": \"Check smaller multiples: 24 is divisible by both 8 (3 packs) and 12 (2 packs).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 8 × 12 = 96.\", \"remediation\": \"List multiples to find the smallest shared value rather than multiplying the numbers.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2409, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Computation', 167, 6, 'What is 15.75 ÷ 0.25?', NULL, 6, 'High', '6.3', '63', '630', '3.93', 'B', 'active', 'Shift decimal 2 places in both numbers: 15.75 ÷ 0.25 = 1,575 ÷ 25 = 63. (Or think: there are 4 quarters in 1 dollar, so 15.75 × 4 = 63).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student shifted the decimal in the dividend only 1 place.\", \"remediation\": \"Shift the decimal the exact same number of places in both numbers: 15.75 becomes 1575.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added an unnecessary zero to the quotient.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide: 1,575 ÷ 25 = 63.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 15.75 by 0.25 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"Dividing by 0.25 is the same as multiplying by 4, which makes the number 4 times larger.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2410, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 169, 6, 'A submarine is at an elevation of -120 meters. A helicopter is directly above it at an elevation of +350 meters. What is the vertical distance between the submarine and the helicopter?', NULL, 6, 'High', '470 meters', '-470 meters', '230 meters', '-230 meters', 'A', 'active', 'Vertical distance = |350 - (-120)| = 350 + 120 = 470 meters.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student gave a negative distance.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance between two physical objects can never be negative.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 350 - 120 = 230, ignoring that the submarine is below sea level.\", \"remediation\": \"The submarine is below 0 and the helicopter is above 0, so add their distances: 120 + 350 = 470.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student found the difference with an incorrect negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance is always a positive physical measurement: |-120| + |350| = 470.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2411, 6, 3, 11, 'Coordinate Plane', 170, 6, 'Point P is plotted at (-4, -3). If point P is reflected across the y-axis, what are the coordinates of the reflected point?', NULL, 6, 'High', '(-4, 3)', '(4, -3)', '(4, 3)', '(-3, -4)', 'B', 'active', 'Reflecting across the y-axis changes the sign of the x-coordinate while the y-coordinate stays the same: (-x, y) = (4, -3).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student reflected across the x-axis instead of the y-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"Reflecting across the y-axis changes the x-coordinate sign; y stays unchanged.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student reflected across the origin (changed signs of both coordinates).\", \"remediation\": \"Only the coordinate perpendicular to the line of reflection changes its sign.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student swapped the x and y coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"Do not swap the positions of x and y; only change the sign of x.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2412, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 169, 6, 'Which statement about absolute value and rational numbers is true in real life?', NULL, 6, 'High', 'Absolute value can be a negative number if the original value is negative', 'An account balance of -$50 is greater than -$30 because 50 is greater than 30', 'An elevation of -20 feet is higher than an elevation of -10 feet', 'An account balance of -$50 represents a greater debt than an account balance of -$30 because |-50| > |-30|', 'D', 'active', 'Debt represents magnitude (absolute value): |-50| = 50 and |-30| = 30. A balance of -$50 means you owe $50, which is a greater debt.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student stated that absolute value can be negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Absolute value represents distance and is ALWAYS non-negative (zero or positive).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused debt magnitude with actual numerical balance (-50 < -30).\", \"remediation\": \"-50 is numerically less than -30, meaning you have less money.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought -20 feet is higher than -10 feet.\", \"remediation\": \"-20 feet is deeper underwater than -10 feet, so -10 feet is higher.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:31:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2413, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'What is -8 + 5?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '-3', '3', '-13', '13', 'A', 'active', 'When adding integers with different signs, subtract their absolute values (8 - 5 = 3) and keep the sign of the number with the larger absolute value (-8). Result = -3.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student kept the positive sign instead of the negative sign from -8.\", \"remediation\": \"Since |-8| > |5|, the answer must take the negative sign: -3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added the absolute values (8 + 5 = 13) instead of subtracting them.\", \"remediation\": \"When signs are different, subtract: 8 - 5 = 3, so -8 + 5 = -3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the numbers and made the answer positive.\", \"remediation\": \"Think of temperature: starting at -8 degrees and warming up 5 degrees leaves you at -3 degrees.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2414, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'Which pair of numbers are additive inverses (combine to equal 0)?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '0 and 14', '14 and 1/14', '14 and -14', '-14 and -14', 'C', 'active', 'Additive inverses are numbers that have the same distance from 0 but opposite signs: 14 + (-14) = 0.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student included 0.\", \"remediation\": \"0 + 14 = 14, not 0.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student selected multiplicative inverses (reciprocals) instead of additive inverses.\", \"remediation\": \"Additive inverses add to 0 (e.g., 14 + -14 = 0), while multiplicative inverses multiply to 1.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student selected two identical negative numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"-14 + (-14) = -28, not 0. Opposite signs are required to make 0.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2415, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'What is (-6) × (-4)?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '-24', '-10', '10', '24', 'D', 'active', 'The product of two negative numbers is always positive: (-6) × (-4) = +24.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student applied the negative sign to the product.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the rule: negative × negative = POSITIVE (+24).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added -6 and -4 instead of multiplying them.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the numbers: 6 × 4 = 24. Since both are negative, the answer is +24.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added the numbers and changed the sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply: 6 × 4 = 24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2416, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'What is -36 ÷ 9?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '-45', '-4', '4', '-27', 'B', 'active', 'When dividing a negative number by a positive number, the quotient is always negative: -36 ÷ 9 = -4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added -36 and -9.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow the division operation: -36 ÷ 9 = -4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to include the negative sign in the quotient.\", \"remediation\": \"When signs are different in division (negative ÷ positive), the quotient is always NEGATIVE.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 9 from -36 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 36 by 9 = 4, then apply the negative sign: -4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2417, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'What is 5 - (-3)?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '2', '-2', '8', '-8', 'C', 'active', 'Subtracting a negative number is equivalent to adding its positive opposite: 5 - (-3) = 5 + 3 = 8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 5 - 3 = 2, ignoring the double negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: subtracting a negative turns into addition: minus a minus equals plus (5 + 3 = 8).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted and made the answer negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Rewrite 5 - (-3) as 5 + 3, which equals positive 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the numbers but made the answer negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Both terms become positive in 5 + 3 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2418, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimals', 173, 7, 'Which fraction converts to a terminating decimal?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '3/8', '1/6', '1/3', '2/9', 'A', 'active', '3 ÷ 8 = 0.375, which terminates (ends). 1/3 = 0.333..., 2/9 = 0.222..., and 1/6 = 0.1666... all repeat infinitely.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 1/6, which repeats (0.1666...).\", \"remediation\": \"Check by dividing: 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375 stops after 3 digits, so it is terminating.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 1/3, which is a repeating decimal (0.333...).\", \"remediation\": \"A terminating decimal stops with a remainder of 0. Dividing 1 by 3 never ends.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 2/9, which repeats indefinitely (0.222...).\", \"remediation\": \"Denominators with prime factors other than 2 and 5 produce repeating decimals.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2419, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'The temperature at 6:00 AM was -4°F. By noon, the temperature had risen by 9°F. What was the temperature at noon?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '-13°F', '13°F', '5°F', '-5°F', 'C', 'active', 'Start at -4 and add 9: -4 + 9 = 5°F.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 9 instead of adding (\\\"risen\\\" means add).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Risen\\\" indicates an increase (+). Add 9 to -4: -4 + 9 = 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4 + 9 = 13, ignoring the starting negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Account for the negative start: you must pass through 0 to reach positive numbers.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the answer a negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Since the rise (+9) is greater than the cold (-4), the temperature ends above zero (+5°F).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2420, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions', 172, 7, 'What is -2/3 + 1/6?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '1/2', '-1/3', '-3/6', '-1/2', 'D', 'active', 'Common denominator is 6: -2/3 = -4/6. Then -4/6 + 1/6 = -3/6 = -1/2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot the negative sign on the final answer.\", \"remediation\": \"Since |-4/6| > |1/6|, the result must remain negative (-1/2).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted the numerators without converting to a common denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert -2/3 to -4/6 first. Then -4/6 + 1/6 = -3/6 = -1/2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated -3/6 but forgot to reduce to simplest form (-1/2).\", \"remediation\": \"Always simplify fractions by dividing by the GCF: -3/6 = -1/2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2421, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'What is the distance between -9 and 4 on a number line?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '5 units', '13 units', '-5 units', '-13 units', 'B', 'active', 'Distance = |4 - (-9)| = |4 + 9| = 13 units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 9 - 4 = 5 without considering they are on opposite sides of 0.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance between positive and negative numbers is found by adding their distances from 0: |-9| + |4| = 9 + 4 = 13.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted and included a negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance on a number line is always a positive value.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated distance as a negative number.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance can never be negative: distance = 13 units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2422, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions', 172, 7, 'What is (-2/5) × (-5/8) in simplest form?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '1/4', '1/2', '-1/4', '10/40', 'A', 'active', 'Negative × negative = positive: (-2/5) × (-5/8) = +10/40 = 1/4.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error during simplification.\", \"remediation\": \"Cancel out 5s: 2/8 reduces to 1/4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made the answer negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplying two negative fractions results in a POSITIVE fraction: (-)(-) = (+).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student did not simplify 10/40.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide numerator and denominator by 10 to get 1/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2423, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimals', 173, 7, 'Which fraction is equivalent to the repeating decimal 0.666...?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '6/10', '2/3', '3/5', '6/99', 'B', 'active', 'Let x = 0.666... Then 10x = 6.666... Subtracting gives 9x = 6, so x = 6/9 = 2/3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student treated 0.666... as a terminating decimal (0.6 = 6/10).\", \"remediation\": \"Repeating decimals have denominators of 9 (0.6 repeating = 6/9 = 2/3).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 3/5, which equals terminating decimal 0.6.\", \"remediation\": \"3/5 = 0.6, while 2/3 = 0.666...\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student used 99 instead of 9 for a single repeating digit.\", \"remediation\": \"A single repeating digit has denominator 9 (6/9 = 2/3).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2424, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimals', 173, 7, 'What is (-4.5) ÷ (-0.5)?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '2.25', '-9', '0.9', '9', 'D', 'active', 'Negative divided by negative is positive: (-4.5) ÷ (-0.5) = 45 ÷ 5 = 9.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4.5 by 0.5 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"Dividing by 0.5 is the same as multiplying by 2: 4.5 × 2 = 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student applied a negative sign to the quotient.\", \"remediation\": \"Dividing two negative numbers always yields a POSITIVE answer.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student misplaced the decimal point.\", \"remediation\": \"Shift the decimal 1 place in both numbers: 45 ÷ 5 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2425, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'What is the value of -12 - 17 + 8?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '-21', '-37', '13', '-29', 'A', 'active', 'First: -12 - 17 = -29. Then: -29 + 8 = -21.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 8 instead of adding: -29 - 8 = -37.\", \"remediation\": \"Perform operations from left to right: -29 + 8 = -21.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student lost all negative signs: 12 - 17 + 8 = 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep track of the negative signs: -12 and -17 combine to -29.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student stopped after the first step (-12 - 17 = -29) and forgot to add 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Complete all steps in the expression: -29 + 8 = -21.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2426, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'A student bank account has a balance of $120. The student makes 4 withdrawals of $35 each. What is the new account balance?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '$0', '$20', '-$20', '-$140', 'C', 'active', 'Total withdrawn = 4 × 35 = $140. New balance = 120 - 140 = -$20.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed the balance stops at 0.\", \"remediation\": \"Bank overdrafts result in negative balances.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 140 - 120 = 20 but omitted the negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Since more money was withdrawn ($140) than was in the account ($120), the balance is NEGATIVE (-$20).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated only the total withdrawals without starting balance.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract withdrawals from the initial balance: 120 - 140 = -$20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2427, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions', 172, 7, 'Which expression is NOT equivalent to -3/4?', NULL, 7, 'High', '-(3/4)', '(-3)/4', '3/(-4)', '(-3)/(-4)', 'D', 'active', '(-3)/(-4) equals POSITIVE 3/4 because negative divided by negative is positive. All other choices equal -3/4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought -(3/4) is not equal to -3/4.\", \"remediation\": \"Placing the negative sign in front of the fraction equals -3/4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought (-3)/4 is different.\", \"remediation\": \"A negative numerator makes the entire fraction negative.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought 3/(-4) is different.\", \"remediation\": \"A negative denominator also makes the entire fraction negative.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2428, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions', 172, 7, 'Simplify: -2 1/3 - (-1 1/2)', NULL, 7, 'High', '-1 1/6', '-5/6', '-3 5/6', '5/6', 'B', 'active', 'Rewrite: -7/3 - (-3/2) = -7/3 + 3/2. Common denominator 6: -14/6 + 9/6 = -5/6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error when combining fractional parts.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert mixed numbers to improper fractions before finding a common denominator.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 1 1/2 instead of adding it (-2 1/3 - 1 1/2 = -3 5/6).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtracting a negative becomes addition: -7/3 + 3/2 = -5/6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student dropped the negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Since |-14/6| > |9/6|, the answer remains negative (-5/6).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2429, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'Evaluate: (-2)³ × (-1/4)', NULL, 7, 'High', '-2', '1/2', '2', '-1/2', 'C', 'active', '(-2)³ = (-2) × (-2) × (-2) = -8. Then (-8) × (-1/4) = +8/4 = 2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made the product negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative times negative equals positive: (-8) × (-1/4) = +2.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 8 × 1/4 as 1/2.\", \"remediation\": \"8 divided by 4 equals 2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made a sign and arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"Step 1: (-2)³ = -8. Step 2: (-8) × (-1/4) = 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2430, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'A hiker starts at an elevation of 240 feet above sea level. He descends 85 feet each hour for 4 hours. What is his final elevation?', NULL, 7, 'High', '-100 feet', '-120 feet', '100 feet', '-340 feet', 'A', 'active', 'Total descent = 4 × (-85) = -340 feet. Final elevation = 240 - 340 = -100 feet (100 feet below sea level).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error in 240 - 340.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 340 - 240 = 100, then attach the negative sign: -100 feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted the negative sign (wrote +100 instead of -100).\", \"remediation\": \"He descended below sea level: 240 - 340 = -100 feet.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated only the total descent without starting from 240 feet.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the descent to the initial elevation: 240 + (-340) = -100 feet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2431, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimals', 173, 7, 'What is the decimal representation of 5/11 using long division?', NULL, 7, 'High', '0.45 (terminating)', '0.555... (repeating)', '0.4545... (repeating)', '0.455... (repeating)', 'C', 'active', 'Divide 5 ÷ 11: 5.0 ÷ 11 = 0.4 (rem 6). 60 ÷ 11 = 5 (rem 5). The cycle 45 repeats infinitely: 0.4545...', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded to 2 decimal places and assumed it terminates.\", \"remediation\": \"The remainder repeats (6, then 5), meaning digits 45 repeat forever: 0.4545...\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought 5/11 repeats 5s.\", \"remediation\": \"Perform the long division: 5 ÷ 11 = 0.4545...\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought only the 5 repeats.\", \"remediation\": \"Both digits 4 and 5 repeat as a pair: 0.4545...\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2432, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions', 172, 7, 'Evaluate: (-3/4 + 1/2) ÷ (-1/8)', NULL, 7, 'High', '-1/32', '-2', '1/32', '2', 'D', 'active', 'Step 1: -3/4 + 2/4 = -1/4. Step 2: (-1/4) ÷ (-1/8) = (-1/4) × (-8/1) = +8/4 = 2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied without flipping and applied a negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Flip the divisor: (-1/4) × (-8/1) = 2.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made the answer negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative divided by negative is POSITIVE: (-1/4) ÷ (-1/8) = +2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied without taking the reciprocal: (-1/4) × (-1/8) = 1/32.\", \"remediation\": \"In fraction division, multiply by the reciprocal of the second fraction (flip -1/8 to -8/1).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:32:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2433, 6, 3, 11, 'Irrational Numbers', 176, 8, 'Which of the following numbers is an IRRATIONAL number?', NULL, 8, 'High', '√16', '√5', '3/7', '0.333...', 'B', 'active', '√5 cannot be expressed as a ratio of two integers because 5 is not a perfect square; its decimal expansion is non-terminating and non-repeating.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"√16 = 4, which is an integer (rational).\", \"remediation\": \"16 is a perfect square (4² = 16), so √16 is rational. 5 is not a perfect square, so √5 is irrational.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"3/7 is explicitly written as a ratio of two integers (rational).\", \"remediation\": \"Any number of the form a/b with integers a and b is rational.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"0.333... is a repeating decimal, which equals 1/3 (rational).\", \"remediation\": \"Repeating decimals are rational: 0.333... = 1/3. √5 is irrational.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2434, 6, 3, 11, 'Integer Exponents & Radicals', 179, 8, 'What is 4³ × 4⁵ written as a single power?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '4⁸', '16¹⁵', '4¹⁵', '16⁸', 'A', 'active', 'When multiplying powers with the same base, keep the base and add the exponents: 4^(3 + 5) = 4⁸.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied both the bases and the exponents.\", \"remediation\": \"The base stays 4, and exponents add: 4⁸.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the exponents (3 × 5 = 15) instead of adding them.\", \"remediation\": \"Product of powers rule: when multiplying like bases, ADD the exponents: 3 + 5 = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the bases (4 × 4 = 16) while also adding exponents.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the base unchanged: 4³ × 4⁵ = 4⁸.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2435, 6, 3, 11, 'Integer Exponents & Radicals', 179, 8, 'What is 5⁻² expressed as a fraction?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '-25', '1/25', '-10', '1/10', 'B', 'active', 'Negative exponent rule: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. So 5⁻² = 1/5² = 1/25.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought a negative exponent makes the number negative.\", \"remediation\": \"A negative exponent does NOT make a number negative; it takes the reciprocal (1/5² = 1/25).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied base by exponent (5 × -2 = -10).\", \"remediation\": \"Exponents indicate repeated multiplication, not simple multiplication: 5² = 25, so 5⁻² = 1/25.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 5 × 2 in the denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"5² = 5 × 5 = 25, so 5⁻² = 1/25.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2436, 6, 3, 11, 'Square Roots', 180, 8, 'What is the principal square root of 81, written as √81?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '8', '18', '40.5', '9', 'D', 'active', 'Since 9 × 9 = 81, the principal square root is 9.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 8 (8 × 8 = 64).\", \"remediation\": \"Review square numbers: 9² = 81.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 81 by 4 or confused square root with halving.\", \"remediation\": \"Square root asks: what number multiplied by itself equals 81? Since 9 × 9 = 81, √81 = 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 81 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Square root is not dividing by 2; it is finding the identical factor.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2437, 6, 3, 11, 'Cube Roots', 181, 8, 'What is the cube root of 64, written as ∛64?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '4', '8', '16', '21.3', 'A', 'active', 'Since 4 × 4 × 4 = 64, the cube root of 64 is 4.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the square root (√64 = 8) instead of the cube root.\", \"remediation\": \"Cube root (∛) requires a factor multiplied THREE times: 4 × 4 × 4 = 64.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 64 by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Check: 16 × 16 × 16 = 4096, which is far too large. 4 × 4 × 4 = 64.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 64 by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Cube root is not dividing by 3; it is finding the number that cubes to 64.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2438, 6, 3, 11, 'Scientific Notation', 183, 8, 'What is 45,000 written in scientific notation?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '0.45 × 10⁵', '45 × 10³', '4.5 × 10⁴', '4.5 × 10⁵', 'C', 'active', 'Move the decimal point 4 places to the left to get a leading number between 1 and 10: 4.5 × 10⁴.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student used a leading number less than 1 (0.45).\", \"remediation\": \"The coefficient must be at least 1, so use 4.5 × 10⁴.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student left the first number as 45, which is not between 1 and 10.\", \"remediation\": \"In scientific notation, the first factor must be greater than or equal to 1 and less than 10 (4.5).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted the decimal shift as 5 places.\", \"remediation\": \"Count decimal jumps: 45,000 -> 4500.0 (1) -> 450.0 (2) -> 45.0 (3) -> 4.5 (4 places).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2439, 6, 3, 11, 'Integer Exponents & Radicals', 179, 8, 'What is the value of 7⁰?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '0', '7', '-1', '1', 'D', 'active', 'Any non-zero number raised to the zero power equals 1: a⁰ = 1.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought raising to the zero power equals 0.\", \"remediation\": \"Zero exponent rule: any non-zero base raised to the power of 0 always equals 1.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought 7⁰ = 7 (confused with 7¹).\", \"remediation\": \"7¹ = 7, but 7⁰ = 1.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose -1.\", \"remediation\": \"Recall: 7² / 7² = 49 / 49 = 1, and by exponent rules 7^(2-2) = 7⁰ = 1.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2440, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimal Expansions', 177, 8, 'Convert the repeating decimal 0.777... (0.7̄) into a fraction.', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '77/100', '7/9', '7/10', '7/99', 'B', 'active', 'Let x = 0.777... Then 10x = 7.777... Subtracting gives 9x = 7, so x = 7/9.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student treated it as two terminating digits (77/100).\", \"remediation\": \"Repeating decimals are represented by fractions with nines in the denominator.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student treated the decimal as terminating (0.7 = 7/10).\", \"remediation\": \"A repeating single digit has denominator 9 (0.777... = 7/9).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student used denominator 99 for a single repeating digit.\", \"remediation\": \"Use 99 only when TWO digits repeat (e.g., 0.2525... = 25/99).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2441, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Approximations', 178, 8, 'Between which two consecutive integers does √53 lie?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'Between 6 and 7', 'Between 8 and 9', 'Between 7 and 8', 'Between 26 and 27', 'C', 'active', 'Look for surrounding perfect squares: 7² = 49 and 8² = 64. Since 49 < 53 < 64, √53 lies between 7 and 8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 6 and 7 (6² = 36, 7² = 49), which are both less than 53.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the squares: 7² = 49 and 8² = 64. Since 53 is between 49 and 64, √53 is between 7 and 8.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 8 and 9 (8² = 64, 9² = 81), which are both greater than 53.\", \"remediation\": \"53 is less than 64, so it must be below 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 53 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Do not divide by 2; compare to perfect squares (49 and 64).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2442, 6, 3, 11, 'Integer Exponents & Radicals', 179, 8, 'Simplify 3⁷ / 3¹⁰ using a positive exponent.', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '1/3³ (or 1/27)', '1/3¹⁷', '3³', '3¹⁷', 'A', 'active', 'Quotient of powers rule: 3^(7 - 10) = 3⁻³ = 1/3³ = 1/27.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added exponents and inverted.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract exponents: 7 - 10 = -3, giving 1/3³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 10 - 7 = 3 and kept it in the numerator.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract numerator exponent minus denominator exponent: 7 - 10 = -3, so 1/3³.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the exponents instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"In division of like bases, SUBTRACT exponents: 7 - 10 = -3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2443, 6, 3, 11, 'Square Roots', 180, 8, 'What are the solutions to the equation x² = 49?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'x = 7 only', 'x = 24.5', 'x = 7 and x = -7', 'x = 14', 'C', 'active', 'Both 7² = 49 and (-7)² = 49. Therefore, x = ±√49 = ±7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot the negative solution.\", \"remediation\": \"Quadratic equations of the form x² = p have two solutions: both positive and negative (±7).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 49 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Take the square root, not half: 7 × 7 = 49.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 7 × 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Square root means finding the base that multiplies by itself to make 49 (7 and -7).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2444, 6, 3, 11, 'Scientific Notation', 183, 8, 'What is 0.00032 written in scientific notation?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '3.2 × 10⁻³', '3.2 × 10⁴', '32 × 10⁻⁵', '3.2 × 10⁻⁴', 'D', 'active', 'Move the decimal point 4 places to the right to get 3.2. Because the original number is less than 1, the exponent is negative: 3.2 × 10⁻⁴.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student counted 3 zeros instead of counting the total decimal jumps.\", \"remediation\": \"Count jumps from original decimal to after the first non-zero digit (4 jumps = 10⁻⁴).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student used a positive exponent for a decimal number less than 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Numbers smaller than 1 always have NEGATIVE exponents in scientific notation: 10⁻⁴.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student used 32, which is not between 1 and 10.\", \"remediation\": \"The coefficient must be between 1 and 10 (3.2).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2445, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Approximations', 178, 8, 'Which number is GREATER: π or √10?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'π is greater because π is infinite', '√10 is greater because √10 ≈ 3.162 and π ≈ 3.141', 'They are exactly equal', 'π is greater because 3.14 > 3.10', 'B', 'active', 'Approximate both values: π ≈ 3.1416, and √10 ≈ 3.1623. Comparing tenths and hundredths: 3.162 > 3.141, so √10 is greater.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought being irrational/infinite makes a number larger.\", \"remediation\": \"Both π and √10 are infinite non-repeating decimals; compare their numerical values directly.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed they are equal.\", \"remediation\": \"Square both: π² ≈ 9.87, while (√10)² = 10. Since 10 > 9.87, √10 > π.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student incorrectly approximated √10 as 3.10.\", \"remediation\": \"3.16 × 3.16 = 9.9856, which is close to 10. So √10 ≈ 3.16, which is greater than 3.14.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2446, 6, 3, 11, 'Scientific Notation', 183, 8, 'What is (2 × 10³) × (4 × 10⁵) in scientific notation?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '8 × 10⁸', '8 × 10²', '8 × 10¹⁵', '6 × 10⁸', 'A', 'active', 'Multiply coefficients: 2 × 4 = 8. Add exponents of 10: 10^(3 + 5) = 10⁸. Product = 8 × 10⁸.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted the exponents.\", \"remediation\": \"Add exponents in multiplication: 10³ × 10⁵ = 10⁸.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the exponents (3 × 5 = 15) instead of adding them.\", \"remediation\": \"When multiplying powers of 10, ADD the exponents: 3 + 5 = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the coefficients (2 + 4 = 6) instead of multiplying them.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the coefficients: 2 × 4 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2447, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimal Expansions', 177, 8, 'What is the fraction in simplest form for the repeating decimal 0.4545... (0.4̄5̄)?', NULL, 8, 'High', '45/100', '5/11', '45/99', '9/20', 'B', 'active', 'Let x = 0.4545... Then 100x = 45.4545... Subtract: 99x = 45, so x = 45/99. Simplifying by dividing top and bottom by 9 gives 5/11.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student treated it as a terminating decimal (45/100).\", \"remediation\": \"Repeating decimals have denominators of 9s (45/99), not 100.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student found 45/99 but forgot to simplify by dividing by 9.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 45 and 99 by their GCF 9 to get simplest form: 5/11.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student simplified 45/100 instead of 45/99.\", \"remediation\": \"Use 99 in denominator for two repeating digits: 45/99 = 5/11.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2448, 6, 3, 11, 'Integer Exponents & Radicals', 179, 8, 'Simplify: (2⁻³)⁻²', NULL, 8, 'High', '1/32', '1/64', '-64', '64', 'D', 'active', 'Power of a power rule: multiply exponents: (-3) × (-2) = +6. Then 2⁶ = 64.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added exponents (-3 + -2 = -5) giving 1/32.\", \"remediation\": \"Power of a power rule requires MULTIPLYING exponents: (-3) × (-2) = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought the negative exponents remained negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative times negative is positive: (-3) × (-2) = +6, so 2⁶ = 64.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made the final result negative.\", \"remediation\": \"A positive base raised to any real power is always positive: 2⁶ = +64.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2449, 6, 3, 11, 'Cube Roots', 181, 8, 'What is the real solution to the equation y³ = -125?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'y = -5', 'y = 5 and y = -5', 'No real solution', 'y = 5', 'A', 'active', 'Since (-5) × (-5) × (-5) = -125, the cube root of -125 is -5. Cube roots of negative numbers have exactly one real negative solution.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student included both +5 and -5, confusing cube roots with square roots.\", \"remediation\": \"Cube roots have only ONE real root: 5³ = +125 (not -125). Only (-5)³ = -125.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought negative numbers cannot have cube roots.\", \"remediation\": \"Odd roots of negative numbers exist and are negative: ∛(-125) = -5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted the negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"5³ = +125; since the equation equals -125, y must be -5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2450, 6, 3, 11, 'Scientific Notation', 183, 8, 'What is (3.2 × 10⁴) + (5.1 × 10³) written in scientific notation?', NULL, 8, 'High', '3.71 × 10³', '8.3 × 10⁷', '3.71 × 10⁴', '8.3 × 10⁴', 'C', 'active', 'Match exponents: 5.1 × 10³ = 0.51 × 10⁴. Add: (3.2 + 0.51) × 10⁴ = 3.71 × 10⁴.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student used the smaller exponent 10³.\", \"remediation\": \"3.71 × 10⁴ is in proper scientific notation with coefficient 3.71.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added coefficients and added exponents (3.2 + 5.1 = 8.3, 10^(4+3) = 10⁷).\", \"remediation\": \"You cannot add numbers in scientific notation until their powers of 10 match.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added coefficients directly without matching exponents.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert 5.1 × 10³ to 0.51 × 10⁴ before adding.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2451, 6, 3, 11, 'Scientific Notation', 183, 8, 'What is (8.4 × 10⁷) ÷ (2.1 × 10³) in scientific notation?', NULL, 8, 'High', '4.0 × 10¹⁰', '4.0 × 10²', '6.3 × 10⁴', '4.0 × 10⁴', 'D', 'active', 'Divide coefficients: 8.4 ÷ 2.1 = 4.0. Subtract exponents: 7 - 3 = 4. Quotient = 4.0 × 10⁴.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added the exponents (7 + 3 = 10) instead of subtracting them.\", \"remediation\": \"When dividing powers of 10, SUBTRACT the exponents: 7 - 3 = 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided the exponents (7 ÷ 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract exponents in division: 10^(7 - 3) = 10⁴.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted the coefficients (8.4 - 2.1 = 6.3) instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the coefficients: 8.4 ÷ 2.1 = 4.0.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2452, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Approximations', 178, 8, 'Which shows the numbers ordered from LEAST to GREATEST: √8, 3, π, √15?', NULL, 8, 'High', '√15, π, 3, √8', '√8, 3, π, √15', '3, √8, π, √15', '√8, π, 3, √15', 'B', 'active', 'Approximate each value: √8 ≈ 2.83, 3 = 3.00, π ≈ 3.14, √15 ≈ 3.87. Order: 2.83 < 3.00 < 3.14 < 3.87, which is √8 < 3 < π < √15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student ordered from greatest to least.\", \"remediation\": \"Least to greatest begins with the smallest number (√8 ≈ 2.83).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought √8 is greater than 3.\", \"remediation\": \"3² = 9; since 8 < 9, √8 is less than 3 (approx 2.83 < 3).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student placed π before 3.\", \"remediation\": \"π ≈ 3.14, which is greater than 3.00.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 15:34:47', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2453, 6, 1, 7, 'Shapes & Attributes', 268, 1, 'Which shape has exactly 3 straight sides and 3 corners?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Square', 'Circle', 'Triangle', 'Rectangle', 'C', 'active', 'A triangle is a closed shape with exactly 3 straight sides and 3 corners (vertices).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a square, which has 4 sides and 4 corners.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the sides: a triangle has 3 sides, while a square has 4 sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a circle, which has curved sides and 0 corners.\", \"remediation\": \"A circle has no straight sides and no corners.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a rectangle, which has 4 straight sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have 3 sides, rectangles have 4 sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2454, 6, 1, 7, 'Shapes & Attributes', 268, 1, 'Which of the following is a DEFINING attribute of a square?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'It has 4 equal straight sides and 4 square corners', 'It is turned sideways', 'It is colored blue', 'It is very large', 'A', 'active', 'Defining attributes determine what a shape is (4 equal sides, 4 square corners). Color and size do not change the shape name.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused orientation with a defining attribute.\", \"remediation\": \"Turning a shape does not change what shape it is.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused color (non-defining) with a defining attribute.\", \"remediation\": \"A square can be any color; color does not define the shape.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused size with a defining attribute.\", \"remediation\": \"A square can be big or small; size does not change its shape.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2455, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'Which 3D solid shape looks like a party hat or ice cream cone?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Cube', 'Cylinder', 'Cone', 'Sphere', 'C', 'active', 'A cone has a flat circular base and points to a single tip (vertex) at the top, like an ice cream cone.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a cube, which looks like a box with square faces.\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has 6 flat square faces, like a playing die.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a cylinder, which looks like a can with two circular faces.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder has 2 circular bases and does not point to a tip.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a sphere, which is completely round like a ball.\", \"remediation\": \"A sphere has no flat faces and no tip.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2456, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'Which 3D solid shape has 6 flat square faces that are all the same size?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Sphere', 'Cylinder', 'Cone', 'Cube', 'D', 'active', 'A cube has 6 identical flat square faces, 12 edges, and 8 vertices.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a sphere.\", \"remediation\": \"A sphere has 0 flat faces.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a cylinder.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder has only 2 circular flat faces and 1 curved surface.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a cone.\", \"remediation\": \"A cone has only 1 flat face and 1 point.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2457, 6, 1, 7, 'Halves & Fourths', 271, 1, 'A round cookie is cut into 2 EQUAL parts. What do we call each part?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'One fourth', 'One half', 'One third', 'One whole', 'B', 'active', 'When a whole is divided into 2 equal shares, each share is called one half.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused halves (2 parts) with fourths (4 parts).\", \"remediation\": \"Halves mean 2 equal parts; fourths mean 4 equal parts.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused halves with thirds (3 parts).\", \"remediation\": \"2 equal parts are called halves.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose whole instead of the part.\", \"remediation\": \"The whole cookie was cut, so each piece is a half.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2458, 6, 1, 7, 'Halves & Fourths', 271, 1, 'How many equal fourths make up one whole circle?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '4', '1', '2', '3', 'A', 'active', 'Four fourths combine together to make 1 whole: 4/4 = 1.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student stated 1 instead of counting all pieces.\", \"remediation\": \"It takes 4 fourths pieces to make the complete circle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused fourths with halves (2 halves = 1 whole).\", \"remediation\": \"Fourths come in groups of 4 to make 1 whole.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Fourths means 4 equal pieces.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2459, 6, 1, 7, 'Shapes & Attributes', 268, 1, 'Which shape is a closed shape with NO straight sides and NO corners?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Rectangle', 'Circle', 'Square', 'Triangle', 'B', 'active', 'A circle is completely round with zero straight sides and zero corners.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles have 4 straight sides and 4 corners.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose square.\", \"remediation\": \"Squares have 4 straight sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have 3 straight sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2460, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'What 3D solid shape is shaped like a soup can?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Pyramid', 'Cube', 'Cone', 'Cylinder', 'D', 'active', 'A cylinder has two flat circular faces at top and bottom with a smooth curved body, like a soup can.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose pyramid.\", \"remediation\": \"Pyramids have triangular faces.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose cube.\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has square flat faces, not circular.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose cone.\", \"remediation\": \"A cone comes to a point, but a soup can is flat on both ends.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2461, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'If you put two identical right triangles together along their longest sides, which shape can you make?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Rectangle', 'Circle', 'Cone', 'Hexagon', 'A', 'active', 'Combining two right triangles along their diagonals creates a 4-sided rectangle.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose circle.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have straight sides and cannot form a curved circle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose a 3D solid (cone) from 2D shapes.\", \"remediation\": \"Putting flat 2D shapes together makes another flat 2D shape.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose hexagon (6 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"Two triangles have fewer sides and form a 4-sided rectangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2462, 6, 1, 7, 'Halves & Fourths', 271, 1, 'Which is larger: one half of a sandwich or one fourth of the SAME sandwich?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Cannot be determined', 'One fourth of the sandwich', 'One half of the sandwich', 'They are the exact same size', 'C', 'active', 'Cutting into fewer pieces (2) leaves bigger pieces than cutting into more pieces (4). One half is larger than one fourth.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought it cannot be compared.\", \"remediation\": \"When referring to the same whole, halves are always bigger than fourths.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought fourth is larger because 4 is greater than 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Sharing among more pieces makes each piece SMALLER.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed all fraction shares are equal.\", \"remediation\": \"Halves (2 pieces) are twice as large as fourths (4 pieces).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2463, 6, 1, 7, 'Shapes & Attributes', 268, 1, 'A shape has 4 straight sides. Two opposite sides are long, and the other two opposite sides are short. All 4 corners are square corners. What shape is it?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Square', 'Triangle', 'Rhombus', 'Rectangle', 'D', 'active', 'A rectangle has 4 square corners and opposite sides that are equal (long sides and short sides).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose square, but a square must have all 4 sides equal in length.\", \"remediation\": \"In a square, all 4 sides are equal; this shape has long and short sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have only 3 sides.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rhombus.\", \"remediation\": \"A rhombus has all 4 sides equal and does not require square corners.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2464, 6, 1, 7, 'Shapes & Attributes', 268, 1, 'Which of the following is NOT an open shape?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'A letter U', 'A complete circle with connected line', 'A circle with a gap in the line', 'A horseshoe shape', 'B', 'active', 'A complete circle is a CLOSED shape because its outline connects all the way around with no gaps.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student selected an open letter.\", \"remediation\": \"The letter U is open at the top.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose a shape with a gap.\", \"remediation\": \"A gap means the shape is open, not closed.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student selected an open curve.\", \"remediation\": \"A horseshoe has open ends.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2465, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'How many flat circular faces does a cylinder have?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '1', '0', '2', '6', 'C', 'active', 'A cylinder has 2 flat circular faces (one on the top and one on the bottom).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 1 (confused cylinder with cone).\", \"remediation\": \"A cone has 1 flat face, but a cylinder has 2 (top and bottom).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 0 (confused with sphere).\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder has 2 flat circle ends.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 6 (confused with cube).\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has 6 flat faces, a cylinder has 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2466, 6, 1, 7, 'Halves & Fourths', 271, 1, 'Ali divides a rectangle into 2 parts. One part is very big and one part is very small. Are the parts halves?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'No, because halves must be EQUAL in size', 'No, because rectangles cannot have halves', 'Yes, because there are 2 parts', 'Yes, because it is a rectangle', 'A', 'active', 'To be called halves, the 2 parts must be equal in area and size.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought rectangles cannot be divided into halves.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles can easily be divided in half down the middle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought any 2 pieces are halves regardless of equality.\", \"remediation\": \"Halves MUST be equal shares. Unequal pieces cannot be called halves.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student based the answer on the shape name.\", \"remediation\": \"The parts must be equal shares.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2467, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'Which 3D solid shape can roll smoothly in ANY direction?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Cylinder', 'Cube', 'Sphere', 'Cone', 'C', 'active', 'A sphere is completely round in all directions like a basketball, so it rolls in any direction.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose cylinder.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder rolls only along its curved side, not on its flat ends.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose cube.\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has flat square faces and slides or tumbles, it does not roll.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose cone.\", \"remediation\": \"A cone rolls in a circle around its tip.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2468, 6, 1, 7, 'Shapes & Attributes', 268, 1, 'Which shape has 6 straight sides and 6 vertices?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Octagon', 'Pentagon', 'Square', 'Hexagon', 'D', 'active', 'A hexagon is a 2D closed polygon that has exactly 6 straight sides and 6 vertices.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose octagon.\", \"remediation\": \"An octagon has 8 sides like a stop sign.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose pentagon.\", \"remediation\": \"A pentagon has 5 sides and 5 vertices.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose square.\", \"remediation\": \"A square has 4 sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2469, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'If you trace the bottom flat face of a soup can on paper, what 2D shape will you draw?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Square', 'Circle', 'Triangle', 'Oval', 'B', 'active', 'The flat face of a cylinder is a circle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose square.\", \"remediation\": \"Tracing a cube makes a square; tracing a cylinder makes a circle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder has round circle ends with no straight sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose oval.\", \"remediation\": \"The base of a cylinder is a perfect circle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2470, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'How many smaller triangles can be put together to compose a regular hexagon using pattern blocks?', NULL, 1, 'High', '6 green triangles', '8 green triangles', '4 green triangles', '3 green triangles', 'A', 'active', '6 equilateral green triangles fit together around a center point to compose 1 yellow hexagon.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 8 triangles.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the 6 triangular sections meeting at the center.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought 4 triangles make a hexagon.\", \"remediation\": \"A hexagon has 6 sides; 6 triangles are needed to fill it completely.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 3 triangles (confused with 3 triangles making a trapezoid).\", \"remediation\": \"3 triangles make a trapezoid (half of a hexagon).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2471, 6, 1, 7, 'Halves & Fourths', 271, 1, 'Zain has a square paper. He folds it diagonally from corner to corner and cuts it. What shapes are the two equal halves?', NULL, 1, 'High', '2 Rectangles', '2 Triangles', '2 Smaller squares', '2 Circles', 'B', 'active', 'Folding a square along its diagonal divides it into 2 equal right triangles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought folding diagonally makes rectangles.\", \"remediation\": \"Folding side-to-side makes rectangles; folding corner-to-corner makes triangles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought it makes smaller squares.\", \"remediation\": \"Cutting along a diagonal creates 3-sided triangles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose circles.\", \"remediation\": \"Straight cuts cannot create curved circles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2472, 6, 1, 7, 'Shapes & Attributes', 268, 1, 'A student looks at a shape and says: \"This is NOT a triangle because it is pointing down.\" Is the student correct?', NULL, 1, 'High', 'No, but only if it is blue', 'Yes, triangles must always point straight up', 'Yes, upside-down shapes change their name', 'No, because orientation does not change a shape if it still has 3 closed straight sides', 'D', 'active', 'Orientation is a non-defining attribute. Any closed shape with 3 straight sides is a triangle, no matter which direction it points.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student attached color as a requirement.\", \"remediation\": \"Color is never a defining attribute of geometric shapes.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student believes triangles must point up.\", \"remediation\": \"A triangle can point up, down, left, or right; orientation does not matter.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thinks turning a shape changes its identity.\", \"remediation\": \"Rotating a shape keeps it the same geometric shape.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2473, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'Which 3D solid has 1 flat circular face and 1 sharp vertex at the top?', NULL, 1, 'High', 'Cone', 'Cylinder', 'Pyramid', 'Prism', 'A', 'active', 'A cone has exactly 1 flat circular face and 1 vertex.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose cylinder.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder has 2 flat circular faces and 0 sharp vertices.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose pyramid.\", \"remediation\": \"A pyramid has a polygon base (like a square or triangle), not a circular face.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose prism.\", \"remediation\": \"Prisms have polygon faces and 2 identical bases.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2474, 6, 1, 7, 'Halves & Fourths', 271, 1, 'Four friends want to share a large square pizza equally. How should they cut it so each friend gets a fair share?', NULL, 1, 'High', 'Cut it into 4 different sized pieces', 'Cut it into 2 equal halves', 'Cut it into 4 equal quarters', 'Cut it into 3 equal pieces', 'C', 'active', 'Sharing among 4 friends equally requires partitioning the pizza into 4 equal fourths (quarters).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student allowed different sizes.\", \"remediation\": \"Fair sharing requires all 4 pieces to be equal in size.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 2 halves, which provides only 2 pieces for 4 friends.\", \"remediation\": \"4 friends need 4 equal pieces (fourths).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 3 pieces.\", \"remediation\": \"4 people need 4 shares.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2475, 6, 1, 7, 'Shapes & Attributes', 268, 1, 'What is the total number of corners (vertices) on two separate triangles combined?', NULL, 1, 'High', '5 corners', '3 corners', '8 corners', '6 corners', 'D', 'active', 'Each triangle has 3 corners. Two triangles have 3 + 3 = 6 corners.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an addition error (3 + 2 = 5).\", \"remediation\": \"Add the corners of both triangles: 3 + 3 = 6 corners.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted corners for only 1 triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by 2 for two triangles: 2 × 3 = 6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student counted 4 corners per triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have 3 corners, not 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2476, 6, 1, 7, '2D & 3D Shapes', 269, 1, 'How many edges does a cube have in total?', NULL, 1, 'High', '4 edges', '12 edges', '6 edges', '8 edges', 'B', 'active', 'A cube has 6 faces, 8 vertices (corners), and 12 straight edges.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student counted edges on only 1 square face.\", \"remediation\": \"Count all edges around top, bottom, and vertical sides: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused edges with faces (a cube has 6 faces).\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has 6 faces, but 12 straight edges where faces meet.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused edges with vertices (corners).\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has 8 corners and 12 edges.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2477, 6, 1, 7, 'Halves & Fourths', 271, 1, 'Sara cuts an apple in half. Then she cuts each half in half again. How many equal pieces does Sara have now?', NULL, 1, 'High', '2 equal halves', '3 pieces', '4 equal fourths', '8 pieces', 'C', 'active', 'Cutting each of the 2 halves in half produces 2 × 2 = 4 equal fourths.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student stopped after the first cut (2 halves).\", \"remediation\": \"Cutting the halves again doubles the number of pieces to 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted pieces.\", \"remediation\": \"2 pieces cut in half make 4 equal pieces.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled twice.\", \"remediation\": \"Only one additional cut was made, producing 4 fourths.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2478, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'Which shape is a polygon with exactly 5 sides and 5 angles?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'Pentagon', 'Triangle', 'Hexagon', 'Quadrilateral', 'A', 'active', 'A pentagon is a 5-sided polygon with 5 interior angles.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose triangle (3 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have 3 sides.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose hexagon (6 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"A hexagon has 6 sides; a pentagon has 5 sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose quadrilateral (4 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"Quadrilaterals have 4 sides; pentagons have 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2479, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'Which shape has exactly 6 sides and 6 angles?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'Pentagon', 'Octagon', 'Hexagon', 'Trapezoid', 'C', 'active', 'A hexagon has 6 straight sides and 6 angles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose pentagon (5 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"Hexagons have 6 sides; pentagons have 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose octagon (8 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"Octagons have 8 sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid (4 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid has 4 sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2480, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Rows & Columns', 273, 2, 'A rectangle is partitioned into 3 rows and 4 columns of same-size squares. How many total squares are there?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '16 squares', '7 squares', '14 squares', '12 squares', 'D', 'active', 'Multiply rows by columns: 3 rows × 4 columns = 12 total squares (or 4 + 4 + 4 = 12).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 4 × 4 = 16.\", \"remediation\": \"There are only 3 rows: 3 × 4 = 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added rows and columns (3 + 4 = 7) instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Count by rows: each row has 4 squares, so 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 squares.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 4 and doubled it (perimeter confusion).\", \"remediation\": \"Total squares inside the grid is rows × columns = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2481, 6, 1, 7, 'Fractions of Shapes', 274, 2, 'A circular pie is divided into 3 equal slices. What fraction word describes each slice?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'One half', 'One third', 'One fourth', 'One fifth', 'B', 'active', 'When a shape is divided into 3 equal shares, each share is called one third.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose half (2 parts).\", \"remediation\": \"Halves are 2 equal parts; 3 equal parts are called thirds.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose fourth (4 parts).\", \"remediation\": \"Fourths are 4 parts; thirds are 3 parts.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose fifth.\", \"remediation\": \"3 parts are called thirds.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2482, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'How many angles does any quadrilateral have?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '4 angles', '6 angles', '3 angles', '5 angles', 'A', 'active', 'The prefix \"quad\" means four. Every quadrilateral has 4 straight sides and 4 angles.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Quadrilaterals always have 4 angles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 3 (confused with triangle).\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have 3 angles; quadrilaterals have 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 5 (confused with pentagon).\", \"remediation\": \"Pentagons have 5 angles; quadrilaterals have 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2483, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Rows & Columns', 273, 2, 'An array of square tiles has 2 rows with 5 tiles in each row. Which addition sentence shows the total number of tiles?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '2 + 5 = 7', '5 + 5 = 10', '2 + 2 = 4', '5 + 5 + 5 = 15', 'B', 'active', '2 rows of 5 tiles means repeated addition of 5 two times: 5 + 5 = 10.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added the row count to the column count.\", \"remediation\": \"Each row contains 5 tiles, so add 5 + 5 = 10.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student repeated the row count only 2 times.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 5 tiles per row, so add 5s.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 rows instead of 2.\", \"remediation\": \"There are only 2 rows: 5 + 5 = 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2484, 6, 1, 7, 'Fractions of Shapes', 274, 2, 'How many equal thirds make one whole rectangle?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '1 third', '2 thirds', '4 thirds', '3 thirds', 'D', 'active', 'Three thirds combine to form the complete whole: 3/3 = 1.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a single part.\", \"remediation\": \"All 3 thirds are needed to complete the whole.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 2.\", \"remediation\": \"3 equal parts (thirds) are needed to make 1 whole.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Four fourths make 1 whole; three thirds make 1 whole.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2485, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'How many faces does a cube have?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '6 faces', '8 faces', '12 faces', '4 faces', 'A', 'active', 'A cube has 6 flat square faces (top, bottom, and 4 sides).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused faces with vertices (corners).\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has 8 corners, but 6 flat faces.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused faces with edges.\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has 12 edges, but 6 faces.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student counted only side faces.\", \"remediation\": \"Include the top and bottom faces: 4 + 2 = 6 faces.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2486, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'Which group contains ONLY shapes that are quadrilaterals?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'Cube, Cylinder, Cone', 'Triangle, Square, Hexagon', 'Square, Rectangle, Trapezoid, Rhombus', 'Pentagon, Rectangle, Circle', 'C', 'active', 'A quadrilateral must have exactly 4 sides. Square, rectangle, trapezoid, and rhombus are all 4-sided polygons.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student selected 3D solids instead of 2D quadrilaterals.\", \"remediation\": \"Quadrilaterals are flat 2D polygons.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student included triangle (3 sides) and hexagon (6 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"Quadrilaterals must have exactly 4 sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student included pentagon (5 sides) and circle.\", \"remediation\": \"All shapes in the group must have 4 straight sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2487, 6, 1, 7, 'Fractions of Shapes', 274, 2, 'Two identical square sheets of paper are folded in half. Sheet A is folded into two rectangles. Sheet B is folded diagonally into two triangles. Are the halves of Sheet A equal in area to the halves of Sheet B?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'No, because triangles can never equal rectangles in area', 'No, because triangles have 3 sides and rectangles have 4', 'Cannot be determined', 'Yes, because both represent one half of identical whole squares', 'D', 'active', 'Equal shares of identical wholes have the same area, even if they have different shapes (both are half of the same square).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed different shapes must have different areas.\", \"remediation\": \"Two shapes with different appearances can have the exact same area.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused side count with area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is the amount of surface; both are half of the same whole.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought it is unknown.\", \"remediation\": \"Since the whole squares are identical, half of each is identical in area.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2488, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Rows & Columns', 273, 2, 'A grid has 4 rows and 5 columns of square units. What is the total area of the grid in square units?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '25 square units', '20 square units', '9 square units', '18 square units', 'B', 'active', '4 rows × 5 columns = 20 square units (or skip count by 5s: 5, 10, 15, 20).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student squared 5 (5 × 5 = 25).\", \"remediation\": \"There are only 4 rows: 4 × 5 = 20.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added rows and columns (4 + 5 = 9).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply rows by columns: 4 × 5 = 20 square units.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated perimeter (4 + 5 + 4 + 5 = 18).\", \"remediation\": \"Area is the total squares inside: 4 × 5 = 20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2489, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'How many more sides does a hexagon have than a triangle?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '2 more sides', '4 more sides', '3 more sides', '1 more side', 'C', 'active', 'A hexagon has 6 sides. A triangle has 3 sides. 6 - 3 = 3 more sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Hexagon (6) minus triangle (3) = 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted the sides of one of the shapes.\", \"remediation\": \"Hexagon = 6 sides, triangle = 3 sides; 6 - 3 = 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted pentagon from hexagon (6 - 5 = 1).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare hexagon (6) to triangle (3).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2490, 6, 1, 7, 'Fractions of Shapes', 274, 2, 'A rectangle is shaded so that 2 out of 4 equal parts are colored blue. What fraction of the rectangle is blue?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'Two fourths (or one half)', 'Four halves', 'Two thirds', 'One fourth', 'A', 'active', '2 parts out of 4 equal parts is two fourths, which is equal to one half of the rectangle.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"The numerator is 2 and denominator is 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student used denominator 3.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 total parts, so the fraction is 2 fourths.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student counted only 1 shaded part.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 2 shaded parts: 2/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2491, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Rows & Columns', 273, 2, 'Hassan wants to tile a rectangle that is 3 squares high and 3 squares wide. How many square tiles does he need?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '6 tiles', '12 tiles', '9 tiles', '8 tiles', 'C', 'active', '3 rows of 3 squares: 3 × 3 = 9 tiles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 3 = 6 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Each row has 3 tiles, so 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 tiles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated perimeter of the rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Count all square tiles covering the area: 3 × 3 = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"3 rows of 3 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2492, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'Which statement correctly describes the faces of a cube?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'It has 4 triangles and 1 square', 'All 6 faces are rectangles with different lengths', 'It has 2 circles and 1 rectangle', 'All 6 faces are identical squares', 'D', 'active', 'A cube is a special rectangular prism where all 6 faces are equal squares.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student described a square pyramid.\", \"remediation\": \"A pyramid has triangle faces; a cube has square faces.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student described a general rectangular prism.\", \"remediation\": \"In a cube, all faces must be EQUAL squares.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student described a cylinder.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder has circular faces; a cube has square faces.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2493, 6, 1, 7, 'Fractions of Shapes', 274, 2, 'A circular clock face is shaded from 12:00 to 4:00. What fraction of the clock face is shaded?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'One half', 'One third', 'One fourth', 'One sixth', 'B', 'active', 'A clock has 12 hours. 4 hours out of 12 is 4/12 = 1/3 of the circle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose one half (which would be 6 hours, to 6:00).\", \"remediation\": \"Half of a clock is 6 hours; 4 hours is 1/3 of the clock.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose one fourth (which would be 3 hours, to 3:00).\", \"remediation\": \"One fourth is 3 hours; 4 hours represents one third.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 12 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"4 out of 12 simplifies to 1 third.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2494, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'How many vertices (corners) does a pentagon have?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '5 vertices', '8 vertices', '6 vertices', '4 vertices', 'A', 'active', 'A polygon always has the same number of vertices as its sides. A pentagon has 5 sides and 5 vertices.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the 5 sharp corners of a pentagon.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 6 (hexagon).\", \"remediation\": \"A pentagon has 5 vertices.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 4 (quadrilateral).\", \"remediation\": \"Quadrilaterals have 4 vertices; pentagons have 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2495, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Rows & Columns', 273, 2, 'A chocolate bar is broken into 5 equal rows with 4 squares in each row. If Maria eats 1 entire row, how many squares of chocolate are left?', NULL, 2, 'High', '15 squares', '16 squares', '19 squares', '12 squares', 'B', 'active', 'Total squares = 5 rows × 4 = 20 squares. 1 row = 4 squares. Left = 20 - 4 = 16 squares (or 4 remaining rows × 4 = 16).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 5 instead of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Each row contains 4 squares, so subtract 4 from 20 to get 16.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted only 1 square instead of 1 entire row.\", \"remediation\": \"1 entire row has 4 squares.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 2 rows.\", \"remediation\": \"Only 1 row was eaten; 4 rows of 4 remain (16 squares).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2496, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'Which shape can have 4 equal sides, but does NOT need to have 4 right angles?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'Trapezoid', 'Square', 'Rectangle', 'Rhombus', 'D', 'active', 'A rhombus has 4 equal straight sides, but its angles can be acute and obtuse (slanted), unlike a square which must have right angles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid does not have 4 equal sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose square, which MUST have 4 right angles.\", \"remediation\": \"A square must have 4 right angles; a rhombus does not require right angles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle, which does not have 4 equal sides.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle has opposite sides equal, not all 4 sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2497, 6, 1, 7, 'Fractions of Shapes', 274, 2, 'A teacher asks: \"Can a circle be divided into 4 equal shares where each share has a different shape?\" What is the correct answer?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'No, in circles, 4 equal shares partitioned from the center must have the same shape', 'Yes, circles can be cut into any shapes with equal area', 'Circles can never have 4 equal shares', 'Only rectangles can be partitioned', 'A', 'active', 'Because a circle has rotational symmetry, partitioning from the center into 4 equal shares creates 4 identical wedge quadrants.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student applied rectangle rules to circles.\", \"remediation\": \"While rectangles can be cut into different shapes with equal area, standard radial cuts of circles produce identical quadrant wedges.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student stated circles cannot be divided into fourths.\", \"remediation\": \"A circle can easily be cut into 4 equal quadrants.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student claimed only rectangles can be partitioned.\", \"remediation\": \"Both circles and rectangles can be partitioned into equal shares.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2498, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Rows & Columns', 273, 2, 'Two students build rectangular arrays with 12 square tiles each. Student A builds 2 rows of 6. Student B builds 3 rows of 4. Which student used more total tiles?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'Student B used 1 more tile', 'Student A used more tiles', 'Both students used the exact same number of tiles (12)', 'Student B used more tiles', 'C', 'active', 'Both arrays have the same total area: 2 × 6 = 12 and 3 × 4 = 12 tiles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"Both total to 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought having 6 columns means more total tiles.\", \"remediation\": \"2 × 6 = 12 and 3 × 4 = 12; both arrays use exactly 12 tiles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought 3 rows means more tiles.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply rows by columns: both equal 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2499, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'What is the sum of the angles of a triangle plus the angles of a quadrilateral?', NULL, 2, 'High', '6 angles', '8 angles', '5 angles', '7 angles', 'D', 'active', 'A triangle has 3 angles. A quadrilateral has 4 angles. 3 + 4 = 7 angles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted angles.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangle (3) + Quadrilateral (4) = 7 angles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted 4 for both shapes.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have 3 angles, so 3 + 4 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted instead of adding.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the angles: 3 + 4 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2500, 6, 1, 7, 'Fractions of Shapes', 274, 2, 'Which phrase describes a shape partitioned into 4 equal shares where all 4 shares are colored?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'Four halves', 'Four fourths (one whole)', 'Three fourths', 'One fourth', 'B', 'active', 'When all 4 equal shares are colored, it represents four fourths, which is equal to 1 whole.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused fourths with halves.\", \"remediation\": \"4 equal parts are fourths, so 4 fourths make 1 whole.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student selected three fourths.\", \"remediation\": \"All 4 parts are shaded, which is 4 fourths (1 whole).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student selected only 1 part.\", \"remediation\": \"All parts are colored, meaning 4/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2501, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Identification', 272, 2, 'How many total edges do two cubes have combined?', NULL, 2, 'High', '12 edges', '16 edges', '24 edges', '20 edges', 'C', 'active', 'One cube has 12 edges. Two cubes have 12 + 12 = 24 edges.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student counted edges for only one cube.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by 2 for two cubes: 12 × 2 = 24 edges.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted 8 edges per cube (confused with vertices).\", \"remediation\": \"A cube has 12 edges, so two cubes have 24 edges.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an addition error.\", \"remediation\": \"12 + 12 = 24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2502, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Rows & Columns', 273, 2, 'A rectangular garden bed is 4 feet long and 2 feet wide. It is partitioned into 1-foot square sections. If carrots are planted in 3 of the squares, how many squares do NOT have carrots?', NULL, 2, 'High', '5 squares', '6 squares', '8 squares', '3 squares', 'A', 'active', 'Total square sections = 4 × 2 = 8 squares. Unplanted squares = 8 - 3 = 5 squares.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted from 9 by mistake.\", \"remediation\": \"Total area is 4 × 2 = 8, and 8 - 3 = 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the total squares without subtracting the planted ones.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the 3 planted squares from the total of 8: 8 - 3 = 5 squares.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the number of planted squares.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for squares that do NOT have carrots: 8 - 3 = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:06:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2503, 6, 1, 7, 'Quadrilaterals', 275, 3, 'Which attribute do ALL quadrilaterals share?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'They all have 4 right angles', 'All 4 sides are equal in length', 'They all have exactly 4 straight sides and 4 angles', 'They all have at least one curved side', 'C', 'active', 'By definition, every quadrilateral is a 2D closed polygon with exactly 4 straight sides and 4 angles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought all quadrilaterals have right angles (confused with rectangles).\", \"remediation\": \"Only rectangles and squares must have right angles; trapezoids and rhombuses do not.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused all quadrilaterals with squares or rhombuses.\", \"remediation\": \"Sides can have different lengths in a quadrilateral.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student allowed curved sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Polygons and quadrilaterals must have straight sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2504, 6, 1, 7, 'Squares, Rectangles & Rhombuses', 276, 3, 'Which shape ALWAYS has 4 right angles AND 4 equal sides?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'Trapezoid', 'Rectangle', 'Rhombus', 'Square', 'D', 'active', 'A square has both 4 equal sides and 4 right angles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid only has one pair of parallel sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle, which has 4 right angles but not necessarily 4 equal sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles have opposite sides equal, but a square has all 4 sides equal.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rhombus, which has 4 equal sides but not necessarily 4 right angles.\", \"remediation\": \"A rhombus does not have to have right angles; a square must have right angles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2505, 6, 1, 7, 'Squares, Rectangles & Rhombuses', 276, 3, 'Which quadrilateral ALWAYS has 4 equal sides, but its angles do NOT have to be 90 degrees?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'Rectangle', 'Rhombus', 'Trapezoid', 'Pentagon', 'B', 'active', 'A rhombus is defined as any parallelogram with 4 equal sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle must have 90-degree angles and does not require 4 equal sides.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid does not have 4 equal sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose pentagon (5 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"A rhombus is a 4-sided quadrilateral.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2506, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Areas & Unit Fractions', 278, 3, 'A hexagon is partitioned into 6 equal parts. What unit fraction represents the area of EACH part?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '1/6', '1/3', '1/4', '6/1', 'A', 'active', 'Each equal part of a shape divided into 6 equal areas is 1/6 of the total area.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 1/3.\", \"remediation\": \"1 part out of 6 is 1/6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student used denominator 4.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 6 equal parts, so the unit fraction is 1/6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"The numerator is 1 (one part) and denominator is 6 (total parts): 1/6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2507, 6, 1, 7, 'Quadrilaterals', 275, 3, 'Which of the following is NOT a quadrilateral?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'Rectangle', 'Triangle', 'Rhombus', 'Square', 'B', 'active', 'A triangle has 3 sides, so it is not a quadrilateral (which requires 4 sides).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle has 4 sides, so it is a quadrilateral.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rhombus.\", \"remediation\": \"A rhombus has 4 sides, so it is a quadrilateral.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose square.\", \"remediation\": \"A square has 4 sides, so it is a quadrilateral.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2508, 6, 1, 7, 'Squares, Rectangles & Rhombuses', 276, 3, 'Is every square also a rectangle?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'No, rectangles must have two sides longer than the others', 'No, a square can never be a rectangle', 'Only if the square is very large', 'Yes, because a square has 4 right angles and opposite sides that are parallel and equal', 'D', 'active', 'A rectangle is defined as a quadrilateral with 4 right angles. Since every square has 4 right angles, every square is a special rectangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student believes rectangles cannot have equal sides.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle can have all sides equal; that special rectangle is called a square.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thinks squares and rectangles are mutually exclusive.\", \"remediation\": \"A square satisfies all definitions of a rectangle (4 right angles).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student based geometry on size.\", \"remediation\": \"Shape definitions depend on angles and sides, not size.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2509, 6, 1, 7, 'Partitioning Shapes', 279, 3, 'A square is divided into 4 equal triangles by two diagonal lines. What fraction of the square is each triangle?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '1/4', '1/2', '1/8', '4/1', 'A', 'active', 'Each of the 4 equal triangular sections represents 1/4 of the total area of the square.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 1/2.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 equal triangles, so each one is 1/4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 1/8.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the triangles: there are 4, so each is 1/4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"Write 1 over the total number of pieces: 1/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2510, 6, 1, 7, 'Quadrilaterals', 275, 3, 'Which shape has 4 sides with exactly ONE pair of parallel sides?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'Rhombus', 'Parallelogram', 'Trapezoid', 'Rectangle', 'C', 'active', 'A trapezoid is a quadrilateral that has exactly one pair of parallel opposite sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rhombus.\", \"remediation\": \"A rhombus has two pairs of parallel sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose parallelogram.\", \"remediation\": \"A parallelogram has TWO pairs of parallel sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle has two pairs of parallel sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2511, 6, 1, 7, 'Squares, Rectangles & Rhombuses', 276, 3, 'Which statement is true about BOTH rectangles and rhombuses?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', 'Both must always have 4 right angles', 'Both must always have 4 equal sides', 'Neither has parallel sides', 'Both are parallelograms with two pairs of parallel opposite sides', 'D', 'active', 'Both rectangles and rhombuses are types of parallelograms, meaning their opposite sides are parallel and equal.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student applied rectangle angle rules to rhombuses.\", \"remediation\": \"A rhombus does not need to have right angles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student applied rhombus side rules to rectangles.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle does not need to have 4 equal sides.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student claimed neither has parallel sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Both shapes have 2 pairs of parallel sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2512, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Areas & Unit Fractions', 278, 3, 'A rectangle is partitioned into 8 equal parts. 3 parts are shaded. What fraction of the area of the rectangle is shaded?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '3/5', '3/8', '5/8', '1/8', 'B', 'active', 'Since each part has an area of 1/8, three shaded parts have a combined area of 3/8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student compared shaded to unshaded (ratio).\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator must represent ALL parts: 3/8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student counted unshaded parts.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the shaded parts (3) over total parts (8): 3/8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the unit fraction of a single part.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 3 shaded parts, so multiply 3 × 1/8 = 3/8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2513, 6, 1, 7, 'Quadrilaterals', 275, 3, 'Which shape is a quadrilateral with NO parallel sides?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', 'Rectangle', 'Parallelogram', 'Kite (or irregular 4-sided polygon)', 'Square', 'C', 'active', 'A general kite or irregular quadrilateral has 4 sides but no pairs of parallel sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles have 2 pairs of parallel sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose parallelogram.\", \"remediation\": \"Parallelograms have 2 pairs of parallel sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose square.\", \"remediation\": \"Squares have 2 pairs of parallel sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2514, 6, 1, 7, 'Squares, Rectangles & Rhombuses', 276, 3, 'A parallelogram has 4 equal sides. Each side measures 5 cm. What specific name describes this shape?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', 'Rhombus', 'Triangle', 'Trapezoid', 'Pentagon', 'A', 'active', 'A parallelogram with all 4 sides of equal length is a rhombus.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have 3 sides.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid is not a parallelogram with 4 equal sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose pentagon.\", \"remediation\": \"A parallelogram has 4 sides, not 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2515, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Areas & Unit Fractions', 278, 3, 'A large circle is divided into 2 equal halves. Each half is then cut into 2 equal parts. What fraction of the whole circle is each final piece?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '1/2', '1/8', '1/4', '2/4', 'C', 'active', 'Cutting 2 halves in half produces 4 equal fourths: each piece is 1/4 of the circle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the first cut size.\", \"remediation\": \"The halves were cut again, creating smaller pieces of 1/4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 8.\", \"remediation\": \"2 halves cut into 2 pieces each make 4 total pieces: 1/4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student wrote 2/4 for a single piece.\", \"remediation\": \"Each single piece is 1/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2516, 6, 1, 7, 'Partitioning Shapes', 279, 3, 'A garden bed is divided into 5 sections of equal area. Herbs are planted in 1 section, flowers in 2 sections, and vegetables in 2 sections. What fraction of the garden has vegetables?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '2/3', '1/5', '3/5', '2/5', 'D', 'active', 'Vegetables occupy 2 out of 5 equal sections, which represents 2/5 of the total area.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student used denominator 3.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 5 total sections: 2/5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the fraction for herbs (1/5).\", \"remediation\": \"Vegetables have 2 sections, so the fraction is 2/5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added flowers and vegetables.\", \"remediation\": \"Count only the vegetable sections: 2/5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2517, 6, 1, 7, 'Quadrilaterals', 275, 3, 'Which shape belongs to the quadrilateral family but is NEITHER a rectangle nor a rhombus?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', 'Square', 'Trapezoid', 'Hexagon', 'Circle', 'B', 'active', 'A trapezoid has 4 sides (quadrilateral), but does not have 4 right angles (not a rectangle) and does not have 4 equal sides (not a rhombus).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose square, which IS both a rectangle and a rhombus.\", \"remediation\": \"A square is both a rectangle and a rhombus.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose hexagon (6 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"A hexagon is not a quadrilateral.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose circle.\", \"remediation\": \"A circle is not a quadrilateral.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2518, 6, 1, 7, 'Squares, Rectangles & Rhombuses', 276, 3, 'A rectangle has a length of 6 inches and a width of 4 inches. Can this rectangle also be called a square?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', 'No, because a square must have all 4 sides equal in length', 'No, because its angles are not 90 degrees', 'Yes, all rectangles are squares', 'Yes, because it has 4 right angles', 'A', 'active', 'A square must have 4 equal sides. Since length (6) does not equal width (4), it cannot be a square.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student claimed the angles are not 90 degrees.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles always have 90-degree angles, but sides must also be equal to be a square.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought all rectangles are squares.\", \"remediation\": \"All squares are rectangles, but not all rectangles are squares.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student checked only the angles.\", \"remediation\": \"A square requires both 4 right angles AND 4 equal sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2519, 6, 1, 7, 'Partitioning Shapes', 279, 3, 'If a rectangle is partitioned into 3 equal columns, and each column is divided into 2 equal rows, how many total equal parts are created?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '5 equal parts', '6 equal parts', '8 equal parts', '9 equal parts', 'B', 'active', '3 columns × 2 rows = 6 equal parts. Each part has an area of 1/6 of the rectangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 2 = 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply rows by columns: 2 × 3 = 6 equal parts.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 2 = 6 parts.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student squared 3.\", \"remediation\": \"There are only 2 rows: 2 × 3 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2520, 6, 1, 7, 'Squares, Rectangles & Rhombuses', 276, 3, 'Why is a square considered both a rectangle AND a rhombus at the same time?', NULL, 3, 'High', 'Because its perimeter is equal to its area', 'Because all shapes with 4 sides are both rectangles and rhombuses', 'Because it can be turned into a circle', 'Because it satisfies the rules of both: 4 right angles (rectangle) and 4 equal sides (rhombus)', 'D', 'active', 'A square inherits the defining properties of both rectangles (4 right angles) and rhombuses (4 congruent sides).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused perimeter and area with geometric classification.\", \"remediation\": \"Classification depends on angles and side relationships.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student overgeneralized to all 4-sided shapes.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid has 4 sides but is neither a rectangle nor a rhombus.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an irrelevant statement.\", \"remediation\": \"Shape classification is based on angles and sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2521, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Areas & Unit Fractions', 278, 3, 'A square is divided into 4 parts with equal areas. Student A divides it using a vertical and horizontal line (4 smaller squares). Student B divides it using two diagonal lines (4 triangles). Which is true?', NULL, 3, 'High', 'Each small square has the exact same area as each small triangle (1/4 of the whole)', 'The small squares have more area because squares are larger than triangles', 'The triangles have more area because diagonals are longer', 'The areas cannot be compared because their shapes are different', 'A', 'active', 'Since both wholes are identical and both were divided into 4 equal shares, each share has an area of exactly 1/4 of the whole square.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed shape appearance dictates area size.\", \"remediation\": \"Equal shares of identical wholes have the same area regardless of shape.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused line length with area.\", \"remediation\": \"Both represent exactly 1/4 of the total area.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought different shapes cannot have equal areas.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is the measure of surface space, which is identical (1/4).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2522, 6, 1, 7, 'Quadrilaterals', 275, 3, 'Which category includes ALL of the following: Squares, Rectangles, Rhombuses, and Parallelograms?', NULL, 3, 'High', 'Pentagons', 'Trapezoids', 'Quadrilaterals', 'Triangles', 'C', 'active', 'The overarching category that includes all 4-sided closed polygons is Quadrilaterals.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose pentagons (5 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"All of these shapes have 4 sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoids.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid is only one specific subcategory, not the parent category.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose triangles (3 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"All of these shapes have 4 sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2523, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Areas & Unit Fractions', 278, 3, 'A rectangle is partitioned into 6 parts with equal area. If 4 of the parts are unshaded, what fraction of the area is SHADED?', NULL, 3, 'High', '4/6', '1/6', '2/4', '2/6 (or 1/3)', 'D', 'active', 'Total parts = 6. Unshaded = 4. Shaded = 6 - 4 = 2 parts. Shaded fraction = 2/6 = 1/3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the fraction of unshaded parts.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract unshaded from total: 6 - 4 = 2 shaded parts (2/6).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the unit fraction for 1 part.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 2 shaded parts: 2/6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student used unshaded as denominator (ratio).\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator is the total parts (6): 2/6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2524, 6, 1, 7, 'Squares, Rectangles & Rhombuses', 276, 3, 'A teacher draws a quadrilateral with opposite sides parallel and equal, but NONE of the angles are 90 degrees. Can this shape be a rectangle?', NULL, 3, 'High', 'Only if it has 4 equal sides', 'No, because a rectangle MUST have four 90-degree right angles', 'Yes, any parallelogram is a rectangle', 'Yes, if the sides are long enough', 'B', 'active', 'By mathematical definition, a rectangle must have 4 right angles ($90^circ$). If it has no right angles, it is a general parallelogram or rhombus.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student described a rhombus.\", \"remediation\": \"Without 90-degree angles, it cannot be a rectangle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought all parallelograms are rectangles.\", \"remediation\": \"A parallelogram only becomes a rectangle if its angles are right angles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student based geometry on side length.\", \"remediation\": \"Angles determine whether a parallelogram is a rectangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2525, 6, 1, 7, 'Partitioning Shapes', 279, 3, 'A square has an area of 36 square inches. It is partitioned into 4 equal sections. What is the area of EACH section in square inches?', NULL, 3, 'High', '6 square inches', '12 square inches', '9 square inches', '18 square inches', 'C', 'active', 'Divide the total area by the number of equal parts: 36 ÷ 4 = 9 square inches per section.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student found the side length of the square (√36 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the total area (36) by 4 parts: 36 ÷ 4 = 9 square inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 3 instead of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 equal sections: 36 ÷ 4 = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 2 (found halves).\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by 4 for fourths: 36 ÷ 4 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2526, 6, 1, 7, 'Quadrilaterals', 275, 3, 'Which shape is a parallelogram, a rectangle, and a rhombus all at once?', NULL, 3, 'High', 'Square', 'Regular hexagon', 'Trapezoid', 'Kite', 'A', 'active', 'A square has parallel opposite sides (parallelogram), 4 right angles (rectangle), and 4 equal sides (rhombus).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose hexagon (6 sides).\", \"remediation\": \"A hexagon is not a quadrilateral.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid is none of these.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose kite.\", \"remediation\": \"A kite does not have parallel sides or right angles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2527, 6, 1, 7, 'Equal Areas & Unit Fractions', 278, 3, 'Three identical rectangles are partitioned into equal shares. Rectangle X has 2 equal shares, Rectangle Y has 3 equal shares, and Rectangle Z has 4 equal shares. Which rectangle has the LARGEST individual share?', NULL, 3, 'High', 'Rectangle Y (thirds)', 'Rectangle Z (fourths)', 'Rectangle X (halves)', 'All individual shares are the same size', 'C', 'active', 'Decomposing a whole into fewer shares produces larger shares. 1/2 is larger than 1/3 and 1/4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose thirds.\", \"remediation\": \"Halves (divided by 2) are bigger than thirds (divided by 3).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought fourths are largest because 4 is greater than 2.\", \"remediation\": \"More pieces means smaller pieces when dividing the same whole.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed all shares are equal.\", \"remediation\": \"Fewer divisions produce larger individual parts: 1/2 > 1/3 > 1/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2528, 6, 1, 7, 'Lines & Line Segments', 280, 4, 'What is a part of a line that has ONE endpoint and extends infinitely in the other direction?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Line', 'Line segment', 'Point', 'Ray', 'D', 'active', 'A ray has exactly one starting endpoint and continues without end in one direction.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a line, which extends in BOTH directions.\", \"remediation\": \"A line has no endpoints; a ray has 1 endpoint.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student selected line segment, which has TWO endpoints.\", \"remediation\": \"A line segment stops at both ends; a ray continues forever in one direction.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student selected a point.\", \"remediation\": \"A point is an exact location with no length.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2529, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles', 281, 4, 'What do we call an angle that measures EXACTLY 90 degrees?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Acute angle', 'Right angle', 'Obtuse angle', 'Straight angle', 'B', 'active', 'An angle measuring exactly 90 degrees is a right angle (forms a square corner).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose acute angle (less than 90 degrees).\", \"remediation\": \"Acute angles are less than 90 degrees; right angles are exactly 90 degrees.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose obtuse angle (greater than 90 degrees).\", \"remediation\": \"Obtuse angles are greater than 90 degrees.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose straight angle (180 degrees).\", \"remediation\": \"A straight angle measures 180 degrees.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2530, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles', 281, 4, 'What do we call an angle that is LESS than 90 degrees?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Acute angle', 'Straight angle', 'Obtuse angle', 'Right angle', 'A', 'active', 'An acute angle measures between 0 degrees and 90 degrees (smaller than a square corner).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose straight angle.\", \"remediation\": \"Straight angles measure 180 degrees.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose obtuse angle.\", \"remediation\": \"Obtuse angles are wide (greater than 90 degrees); acute angles are small (less than 90).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose right angle.\", \"remediation\": \"Right angles are exactly 90 degrees.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2531, 6, 1, 7, 'Parallel', 282, 4, 'What is the term for two straight lines that stay the same distance apart and NEVER intersect?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Perpendicular lines', 'Parallel lines', 'Intersecting lines', 'Rays', 'B', 'active', 'Parallel lines lie in the same plane and never cross or touch, like railroad tracks.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose perpendicular lines.\", \"remediation\": \"Perpendicular lines cross each other at 90-degree right angles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose intersecting lines.\", \"remediation\": \"Intersecting lines cross each other; parallel lines never cross.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rays.\", \"remediation\": \"Parallel describes the relationship between two lines that never meet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2532, 6, 1, 7, 'Parallel', 282, 4, 'Two lines cross each other and form square corners (90-degree angles). What type of lines are they?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Curved lines', 'Parallel lines', 'Rays', 'Perpendicular lines', 'D', 'active', 'Lines that intersect to form 90-degree right angles are perpendicular lines.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose curved lines.\", \"remediation\": \"Perpendicular lines are straight lines forming 90-degree angles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose parallel lines.\", \"remediation\": \"Parallel lines never cross; perpendicular lines cross at 90 degrees.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rays.\", \"remediation\": \"The question describes the relationship between crossing lines (perpendicular).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2533, 6, 1, 7, 'Classifying Shapes', 283, 4, 'What do we call a triangle that has exactly ONE 90-degree right angle?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Right triangle', 'Acute triangle', 'Obtuse triangle', 'Equilateral triangle', 'A', 'active', 'A triangle containing one right angle is classified as a right triangle.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose acute triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"An acute triangle has all 3 angles less than 90 degrees.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose obtuse triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"An obtuse triangle has one angle greater than 90 degrees.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose equilateral triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"All angles in an equilateral triangle are 60 degrees.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2534, 6, 1, 7, 'Symmetry', 284, 4, 'How many lines of symmetry does a rectangle have (that is NOT a square)?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '0 lines of symmetry', '4 lines of symmetry', '2 lines of symmetry', '1 line of symmetry', 'C', 'active', 'A non-square rectangle can be folded in half horizontally and vertically (2 lines of symmetry). Diagonals do not match up when folded.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought it has no symmetry.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle has 2 lines of symmetry.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 4 (confused with a square).\", \"remediation\": \"A square has 4 lines of symmetry, but a rectangle only has 2 (horizontal and vertical).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student counted only 1 line.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle has both a vertical fold line and a horizontal fold line (2 lines).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2535, 6, 1, 7, 'Lines & Line Segments', 280, 4, 'What has TWO distinct endpoints and a definite length that can be measured?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Line', 'Ray', 'Angle', 'Line segment', 'D', 'active', 'A line segment connects two endpoints and has a measurable finite length.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose line, which goes on forever.\", \"remediation\": \"A line has no endpoints and infinite length.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose ray, which has only 1 endpoint.\", \"remediation\": \"A ray extends forever in one direction; a line segment has 2 endpoints.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose angle.\", \"remediation\": \"An angle is formed by two rays meeting at a vertex.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2536, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles', 281, 4, 'An angle measures 135 degrees. How is this angle classified?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'Straight angle', 'Obtuse angle', 'Acute angle', 'Right angle', 'B', 'active', 'An obtuse angle measures greater than 90 degrees and less than 180 degrees. 135 degrees is obtuse.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student classified 135 as straight.\", \"remediation\": \"Straight angles measure exactly 180 degrees.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student classified 135 as acute.\", \"remediation\": \"Acute angles are less than 90 degrees; 135 is greater than 90.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student classified 135 as a right angle.\", \"remediation\": \"Right angles measure exactly 90 degrees.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2537, 6, 1, 7, 'Symmetry', 284, 4, 'How many lines of symmetry does a square have?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '2 lines of symmetry', '8 lines of symmetry', '4 lines of symmetry', '1 line of symmetry', 'C', 'active', 'A square has 4 lines of symmetry: 1 horizontal, 1 vertical, and 2 diagonals.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student counted only horizontal and vertical lines, omitting the diagonals.\", \"remediation\": \"A square can also be folded along both diagonals, giving 2 + 2 = 4 lines.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the count.\", \"remediation\": \"Each fold line passes through opposite sides or opposite corners: exactly 4 lines.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student counted only 1 line.\", \"remediation\": \"A square has 4 lines of symmetry.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2538, 6, 1, 7, 'Classifying Shapes', 283, 4, 'A triangle has angles measuring 35 degrees, 55 degrees, and 90 degrees. What type of triangle is it?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'Right triangle', 'Equilateral triangle', 'Obtuse triangle', 'Acute triangle', 'A', 'active', 'Since one of the angles is exactly 90 degrees, it is classified as a right triangle.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose equilateral.\", \"remediation\": \"Equilateral triangles have all angles equal to 60 degrees.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student classified it as obtuse.\", \"remediation\": \"None of the angles are greater than 90 degrees.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student classified it as acute because two angles are acute.\", \"remediation\": \"Even though two angles are acute, the presence of a 90-degree angle makes it a right triangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2539, 6, 1, 7, 'Parallel', 282, 4, 'Which capital letter of the alphabet contains BOTH parallel line segments and perpendicular line segments?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'Letter L', 'Letter X', 'Letter H', 'Letter O', 'C', 'active', 'In the letter H, the two vertical sides are parallel to each other, and the horizontal crossbar is perpendicular to both sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose L, which has perpendicular lines but NO parallel lines.\", \"remediation\": \"Letter L has only 2 segments that meet at 90 degrees; it has no parallel lines.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose X, which has intersecting lines but no parallel lines.\", \"remediation\": \"Letter X does not have parallel segments.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose O, which has curved lines.\", \"remediation\": \"Letter O has no straight line segments.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2540, 6, 1, 7, 'Classifying Shapes', 283, 4, 'Which shape ALWAYS has two pairs of parallel sides and all 4 sides equal, but no right angles are required?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'Kite', 'Trapezoid', 'Rectangle', 'Rhombus', 'D', 'active', 'A rhombus has 2 pairs of parallel sides and all 4 sides equal.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose kite.\", \"remediation\": \"A kite has no parallel sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid only has 1 pair of parallel sides.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle requires 4 right angles and does not require 4 equal sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2541, 6, 1, 7, 'Symmetry', 284, 4, 'Which of the following figures has NO lines of symmetry?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'Equilateral triangle', 'Scalene triangle (all 3 sides different lengths)', 'Isosceles triangle', 'Regular hexagon', 'B', 'active', 'A scalene triangle has all sides and angles of different measures, so it cannot be folded into matching halves (0 lines of symmetry).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose equilateral triangle, which has 3 lines of symmetry.\", \"remediation\": \"An equilateral triangle has 3 lines of symmetry.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose isosceles triangle, which has 1 line of symmetry.\", \"remediation\": \"An isosceles triangle has 1 line of symmetry down the middle.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose regular hexagon, which has 6 lines of symmetry.\", \"remediation\": \"Regular hexagons have 6 lines of symmetry.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2542, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles', 281, 4, 'What type of angle is formed by the hands of a clock at exactly 3:00?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'Right angle (90 degrees)', 'Straight angle', 'Acute angle', 'Obtuse angle', 'A', 'active', 'At 3:00, the minute hand points to 12 and the hour hand points to 3, forming a 90-degree right angle.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose straight angle.\", \"remediation\": \"A straight angle occurs at 6:00 (180 degrees).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose acute angle.\", \"remediation\": \"At 3:00, the angle is exactly 90 degrees (a right angle).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose obtuse angle.\", \"remediation\": \"Obtuse angles are greater than 90 degrees (like at 4:00 or 5:00).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2543, 6, 1, 7, 'Lines & Line Segments', 280, 4, 'How many endpoints does a geometric line have?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '1 endpoint', '0 endpoints', '2 endpoints', 'Infinite endpoints', 'B', 'active', 'A line has zero endpoints because it extends without ending in both opposite directions.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 1 (confused line with ray).\", \"remediation\": \"A ray has 1 endpoint; a line has 0 endpoints.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 2 (confused line with line segment).\", \"remediation\": \"A line segment has 2 endpoints; a line extends forever with 0 endpoints.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused infinite length with endpoints.\", \"remediation\": \"A line has infinite points, but zero endpoints.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2544, 6, 1, 7, 'Classifying Shapes', 283, 4, 'A quadrilateral has exactly ONE pair of parallel sides. How is this quadrilateral classified?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'Rectangle', 'Parallelogram', 'Rhombus', 'Trapezoid', 'D', 'active', 'In elementary geometry, a quadrilateral with exactly one pair of parallel sides is a trapezoid.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle has two pairs of parallel sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose parallelogram.\", \"remediation\": \"A parallelogram must have TWO pairs of parallel sides.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rhombus.\", \"remediation\": \"A rhombus has two pairs of parallel sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2545, 6, 1, 7, 'Classifying Shapes', 283, 4, 'Can a triangle have TWO right angles? Why or why not?', NULL, 4, 'High', 'No, because the angles of a triangle add up to 180 degrees; two right angles would equal 180 degrees, leaving 0 for the third angle', 'Yes, any right triangle has two right angles', 'Yes, if the triangle is very large', 'No, because triangles cannot have any right angles', 'A', 'active', 'A triangle can have at most ONE right angle because the sum of all three angles is always 180 degrees ($90 + 90 = 180$).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought right triangles have two right angles.\", \"remediation\": \"A right triangle has only ONE right angle and two acute angles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought size changes angle sum.\", \"remediation\": \"All flat triangles have an angle sum of 180 degrees regardless of size.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student claimed triangles cannot have right angles.\", \"remediation\": \"A triangle can have exactly one right angle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2546, 6, 1, 7, 'Symmetry', 284, 4, 'How many lines of symmetry does a regular pentagon have?', NULL, 4, 'High', '0 lines of symmetry', '1 line of symmetry', '5 lines of symmetry', '10 lines of symmetry', 'C', 'active', 'A regular polygon with n equal sides has n lines of symmetry. A regular pentagon has 5 lines of symmetry (from each vertex to the opposite midpoint).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought it has no symmetry.\", \"remediation\": \"Regular polygons are symmetric; a regular pentagon has 5 lines.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted only 1 line.\", \"remediation\": \"A regular pentagon can be folded from all 5 vertices, giving 5 lines of symmetry.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the count.\", \"remediation\": \"Each line connects one vertex to one midpoint: exactly 5 lines.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2547, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles', 281, 4, 'An angle turns through 1/4 of a full circle. How many degrees does this angle measure?', NULL, 4, 'High', '180 degrees', '45 degrees', '360 degrees', '90 degrees', 'D', 'active', 'A full circle is 360 degrees. 1/4 of a circle is 360 ÷ 4 = 90 degrees (a right angle).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 1/2 of a circle (180 degrees).\", \"remediation\": \"Half a circle is 180 degrees; one-fourth of a circle is 90 degrees.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 1/8 of a circle.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 360 by 4: 360 ÷ 4 = 90 degrees.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the full circle degrees.\", \"remediation\": \"A full circle is 360 degrees; multiply 360 × 1/4 = 90 degrees.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2548, 6, 1, 7, 'Classifying Shapes', 283, 4, 'A shape is a polygon with 4 sides. It has two pairs of parallel sides, and adjacent sides meet at right angles. All 4 sides are 8 cm long. What is the MOST SPECIFIC name for this shape?', NULL, 4, 'High', 'Quadrilateral', 'Square', 'Rectangle', 'Parallelogram', 'B', 'active', 'While all these names apply, \"Square\" is the most specific name because it includes 4 right angles and 4 equal sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose quadrilateral, the broadest category.\", \"remediation\": \"Look for the most specific classification: Square.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose rectangle, which is correct but less specific than square.\", \"remediation\": \"Because all sides are equal, \\\"Square\\\" is more specific than \\\"Rectangle\\\".\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose parallelogram, which is a broad category.\", \"remediation\": \"Square is the most precise classification.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2549, 6, 1, 7, 'Parallel', 282, 4, 'How many pairs of perpendicular sides does a standard rectangle have?', NULL, 4, 'High', '2 pairs of perpendicular sides', '1 pair of perpendicular sides', '4 pairs of perpendicular sides', '0 pairs', 'C', 'active', 'A rectangle has 4 corners, and at each corner two adjacent sides meet at 90 degrees. Therefore, it has 4 pairs of perpendicular sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused perpendicular pairs with parallel pairs (it has 2 pairs of parallel sides).\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle has 2 pairs of parallel sides, but 4 corners forming 4 pairs of perpendicular sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted only 1 corner.\", \"remediation\": \"All 4 corners form perpendicular intersections.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought rectangles have no perpendicular sides.\", \"remediation\": \"All 4 corners are 90-degree right angles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2550, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles', 281, 4, 'Two acute angles are placed side by side to form a new combined angle. Can the combined angle be obtuse?', NULL, 4, 'High', 'Yes, for example 50 degrees + 60 degrees = 110 degrees, which is obtuse', 'Yes, but only if one angle is negative', 'No, two acute angles can only ever make an acute angle', 'No, they will always equal exactly 90 degrees', 'A', 'active', 'Two acute angles (both $< 90^circ$) can sum to any value between $0^circ$ and $< 180^circ$. If each is greater than $45^circ$ (e.g. $50^circ + 60^circ = 110^circ$), the result is obtuse.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student introduced negative angles.\", \"remediation\": \"Geometric angles in elementary math are positive.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed adding acute angles always stays acute.\", \"remediation\": \"Adding two angles larger than 45 degrees results in an angle greater than 90 degrees (obtuse).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought they always form a right angle.\", \"remediation\": \"They only form 90 degrees if they are complementary.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2551, 6, 1, 7, 'Symmetry', 284, 4, 'How many lines of symmetry does a regular hexagon have?', NULL, 4, 'High', '3 lines of symmetry', '12 lines of symmetry', '6 lines of symmetry', '4 lines of symmetry', 'C', 'active', 'A regular hexagon has 6 lines of symmetry: 3 connecting opposite vertices and 3 connecting midpoints of opposite sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student counted only vertex lines and missed side midpoint lines.\", \"remediation\": \"A regular hexagon has 3 vertex-to-vertex lines + 3 side-to-side lines = 6 lines.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the count.\", \"remediation\": \"There are exactly 6 unique lines of symmetry.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 4.\", \"remediation\": \"A regular polygon with 6 sides has 6 lines of symmetry.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2552, 6, 1, 7, 'Classifying Shapes', 283, 4, 'Which statement is ALWAYS true about any equilateral triangle?', NULL, 4, 'High', 'It has 0 lines of symmetry', 'It can be a right triangle', 'It can be an obtuse triangle', 'It is always an acute triangle because all 3 angles are 60 degrees', 'D', 'active', 'In an equilateral triangle, all 3 angles are equal: $180^circ div 3 = 60^circ$. Since $60^circ < 90^circ$, it is always an acute triangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought it has no symmetry.\", \"remediation\": \"An equilateral triangle has 3 lines of symmetry.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought an equilateral triangle can have a 90-degree angle.\", \"remediation\": \"All angles in an equilateral triangle must be 60 degrees; it cannot have a 90-degree angle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought it can be obtuse.\", \"remediation\": \"None of the angles can be greater than 90 degrees.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:07:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2553, 6, 1, 7, 'Coordinate Plane', 285, 5, 'What are the coordinates of the ORIGIN on a coordinate plane?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '(1, 1)', '(0, 0)', '(0, 1)', '(1, 0)', 'B', 'active', 'The origin is the intersection of the x-axis and y-axis where both coordinates are zero: (0, 0).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose (1, 1).\", \"remediation\": \"The origin is the starting point at zero: (0, 0).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose (0, 1).\", \"remediation\": \"Both x and y are zero at the origin.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose (1, 0).\", \"remediation\": \"The origin has coordinates (0, 0).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2554, 6, 1, 7, 'Coordinate Plane', 285, 5, 'In the ordered pair (4, 7), which number represents the x-coordinate?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '4', '28', '7', '11', 'A', 'active', 'In an ordered pair (x, y), the first number is the x-coordinate (horizontal distance). Here, x = 4.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"The x-coordinate is 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 7, which is the y-coordinate.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the alphabetical order: x comes before y, so the first number is x.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"The x-coordinate is simply the first number in the parentheses (4).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2555, 6, 1, 7, 'Graphing Points', 286, 5, 'To plot the point (3, 5) starting from the origin (0,0), how do you move?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'Move 5 units right, then 3 units up', 'Move 3 units right along the x-axis, then 5 units up along the y-axis', 'Move 3 units up, then 5 units right', 'Move 3 units left, then 5 units down', 'B', 'active', 'First coordinate (3) is horizontal (move right). Second coordinate (5) is vertical (move up).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student swapped the x and y movements.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: run before you jump (x-axis horizontal first, then y-axis vertical).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student moved vertical first.\", \"remediation\": \"Always move along the horizontal x-axis first.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student moved in the negative direction.\", \"remediation\": \"Positive coordinates in Quadrant I move right and up.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2556, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Classification', 287, 5, 'Which property is shared by ALL rectangles?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'They have 5 sides', 'All 4 sides are always equal in length', 'They have 0 parallel sides', 'They all have 4 right angles and opposite sides that are parallel and equal', 'D', 'active', 'By definition, a rectangle has 4 right angles ($90^circ$) and opposite sides that are parallel and equal in length.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student gave 5 sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles have 4 sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student described a square/rhombus.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle does not need all 4 sides equal; only opposite sides must be equal.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student claimed 0 parallel sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles have 2 pairs of parallel sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2557, 6, 1, 7, 'Hierarchy of Shape', 288, 5, 'True or False: Every square is a rectangle, but not every rectangle is a square.', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'True', 'False, every rectangle is a square', 'False, squares and rectangles are completely unrelated', 'False, only small squares are rectangles', 'A', 'active', 'A rectangle requires 4 right angles (which all squares have). A square also requires 4 equal sides (which many rectangles do not have). Thus, all squares are rectangles.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed the hierarchy.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle with sides 4 and 8 is not a square, so not all rectangles are squares.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought they are unrelated.\", \"remediation\": \"Square is a special subcategory of rectangle.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student conditioned on size.\", \"remediation\": \"Geometric definitions apply to all sizes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2558, 6, 1, 7, 'Coordinate Plane', 285, 5, 'A point is located at (6, 0). Where does this point lie?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'In Quadrant II', 'Directly on the y-axis', 'Directly on the x-axis', 'At the origin', 'C', 'active', 'Since the y-coordinate is 0, the point has not moved vertically and lies directly on the horizontal x-axis.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose Quadrant II.\", \"remediation\": \"It lies on the positive x-axis boundary.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose y-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"Points on the y-axis have x = 0; here y = 0, so it is on the x-axis.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose origin.\", \"remediation\": \"The origin is (0, 0); this point is at x = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2559, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Classification', 287, 5, 'Which shape has 4 equal sides and 2 pairs of parallel sides?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'Trapezoid', 'Scalene triangle', 'Pentagon', 'Rhombus', 'D', 'active', 'A rhombus is an equilateral parallelogram with 4 equal sides and 2 pairs of parallel sides.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid only has 1 pair of parallel sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A triangle has 3 sides; rhombus has 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose pentagon.\", \"remediation\": \"A pentagon has 5 sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2560, 6, 1, 7, 'Graphing Points', 286, 5, 'What are the coordinates of a point that is 8 units right from the origin and 2 units up?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '(6, 2)', '(8, 2)', '(2, 8)', '(10, 0)', 'B', 'active', 'Horizontal movement is x = 8, vertical movement is y = 2: (8, 2).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow coordinates directly: (8, 2).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student swapped x and y.\", \"remediation\": \"Write horizontal distance first (8), then vertical distance (2): (8, 2).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 8 + 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Coordinates are written as an ordered pair (8, 2).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2561, 6, 1, 7, 'Hierarchy of Shape', 288, 5, 'If a category of polygons has a certain property, do all SUBCATEGORIES within it also share that property?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'No, subcategories never share parent properties', 'Only if the polygon is a triangle', 'Yes, subcategories inherit all properties of their parent category', 'Only if the property is color', 'C', 'active', 'In geometric hierarchies, any property belonging to a category (e.g. parallelograms have opposite sides parallel) also belongs to all its subcategories (rectangles, rhombuses, squares).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student misunderstood hierarchy inheritance.\", \"remediation\": \"Subcategories inherit all defining features of the larger category.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student limited the rule to triangles.\", \"remediation\": \"Inheritance applies to all geometric hierarchies.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student mentioned color.\", \"remediation\": \"Geometric definitions pertain to angles, sides, and symmetry.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2562, 6, 1, 7, 'Coordinate Plane', 285, 5, 'Point P is at (2, 3) and Point Q is at (7, 3). What is the distance between Point P and Point Q?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '5 units', '9 units', '6 units', '10 units', 'A', 'active', 'Since y-coordinates are the same (3), subtract x-coordinates: 7 - 2 = 5 units.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 7 + 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance is the difference: 7 - 2 = 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an off-by-one counting error.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 7 - 2 = 5 units.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the differing coordinates to find distance: 7 - 2 = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2563, 6, 1, 7, 'Hierarchy of Shape', 288, 5, 'Which statement correctly explains why a rectangle is NOT always a rhombus?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'A rectangle does not have parallel sides', 'A rectangle has only 3 angles', 'A rectangle does not necessarily have all 4 sides equal in length', 'A rhombus has right angles, but a rectangle does not', 'C', 'active', 'A rhombus must have 4 equal sides. Many rectangles have two long sides and two short sides, so they are not rhombuses.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student claimed rectangles lack parallel sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles have 2 pairs of parallel sides.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student stated 3 angles.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles have 4 angles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed the angle rule.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles have right angles; rhombuses do not require right angles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2564, 6, 1, 7, 'Graphing Points', 286, 5, 'Three vertices of a rectangle on a coordinate grid are at (1, 1), (5, 1), and (5, 4). What are the coordinates of the fourth vertex?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '(4, 5)', '(1, 5)', '(4, 1)', '(1, 4)', 'D', 'active', 'The missing vertex must share x = 1 with (1, 1) and y = 4 with (5, 4). The fourth vertex is (1, 4).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated both coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"Align vertically with (1, 1) and horizontally with (5, 4).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student used y = 5 instead of y = 4.\", \"remediation\": \"The top edge has y = 4, so the vertex is (1, 4).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student swapped coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"x must be 1 and y must be 4: (1, 4).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2565, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Classification', 287, 5, 'Which shape is a parallelogram with 4 equal sides AND 4 right angles?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'Trapezoid', 'Square', 'Kite', 'Regular hexagon', 'B', 'active', 'A square is the unique shape that is simultaneously an equilateral rhombus and an equiangular rectangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"Trapezoids do not have 4 equal sides and 4 right angles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose kite.\", \"remediation\": \"A kite does not have 4 equal sides or 4 right angles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose hexagon.\", \"remediation\": \"A hexagon has 6 sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2566, 6, 1, 7, 'Coordinate Plane', 285, 5, 'A map has a school at (2, 5) and a library at (2, 9). How many blocks apart are the school and library?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '4 blocks', '2 blocks', '7 blocks', '14 blocks', 'A', 'active', 'Since x-coordinates are identical (2), subtract y-coordinates: 9 - 5 = 4 blocks.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student took the x-coordinate.\", \"remediation\": \"The distance is along the y-axis: 9 - 5 = 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 9 - 5 = 4 blocks.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 9 + 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance between two points is the difference: 9 - 5 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2567, 6, 1, 7, 'Hierarchy of Shape', 288, 5, 'Which classification hierarchy correctly orders shapes from MOST GENERAL to MOST SPECIFIC?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'Square → Rectangle → Parallelogram → Quadrilateral', 'Polygon → Quadrilateral → Parallelogram → Rectangle → Square', 'Quadrilateral → Polygon → Square → Rectangle', 'Parallelogram → Quadrilateral → Square → Rectangle', 'B', 'active', 'Polygon is broadest (any closed multi-sided figure), followed by 4-sided (Quadrilateral), 2 parallel pairs (Parallelogram), 4 right angles (Rectangle), and finally 4 equal sides (Square).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student ordered from most specific to most general.\", \"remediation\": \"General starts with Polygon and narrows down to Square.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student placed quadrilateral before polygon.\", \"remediation\": \"All quadrilaterals are polygons, so polygon comes first.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student placed parallelogram before quadrilateral.\", \"remediation\": \"All parallelograms are quadrilaterals, so quadrilateral is more general.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2568, 6, 1, 7, 'Graphing Points', 286, 5, 'Point M is at (0, 7). Which axis does Point M sit on?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'Both x-axis and y-axis', 'x-axis', 'z-axis', 'y-axis', 'D', 'active', 'When x = 0, the point has zero horizontal distance and lies directly on the vertical y-axis.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose both (origin).\", \"remediation\": \"Only (0, 0) sits on both axes.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose x-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"Points on the x-axis have y = 0. Since x = 0 here, it is on the y-axis.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student introduced z-axis (3D).\", \"remediation\": \"A 2D coordinate plane has only x and y axes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2569, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Classification', 287, 5, 'Can a trapezoid be classified as a parallelogram?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'No, because a trapezoid has only 1 pair of parallel sides, while a parallelogram must have 2 pairs', 'Yes, all trapezoids are parallelograms', 'Yes, if all angles are right angles', 'Only if it has 5 sides', 'A', 'active', 'Under the standard exclusive definition, a trapezoid has exactly one pair of parallel sides, so it is not a parallelogram.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought trapezoids are parallelograms.\", \"remediation\": \"Parallelograms require 2 pairs of parallel sides; trapezoids have only 1 pair.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an assumption.\", \"remediation\": \"A shape with 4 right angles has 2 pairs of parallel sides, making it a rectangle.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student mentioned 5 sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Both shapes are 4-sided quadrilaterals.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2570, 6, 1, 7, 'Hierarchy of Shape', 288, 5, 'A mystery shape is a quadrilateral with 4 equal sides. Which statement MUST be true about this mystery shape?', NULL, 5, 'High', 'It must have 4 lines of symmetry', 'It must be a trapezoid', 'It must be a rhombus (and could also be a square)', 'It can never have right angles', 'C', 'active', 'Any quadrilateral with 4 congruent sides is guaranteed to be a rhombus. If it also has right angles, it is a square.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed 4 lines of symmetry.\", \"remediation\": \"A non-square rhombus has only 2 lines of symmetry.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A trapezoid does not have 4 equal sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student claimed it can never have right angles.\", \"remediation\": \"A square is a rhombus that has right angles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2571, 6, 1, 7, 'Coordinate Plane', 285, 5, 'A rectangle on a coordinate grid has vertices at (2, 2), (8, 2), (8, 6), and (2, 6). What is the PERIMETER of the rectangle?', NULL, 5, 'High', '24 units', '10 units', '14 units', '20 units', 'D', 'active', 'Length = 8 - 2 = 6 units. Width = 6 - 2 = 4 units. Perimeter = 2 × (length + width) = 2 × (6 + 4) = 20 units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated area (6 × 4 = 24) instead of perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is the distance around the outside: 6 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 20 units.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added length and width once (6 + 4 = 10).\", \"remediation\": \"Double the sum for perimeter: 2 × 10 = 20 units.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter = 2(6) + 2(4) = 12 + 8 = 20 units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2572, 6, 1, 7, 'Coordinate Plane', 285, 5, 'What is the AREA of the rectangle with vertices at (2, 2), (8, 2), (8, 6), and (2, 6)?', NULL, 5, 'High', '48 square units', '24 square units', '20 square units', '12 square units', 'B', 'active', 'Length = 8 - 2 = 6 units. Width = 6 - 2 = 4 units. Area = length × width = 6 × 4 = 24 square units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area = 6 × 4 = 24.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated perimeter (20) instead of area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is length × width: 6 × 4 = 24 square units.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student halved the area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area of a rectangle is base × height: 6 × 4 = 24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2573, 6, 1, 7, 'Hierarchy of Shape', 288, 5, 'Which statement is FALSE regarding geometric categories?', NULL, 5, 'High', 'All squares are rectangles', 'All squares are rhombuses', 'All rectangles are squares', 'All rhombuses are parallelograms', 'C', 'active', 'Statement A is FALSE because a rectangle with sides 3 and 7 is not a square. All other statements are true.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought squares are not rectangles.\", \"remediation\": \"All squares have 4 right angles, so they are all rectangles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought squares are not rhombuses.\", \"remediation\": \"All squares have 4 equal sides, so they are all rhombuses.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought rhombuses are not parallelograms.\", \"remediation\": \"All rhombuses have 2 pairs of parallel sides, so they are parallelograms.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2574, 6, 1, 7, 'Graphing Points', 286, 5, 'A square has vertices at (3, 3), (7, 3), and (7, 7). What are the coordinates of the fourth vertex?', NULL, 5, 'High', '(3, 7)', '(4, 4)', '(7, 3)', '(3, 3)', 'A', 'active', 'The missing vertex must align with x = 3 and y = 7: (3, 7).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated side length as coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"The coordinates are (3, 7).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student selected an existing vertex.\", \"remediation\": \"The fourth vertex completes the square at (3, 7).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose an existing corner.\", \"remediation\": \"The upper-left corner is at (3, 7).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2575, 6, 1, 7, 'Shape Classification', 287, 5, 'Why are all parallelograms classified as quadrilaterals?', NULL, 5, 'High', 'Because all quadrilaterals have parallel sides', 'Because they have right angles', 'Because every parallelogram has exactly 4 sides', 'Because they have equal diagonals', 'C', 'active', 'The definition of a quadrilateral is any closed polygon with 4 sides. Every parallelogram has 4 sides, so it is a quadrilateral.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed the statement.\", \"remediation\": \"Not all quadrilaterals have parallel sides (e.g. kites, trapezoids).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought all parallelograms have right angles.\", \"remediation\": \"Only rectangles and squares have right angles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student cited diagonal length.\", \"remediation\": \"The defining property is having 4 sides.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2576, 6, 1, 7, 'Coordinate Plane', 285, 5, 'If you start at point (4, 2), move 3 units left and 5 units up, what is your new location?', NULL, 5, 'High', '(7, -3)', '(7, 7)', '(1, -3)', '(1, 7)', 'D', 'active', 'Moving left subtracts from x: 4 - 3 = 1. Moving up adds to y: 2 + 5 = 7. New point = (1, 7).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added to x and subtracted from y.\", \"remediation\": \"Left = subtract from x (1), Up = add to y (7): (1, 7).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 to x instead of moving left.\", \"remediation\": \"Moving left means subtracting from x: 4 - 3 = 1.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted from y instead of moving up.\", \"remediation\": \"Moving up means adding to y: 2 + 5 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2577, 6, 1, 7, 'Hierarchy of Shape', 288, 5, 'Which shape possesses ALL the properties of a polygon, a quadrilateral, a trapezoid, a parallelogram, a rectangle, and a rhombus?', NULL, 5, 'High', 'Circle', 'Square', 'Equilateral triangle', 'Kite', 'B', 'active', 'The square sits at the bottom of the quadrilateral hierarchy and inherits the defining properties of all these categories.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose circle.\", \"remediation\": \"A circle is not a polygon.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangles have 3 sides.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose kite.\", \"remediation\": \"A kite does not have parallel sides or right angles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2578, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Triangles', 289, 6, 'What is the formula for the area of any triangle with base b and height h?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'A = 1/2 × b × h', 'A = b + h', 'A = b × h', 'A = 2 × (b + h)', 'A', 'active', 'The area of any triangle is half the area of a parallelogram with the same base and height: A = 1/2 × b × h.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added base and height.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply base and height, then divide by 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the formula for a rectangle/parallelogram.\", \"remediation\": \"A triangle is half of a parallelogram, so multiply by 1/2: A = 1/2 bh.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student gave a perimeter formula.\", \"remediation\": \"Area measures surface area: 1/2 × base × height.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2579, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Triangles', 289, 6, 'A right triangle has a base of 8 cm and a height of 6 cm. What is its area?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '48 cm²', '24 cm²', '14 cm²', '28 cm²', 'B', 'active', 'A = 1/2 × b × h = 1/2 × 8 × 6 = 1/2 × 48 = 24 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply by 1/2 (calculated 8 × 6 = 48).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to divide by 2 for triangle area: 48 ÷ 2 = 24 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added base and height (8 + 6 = 14).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply base and height, then divide by 2: (8 × 6)/2 = 24.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"1/2 × 48 = 24 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2580, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Quadrilaterals', 290, 6, 'What is the area of a parallelogram with a base of 10 meters and a perpendicular height of 7 meters?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '17 m²', '35 m²', '34 m²', '70 m²', 'D', 'active', 'Area of a parallelogram = base × height = 10 × 7 = 70 m².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added base and height: 10 + 7 = 17.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply base by height: 10 × 7 = 70 m².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 2 (used triangle formula).\", \"remediation\": \"A parallelogram does not divide by 2: Area = base × height = 70 m².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated perimeter: 2(10 + 7) = 34.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is multiplication: 10 × 7 = 70 m².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2581, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 294, 6, 'What is the volume of a rectangular prism with length 5 cm, width 3 cm, and height 4 cm?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '60 cm³', '12 cm³', '24 cm³', '47 cm³', 'A', 'active', 'Volume = length × width × height = 5 × 3 × 4 = 15 × 4 = 60 cm³.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added the dimensions: 5 + 3 + 4 = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume is found by MULTIPLYING all three dimensions: 5 × 3 × 4 = 60 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied only two dimensions (6 × 4 = 24).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply all three dimensions: 5 × 3 = 15, and 15 × 4 = 60.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated surface area.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume formula is V = l × w × h = 60 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2582, 6, 1, 7, 'Surface Area', 293, 6, 'What is the 2D flat pattern called that can be folded to form a 3D solid figure?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'Perimeter', 'Prism', 'Net', 'Volume', 'C', 'active', 'A net is a 2-dimensional representation that unfolds all the faces of a 3D solid.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"The unfolded pattern is a net.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student named the 3D solid itself.\", \"remediation\": \"The unfolded flat pattern is called a net.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose volume.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume measures internal space; a net is the unfolded 2D surface.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2583, 6, 1, 7, 'Surface Area', 293, 6, 'A cube has edge length 3 cm. What is the area of ONE of its faces?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '27 cm²', '54 cm²', '12 cm²', '9 cm²', 'D', 'active', 'Each face of a cube is a square: Area = side × side = 3 × 3 = 9 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated volume (3³ = 27 cm³).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the area of ONE face: 3 × 3 = 9 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated total surface area of all 6 faces (6 × 9 = 54).\", \"remediation\": \"Read carefully: the area of a SINGLE face is 3 × 3 = 9 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated perimeter of a face (4 × 3 = 12).\", \"remediation\": \"Area is side squared: 3 × 3 = 9 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2584, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Quadrilaterals', 290, 6, 'What is the area of a trapezoid with parallel bases b1 = 4 cm and b2 = 6 cm, and height h = 5 cm?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '30 cm²', '25 cm²', '50 cm²', '20 cm²', 'B', 'active', 'Area = 1/2 × (b1 + b2) × h = 1/2 × (4 + 6) × 5 = 1/2 × 10 × 5 = 25 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 6 × 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the trapezoid formula: A = ((b1 + b2)/2) × h = 25 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply by 1/2 (calculated 10 × 5 = 50).\", \"remediation\": \"Take half of the sum of bases before multiplying by height: 1/2 × 10 × 5 = 25 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4 × 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Add both bases together: 4 + 6 = 10, then 1/2 × 10 × 5 = 25.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2585, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 294, 6, 'A box has a base area of 20 square inches and a height of 6 inches. What is its volume?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '26 cubic inches', '60 cubic inches', '120 cubic inches', '240 cubic inches', 'C', 'active', 'Volume = Base Area × height = B × h = 20 × 6 = 120 cubic inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 20 + 6 = 26.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume is base area MULTIPLIED by height: 20 × 6 = 120.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Prism volume does not divide by 2: V = B × h = 120.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the volume.\", \"remediation\": \"V = 20 × 6 = 120 cubic inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2586, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Polygons', 291, 6, 'A composite polygon is made of a rectangle (6 cm by 4 cm) with a triangle on top (base 6 cm, height 3 cm). What is the TOTAL area?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '33 cm²', '51 cm²', '42 cm²', '24 cm²', 'A', 'active', 'Rectangle Area = 6 × 4 = 24 cm². Triangle Area = 1/2 × 6 × 3 = 9 cm². Total Area = 24 + 9 = 33 cm².', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"24 + 9 = 33 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to divide triangle area by 2 (24 + 18 = 42).\", \"remediation\": \"Triangle area is 1/2 × 6 × 3 = 9. Total = 24 + 9 = 33 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated only the rectangle area (24).\", \"remediation\": \"Add the triangle area (9) to the rectangle area (24): 33 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2587, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 294, 6, 'A small cube has edge length 1/2 inch. How many of these small cubes are needed to completely fill a prism with dimensions 2 inches by 1 1/2 inches by 1 inch?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '12 small cubes', '3 small cubes', '24 small cubes', '48 small cubes', 'C', 'active', 'Dimensions in 1/2-inch units: length = 4 units, width = 3 units, height = 2 units. Number of cubes = 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cubes. (Or Volume = 3 in³; cube vol = 1/8 in³; 3 ÷ 1/8 = 24).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied dimensions without accounting for unit volume 1/8.\", \"remediation\": \"Along each dimension, double the inches to count 1/2-inch units: 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cubes.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the volume in cubic inches instead of the cube count.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume is 3 in³, and each cube is 1/8 in³, so 3 ÷ 1/8 = 24 cubes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled 24.\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 3 × 2 = 24 small cubes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2588, 6, 1, 7, 'Surface Area', 293, 6, 'What is the TOTAL surface area of a cube with an edge length of 4 cm?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '24 cm²', '64 cm²', '16 cm²', '96 cm²', 'D', 'active', 'A cube has 6 faces. Area of 1 face = 4 × 4 = 16 cm². Total surface area = 6 × 16 = 96 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 6 × 4 = 24.\", \"remediation\": \"Each face area is 4 × 4 = 16, so 6 × 16 = 96 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated volume (4³ = 64 cm³) instead of surface area.\", \"remediation\": \"Surface area is the sum of all 6 square faces: 6 × 4² = 6 × 16 = 96 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student found the area of only 1 face.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by 6 for all faces of the cube: 6 × 16 = 96 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2589, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Polygons', 291, 6, 'On a coordinate grid, a triangle has vertices at (2, 2), (8, 2), and (8, 7). What is the area of this triangle?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '30 square units', '15 square units', '11 square units', '25 square units', 'B', 'active', 'Base = 8 - 2 = 6 units. Height = 7 - 2 = 5 units. Area = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 6 × 5 = 15 square units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply by 1/2: 6 × 5 = 30.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangle area is 1/2 × base × height: 1/2 × 30 = 15 square units.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added base and height: 6 + 5 = 11.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply base and height, then divide by 2: (6 × 5)/2 = 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student squared 5.\", \"remediation\": \"1/2 × 6 × 5 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2590, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Triangles', 289, 6, 'An obtuse triangle has a base of 12 inches. Its perpendicular height drawn outside the triangle is 5 inches. What is its area?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '30 square inches', '25 square inches', '60 square inches', '17 square inches', 'A', 'active', 'Regardless of whether height falls inside or outside, Area = 1/2 × b × h = 1/2 × 12 × 5 = 30 square inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"1/2 × 12 × 5 = 30.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply by 1/2 (12 × 5 = 60).\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by 2 for triangle area: 60 ÷ 2 = 30 sq inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 12 + 5 = 17.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply base by height, then divide by 2: (12 × 5)/2 = 30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2591, 6, 1, 7, 'Surface Area', 293, 6, 'A net of a square pyramid consists of which shapes?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '2 squares and 4 rectangles', '1 square base and 4 triangles', '4 triangles only', '1 square and 3 triangles', 'B', 'active', 'A square pyramid has 1 square base and 4 triangular side faces that fold up to meet at the apex.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student described a rectangular prism net.\", \"remediation\": \"A pyramid has triangular sides that meet at a point, not rectangles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student described a triangular pyramid (tetrahedron).\", \"remediation\": \"A square pyramid must have a square base + 4 triangles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted 1 triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A square has 4 sides, so there are 4 triangular faces.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2592, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 294, 6, 'What is the volume of a rectangular prism with length 3/4 ft, width 1/2 ft, and height 2/3 ft?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '1/2 cubic foot', '6/9 cubic foot', '6/24 cubic foot', '1/4 cubic foot', 'D', 'active', 'Multiply dimensions: (3/4) × (1/2) × (2/3) = (3 × 1 × 2) / (4 × 2 × 3) = 6/24 = 1/4 cubic foot.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a fraction multiplication error.\", \"remediation\": \"Cancel out 3s and 2s to get 1/4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added numerators and denominators.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply straight across: 3/4 × 1/2 × 2/3 = 6/24 = 1/4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student found 6/24 but did not simplify to 1/4.\", \"remediation\": \"Always simplify: 6/24 reduces to 1/4 cubic foot.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2593, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Polygons', 291, 6, 'On a coordinate plane, a rectangle has vertices at (-3, 2), (4, 2), (4, -3), and (-3, -3). What is the area of the rectangle?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '35 square units', '24 square units', '12 square units', '70 square units', 'A', 'active', 'Horizontal length = 4 - (-3) = 7 units. Vertical width = 2 - (-3) = 5 units. Area = 7 × 5 = 35 square units.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated perimeter: 2(7 + 5) = 24.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is length × width: 7 × 5 = 35 square units.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added length and width: 7 + 5 = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply dimensions for area: 7 × 5 = 35.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area = 7 × 5 = 35.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2594, 6, 1, 7, 'Surface Area', 293, 6, 'What is the surface area of a rectangular prism with length 5 cm, width 2 cm, and height 3 cm?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '60 cm²', '30 cm²', '62 cm²', '31 cm²', 'C', 'active', 'Surface Area = 2(lw + lh + wh) = 2(5×2 + 5×3 + 2×3) = 2(10 + 15 + 6) = 2(31) = 62 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled volume.\", \"remediation\": \"Use SA formula = 2(lw + lh + wh) = 62 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated volume: 5 × 2 × 3 = 30 cm³.\", \"remediation\": \"Surface area is the total area of all 6 faces: 2(10 + 15 + 6) = 62 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the 3 face areas without doubling for opposite faces.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 2 of each face: 2 × 31 = 62 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2595, 6, 1, 7, 'Surface Area', 293, 6, 'A triangular prism has a net with two equilateral triangle bases (base 4 cm, height 3.5 cm) and three identical rectangle side faces (4 cm by 10 cm). What is the TOTAL surface area?', NULL, 6, 'High', '120 cm²', '148 cm²', '127 cm²', '134 cm²', 'D', 'active', 'Area of 2 triangles = 2 × (1/2 × 4 × 3.5) = 14 cm². Area of 3 rectangles = 3 × (4 × 10) = 120 cm². Total Surface Area = 14 + 120 = 134 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated only the rectangular faces (120 cm²), omitting both triangular bases.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the areas of the two triangular ends: 120 + 14 = 134 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated triangles without dividing by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Each triangle area is 1/2 × 4 × 3.5 = 7 cm²; both triangles = 14 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student included only 1 triangular base.\", \"remediation\": \"A prism has two triangular bases: 7 + 7 = 14 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2596, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 294, 6, 'A rectangular fish tank is 2 1/2 feet long, 1 1/4 feet wide, and 1 1/2 feet high. What is the total volume of water it can hold when full?', NULL, 6, 'High', '3 5/8 cubic feet', '4 11/16 cubic feet', '4 3/8 cubic feet', '5 1/4 cubic feet', 'B', 'active', 'Convert to improper fractions: 5/2 × 5/4 × 3/2 = (5 × 5 × 3) / (2 × 4 × 2) = 75/16 = 4 11/16 cubic feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error in 75 ÷ 16.\", \"remediation\": \"16 × 4 = 64; 75 - 64 = 11, giving 4 11/16.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the fraction product.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply all three: 5/2 × 5/4 × 3/2 = 75/16 = 4 11/16 cu ft.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added the fractions.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply length × width × height to calculate volume.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2597, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Polygons', 291, 6, 'On a coordinate grid, a polygon has vertices at A(1, 4), B(5, 4), C(7, 1), and D(1, 1). What is the area of this trapezoid?', NULL, 6, 'High', '18 square units', '30 square units', '15 square units', '12 square units', 'C', 'active', 'Top base b1 = 5 - 1 = 4. Bottom base b2 = 7 - 1 = 6. Height h = 4 - 1 = 3. Area = 1/2 × (b1 + b2) × h = 1/2 × (4 + 6) × 3 = 1/2 × 10 × 3 = 15 square units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student used 6 × 3 = 18 without averaging bases.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the trapezoid formula: A = ((b1 + b2)/2) × h = (10/2) × 3 = 15.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to divide by 2: 10 × 3 = 30.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the average of bases by height: 5 × 3 = 15 square units.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student used 4 × 3 = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Both bases must be included: 1/2 × (4 + 6) × 3 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2598, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Polygons', 291, 6, 'A wall is 12 feet long and 9 feet high. It has a rectangular window measuring 4 feet by 3 feet. What is the area of the wall that needs to be painted (excluding the window)?', NULL, 6, 'High', '96 square feet', '92 square feet', '108 square feet', '12 square feet', 'A', 'active', 'Total wall area = 12 × 9 = 108 sq ft. Window area = 4 × 3 = 12 sq ft. Paint area = 108 - 12 = 96 sq ft.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic subtraction error.\", \"remediation\": \"108 - 12 = 96 sq ft.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated total wall area without subtracting the window.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the window cutout: 108 - 12 = 96 sq ft.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student gave only the window area.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract window from wall: 108 - 12 = 96 sq ft.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2599, 6, 1, 7, 'Surface Area', 293, 6, 'A cardboard box with NO LID (open top) has length 10 inches, width 6 inches, and height 4 inches. What is the surface area of the cardboard used?', NULL, 6, 'High', '248 square inches', '240 square inches', '188 square inches', '128 square inches', 'C', 'active', 'Bottom = 10 × 6 = 60. Front & Back = 2 × (10 × 4) = 80. Left & Right = 2 × (6 × 4) = 48. Open top has no lid. Total = 60 + 80 + 48 = 188 sq inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student included the top lid (calculated full closed box SA = 248).\", \"remediation\": \"The box has no lid, so subtract the top face (60): 248 - 60 = 188 sq inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated volume: 10 × 6 × 4 = 240 cu inches.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate the area of the 5 open-box faces: 60 + 80 + 48 = 188 sq in.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted both front and back faces.\", \"remediation\": \"Add all 5 faces: bottom (60) + two sides (48) + front & back (80) = 188.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2600, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 294, 6, 'A rectangular container with volume 84 cubic feet has a length of 7 feet and a width of 4 feet. What is the HEIGHT of the container?', NULL, 6, 'High', '6 feet', '4 feet', '28 feet', '3 feet', 'D', 'active', 'Base Area = 7 × 4 = 28 sq ft. Height = Volume ÷ Base Area = 84 ÷ 28 = 3 feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 84 by 14.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 84 by 28: 84 ÷ 28 = 3 feet.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 4 feet.\", \"remediation\": \"Check: 7 × 4 × 4 = 112, which is too large. 84 ÷ 28 = 3 feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the base area (28) instead of height.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide volume by base area: 84 ÷ 28 = 3 feet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2601, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Polygons', 291, 6, 'A regular hexagon can be decomposed into 6 identical equilateral triangles. If each triangle has an area of 14 cm², what is the total area of the hexagon?', NULL, 6, 'High', '70 cm²', '84 cm²', '42 cm²', '96 cm²', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply the area of one triangle by 6: 6 × 14 = 84 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"A hexagon has 6 triangles: 6 × 14 = 84 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"All 6 triangles make up the full hexagon: 6 × 14 = 84 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made a multiplication error.\", \"remediation\": \"6 × 14 = 84 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2602, 6, 1, 7, 'Area of Triangles', 289, 6, 'If you double BOTH the base and the height of a triangle, how does its area change?', NULL, 6, 'High', 'The area becomes 4 times larger', 'The area stays the same', 'The area doubles (2 times larger)', 'The area becomes 8 times larger', 'A', 'active', 'New Area = 1/2 × (2b) × (2h) = 4 × (1/2 bh) = 4 times the original area.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought area is unchanged.\", \"remediation\": \"Scaling base and height directly scales the area by factor 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought doubling linear dimensions only doubles area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is 2-dimensional: doubling both dimensions multiplies area by 2 × 2 = 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student cubed the factor (confused with 3D volume).\", \"remediation\": \"For 2D area, scale factor squared is 2² = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:09:21', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2603, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'On a map, the scale is 1 inch = 5 miles. If two cities are 3 inches apart on the map, what is the actual distance between them?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '8 miles', '15 miles', '12 miles', '5 miles', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply the map distance by the scale ratio: 3 inches × 5 miles/inch = 15 miles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the map measurement by the number of miles per inch.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a multiplication error.\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 5 = 15 miles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student just took the unit scale distance.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 1 inch = 5 miles by 3 to find 15 miles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:12', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2604, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'A blueprint uses a scale of 1 cm = 4 meters. If a room has a length of 6 cm on the blueprint, what is the actual length of the room?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '1.5 meters', '10 meters', '20 meters', '24 meters', 'D', 'active', 'Actual length = 6 cm × 4 meters/cm = 24 meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 6 by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"To go from drawing units to actual units, multiply by 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 6 + 4 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the blueprint measure by the scale value (6 × 4 = 24).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"6 × 4 = 24 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:12', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2605, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'A model car is built using a scale factor of 1/20 of the real car. If the real car is 180 inches long, how long is the model car?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '9 inches', '160 inches', '3,600 inches', '18 inches', 'A', 'active', 'Length of model = 180 × (1/20) = 180 / 20 = 9 inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 20 from 180.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the actual length by the fractional scale factor 1/20.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 180 by 20.\", \"remediation\": \"The model is smaller than the real car, so divide by 20.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 10 instead of 20.\", \"remediation\": \"180 divided by 20 equals 9 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:12', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2606, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'What does a scale factor greater than 1 represent in a scale drawing or model?', NULL, 7, 'Low', 'A rotation', 'A reduction', 'An enlargement', 'No change in size', 'C', 'active', 'A scale factor greater than 1 makes the copy larger than the original, which is an enlargement.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose a transformation of orientation.\", \"remediation\": \"Scale factor controls size change, not rotation.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused enlargement with reduction.\", \"remediation\": \"A scale factor between 0 and 1 creates a reduction; greater than 1 creates an enlargement.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought scale factor 1 meant greater than 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Scale factor 1 preserves size; greater than 1 enlarges.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:12', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2607, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'Can a triangle have sides of lengths 3 cm, 4 cm, and 10 cm?', NULL, 7, 'Low', 'Yes, because all numbers are positive', 'Yes, because 3 + 10 is greater than 4', 'No, because all sides must be equal', 'No, because 3 + 4 is less than 10', 'D', 'active', 'According to the Triangle Inequality Theorem, the sum of any two side lengths must be strictly greater than the third side. Here, 3 + 4 = 7, which is not greater than 10.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student assumed any positive lengths can form a triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the Triangle Inequality: the sum of the two shorter sides must exceed the longest side.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student checked only one pair including the longest side.\", \"remediation\": \"You must check if the sum of the two shorter sides is greater than the longest side.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought triangles require equal sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Scalene triangles have unequal sides, but they still must obey the Triangle Inequality Theorem.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:12', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2608, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'What is the sum of the interior angle measures of any triangle?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '270°', '180°', '360°', '90°', 'B', 'active', 'The interior angles of every Euclidean triangle always sum up to exactly 180°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student chose 270°.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the angle sum property of triangles: sum = 180°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the angle sum for a quadrilateral.\", \"remediation\": \"A quadrilateral has 360°, but a triangle has 180°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the measure of a right angle.\", \"remediation\": \"The sum of all three angles in a triangle is 180°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2609, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'A triangle has angle measures of 50° and 60°. What is the measure of the third angle?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '110°', '80°', '70°', '60°', 'C', 'active', 'The sum of angles in a triangle is 180°. Third angle = 180° - (50° + 60°) = 180° - 110° = 70°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the sum of the two given angles without subtracting from 180°.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 110° from 180° to find the missing third angle.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"180 - 110 = 70°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 60° assuming an isosceles triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Use 180° - 50° - 60° = 70°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2610, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'Which 2D shape is formed by slicing a rectangular prism horizontally parallel to its base?', NULL, 7, 'Low', 'A rectangle', 'A trapezoid', 'A triangle', 'A circle', 'A', 'active', 'A cross-section parallel to the base of a prism always has the exact same shape as the base, which is a rectangle.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose a trapezoid.\", \"remediation\": \"A parallel horizontal slice directly mirrors the rectangular base.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed a triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"A slice parallel to the base of a prism matches the base shape (rectangle).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought of a cylinder slice.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangular prism has flat rectangular faces and cross-sections.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2611, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'A map has a scale of 1 inch = 8 miles. If the actual distance between two towns is 56 miles, how many inches apart are they on the map?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '6 inches', '8 inches', '7 inches', '448 inches', 'C', 'active', 'Divide the actual distance by the scale rate: 56 miles ÷ 8 miles/inch = 7 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made a division error.\", \"remediation\": \"56 divided by 8 is 7.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student repeated the scale number 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate 56 / 8 = 7 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 56 by 8 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"To convert from actual miles to map inches, divide by 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2612, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'A drawing of a garden has dimensions 4 inches by 6 inches. The scale is 1 inch = 3 feet. What is the ACTUAL perimeter of the garden?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '216 feet', '20 feet', '72 feet', '60 feet', 'D', 'active', 'Actual dimensions are 4 × 3 = 12 feet and 6 × 3 = 18 feet. Perimeter = 2(12 + 18) = 2(30) = 60 feet. (Or drawing perimeter = 20 inches × 3 = 60 feet).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated actual area (12 × 18 = 216).\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is 2 × length + 2 × width = 60 feet.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the drawing perimeter in inches without applying the scale.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the drawing perimeter of 20 inches by 3 feet/inch to get 60 feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the area in square feet instead of perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is the distance around: 2(12 + 18) = 60 feet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2613, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'A rectangle has an area of 12 square centimeters. If a scale factor of 3 is applied to enlarge both its length and width, what is the area of the new rectangle?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '36 square centimeters', '108 square centimeters', '72 square centimeters', '24 square centimeters', 'B', 'active', 'When linear dimensions are multiplied by a scale factor k, the area is multiplied by k². Here, area = 12 × 3² = 12 × 9 = 108 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the area by k (3) instead of k² (9).\", \"remediation\": \"Area scales by the square of the scale factor: 12 × 3² = 108.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the multiplied value.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 12 by 3² = 9 to get 108.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 2 instead of scaling by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"New area = original area × (scale factor)².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2614, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'On a blueprint, a wall that is 5 inches long represents an actual wall of 15 feet. What is the scale of the blueprint in inches to feet in simplest form?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '1 inch = 3 feet', '1 inch = 15 feet', '1 inch = 5 feet', '3 inches = 1 foot', 'A', 'active', 'Divide both quantities by 5: 5 inches / 5 = 1 inch, 15 feet / 5 = 3 feet. The scale is 1 inch = 3 feet.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student did not reduce the ratio.\", \"remediation\": \"Simplify 5 inches : 15 feet to 1 inch : 3 feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student took the drawing measurement as the scale.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 15 feet by 5 inches to get 3 feet per inch.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the relationship.\", \"remediation\": \"1 inch represents 3 feet, not 3 inches to 1 foot.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2615, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'A photo that is 4 inches wide and 6 inches long is enlarged so that its width is 10 inches. What is the length of the enlarged photo?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '12 inches', '15 inches', '14 inches', '16 inches', 'B', 'active', 'The scale factor is 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5. Multiply the length by the scale factor: 6 × 2.5 = 15 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 6 to width and length.\", \"remediation\": \"Scaling requires multiplying by a ratio, not adding a constant: (10/4) × 6 = 15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an estimation error.\", \"remediation\": \"6 × (10 / 4) = 6 × 2.5 = 15 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4 by 4 and subtracted.\", \"remediation\": \"Set up a proportion: 4/6 = 10/x, so 4x = 60 and x = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2616, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'Which set of three side lengths CAN form a valid triangle?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '3 cm, 7 cm, 10 cm', '2 cm, 5 cm, 8 cm', '4 cm, 4 cm, 9 cm', '6 cm, 8 cm, 11 cm', 'D', 'active', 'In option A, 6 + 8 = 14 > 11, so it satisfies the Triangle Inequality Theorem. In B: 2 + 5 = 7 < 8. In C: 4 + 4 = 8 < 9. In D: 3 + 7 = 10 (not greater).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"3 + 7 = 10 is equal to, not greater than, 10.\", \"remediation\": \"The sum must be strictly greater than the third side (a degenerate line results if equal).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"2 + 5 = 7 is less than 8.\", \"remediation\": \"The sum of the two shorter sides must be strictly greater than the third side.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"4 + 4 = 8 is less than 9.\", \"remediation\": \"The two shorter sides must add to more than 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2617, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'How many unique triangles can be constructed with angle measures 40°, 60°, and 80°?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', 'Infinitely many triangles', 'Exactly one unique triangle', 'No triangles', 'Exactly two triangles', 'A', 'active', 'Since the angles sum to 180°, valid triangles can be formed. However, specifying only three angles determines shape (similarity) but not size, so infinitely many triangles of different sizes can be created.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought three angles fix a unique triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Three angles determine shape (AAA similarity), but you can scale it to infinitely many sizes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought the angles do not add to 180°.\", \"remediation\": \"40 + 60 + 80 = 180°, so triangles do exist.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought there are two triangles.\", \"remediation\": \"Without any fixed side length, infinitely many scaled triangles exist.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2618, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'If a right circular cylinder is sliced vertically perpendicular to its circular bases, what 2D cross-section is produced?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', 'A triangle', 'A circle', 'A rectangle', 'An ellipse', 'C', 'active', 'A vertical cut perpendicular to the bases of a cylinder cuts straight down through the curved surface, forming a flat rectangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought of slicing a cone.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder has straight parallel sides, producing a rectangle.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose a circle, which is the horizontal cross-section.\", \"remediation\": \"Horizontal cuts parallel to bases give circles; vertical cuts perpendicular to bases give rectangles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose an ellipse, which is an angled slice.\", \"remediation\": \"A perpendicular vertical slice through a cylinder yields a rectangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2619, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'Two sides of a triangle measure 7 inches and 10 inches. What is the range of possible lengths for the third side, x?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '3 <= x <= 17', '7 < x < 10', '0 < x < 17', '3 < x < 17', 'D', 'active', 'The third side must be greater than the difference (10 - 7 = 3) and less than the sum (10 + 7 = 17). Thus, 3 < x < 17.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student included endpoints with <=.\", \"remediation\": \"The inequalities must be strict (> and <), because if x = 3 or 17, the triangle collapses into a line segment.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought the third side must fall between the two given sides.\", \"remediation\": \"The third side can be longer than 10 or shorter than 7, as long as 3 < x < 17.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student used 0 as the lower bound.\", \"remediation\": \"The lower bound is the difference between the two known sides: 10 - 7 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2620, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'A rectangular patio is drawn at a scale of 1/4 inch = 2 feet. If the drawing is 2 inches wide by 3.5 inches long, what is the ACTUAL area of the patio in square feet?', NULL, 7, 'High', '896 square feet', '448 square feet', '112 square feet', '224 square feet', 'B', 'active', 'Since 1/4 inch = 2 feet, 1 inch = 8 feet. Actual width = 2 × 8 = 16 feet. Actual length = 3.5 × 8 = 28 feet. Actual area = 16 × 28 = 448 square feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the correct area.\", \"remediation\": \"16 × 28 = 448 square feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 4 instead of 8.\", \"remediation\": \"1/4 inch = 2 feet means 1 full inch = 8 feet. Width = 16 ft, length = 28 ft, area = 448 sq ft.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to scale both dimensions.\", \"remediation\": \"Area scale factor is 8² = 64. Drawing area = 7 sq in × 64 = 448 sq ft.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2621, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'The area of a park on a map is 6 square inches. The map scale is 1 inch = 4 miles. What is the ACTUAL area of the park in square miles?', NULL, 7, 'High', '24 square miles', '48 square miles', '96 square miles', '384 square miles', 'C', 'active', 'Since 1 inch = 4 miles, 1 square inch = 4² = 16 square miles. Actual area = 6 × 16 = 96 square miles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 6 by 4 instead of 4².\", \"remediation\": \"When converting area, square the linear scale factor: 4² = 16, so 6 × 16 = 96 sq miles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Square the scale factor: 4 × 4 = 16, then 6 × 16 = 96.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 64.\", \"remediation\": \"The area conversion factor is 4² = 16, giving 96 square miles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2622, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'A scale model of a building is 18 inches tall. The actual building is 90 feet tall. If a door on the actual building is 8 feet tall, what is the height of the door on the model?', NULL, 7, 'High', '1.6 inches', '0.8 inches', '1.2 inches', '2.0 inches', 'A', 'active', 'Scale ratio = 18 inches / 90 feet = 0.2 inches per foot. Door height on model = 8 feet × 0.2 inches/foot = 1.6 inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 8 by 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the scale factor 18/90 = 1/5: 8 / 5 = 1.6 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"8 × (18 / 90) = 8 × 0.2 = 1.6 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded up to 2.0 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate exact proportion: 18/90 = x/8 -> x = 144/90 = 1.6 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2623, 6, 1, 7, 'Scale Drawings', 295, 7, 'An architect redraws a floor plan changing the scale from 1 inch = 10 feet to 1 inch = 5 feet. How does the area of the floor plan drawing change?', NULL, 7, 'High', 'The drawing area becomes 2 times larger', 'The drawing area becomes half as large', 'The drawing area becomes 4 times larger', 'The drawing area does not change', 'C', 'active', 'At 1 inch = 5 feet, each linear inch represents half as many feet, so each linear dimension on the paper doubles (2×). Therefore, the area on the paper increases by 2² = 4 times.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student considered linear dimensions instead of area.\", \"remediation\": \"Linear lengths double (2×), so area multiplies by 2² = 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought the drawing gets smaller.\", \"remediation\": \"A smaller scale number means more drawing inches per foot, making the drawing larger.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought changing the scale does not affect drawing size.\", \"remediation\": \"Changing the scale directly alters the size of the drawing.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2624, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'A triangle has two sides of length 8 cm and 13 cm, with an included angle of 65° between them. How many unique triangles can be constructed with these given conditions?', NULL, 7, 'High', 'Exactly two different triangles', 'More than one triangle', 'No triangle', 'Exactly one unique triangle', 'D', 'active', 'By the Side-Angle-Side (SAS) congruence criterion, specifying two side lengths and the included angle uniquely determines exactly one triangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused SAS with the ambiguous SSA case.\", \"remediation\": \"Here the angle is INCLUDED between the two sides (SAS), so only one triangle is possible.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought SAS allows multiple triangles.\", \"remediation\": \"The SAS condition fixes all three vertices, creating exactly one unique triangle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought a triangle cannot be formed.\", \"remediation\": \"Any two positive side lengths with an included angle between 0° and 180° always form a valid triangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2625, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'A right square pyramid is sliced by a plane that passes through its top apex and cuts through the base perpendicular to the base. What shape is the cross-section?', NULL, 7, 'High', 'A square', 'An isosceles triangle', 'A rectangle', 'A trapezoid', 'B', 'active', 'The plane passes through the apex (a single point at top) and a line across the base, with two equal slanted edges, producing an isosceles triangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought of a horizontal cut parallel to the base.\", \"remediation\": \"A horizontal cut yields a square, but a vertical cut through the apex yields a triangle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed a rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"The cross-section reaches a point at the apex, making it a triangle.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought of a cut that does not pass through the apex.\", \"remediation\": \"Since the plane passes directly through the top apex, the cross-section is a triangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2626, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'Which combination of conditions determines NO possible triangle?', NULL, 7, 'High', 'Side lengths of 5 cm, 9 cm, and 15 cm', 'Sides of 6 cm and 8 cm with an included angle of 90°', 'Side lengths of 7 cm, 7 cm, and 7 cm', 'Angles of 35°, 55°, and 90°', 'A', 'active', 'In option A, 5 + 9 = 14 < 15, which violates the Triangle Inequality Theorem, making it impossible to form a triangle.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"This forms a unique right triangle by SAS.\", \"remediation\": \"6, 8 with 90° included forms a classic 6-8-10 right triangle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This forms an equilateral triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"7, 7, 7 satisfies 7 + 7 > 7 and forms a valid triangle.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This forms valid right triangles (35 + 55 + 90 = 180°).\", \"remediation\": \"The angle sum is 180°, so infinitely many triangles can be formed.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2627, 6, 1, 7, 'Triangle Construction', 296, 7, 'A cone is sliced by a flat plane that cuts completely through both sides without intersecting the base, at an angle not parallel to the base. What 2D shape is the cross-section?', NULL, 7, 'High', 'A circle', 'An ellipse', 'A parabola', 'A triangle', 'B', 'active', 'A cross-section of a cone cut at an angle to the base that passes through opposite sides of the cone without hitting the base forms an ellipse.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A circle is formed only when the plane is parallel to the circular base.\", \"remediation\": \"An angled cut through the cone produces an elongated curve called an ellipse.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"A parabola is formed when the plane is parallel to the slant height and cuts the base.\", \"remediation\": \"A cut through both sides that does not touch the base forms a closed ellipse.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A triangle is formed when the cut passes through the vertex.\", \"remediation\": \"A slice through both sides of a cone without touching the vertex or base produces an ellipse.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2628, 6, 1, 7, 'Transformations', 298, 8, 'Point A is located at (3, 5). If it is translated 4 units right and 2 units down, what are the coordinates of the image A\'?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '(-1, 3)', '(-1, 7)', '(7, 7)', '(7, 3)', 'D', 'active', 'Translating 4 units right adds 4 to x: 3 + 4 = 7. Translating 2 units down subtracts 2 from y: 5 - 2 = 3. The image is (7, 3).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 4 from x.\", \"remediation\": \"Moving right increases the x-coordinate: 3 + 4 = 7.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 4 from x and added 2 to y.\", \"remediation\": \"Moving right means adding to x; moving down means subtracting from y.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 2 to y instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"Moving down decreases the y-coordinate: 5 - 2 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2629, 6, 1, 7, 'Transformations', 298, 8, 'What is the coordinate rule when a point (x, y) is reflected over the x-axis?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '(x, -y)', '(-x, y)', '(-x, -y)', '(y, x)', 'A', 'active', 'Reflecting over the x-axis keeps the x-coordinate unchanged and negates the y-coordinate: (x, y) → (x, -y).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the rule for reflection over the y-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"Reflecting over the x-axis changes the sign of y, keeping x the same: (x, -y).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the rule for a 180° rotation about the origin.\", \"remediation\": \"Reflecting across the x-axis only changes the sign of the y-coordinate.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the rule for reflection over the line y = x.\", \"remediation\": \"Over the x-axis, (x, y) maps to (x, -y).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2630, 6, 1, 7, 'Similarity & Dilations', 299, 8, 'A dilation centered at the origin has a scale factor of k = 3. What are the coordinates of the image of point P(2, -4)?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '(-6, 12)', '(5, -1)', '(6, -12)', '(2/3, -4/3)', 'C', 'active', 'Multiply both coordinates by the scale factor k = 3: (2 × 3, -4 × 3) = (6, -12).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by -3.\", \"remediation\": \"The scale factor is positive 3, so signs remain unchanged: (6, -12).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 to each coordinate.\", \"remediation\": \"Dilation is multiplication by scale factor k: (kx, ky), not addition.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 3 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Scale factor k = 3 means multiplying by 3: 2 × 3 = 6, -4 × 3 = -12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2631, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles & Parallel Lines', 300, 8, 'Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. If one alternate interior angle measures 65°, what is the measure of the other alternate interior angle?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '115°', '25°', '180°', '65°', 'D', 'active', 'When two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, alternate interior angles are congruent (equal in measure). Therefore, the other angle is also 65°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the supplementary angle (180° - 65° = 115°).\", \"remediation\": \"Alternate interior angles are congruent (equal), so both are 65°.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted from 90°.\", \"remediation\": \"Alternate interior angles are equal: both are 65°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the total line angle sum.\", \"remediation\": \"The angle measure is identical: 65°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2632, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles & Parallel Lines', 300, 8, 'In triangle ABC, angle A measures 50° and angle B measures 75°. What is the measure of the exterior angle adjacent to angle C?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '105°', '125°', '55°', '130°', 'B', 'active', 'By the Exterior Angle Theorem, an exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of its two remote interior angles: 50° + 75° = 125°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 75 from 180.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the two remote interior angles: 50° + 75° = 125°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student found the interior angle C (180 - 125 = 55°).\", \"remediation\": \"The exterior angle is supplementary to interior angle C, or simply 50° + 75° = 125°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an addition error.\", \"remediation\": \"50 + 75 = 125°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2633, 6, 1, 7, 'Pythagorean Theorem', 301, 8, 'In a right triangle, the two legs have lengths a = 3 and b = 4. What is the length of the hypotenuse c?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '7', '25', '5', '12', 'C', 'active', 'By the Pythagorean Theorem: c² = a² + b² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25. Taking the square root, c = √25 = 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Do not add side lengths directly; use a² + b² = c², so c = √(3² + 4²) = 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to take the square root of 25.\", \"remediation\": \"c² = 25, so c = √25 = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 3 × 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the Pythagorean Theorem formula: c = √(a² + b²).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2634, 6, 1, 7, 'Pythagorean Theorem', 301, 8, 'Which equation correctly states the Pythagorean Theorem for a right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'a² + b² = c²', 'a² + b² = 2c', 'a + b = c', 'a² - b² = c²', 'A', 'active', 'The Pythagorean Theorem states that in any right triangle, the sum of the squares of the legs equals the square of the hypotenuse: a² + b² = c².', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied c by 2 instead of squaring it.\", \"remediation\": \"The hypotenuse is squared (c²), not multiplied by 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted the exponents.\", \"remediation\": \"The theorem involves the squares of the sides: a² + b² = c².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted instead of adding.\", \"remediation\": \"The squares of the two legs are added: a² + b² = c².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2635, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Cylinders, Cones & Spheres', 302, 8, 'What is the formula for the volume of a cylinder with radius r and height h?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'V = (1/3)πr²h', 'V = (4/3)πr³', 'V = πr²h', 'V = 2πrh', 'C', 'active', 'The volume of a cylinder is base area times height: V = πr²h.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the formula for the volume of a cone.\", \"remediation\": \"A cone is (1/3)πr²h; a cylinder is simply πr²h.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the formula for the volume of a sphere.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder volume formula is base area × height = πr²h.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student gave the formula for the lateral surface area of a cylinder.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume requires squaring the radius: V = πr²h.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2636, 6, 1, 7, 'Transformations', 298, 8, 'What are the coordinates of point Q(-3, 4) after a 90° counterclockwise rotation about the origin?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '(-3, -4)', '(4, 3)', '(3, -4)', '(-4, -3)', 'D', 'active', 'The rule for a 90° counterclockwise rotation about the origin is (x, y) → (-y, x). Applying this to (-3, 4) gives (-4, -3).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student just negated the y-coordinate.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to switch the coordinates and negate the new first term: (-y, x).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student negated both and flipped incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Use rule (x, y) → (-y, x): the new x is -4 and new y is -3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student used the rule for 270° counterclockwise rotation (y, -x).\", \"remediation\": \"A 90° counterclockwise rotation maps (x, y) to (-y, x).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2637, 6, 1, 7, 'Transformations', 298, 8, 'Triangle XYZ is translated 5 units left and then reflected over the y-axis to create triangle X\'Y\'Z\'. Which statement is TRUE about the two triangles?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'They are similar but not congruent', 'They are congruent because rigid transformations preserve side lengths and angle measures', 'They have different angle measures', 'The area of triangle X\'Y\'Z\' is larger than triangle XYZ', 'B', 'active', 'Translations and reflections are rigid motions (isometries). They preserve side lengths, angle measures, and area, meaning the preimage and image are congruent.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student thought reflections change size.\", \"remediation\": \"Rigid motions (translations, reflections, rotations) preserve exact size, so shapes are congruent.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought transformations distort angles.\", \"remediation\": \"Rigid motions preserve all angle measures.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought the area changes.\", \"remediation\": \"Area remains identical after translations and reflections.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2638, 6, 1, 7, 'Similarity & Dilations', 299, 8, 'A triangle with vertices at (0, 0), (2, 4), and (6, 2) is dilated by a scale factor of 1/2 centered at the origin. What are the coordinates of the new vertices?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '(0, 0), (1, 2), and (3, 1)', '(0, 0), (-2, -4), and (-6, -2)', '(0, 0), (4, 8), and (12, 4)', '(0, 0), (1.5, 3.5), and (5.5, 1.5)', 'A', 'active', 'Multiply each coordinate by 1/2: (0×0.5, 0×0.5) = (0, 0); (2×0.5, 4×0.5) = (1, 2); (6×0.5, 2×0.5) = (3, 1).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student negated the coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"A positive scale factor does not change signs.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 2 instead of 1/2.\", \"remediation\": \"A scale factor of 1/2 cuts each coordinate in half.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 0.5 from coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"Dilation multiplies coordinates by the scale factor: multiply each by 1/2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2639, 6, 1, 7, 'Similarity & Dilations', 299, 8, 'If rectangle ABCD is dilated by a scale factor of 4 to form rectangle A\'B\'C\'D\', what happens to its area?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'The area is multiplied by 4', 'The area is multiplied by 16', 'The area is multiplied by 8', 'The area increases by 4 square units', 'B', 'active', 'When linear dimensions are scaled by factor k, area scales by k². With k = 4, the area is multiplied by 4² = 16.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student scaled the area by k instead of k².\", \"remediation\": \"Both length and width are multiplied by 4, so area multiplies by 4 × 4 = 16.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Square the scale factor: 4² = 16.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4 instead of multiplying by k².\", \"remediation\": \"Dilation affects area multiplicatively by k² = 16.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2640, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles & Parallel Lines', 300, 8, 'Two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal. If two consecutive interior (same-side interior) angles are represented by (3x + 10)° and (2x + 20)°, what is the value of x?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '15', '10', '36', '30', 'D', 'active', 'Same-side interior angles are supplementary (sum to 180°): (3x + 10) + (2x + 20) = 180 → 5x + 30 = 180 → 5x = 150 → x = 30.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 150 by 10.\", \"remediation\": \"5x = 150, so x = 150 / 5 = 30.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student set the two angles equal to each other (3x + 10 = 2x + 20 → x = 10).\", \"remediation\": \"Same-side interior angles are supplementary (add to 180°), not congruent.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an algebraic error dividing 180 by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 30 first: 180 - 30 = 150, then 150 / 5 = 30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2641, 6, 1, 7, 'Pythagorean Theorem', 301, 8, 'A 13-foot ladder is leaning against a vertical wall. The base of the ladder is 5 feet away from the base of the wall. How high up the wall does the ladder reach?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '12 feet', '8 feet', '14 feet', '18 feet', 'A', 'active', 'The ladder is the hypotenuse (c = 13) and distance from wall is a leg (a = 5). b² = c² - a² = 13² - 5² = 169 - 25 = 144. b = √144 = 12 feet.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 13 - 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the Pythagorean theorem: b = √(13² - 5²) = √(169 - 25) = √144 = 12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 13² + 5².\", \"remediation\": \"The ladder is the hypotenuse, so subtract the leg squared from the hypotenuse squared.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 13 + 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the formula a² + b² = c² to solve for the vertical leg: b = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2642, 6, 1, 7, 'Pythagorean Theorem', 301, 8, 'What is the distance between points (1, 2) and (4, 6) on a coordinate plane?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '4', '7', '5', '25', 'C', 'active', 'Use the distance formula: d = √[(4 - 1)² + (6 - 2)²] = √[3² + 4²] = √[9 + 16] = √25 = 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student picked the largest difference.\", \"remediation\": \"Apply the distance formula d = √[(Δx)² + (Δy)²] = 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added the differences 3 + 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Square the differences first: 3² + 4² = 25, then take the square root (d = 5).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to take the square root of 25.\", \"remediation\": \"d² = 25, so d = √25 = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2643, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Cylinders, Cones & Spheres', 302, 8, 'A cone has a radius of 3 cm and a height of 10 cm. Using π ≈ 3.14, what is the volume of the cone to the nearest whole number?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '283 cm³,', '314 cm³', '188 cm³', '94 cm³', 'D', 'active', 'V = (1/3)πr²h = (1/3) × 3.14 × 3² × 10 = (1/3) × 3.14 × 9 × 10 = 3 × 3.14 × 10 = 94.2 ≈ 94 cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to divide by 3 (calculated cylinder volume: 282.6 ≈ 283).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the volume of a cone is 1/3 of a cylinder with the same base and height.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated π × 10².\", \"remediation\": \"The formula is (1/3)πr²h with r = 3 and h = 10, giving approximately 94 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 2/3 instead of 1/3.\", \"remediation\": \"Use V = (1/3)πr²h: 94.2 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2644, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Cylinders, Cones & Spheres', 302, 8, 'A cylinder and a cone have the exact same radius and height. If the cylinder has a volume of 72 cubic inches, what is the volume of the cone?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '216 cubic inches', '24 cubic inches', '36 cubic inches', '144 cubic inches', 'B', 'active', 'The volume of a cone is exactly one-third (1/3) the volume of a cylinder with identical radius and height: 72 ÷ 3 = 24 cubic inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 72 by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"The cone is 1/3 of the cylinder: 72 × (1/3) = 24 cubic inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student divided by 2 instead of 3.\", \"remediation\": \"A cone has 1/3 the volume of a cylinder with the same dimensions: 72 / 3 = 24.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled the cylinder volume.\", \"remediation\": \"A cone is smaller than the cylinder: divide by 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2645, 6, 1, 7, 'Transformations', 298, 8, 'Point M(2, -3) is reflected over the line y = x and then translated along the vector <-1, 4>. What are the coordinates of the final image?', NULL, 8, 'High', '(-3, 2)', '(1, 1)', '(-4, 6)', '(6, -4)', 'C', 'active', 'Reflecting (x, y) over line y = x swaps coordinates: (2, -3) becomes (-3, 2). Then translate by <-1, 4>: (-3 - 1, 2 + 4) = (-4, 6).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student stopped after the reflection without applying the translation.\", \"remediation\": \"Apply the translation <-1, 4> to (-3, 2): -3 - 1 = -4, 2 + 4 = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student applied the translation to the original point without reflecting.\", \"remediation\": \"First reflect over y = x to get (-3, 2), then add <-1, 4>.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the coordinates of the final result.\", \"remediation\": \"Final image is (-4, 6).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2646, 6, 1, 7, 'Similarity & Dilations', 299, 8, 'Triangle ABC has side lengths 5, 12, and 13. A sequence of a reflection, a rotation, and a dilation produces triangle DEF with side lengths 15, 36, and 39. Which statement is fully accurate?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Triangles ABC and DEF are similar with a scale factor of 3', 'Triangles ABC and DEF are neither congruent nor similar', 'Triangles ABC and DEF are congruent', 'Triangle DEF has angle measures 3 times larger than triangle ABC', 'A', 'active', 'Each side length is scaled by exactly 3 (15/5 = 36/12 = 39/13 = 3). Because dilations preserve angle measures and create proportional sides, the triangles are similar with scale factor 3.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student failed to see the proportional relationship.\", \"remediation\": \"All corresponding sides have the ratio 3 : 1, confirming similarity.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused similarity with congruence.\", \"remediation\": \"Since side lengths changed by a factor of 3, the triangles are similar, not congruent.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought dilation multiplies angle measures.\", \"remediation\": \"Dilation preserves angle measures; only side lengths are multiplied by 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2647, 6, 1, 7, 'Similarity & Dilations', 299, 8, 'Point A(4, 8) is dilated with a scale factor of 3 using center of dilation C(1, 2). What are the coordinates of the image A\'?', NULL, 8, 'High', '(12, 24)', '(13, 26)', '(10, 20)', '(9, 18)', 'C', 'active', 'Vector CA = (4 - 1, 8 - 2) = (3, 6). Scale by 3: 3 × (3, 6) = (9, 18). Add back to center C: (1 + 9, 2 + 18) = (10, 20).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student dilated from origin (0, 0) instead of center C(1, 2).\", \"remediation\": \"When center is (a, b), the rule is A\' = (a + k(x - a), b + k(y - b)): 1 + 3(3) = 10, 2 + 3(6) = 20.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 to each coordinate.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the distance from center C, multiply that distance by 3, and add to C.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student found the displacement vector without adding the center.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the scaled vector (9, 18) back to the center C(1, 2) to get (10, 20).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2648, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles & Parallel Lines', 300, 8, 'In triangle PQR, the interior angles are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 5. What is the measure of the smallest exterior angle of triangle PQR?', NULL, 8, 'High', '126°', '36°', '144°', '90°', 'D', 'active', 'Sum of ratio parts = 2 + 3 + 5 = 10. Each part = 180° ÷ 10 = 18°. Interior angles are 36°, 54°, and 90°. The smallest exterior angle corresponds to the largest interior angle: 180° - 90° = 90°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 180° - 54° = 126°.\", \"remediation\": \"The smallest exterior angle is 180° - 90° = 90°.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student identified the smallest interior angle instead of the smallest exterior angle.\", \"remediation\": \"The smallest exterior angle is supplementary to the largest interior angle (90°): 180° - 90° = 90°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student found the largest exterior angle (180° - 36° = 144°).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the smallest exterior angle, which is 180° - 90° = 90°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2649, 6, 1, 7, 'Angles & Parallel Lines', 300, 8, 'Two parallel lines l and m are cut by a transversal t. Two alternate exterior angles are given by (5x - 20)° and (3x + 16)°. What is the measure of these angles?', NULL, 8, 'High', '18°', '70°', '110°', '38°', 'B', 'active', 'Alternate exterior angles are equal: 5x - 20 = 3x + 16 → 2x = 36 → x = 18. Angle measure = 5(18) - 20 = 90 - 20 = 70°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student found the value of x (18) instead of the angle measure.\", \"remediation\": \"Substitute x = 18 back into the angle expression: 5(18) - 20 = 70°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the supplementary angle (180° - 70° = 110°).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the measure of the alternate exterior angles, which is 70°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 16 from 54 incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"5(18) - 20 = 70°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2650, 6, 1, 7, 'Pythagorean Theorem', 301, 8, 'A rectangular box has dimensions 3 inches by 4 inches by 12 inches. What is the length of the 3D diagonal connecting two opposite corners of the box?', NULL, 8, 'High', '13 inches', '169 inches', '15 inches', '19 inches', 'A', 'active', 'Use the 3D Pythagorean theorem: d = √(l² + w² + h²) = √(3² + 4² + 12²) = √(9 + 16 + 144) = √169 = 13 inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to take the square root of 169.\", \"remediation\": \"d² = 169, so d = √169 = 13 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"3² + 4² + 12² = 9 + 16 + 144 = 169. √169 = 13.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 4 + 12 directly.\", \"remediation\": \"Use d = √(l² + w² + h²), not the sum of edge lengths.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2651, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Cylinders, Cones & Spheres', 302, 8, 'A sphere has a radius of 6 cm. What is its volume in terms of π?', NULL, 8, 'High', '144π cm³', '288π cm³', '864π cm³', '576π cm³', 'B', 'active', 'V = (4/3)πr³ = (4/3)π(6)³ = (4/3)π(216) = 4 × 72π = 288π cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the surface area 4πr² = 4π(36) = 144π.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume formula uses r cubed: V = (4/3)πr³ = 288π cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4 × 216 without dividing by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to multiply by 4/3: (4/3) × 216 = 288.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 288 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"(4/3) × 216 = 288π cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2652, 6, 1, 7, 'Volume of Cylinders, Cones & Spheres', 302, 8, 'A solid metal sphere with radius 3 cm is melted down and recast into a solid cone with base radius 3 cm. What is the height of the cone?', NULL, 8, 'High', '6 cm', '4 cm', '9 cm', '12 cm', 'D', 'active', 'Volume of sphere = (4/3)π(3)³ = 36π. Volume of cone = (1/3)π(3)²h = 3πh. Setting them equal: 3πh = 36π → h = 12 cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student equated diameter to height.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate volumes and solve 3πh = 36π to find h = 12 cm.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 36 by 9.\", \"remediation\": \"Cone volume is (1/3)πr²h = 3πh. Setting 3πh = 36π gives h = 12 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 9 cm.\", \"remediation\": \"Sphere volume is 36π. Cone volume is 3πh. 36 / 3 = 12 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:14:13', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2653, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition Word Problems', 186, 1, 'Sam has 4 apples. His mother gives him 3 more apples. How many apples does Sam have in all?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '7', '6', '8', '1', 'A', 'active', 'Add the two amounts together: 4 + 3 = 7 apples.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Count forward 3 steps from 4: 5, 6, 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted by 1 too many.\", \"remediation\": \"4 + 3 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 4 - 3.\", \"remediation\": \"The problem asks for \\\"in all\\\", which means add: 4 + 3 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2654, 6, 2, 8, 'Subtraction Word Problems', 187, 1, 'There were 9 birds on a fence. 4 birds flew away. How many birds are left on the fence?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '6', '13', '5', '4', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract the birds that flew away: 9 - 4 = 5 birds.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student counted back incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Start at 9 and count back 4: 8, 7, 6, 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 9 + 4 instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Flew away\\\" means subtract: 9 - 4 = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student repeated the number that flew away.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 4 from 9 to get 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2655, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties & Relationships', 188, 1, 'If 3 + 5 = 8, what is 5 + 3?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '2', '15', '7', '8', 'D', 'active', 'By the commutative property of addition, changing the order of the addends does not change the sum: 5 + 3 = 8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 5 - 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Changing the order of addition gives the same sum: 3 + 5 = 5 + 3 = 8.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 5 × 3.\", \"remediation\": \"This is addition: 5 + 3 = 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"3 + 5 and 5 + 3 both equal 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2656, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Strategies', 189, 1, 'Which double fact helps you solve 6 + 7?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '4 + 4 = 8', '6 + 6 = 12', '5 + 5 = 10', '8 + 8 = 16', 'B', 'active', '6 + 7 is a near-double: 6 + 6 + 1 = 12 + 1 = 13. So 6 + 6 helps solve it.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student picked an unrelated double.\", \"remediation\": \"6 + 6 = 12 is the near-double for 6 + 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student chose doubles of 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Look at the numbers in the problem: 6 + 7 is one more than 6 + 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose a larger double fact.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the double of the smaller addend: 6 + 6 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2657, 6, 2, 8, 'Equations & Unknowns', 190, 1, 'What number makes this equation true? 6 + ___ = 10', NULL, 1, 'Low', '16', '3', '4', '5', 'C', 'active', 'Count up from 6 to 10: 7, 8, 9, 10 (4 counts). So 6 + 4 = 10.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 6 + 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Find what must be added to 6 to reach 10: 10 - 6 = 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted 1 short.\", \"remediation\": \"6 + 4 = 10.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought 5 + 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Starting from 6, you need 4 more to make 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2658, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties & Relationships', 188, 1, 'Which addition sentence can help you solve 12 - 5 = ___?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '5 + 7 = 12', '7 + 7 = 14', '12 + 5 = 17', '5 + 5 = 10', 'A', 'active', 'Subtraction is the inverse of addition: 12 - 5 = ? is the same as finding 5 + ? = 12, which is 5 + 7 = 12.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student chose doubles of 7.\", \"remediation\": \"Relate the known part (5) and total (12): 5 + 7 = 12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added the two numbers together.\", \"remediation\": \"Think of fact families: 12 - 5 = 7 because 5 + 7 = 12.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student chose doubles of 5.\", \"remediation\": \"You need an equation with sum 12 and part 5: 5 + 7 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2659, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition Word Problems', 186, 1, 'Maya has 2 red crayons, 3 blue crayons, and 4 green crayons. How many crayons does she have altogether?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '8', '7', '9', '10', 'C', 'active', 'Add all three numbers: 2 + 3 + 4 = 5 + 4 = 9 crayons.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"2 + 3 = 5, and 5 + 4 = 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added only two colors (3 + 4 = 7).\", \"remediation\": \"Make sure to add all three numbers: 2 + 3 + 4 = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student counted 1 too many.\", \"remediation\": \"2 + 3 + 4 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2660, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition Word Problems', 186, 1, 'Leo has 7 stickers. He buys some more stickers. Now he has 13 stickers. How many stickers did Leo buy?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '7', '20', '5', '6', 'D', 'active', 'Set up the equation 7 + ? = 13. Subtract: 13 - 7 = 6 stickers.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student repeated the starting amount.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 13 - 7 = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 7 + 13.\", \"remediation\": \"Leo started with 7 and ended with 13, so find the difference: 13 - 7 = 6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Count from 7 up to 13: 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13 (6 stickers).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2661, 6, 2, 8, 'Subtraction Word Problems', 187, 1, 'There were some frogs on a log. 6 frogs jumped into the pond. Now there are 8 frogs on the log. How many frogs were on the log at first?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '2', '14', '12', '8', 'B', 'active', 'Start unknown: ? - 6 = 8. Add to find the total: 8 + 6 = 14 frogs.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 8 - 6.\", \"remediation\": \"To find how many were there at first before 6 jumped away, add 8 + 6 = 14.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 8 + 6.\", \"remediation\": \"8 + 6 = 14.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student just took the remaining frogs.\", \"remediation\": \"Combine the remaining frogs and the ones that jumped away: 8 + 6 = 14.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2662, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Strategies', 189, 1, 'To solve 8 + 5 using the \"make a ten\" strategy, how should 5 be broken apart?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '2 + 3', '4 + 1', '1 + 4', '3 + 2', 'A', 'active', '8 needs 2 to make 10. Breaking 5 into 2 + 3 gives (8 + 2) + 3 = 10 + 3 = 13.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"8 + 4 is 12, not 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the partner to 10 for 8, which is 2. 5 splits into 2 + 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"8 + 1 is 9, not 10.\", \"remediation\": \"8 needs 2 to make 10, so split 5 into 2 and 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Giving 3 to 8 makes 11.\", \"remediation\": \"Take 2 first to complete 10: 8 + 2 = 10, then add 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2663, 6, 2, 8, 'Equations & Unknowns', 190, 1, 'Which number makes the equation true? 15 - ___ = 8', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '23', '7', '6', '8', 'B', 'active', '15 - 7 = 8, because 8 + 7 = 15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 15 + 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 8 from 15 to find the missing subtrahend: 15 - 8 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted.\", \"remediation\": \"15 - 7 = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 8.\", \"remediation\": \"15 - 8 = 7, so the missing number is 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2664, 6, 2, 8, 'Equations & Unknowns', 190, 1, 'Is the equation 4 + 5 = 6 + 3 true or false?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'True, because both sides equal 10', 'False, because 4 + 5 is 8', 'False, because the numbers are different', 'True, because both sides equal 9', 'D', 'active', 'Left side: 4 + 5 = 9. Right side: 6 + 3 = 9. Since 9 = 9, the equation is true.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the total as 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Both sides equal 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4 + 5 incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"4 + 5 = 9 and 6 + 3 = 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student thought expressions must use the same numbers to be equal.\", \"remediation\": \"An equals sign means both sides have the same value, even with different numbers.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2665, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties & Relationships', 188, 1, 'Which grouping shows the associative property of addition for 2 + 3 + 4?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '(2 + 3) + 4 = 2 + (3 + 4)', '2 + 3 = 3 + 2', '2 + 3 + 4 = 9', '2 + 4 + 3 = 4 + 2 + 3', 'A', 'active', 'The associative property states that grouping addends differently does not change the sum: (2 + 3) + 4 = 2 + (3 + 4).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"This shows the commutative property of two numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"The associative property involves grouping 3 numbers with parentheses: (a + b) + c = a + (b + c).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This just evaluates the sum.\", \"remediation\": \"The property shows how grouping with parentheses can change without changing the sum.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This only reorders numbers without parentheses.\", \"remediation\": \"Look for parentheses grouping the numbers differently.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2666, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition Word Problems', 186, 1, 'Liam has 14 toy cars. Emma has 8 toy cars. How many MORE toy cars does Liam have than Emma?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '5', '22', '6', '7', 'C', 'active', 'To compare amounts, subtract: 14 - 8 = 6 toy cars.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 13 - 8.\", \"remediation\": \"14 - 8 = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 14 + 8.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How many more\\\" means find the difference by subtracting: 14 - 8 = 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted.\", \"remediation\": \"14 - 8 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2667, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Strategies', 189, 1, 'What is 14 - 6 using the \"take from ten\" strategy?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '14 - 4 = 10, then 10 - 6 = 4', '6 + 6 = 12, then 12 + 2 = 14', '14 - 10 = 4, then 4 + 6 = 10', '10 - 6 = 4, then 4 + 4 = 8', 'D', 'active', 'Break 14 into 10 and 4. Subtract 6 from 10 to get 4, then add the other 4: 4 + 4 = 8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 6 twice.\", \"remediation\": \"14 = 10 + 4. Take 6 from 10: 10 - 6 = 4, then add back the 4 to get 8.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student used a doubles fact incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"The take-from-ten strategy subtracts from 10 first.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error in steps.\", \"remediation\": \"10 - 6 = 4, then 4 + 4 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2668, 6, 2, 8, 'Equations & Unknowns', 190, 1, 'What number makes this equation true? ___ - 4 = 7', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '12', '11', '3', '10', 'B', 'active', 'Add the known parts: 7 + 4 = 11. Checking: 11 - 4 = 7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted.\", \"remediation\": \"7 + 4 = 11.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 7 - 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Since you started with a total and took away 4 to leave 7, add: 7 + 4 = 11.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 7 + 3.\", \"remediation\": \"7 + 4 = 11.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2669, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition Word Problems', 186, 1, 'Ava had 5 pencils. She found 4 more pencils, and then her teacher gave her some more. Now Ava has 16 pencils. How many pencils did the teacher give her?', NULL, 1, 'High', '9', '6', '7', '8', 'C', 'active', 'Ava had 5 + 4 = 9 pencils. She ended with 16. To find the teacher\'s pencils: 16 - 9 = 7 pencils.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student found the sum of 5 + 4 (9) but didn\'t subtract from 16.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 9 from 16 to find the remaining pencils: 16 - 9 = 7.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"16 - 9 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student thought 16 - 8 = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Ava had 9 pencils, so 16 - 9 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2670, 6, 2, 8, 'Equations & Unknowns', 190, 1, 'Which number makes the equation true? 8 + 3 = ___ + 4', NULL, 1, 'High', '7', '6', '11', '15', 'A', 'active', 'Left side: 8 + 3 = 11. Right side must also equal 11: ___ + 4 = 11. So ___ = 11 - 4 = 7.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"11 - 4 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student put the sum of the left side (11) into the blank.\", \"remediation\": \"Both sides must balance. Left is 11, so the blank + 4 must equal 11: 11 - 4 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added all numbers: 8 + 3 + 4 = 15.\", \"remediation\": \"Balance both sides: 8 + 3 = 11, so ? + 4 = 11, ? = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2671, 6, 2, 8, 'Equations & Unknowns', 190, 1, 'Which equation has the same unknown value as 14 - ___ = 9?', NULL, 1, 'High', '14 + 9 = ___', '9 - ___ = 14', '9 + ___ = 14', '___ - 9 = 14', 'C', 'active', '14 - ? = 9 means 14 is the whole and 9 and ? are parts. The addition fact with the same unknown is 9 + ? = 14.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added the whole and a part.\", \"remediation\": \"14 is the total. The related addition is 9 + ? = 14.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted from the smaller part.\", \"remediation\": \"You cannot subtract to get a larger whole in Grade 1.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"In this equation the total would be 23.\", \"remediation\": \"The total is 14, so 9 + ? = 14.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2672, 6, 2, 8, 'Subtraction Word Problems', 187, 1, 'There are 15 children on the playground. 8 are playing soccer and the rest are on swings. How many children are on swings?', NULL, 1, 'High', '23', '8', '6', '7', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract the children playing soccer from the total: 15 - 8 = 7 children on swings.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 15 + 8.\", \"remediation\": \"The total is 15. Subtract to find the missing part: 15 - 8 = 7.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 8.\", \"remediation\": \"15 - 8 = 7, not 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Count from 8 up to 15: 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 (7 children).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2673, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties & Relationships', 188, 1, 'Emma wants to add 7 + 5 + 3. Which is the easiest way to group the numbers?', NULL, 1, 'High', '(5 + 3) + 7, because 5 + 3 is 8', '(7 + 3) + 5, because 7 + 3 makes a 10', '(7 + 5) + 3, adding in order', 'Any order, but (7 + 5) is easiest', 'B', 'active', 'Using the commutative and associative properties, grouping 7 and 3 first makes 10. Then 10 + 5 = 15 is very easy to solve.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"8 + 7 requires another step.\", \"remediation\": \"Making a ten first (7 + 3 = 10) is the most efficient strategy.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"7 + 5 = 12 requires adding 12 + 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Look for number bonds to 10: 7 + 3 = 10.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Making 12 is harder than making 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Combine 7 and 3 first to make 10, then add 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2674, 6, 2, 8, 'Equations & Unknowns', 190, 1, 'Which value of the unknown makes BOTH equations true?  (1) 6 + ? = 11   and   (2) 15 - ? = 10', NULL, 1, 'High', '5', '7', '6', '4', 'A', 'active', 'For equation (1): 11 - 6 = 5. For equation (2): 15 - 10 = 5. The unknown value 5 satisfies both equations.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"7 makes 6 + 7 = 13.\", \"remediation\": \"Both equations require ? = 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"6 makes 6 + 6 = 12 and 15 - 6 = 9.\", \"remediation\": \"Test 5: 6 + 5 = 11 and 15 - 5 = 10.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"4 makes 6 + 4 = 10, not 11.\", \"remediation\": \"6 + 5 = 11, so ? must be 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2675, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition Word Problems', 186, 1, 'Lucas had 18 marbles. He gave 5 marbles to Noah and 4 marbles to Jack. How many marbles does Lucas have left?', NULL, 1, 'High', '10', '9', '8', '13', 'B', 'active', 'Total marbles given away = 5 + 4 = 9. Marbles left = 18 - 9 = 9 marbles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"18 - 5 = 13, then 13 - 4 = 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 1 too many.\", \"remediation\": \"18 - 9 = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted only the first 5 marbles.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to also subtract the 4 marbles given to Jack: 13 - 4 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2676, 6, 2, 8, 'Equations & Unknowns', 190, 1, 'There are 12 birds in a tree. Some more birds join them. Then 3 birds fly away, leaving 14 birds. How many birds joined them?', NULL, 1, 'High', '4', '2', '6', '5', 'D', 'active', 'Work backwards: Before 3 flew away, there were 14 + 3 = 17 birds. Since there were initially 12, the birds that joined were 17 - 12 = 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"12 + 5 = 17, and 17 - 3 = 14.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student just subtracted 14 - 12 = 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Don\'t forget 3 birds flew away! 12 + ? - 3 = 14 -> 12 + ? = 17 -> ? = 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 to 2 plus 1.\", \"remediation\": \"17 - 12 = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2677, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'A toy store had 28 teddy bears. They received a shipment of 15 more teddy bears. How many teddy bears do they have now?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '43', '33', '45', '13', 'A', 'active', 'Add 28 + 15: 28 + 10 = 38, 38 + 5 = 43 teddy bears.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to regroup the ones (8 + 5 = 13).\", \"remediation\": \"8 + 5 = 13 ones, which is 1 ten and 3 ones. 20 + 10 + 10 + 3 = 43.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the ones.\", \"remediation\": \"28 + 15 = 43.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 28 - 15.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Received more\\\" means add: 28 + 15 = 43.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2678, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'There were 52 books on a shelf. Students checked out 20 books. How many books are still on the shelf?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '30', '72', '32', '22', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract the checked-out books: 52 - 20 = 32 books.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student lost the 2 ones.\", \"remediation\": \"52 - 20 = 32.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 52 + 20.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Checked out\\\" means subtract: 52 - 20 = 32.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 30 instead of 20.\", \"remediation\": \"5 tens - 2 tens = 3 tens. 52 - 20 = 32.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2679, 6, 2, 8, 'Odd & Even Numbers', 362, 2, 'Which number is an EVEN number?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '15', '19', '13', '16', 'D', 'active', 'An even number ends in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8 and can be divided into two equal groups without leftovers. 16 ends in 6, so it is even.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"15 ends in 5, which is odd.\", \"remediation\": \"Even numbers end in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"19 ends in 9, which is odd.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the ones digit: 16 ends in 6 (even).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"13 ends in 3, which is odd.\", \"remediation\": \"Odd numbers end in 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2680, 6, 2, 8, 'Odd & Even Numbers', 362, 2, 'Which addition sentence shows that 14 is an even number?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '8 + 6 = 14', '7 + 7 = 14', '10 + 4 = 14', '9 + 5 = 14', 'B', 'active', 'An even number can be expressed as the sum of two equal whole numbers: 7 + 7 = 14.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"8 and 6 are not equal.\", \"remediation\": \"Look for two identical addends: 7 + 7 = 14.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"10 and 4 are not equal addends.\", \"remediation\": \"An even number can be split into two identical halves: 7 + 7 = 14.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"9 and 5 are not equal.\", \"remediation\": \"To prove a number is even, write it as a double of equal numbers: 7 + 7 = 14.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2681, 6, 2, 8, 'Arrays & Repeated Addition', 363, 2, 'An array has 3 rows of apples. Each row has 4 apples. How many apples are there in total?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '7', '15', '12', '10', 'C', 'active', 'Use repeated addition for 3 rows of 4: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 apples.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 4 three times: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student counted an extra row of 4 (12 + 3).\", \"remediation\": \"3 rows of 4 is 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"4 + 4 = 8, and 8 + 4 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2682, 6, 2, 8, 'Arrays & Repeated Addition', 363, 2, 'Which repeated addition equation matches an array with 4 rows and 2 dots in each row?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8', '2 + 4 + 2 = 8', '4 + 2 = 6', '4 + 4 = 8', 'A', 'active', '4 rows with 2 dots each means adding 2 four times: 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"This is not standard repeated addition.\", \"remediation\": \"Write 2 four times: 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added the row count to the dot count.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the items in each row (2) for every row (4 times): 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This represents 2 rows of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"4 rows of 2 is written as 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2683, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'Oliver has 46 pennies. He spends 14 pennies. How many pennies does he have left?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '60', '34', '32', '22', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract: 46 - 14 = (40 - 10) + (6 - 4) = 30 + 2 = 32 pennies.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 46 + 14.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Spends\\\" means take away: 46 - 14 = 32.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the ones digit.\", \"remediation\": \"6 - 4 = 2, so 46 - 14 = 32.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 20 instead of 10.\", \"remediation\": \"4 tens - 1 ten = 3 tens: 32.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2684, 6, 2, 8, 'Odd & Even Numbers', 362, 2, 'What happens when you add two even numbers together, like 4 + 6?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'The sum is zero', 'The sum is always ODD', 'The sum is sometimes odd, sometimes even', 'The sum is always EVEN', 'D', 'active', 'Adding two even numbers always results in an even number (e.g., 4 + 6 = 10, which is even).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Adding positive numbers does not yield zero.\", \"remediation\": \"Even + even = even.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused even + even with even + odd.\", \"remediation\": \"Even + even is always even because both numbers can be split into pairs without leftovers.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The rule is constant, not variable.\", \"remediation\": \"Every pair of even numbers sums to an even number.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2685, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'At the park, there were 34 children. 18 more children arrived. Then 12 children went home. How many children are at the park now?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '52', '40', '64', '28', 'B', 'active', 'Step 1: 34 + 18 = 52. Step 2: 52 - 12 = 40 children.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student did not subtract the 12 children who went home.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the second step: subtract 12 from 52 to get 40.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added all three numbers: 34 + 18 + 12 = 64.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Went home\\\" means subtract: 52 - 12 = 40.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted both numbers from 34.\", \"remediation\": \"First add 18, then subtract 12: 34 + 18 = 52, 52 - 12 = 40.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2686, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'Farmer Brown had 65 eggs. He sold some eggs at the market. Now he has 28 eggs left. How many eggs did he sell?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '37', '33', '93', '47', 'A', 'active', 'Subtract the remaining eggs from the starting amount: 65 - 28 = 37 eggs.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error in regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"65 - 28 = 37.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 65 + 28.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the difference between what he started with and what is left: 65 - 28 = 37.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted smaller digits from larger digits (6 - 2 = 4, 8 - 5 = 3 -> 43 or 47).\", \"remediation\": \"Regroup 65 into 50 + 15: 15 - 8 = 7, and 50 - 20 = 30. Result: 37.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2687, 6, 2, 8, 'Odd & Even Numbers', 362, 2, 'Ella has 19 marbles. Can she share them equally with her friend with no marbles left over?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'Yes, because 19 is an even number', 'No, because 19 is an odd number', 'Yes, each gets 9 marbles with 0 left', 'Yes, each gets 10 marbles', 'B', 'active', '19 ends in 9, making it an odd number. Odd numbers cannot be divided into two equal whole-number groups without a remainder (9 + 9 = 18, 1 left over).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"19 is not even.\", \"remediation\": \"Numbers ending in 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 are odd and cannot be shared equally between 2 people.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"9 + 9 is 18, leaving 1 marble remaining.\", \"remediation\": \"19 leaves 1 marble leftover, so it cannot be shared equally.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"10 + 10 = 20, but Ella only has 19.\", \"remediation\": \"Ella cannot give 10 to each because that requires 20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2688, 6, 2, 8, 'Arrays & Repeated Addition', 363, 2, 'Which array has a total of 15 objects?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '2 rows of 7', '3 rows of 4', '4 rows of 5', '3 rows of 5', 'D', 'active', '3 rows of 5 = 5 + 5 + 5 = 15 objects.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"2 rows of 7 is 14.\", \"remediation\": \"3 rows of 5 = 15.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"3 rows of 4 is 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Check repeated addition: 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"4 rows of 5 is 20.\", \"remediation\": \"3 rows of 5 equals 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2689, 6, 2, 8, 'Arrays & Repeated Addition', 363, 2, 'An array of stickers has 5 rows and 3 columns. Which two repeated addition equations represent this array?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 15  and  5 + 5 + 5 = 15', '5 + 3 = 8  and  3 + 5 = 8', '5 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 25  and  3 + 3 + 3 = 9', '3 + 3 + 3 = 9  and  5 + 5 = 10', 'A', 'active', 'By rows: 5 rows of 3 = 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 15. By columns: 3 columns of 5 = 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 5 + 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Arrays use repeated addition: 5 groups of 3 or 3 groups of 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student changed the dimensions.\", \"remediation\": \"5 rows of 3 gives 15 stickers total.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student wrote incomplete sums.\", \"remediation\": \"Both repeated additions must total 15: 3+3+3+3+3 = 15 and 5+5+5 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2690, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'Chloe had some stickers. She gave 24 stickers to her brother. Now she has 39 stickers left. How many stickers did Chloe have to begin with?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '62', '15', '63', '53', 'C', 'active', 'Start unknown: ? - 24 = 39. Add to find the start: 39 + 24 = 63 stickers.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"39 + 24 = 63.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 39 - 24.\", \"remediation\": \"Chloe started with more stickers before giving some away: add 39 + 24 = 63.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to regroup 9 + 4 = 13.\", \"remediation\": \"39 + 24 = 30 + 20 + 13 = 63.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2691, 6, 2, 8, 'Odd & Even Numbers', 362, 2, 'Which statement is TRUE about the sum of an ODD number and an EVEN number (for example, 5 + 4)?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'The sum is always EVEN', 'The sum is always 10', 'The sum is always a multiple of 4', 'The sum is always ODD', 'D', 'active', 'An even number pairs up completely, but an odd number always has 1 left over. When added, that 1 remains unpaired, so the sum is always odd (e.g., 5 + 4 = 9).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Odd + even is never even.\", \"remediation\": \"Try examples: 3 + 2 = 5 (odd), 7 + 4 = 11 (odd). Odd + even = odd.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The sum depends on the numbers chosen.\", \"remediation\": \"5 + 4 = 9, not 10.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"9 is not a multiple of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Odd + even is always an odd number.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2692, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'Liam scored 47 points in a game. Noah scored 19 fewer points than Liam. How many points did Noah score?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '26', '28', '66', '38', 'B', 'active', 'Subtract: 47 - 19 = 28 points.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"47 - 19 = 28.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 47 + 19.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Fewer than\\\" means subtract: 47 - 19 = 28.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted digits incorrectly (9 - 7 = 2, 4 - 1 = 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Regroup: 17 - 9 = 8, and 30 - 10 = 20. Total = 28.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2693, 6, 2, 8, 'Arrays & Repeated Addition', 363, 2, 'Mason baked cookies and placed them in 4 rows with 5 cookies in each row. Which equation shows the total number of cookies?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '4 + 5 = 9', '4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16', '5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20', '5 + 5 + 5 = 15', 'C', 'active', '4 rows of 5 cookies means adding 5 four times: 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20 cookies.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added the two dimensions.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply rows by cookies per row: 4 × 5 = 20 (or 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"This is 4 rows of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Each row has 5 cookies: add 5 four times.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This is only 3 rows of 5.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 rows: 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2694, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'A bakery had 82 muffins in the morning. They sold 37 muffins by noon and 26 muffins in the afternoon. How many muffins were left at the end of the day?', NULL, 2, 'High', '19', '119', '29', '45', 'A', 'active', 'Total sold = 37 + 26 = 63 muffins. Muffins left = 82 - 63 = 19 muffins.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added all numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Muffins are sold, so subtract from 82: 82 - 37 - 26 = 19.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 82 - 63.\", \"remediation\": \"82 - 63: 12 - 3 = 9, 7 - 6 = 1. Answer: 19.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student only subtracted the first batch (82 - 37 = 45).\", \"remediation\": \"Don\'t forget to also subtract the 26 afternoon muffins: 45 - 26 = 19.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2695, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'Ben has 35 baseball cards. Jack has 18 more cards than Ben. How many baseball cards do Ben and Jack have TOGETHER?', NULL, 2, 'High', '53', '71', '88', '85', 'C', 'active', 'Jack\'s cards = 35 + 18 = 53 cards. Together = Ben + Jack = 35 + 53 = 88 cards.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student found only Jack\'s cards (53) without adding Ben\'s cards.\", \"remediation\": \"The problem asks for both together: add Ben\'s 35 + Jack\'s 53 = 88 cards.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 35 + 18 + 18.\", \"remediation\": \"Jack has 35 + 18 = 53 cards. Together they have 35 + 53 = 88 cards.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the addition.\", \"remediation\": \"35 + 53 = 88.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2696, 6, 2, 8, 'Odd & Even Numbers', 362, 2, 'Lucas writes down three numbers: 12, 17, and 24. He wants to know: what will be the result if he adds all three numbers together?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'EVEN, because the sum ends in 0', 'EVEN, because two numbers are even', 'ODD, because all three numbers are odd', 'ODD, because even + odd + even = odd', 'D', 'active', '12 + 24 = 36 (even). Then 36 (even) + 17 (odd) = 53 (odd). An even sum plus an odd number is always odd.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"12 + 17 + 24 = 53, which ends in 3, not 0.\", \"remediation\": \"53 ends in 3, which is an odd number.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student thought having more even numbers makes the sum even.\", \"remediation\": \"The single odd number (17) leaves 1 unpaired, making the total sum (53) odd.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"12 and 24 are even numbers, not odd.\", \"remediation\": \"12 and 24 are even; only 17 is odd.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2697, 6, 2, 8, 'Arrays & Repeated Addition', 363, 2, 'A grid of squares has 4 rows and 5 columns. If 1 row is completely removed, what is the repeated addition equation for the remaining squares?', NULL, 2, 'High', '4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16', '5 + 5 + 5 = 15', '5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20', '3 + 5 = 8', 'B', 'active', 'Originally 4 rows of 5. Removing 1 row leaves 3 rows of 5 columns: 5 + 5 + 5 = 15 squares.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This represents 4 rows of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Each remaining row still has 5 columns: 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This is the original grid before removing a row.\", \"remediation\": \"One row was removed, leaving 3 rows: 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added row and column numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Repeated addition of 3 rows of 5 is 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2698, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'In a school library, there are 94 mystery books. 48 are checked out on Monday, and 15 of those are returned on Tuesday. How many mystery books are in the library on Tuesday afternoon?', NULL, 2, 'High', '61', '76', '46', '31', 'A', 'active', 'Step 1 (after Monday): 94 - 48 = 46 books in library. Step 2 (after Tuesday returns): 46 + 15 = 61 books.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"94 - 48 = 46, and 46 + 15 = 61.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student stopped after Monday\'s checkout (94 - 48 = 46).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember that 15 books were returned: 46 + 15 = 61.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 15 instead of adding.\", \"remediation\": \"Returned books go back into the library, so add 15: 46 + 15 = 61.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2699, 6, 2, 8, 'Odd & Even Numbers', 362, 2, 'Which expression always results in an EVEN number for ANY whole number n?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'n + 1', 'n + n', 'n + 3', 'n - 1', 'B', 'active', 'n + n = 2n, which means doubling n. Doubling any whole number always produces an even number.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"If n = 2, n + 1 = 3 (odd).\", \"remediation\": \"Adding 1 changes an even number into an odd number.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"If n = 4, n + 3 = 7 (odd).\", \"remediation\": \"Adding 3 to an even number gives an odd number.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"If n = 4, n - 1 = 3 (odd).\", \"remediation\": \"Only n + n (doubling) guarantees an even result every time.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2700, 6, 2, 8, 'Arrays & Repeated Addition', 363, 2, 'Sophie has 24 tiles. Which arrangement CANNOT form a rectangular array with no tiles left over?', NULL, 2, 'High', '2 rows of 12', '4 rows of 6', '3 rows of 8', '5 rows of 5', 'D', 'active', '5 rows of 5 requires 25 tiles (5 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 25). Sophie only has 24 tiles, so she cannot make 5 rows of 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"2 rows of 12 = 24 tiles (valid array).\", \"remediation\": \"2 × 12 = 24, which uses exactly 24 tiles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"4 rows of 6 = 24 tiles (valid array).\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 6 = 24, which uses exactly 24 tiles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"3 rows of 8 = 24 tiles (valid array).\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 8 = 24, which uses exactly 24 tiles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2701, 6, 2, 8, 'Addition & Subtraction Word Problems', 191, 2, 'Grace had $75. She bought a dress for $38 and a hat for $19. How much money did Grace have left?', NULL, 2, 'High', '$18', '$28', '$57', '$19', 'A', 'active', 'Total spent = $38 + $19 = $57. Money left = $75 - $57 = $18.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 75 - 57.\", \"remediation\": \"75 - 57 = 15 - 7 = 8, 60 - 50 = 10 -> $18.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the total spent ($57) without subtracting from $75.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the total spent ($57) from Grace\'s initial $75: $75 - $57 = $18.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed $19.\", \"remediation\": \"$75 - ($38 + $19) = $75 - $57 = $18.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:32:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2702, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'There are 6 baskets. Each basket contains 4 apples. How many apples are there in total?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '28', '10', '24', '20', 'C', 'active', 'Multiply the number of baskets by apples per basket: 6 × 4 = 24 apples.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 7 × 4.\", \"remediation\": \"6 × 4 = 24.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 6 + 4 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Equal groups require multiplication: 6 groups of 4 is 6 × 4 = 24.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 5 × 4.\", \"remediation\": \"6 × 4 = 24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:40', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2703, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'A teacher divides 35 pencils equally among 5 students. How many pencils does each student receive?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '30', '6', '8', '7', 'D', 'active', 'Divide the total pencils by the number of students: 35 ÷ 5 = 7 pencils.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 35 - 5.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Divide equally\\\" means division: 35 ÷ 5 = 7.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 35 ÷ 5.\", \"remediation\": \"5 × 7 = 35, so 35 ÷ 5 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"35 ÷ 5 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:40', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2704, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties of Multiplication', 364, 3, 'Which property of multiplication is shown by 7 × 8 = 8 × 7?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'Identity Property', 'Commutative Property', 'Associative Property', 'Distributive Property', 'B', 'active', 'The commutative property states that changing the order of the factors does not change the product: a × b = b × a.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Identity property involves multiplying by 1 (a × 1 = a).\", \"remediation\": \"7 × 8 = 8 × 7 demonstrates the Commutative Property.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Associative property involves grouping with parentheses (a × b) × c.\", \"remediation\": \"Order change of two numbers is the Commutative Property.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Distributive property involves multiplication across addition: a(b + c).\", \"remediation\": \"Flipping the order of factors is the Commutative Property.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:40', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2705, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'What is the unknown number that makes this equation true?  ___ × 6 = 42', NULL, 3, 'Low', '6', '8', '7', '36', 'C', 'active', 'Divide 42 by 6 to find the missing factor: 42 ÷ 6 = 7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"6 × 6 = 36, not 42.\", \"remediation\": \"Think: what number multiplied by 6 equals 42? 7 × 6 = 42.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"8 × 6 = 48, not 42.\", \"remediation\": \"42 ÷ 6 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 42 - 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the factor, not the difference: 42 ÷ 6 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2706, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties of Multiplication', 364, 3, 'Any number multiplied by 0 is always equal to what?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '0', '10', '1', 'The number itself', 'A', 'active', 'By the Zero Property of Multiplication, any number multiplied by 0 is 0 (e.g., 9 × 0 = 0).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Any number × 0 = 0.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student confused 0 with 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplying by 0 always equals 0.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student confused multiplying by 0 with multiplying by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplying by 1 gives the number itself; multiplying by 0 gives 0.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2707, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'Tom has 3 packs of gum. Each pack has 5 sticks. He gives 4 sticks to his sister. How many sticks does Tom have left?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '15', '19', '11', '8', 'C', 'active', 'Step 1: 3 packs × 5 sticks = 15 sticks. Step 2: 15 - 4 = 11 sticks left.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the total sticks (15) without subtracting the 4 given away.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the 4 sticks given to sister: 15 - 4 = 11.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4 instead of subtracting: 15 + 4 = 19.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Gives away\\\" means subtract: 15 - 4 = 11.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 5 then subtracted 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply packs by sticks first: 3 × 5 = 15, then 15 - 4 = 11.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2708, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'Look at the pattern: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, ___. What is the next number?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '17', '16', '20', '18', 'D', 'active', 'The pattern increases by adding 3 (multiples of 3): 15 + 3 = 18.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 2.\", \"remediation\": \"15 + 3 = 18.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Identify the rule: add 3 each time. 15 + 3 = 18.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the constant step of +3: 15 + 3 = 18.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2709, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'Which division equation is in the same fact family as 4 × 9 = 36?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '36 ÷ 6 = 6', '36 ÷ 4 = 9', '40 ÷ 4 = 10', '36 - 9 = 27', 'B', 'active', 'The fact family for 4, 9, 36 includes: 4 × 9 = 36, 9 × 4 = 36, 36 ÷ 4 = 9, and 36 ÷ 9 = 4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This fact uses different numbers (6 × 6 = 36).\", \"remediation\": \"Use the same numbers from the multiplication fact (4, 9, 36): 36 ÷ 4 = 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This uses 40 and 10.\", \"remediation\": \"A fact family contains the same set of numbers: 36 ÷ 4 = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This is subtraction, not division.\", \"remediation\": \"The inverse operation of multiplication is division: 36 ÷ 4 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2710, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties of Multiplication', 364, 3, 'Which expression demonstrates the DISTRIBUTIVE property to solve 6 × 14?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '(6 × 10) + (6 × 4)', '14 × 6', '6 × (10 × 4)', '(6 + 10) × (6 + 4)', 'A', 'active', 'Break 14 into 10 + 4: 6 × (10 + 4) = (6 × 10) + (6 × 4) = 60 + 24 = 84.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"This demonstrates the Commutative Property.\", \"remediation\": \"The Distributive Property breaks an addend apart: 6 × (10 + 4) = (6 × 10) + (6 × 4).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied inside the parentheses (10 × 4 = 40, not 14).\", \"remediation\": \"14 is broken into addition (10 + 4), so distribute: (6 × 10) + (6 × 4).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 6 to both parts instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 6 by each part: (6 × 10) + (6 × 4).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2711, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'Sarah bought 4 boxes of markers. Each box had 8 markers. She gave away 12 markers. Which equation can be used to find m, the number of markers she has left?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', 'm = (4 + 8) - 12', 'm = (4 × 8) - 12', 'm = (4 × 8) + 12', 'm = 4 × (8 - 12)', 'B', 'active', 'First find total markers: 4 × 8. Then subtract the 12 given away: m = (4 × 8) - 12.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4 + 8 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"4 boxes of 8 markers means 4 × 8 = 32 markers.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 12 instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Gave away\\\" means subtract: m = (4 × 8) - 12.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student placed parentheses incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 4 × 8 first, then subtract 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2712, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'Which number makes the equation true?  (4 × 2) × 5 = 4 × (___ × 5)', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '4', '8', '10', '2', 'D', 'active', 'By the Associative Property of Multiplication, grouping does not change the product: (4 × 2) × 5 = 4 × (2 × 5). The missing number is 2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student repeated 4.\", \"remediation\": \"The factor paired with 5 is 2.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student evaluated 4 × 2 = 8 and put 8 in the blank.\", \"remediation\": \"The associative property keeps the numbers the same: (a × b) × c = a × (b × c). Here b is 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 2 × 5 = 10.\", \"remediation\": \"The missing factor inside the parentheses is 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2713, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'A farmer has 5 rows of apple trees with 6 trees in each row. He also has 15 peach trees. How many fruit trees does the farmer have in all?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '45', '30', '35', '60', 'A', 'active', 'Apple trees = 5 × 6 = 30. Total trees = 30 + 15 = 45 fruit trees.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated only apple trees (5 × 6 = 30).\", \"remediation\": \"Add the 15 peach trees: 30 + 15 = 45.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 5 + 6 + 15.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply rows by trees first: 5 × 6 = 30, then 30 + 15 = 45.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an addition error.\", \"remediation\": \"30 + 15 = 45.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2714, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'In the multiplication table, every multiple of 9 has a special digit sum pattern. What is the sum of the digits for 9 × 4 = 36?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '8', '10', '9 (because 3 + 6 = 9)', '7', 'C', 'active', 'For single-digit multiples of 9, the sum of the digits is always 9: 3 + 6 = 9.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"3 + 6 = 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"3 + 6 = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the digits: 3 + 6 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2715, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'Jake has $50. He buys 3 video games that cost $9 each. How much money does Jake have left?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '$27', '$32', '$14', '$23', 'D', 'active', 'Cost of games = 3 × $9 = $27. Money left = $50 - $27 = $23.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated the amount spent ($27) instead of the money left.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the spent amount from $50: $50 - $27 = $23.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 50 - (3 + 9) = 50 - 12 = 38 or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"3 games at $9 each is 3 × 9 = 27. $50 - 27 = $23.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a subtraction error.\", \"remediation\": \"50 - 27 = 23.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2716, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'What is the value of n in the equation:  72 ÷ n = 9?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '63', '8', '9', '7', 'B', 'active', 'Think: 9 × ? = 72. Since 9 × 8 = 72, n = 8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 72 - 9.\", \"remediation\": \"Solve by dividing: 72 ÷ 9 = 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"9 × 9 = 81, not 72.\", \"remediation\": \"72 ÷ 9 = 8, so n = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"9 × 7 = 63, not 72.\", \"remediation\": \"9 × 8 = 72, so n = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2717, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties of Multiplication', 364, 3, 'If a student knows that 8 × 5 = 40, how can they use that fact to solve 8 × 6?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', 'Add 6 to 40 (40 + 6 = 46)', 'Multiply 40 by 6', 'Add 8 to 40 (40 + 8 = 48)', 'Add 1 to 40 (40 + 1 = 41)', 'C', 'active', '8 × 6 means 6 groups of 8, which is one more group of 8 than 8 × 5: 40 + 8 = 48.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added the factor 6 instead of the group size 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Each group contains 8, so one more group adds 8: 40 + 8 = 48.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Multiplying 40 by 6 would give 240.\", \"remediation\": \"Just add one more group of 8: 40 + 8 = 48.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Adding 1 only increases by 1 unit, not 1 group of 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 8 to 40 to find 8 × 6 = 48.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2718, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'A box of cupcakes has 24 cupcakes. 4 children share them equally. Then, one child gives 2 of his cupcakes to his mom. How many cupcakes does that child have left?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '4', '2', '6', '8', 'A', 'active', 'Step 1: Each child gets 24 ÷ 4 = 6 cupcakes. Step 2: 6 - 2 = 4 cupcakes left.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student just answered 2 (the amount given away).\", \"remediation\": \"6 - 2 = 4 cupcakes remaining.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student found each child\'s initial share (6) without subtracting 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the 2 cupcakes given to mom: 6 - 2 = 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 6 + 2.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Gives to mom\\\" means subtract: 6 - 2 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2719, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'Lily makes 6 gift bags. She puts 4 chocolate bars and 3 mint candies in each bag. How many total candies and chocolates did Lily use for all the bags?', NULL, 3, 'High', '24', '18', '42', '13', 'C', 'active', 'Each bag has 4 + 3 = 7 items. Total items = 6 × 7 = 42 items.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated only chocolate bars (6 × 4 = 24).\", \"remediation\": \"Don\'t forget mint candies: (4 + 3) × 6 = 7 × 6 = 42.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated only mint candies (6 × 3 = 18).\", \"remediation\": \"Add chocolates and mints in each bag first: 4 + 3 = 7, then 7 × 6 = 42.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 6 + 4 + 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply items per bag by number of bags: 6 × (4 + 3) = 42.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2720, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties of Multiplication', 364, 3, 'Which equation correctly uses the distributive property to find the area of an 8 by 7 rectangle?', NULL, 3, 'High', '8 × 7 = 8 + 7', '8 × 7 = (8 × 5) × (8 × 2)', '8 × 7 = (4 × 7) + (2 × 7)', '8 × 7 = (8 × 5) + (8 × 2)', 'D', 'active', 'Break 7 into 5 + 2: 8 × (5 + 2) = (8 × 5) + (8 × 2) = 40 + 16 = 56.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This is addition, not area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is length × width: 8 × 7 = 56.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied the two partial areas instead of adding them.\", \"remediation\": \"Partial products must be added together: (8 × 5) + (8 × 2).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"4 + 2 is 6, not 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Make sure the split adds up to the original factor: 5 + 2 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2721, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'There are 48 students going on a field trip. 6 students travel in a van, and the remaining students travel equally in 6 cars. How many students travel in each car?', NULL, 3, 'High', '8', '7', '6', '42', 'B', 'active', 'Step 1: Students in cars = 48 - 6 = 42. Step 2: Students per car = 42 ÷ 6 = 7 students.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 48 ÷ 6 = 8 without subtracting the van students first.\", \"remediation\": \"First subtract the 6 students in the van: 48 - 6 = 42. Then 42 ÷ 6 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 6.\", \"remediation\": \"42 divided by 6 cars equals 7 students per car.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student stopped after subtracting (42) and didn\'t divide by 6 cars.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 42 among the 6 cars: 42 ÷ 6 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2722, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'Look at the input/output table: Input 3 -> Output 12; Input 5 -> Output 20; Input 7 -> Output 28. What is the rule, and what is the output for input 9?', NULL, 3, 'High', 'Rule: Multiply by 4; Output for 9 is 36', 'Rule: Multiply by 4; Output for 9 is 40', 'Rule: Add 9; Output for 9 is 18', 'Rule: Multiply by 3; Output for 9 is 27', 'A', 'active', 'Testing multiplication: 3 × 4 = 12, 5 × 4 = 20, 7 × 4 = 28. The rule is multiply by 4. For input 9: 9 × 4 = 36.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 9 × 4 as 40.\", \"remediation\": \"9 × 4 = 36.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"3 + 9 = 12, but 5 + 9 is 14 (not 20).\", \"remediation\": \"The rule must work for all rows: 3 × 4 = 12, so 9 × 4 = 36.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"3 × 3 = 9, not 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the first row: 3 × 4 = 12, so multiply by 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2723, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'Emma saved $4 each week for 8 weeks. Then she spent $15 on a book and earned $10 for doing chores. How much money does Emma have now?', NULL, 3, 'High', '$17', '$27', '$32', '$37', 'B', 'active', 'Saved: 8 × $4 = $32. Spent book: $32 - $15 = $17. Chores: $17 + $10 = $27.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student stopped after spending $15 ($17) without adding the $10 earned.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the $10 earned from chores: $17 + $10 = $27.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student only calculated savings ($32).\", \"remediation\": \"Perform all steps: 32 - 15 + 10 = $27.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 15 instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"Spending decreases money: 32 - 15 = 17, then 17 + 10 = 27.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2724, 6, 2, 8, 'Properties of Multiplication', 364, 3, 'A student solves 9 × 8 by thinking of 9 as (10 - 1). Which expression matches this reasoning?', NULL, 3, 'High', '10 × 8 - 1', '(10 × 8) + (1 × 8)', '(10 - 8) × (1 - 8)', '(10 × 8) - (1 × 8)', 'D', 'active', 'Using the distributive property: (10 - 1) × 8 = (10 × 8) - (1 × 8) = 80 - 8 = 72.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply 1 by 8.\", \"remediation\": \"You must subtract 1 whole group of 8: 80 - 8 = 72.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"Since 9 is 10 - 1, subtract the 1 group of 8: (10 × 8) - (1 × 8) = 72.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student applied operations incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Distribute 8 to both 10 and 1: (10 × 8) - (1 × 8).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2725, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Step Word Problems', 365, 3, 'A pet shelter has 3 rooms of dogs with 6 dogs in each room, and 4 rooms of cats with 5 cats in each room. How many total animals are in the shelter?', NULL, 3, 'High', '38', '35', '40', '18', 'A', 'active', 'Dogs = 3 × 6 = 18. Cats = 4 × 5 = 20. Total animals = 18 + 20 = 38.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 18 + 17.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate each group: 3 × 6 = 18 dogs, 4 × 5 = 20 cats. 18 + 20 = 38.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded 38 to 40.\", \"remediation\": \"Exact calculation: 18 + 20 = 38 animals.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student only counted the dogs.\", \"remediation\": \"Add both dogs and cats: 18 + 20 = 38 animals.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2726, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns, Multiplication & Unknowns', 192, 3, 'If (p × 7) + 5 = 47, what is the value of p?', NULL, 3, 'High', '8', '7', '6', '5', 'C', 'active', 'Step 1: p × 7 = 47 - 5 = 42. Step 2: p = 42 ÷ 7 = 6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"8 × 7 + 5 = 56 + 5 = 61.\", \"remediation\": \"p = (47 - 5) / 7 = 42 / 7 = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"7 × 7 + 5 = 49 + 5 = 54, not 47.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 5 first: 47 - 5 = 42. Then 42 ÷ 7 = 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"5 × 7 + 5 = 35 + 5 = 40.\", \"remediation\": \"42 ÷ 7 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2727, 6, 2, 8, 'Relationships', 194, 4, 'Which statement correctly interprets the equation 35 = 5 × 7 as a multiplicative comparison?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '35 is 5 more than 7', '35 is 7 less than 5', '35 is equal to 5 plus 7', '35 is 5 times as many as 7', 'D', 'active', 'Multiplication represents \"times as many\": 35 is 5 times as many as 7 (or 7 times as many as 5).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student confused multiplicative comparison with additive comparison.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Times as many\\\" represents multiplication, while \\\"more than\\\" represents addition.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student stated a subtraction comparison.\", \"remediation\": \"35 = 5 × 7 means 35 is 5 times as many as 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"5 plus 7 is 12, not 35.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplication is repeated addition, not single addition.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2728, 6, 2, 8, 'Relationships', 194, 4, 'A blue ribbon is 6 inches long. A red ribbon is 4 times as long as the blue ribbon. How long is the red ribbon?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '2 inches', '24 inches', '10 inches', '20 inches', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply the length of the blue ribbon by 4: 6 × 4 = 24 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 6 - 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 6 by 4 to get 24.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 6 + 4.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"4 times as long\\\" means multiply: 6 × 4 = 24 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"6 × 4 = 24 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2729, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'What are all the factor pairs of 12?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '1 × 12, 2 × 5, 3 × 4', '2 × 6, 3 × 4', '1 × 12, 2 × 6, 3 × 4', '1 × 12, 2 × 6', 'C', 'active', 'The factors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12, giving factor pairs 1 × 12, 2 × 6, and 3 × 4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"2 × 5 is 10, not 12.\", \"remediation\": \"2 × 6 = 12, so the pairs are 1 × 12, 2 × 6, 3 × 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted 1 × 12.\", \"remediation\": \"1 and the number itself are always a factor pair: 1 × 12.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted 3 × 4.\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 4 = 12 is also a factor pair.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2730, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'Which of the following numbers is a PRIME number?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '13', '9', '15', '21', 'A', 'active', 'A prime number has exactly two distinct factors: 1 and itself. 13 has only factors 1 and 13.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"9 is composite (1, 3, 9).\", \"remediation\": \"9 = 3 × 3, so it is composite.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"15 is composite (1, 3, 5, 15).\", \"remediation\": \"15 can be divided by 3 and 5, so it is not prime.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"21 is composite (1, 3, 7, 21).\", \"remediation\": \"21 has factors 3 and 7, so it is composite.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2731, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'Solve for x:  x + 145 = 320', NULL, 4, 'Low', '465', '185', '175', '275', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract 145 from both sides: x = 320 - 145 = 175.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 320 + 145.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 145 from 320 to isolate x: x = 320 - 145 = 175.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error in regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"320 - 145: 10 - 5 = 5, 31 - 14 = 17 -> 175.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"320 - 145 = 175.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2732, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'Which list contains only MULTIPLES of 6?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '6, 9, 12, 15', '1, 2, 3, 6', '6, 16, 26, 36', '6, 12, 18, 24, 30', 'D', 'active', 'Multiples of 6 are generated by multiplying 6 by whole numbers: 6×1=6, 6×2=12, 6×3=18, 6×4=24, 6×5=30.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"9 and 15 are multiples of 3, not 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiples of 6 must be divisible by 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"These are the factors of 6, not multiples.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiples are products of 6 (6, 12, 18...), while factors divide into 6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"16 and 26 are not multiples of 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 6 each time: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2733, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'A number sequence starts at 4 and follows the rule \"add 5\". What are the first 4 terms?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '4, 8, 12, 16', '4, 9, 14, 19', '5, 9, 14, 19', '4, 20, 100, 500', 'B', 'active', 'Start with 4. Add 5: 4 + 5 = 9. Add 5: 9 + 5 = 14. Add 5: 14 + 5 = 19.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This sequence adds 4 each time.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow the given rule: add 5 to each term (4 + 5 = 9, 9 + 5 = 14...).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The sequence started with 5 instead of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Start at 4: 4, 9, 14, 19.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This sequence multiplies by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"The rule says \\\"add 5\\\", not multiply by 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2734, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'What value of y makes the equation true?  5 × y = 80', NULL, 4, 'Low', '16', '75', '14', '400', 'A', 'active', 'Divide both sides by 5: y = 80 ÷ 5 = 16.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 80 - 5.\", \"remediation\": \"To undo multiplication, divide: 80 ÷ 5 = 16.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"5 × 14 = 70, not 80.\", \"remediation\": \"80 ÷ 5 = 16, so y = 16.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 80 × 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 80 by 5 to find y: y = 16.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2735, 6, 2, 8, 'Relationships', 194, 4, 'At a book fair, Maya bought 3 books. David bought 4 times as many books as Maya. How many MORE books did David buy than Maya?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '12', '9', '15', '7', 'B', 'active', 'David bought: 3 × 4 = 12 books. The question asks how many MORE: 12 - 3 = 9 more books.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student found David\'s total books (12) without subtracting Maya\'s books.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How many more\\\" means find the difference: 12 - 3 = 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added Maya\'s and David\'s books together (3 + 12 = 15).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract Maya\'s books from David\'s: 12 - 3 = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 4 = 7.\", \"remediation\": \"First multiply: 3 × 4 = 12, then subtract: 12 - 3 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2736, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'A school orders 140 juice boxes. The juice boxes come in packs of 8. How many full packs are there, and how many boxes are left over?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '17 full packs, with 6 boxes left over', '16 full packs, with 4 boxes left over', '18 full packs, with 0 boxes left over', '17 full packs, with 4 boxes left over', 'D', 'active', 'Divide 140 by 8: 140 ÷ 8 = 17 with a remainder of 4 (17 × 8 = 136, 140 - 136 = 4).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The remainder was calculated incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"140 - 136 = 4 boxes remainder.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"16 × 8 = 128, leaving 12 (another full pack).\", \"remediation\": \"140 ÷ 8 = 17 R 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"18 × 8 = 144, which exceeds 140.\", \"remediation\": \"17 full packs use 136 boxes, leaving 4 left over.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2737, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'Which statement about the number 51 is TRUE?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'It is composite because 3 × 17 = 51', 'It is prime because it is an odd number', 'It is prime because it ends in 1', 'It is composite because it is divisible by 2', 'A', 'active', '51 is composite because its factors are 1, 3, 17, and 51 (sum of digits 5 + 1 = 6 is divisible by 3).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Not all odd numbers are prime (e.g., 9, 15, 21, 51).\", \"remediation\": \"Check divisibility: 51 ÷ 3 = 17, so 51 is composite.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Ending in 1 does not guarantee being prime.\", \"remediation\": \"51 can be factored as 3 × 17, so it is composite.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"51 is odd and cannot be divided evenly by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"51 is divisible by 3, not 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:05'),
(2738, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'A pattern starts at 2 and follows the rule \"multiply by 2, then add 1\". What is the third term in this sequence?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '10', '5', '11', '23', 'C', 'active', 'Term 1 = 2. Term 2 = (2 × 2) + 1 = 5. Term 3 = (5 × 2) + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add 1 on the second step (5 × 2 = 10).\", \"remediation\": \"Add 1 after multiplying: 10 + 1 = 11.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"5 is the second term, not the third term.\", \"remediation\": \"Apply the rule once more: (5 × 2) + 1 = 11.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"23 is the fourth term.\", \"remediation\": \"The third term is 11.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2739, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'A movie theater has 240 seats. For a show, 185 adult tickets and some child tickets were sold. All seats were filled. Which equation can be used to find c, the number of child tickets sold?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '240 + 185 = c', '185 - c = 240', 'c = 240 × 185', '185 + c = 240', 'D', 'active', 'The sum of adult tickets (185) and child tickets (c) equals total seats (240): 185 + c = 240.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This adds the total and the part.\", \"remediation\": \"Adult + Child = Total: 185 + c = 240.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Subtracting c from 185 cannot give 240.\", \"remediation\": \"Set up the part-part-whole equation: 185 + c = 240.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This multiplies seats by tickets.\", \"remediation\": \"Use addition for parts of a total: 185 + c = 240.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2740, 6, 2, 8, 'Relationships', 194, 4, 'Elena has 48 photos. This is 6 times as many photos as Marco has. How many photos does Marco have?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '54', '8', '288', '42', 'B', 'active', 'Set up equation: 48 = 6 × m. Divide: m = 48 ÷ 6 = 8 photos.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 48 + 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 48 by 6: Marco has 8 photos.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 48 × 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Elena has MORE photos than Marco. Divide 48 by 6 to find Marco\'s photos: 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 48 - 6.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Times as many\\\" requires division to find the smaller amount: 48 ÷ 6 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2741, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'Solve the equation for k:  (k ÷ 4) + 18 = 25', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '7', '172', '28', '32', 'C', 'active', 'Step 1: k ÷ 4 = 25 - 18 = 7. Step 2: k = 7 × 4 = 28.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"7 is the value of (k ÷ 4), not k itself.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 7 by 4 to solve for k: k = 7 × 4 = 28.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 25 + 18 and multiplied.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 18 first: 25 - 18 = 7, then 7 × 4 = 28.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 7 × 4.\", \"remediation\": \"7 × 4 = 28.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2742, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'Which feature is true of every term in the pattern: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36...?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'Every term is an even number', 'Every term is a prime number', 'Every term is an odd number', 'Every term ends in 6', 'A', 'active', 'Every multiple of 6 is even because 6 is an even number, and any multiple of an even number is always even.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"They have factors 1, 2, 3, 6, so they are composite.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiples of 6 greater than 6 are composite.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"All terms are even.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiples of 6 are all even numbers.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"12, 18, 24, 30 do not end in 6.\", \"remediation\": \"They end in 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 (even digits).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2743, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'A baker has 75 cookies. She wants to pack them into boxes of 6. How many boxes can she fill completely, and how many more cookies does she need to fill one more box?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '12 full boxes; she needs 2 more cookies', '13 full boxes; she needs 3 more cookies', '12 full boxes; she needs 3 more cookies', '11 full boxes; she needs 1 more cookie', 'C', 'active', '75 ÷ 6 = 12 with a remainder of 3 (12 × 6 = 72 cookies packed). To fill the 13th box (needs 6), she needs: 6 - 3 = 3 more cookies.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the remaining needed cookies.\", \"remediation\": \"A box holds 6. Since 3 are left, 6 - 3 = 3 more are needed.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"She cannot fill 13 boxes with only 75 cookies (13 × 6 = 78).\", \"remediation\": \"She can only fill 12 full boxes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"11 × 6 = 66, which is too low.\", \"remediation\": \"75 ÷ 6 = 12 R 3. She fills 12 boxes and needs 3 more cookies.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2744, 6, 2, 8, 'Relationships', 194, 4, 'A zoo has 5 times as many monkeys as lions. There are 24 more monkeys than lions. How many monkeys and lions are there in total?', NULL, 4, 'High', '48', '30', '24', '36', 'D', 'active', 'Let lions = L. Monkeys = 5L. Difference: 5L - L = 4L = 24 -> L = 6 lions. Monkeys = 5 × 6 = 30 monkeys. Total = 6 + 30 = 36 animals.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 24 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Lions = 24 / 4 = 6. Monkeys = 30. Total = 36.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"30 is only the number of monkeys.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the 6 lions to the 30 monkeys: 30 + 6 = 36 total animals.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"24 is the difference between monkeys and lions.\", \"remediation\": \"Find individual amounts: 6 lions and 30 monkeys, total = 36.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2745, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'A concert hall sold 245 tickets for Friday and twice as many for Saturday. If all tickets cost $8 each, what was the total money collected for both nights?', NULL, 4, 'High', '$3,920', '$5,880', '$1,960', '$7,840', 'B', 'active', 'Saturday tickets = 245 × 2 = 490. Total tickets = 245 + 490 = 735. Total money = 735 × $8 = $5,880.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student only calculated Saturday\'s sales (490 × 8 = $3,920).\", \"remediation\": \"Include Friday\'s tickets: (245 + 490) × 8 = 735 × 8 = $5,880.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student only calculated Friday\'s sales (245 × 8 = $1,960).\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate both nights: Friday ($1,960) + Saturday ($3,920) = $5,880.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 10 instead of 8 or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"735 × 8 = $5,880.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2746, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'Which number between 80 and 100 has the GREATEST number of factors?', NULL, 4, 'High', '96 (12 factors)', '84 (12 factors, but smaller than 96)', '81 (5 factors)', '97 (2 factors)', 'A', 'active', 'Factors of 96 are: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 96 (total 12 factors). 97 is prime (2 factors), 81 has 5 factors.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"84 has 12 factors (1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 12, 14, 21, 28, 42, 84), but 96 is the standard composite benchmark.\", \"remediation\": \"96 has 12 factors.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"81 has only 5 factors (1, 3, 9, 27, 81).\", \"remediation\": \"96 has 12 factors, which is significantly more than 81.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"97 is a prime number and has only 2 factors (1 and 97).\", \"remediation\": \"Prime numbers have the fewest factors (only 2).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2747, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'A school has 312 fourth-graders going on a field trip. Each bus can hold 45 students. How many buses are needed so that EVERY student has a seat?', NULL, 4, 'High', '6 buses', '7 buses', '8 buses', '6 buses with 42 students left behind', 'B', 'active', 'Divide 312 ÷ 45 = 6 with a remainder of 42. Since 42 students cannot be left behind, the remainder requires an additional bus: 6 + 1 = 7 buses.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"6 buses only hold 6 × 45 = 270 students, leaving 42 students without a ride.\", \"remediation\": \"In real-world bus problems, round UP to accommodate the remainder: 7 buses.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"8 buses would hold 360 students, which is more than necessary.\", \"remediation\": \"7 buses hold 7 × 45 = 315 seats, which is enough for 312 students.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Students cannot be left behind on a school trip.\", \"remediation\": \"Interpret the remainder by adding 1 more bus: 7 buses.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2748, 6, 2, 8, 'Relationships', 194, 4, 'An artist has 3 containers of beads. Container A has 180 beads. Container B has half as many beads as Container A. Container C has 3 times as many beads as Container B. How many beads are in Container C?', NULL, 4, 'High', '360 beads', '90 beads', '540 beads', '270 beads', 'D', 'active', 'Container B = 180 ÷ 2 = 90 beads. Container C = 90 × 3 = 270 beads.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student doubled Container A.\", \"remediation\": \"Container B = 90, Container C = 90 × 3 = 270.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"90 is Container B, not Container C.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply Container B by 3: 90 × 3 = 270 beads.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied Container A directly by 3 (180 × 3 = 540).\", \"remediation\": \"Container C is 3 times Container B (90), not Container A.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2749, 6, 2, 8, 'Number Patterns', 193, 4, 'Consider the pattern: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, ... What is the rule for this pattern, and what is the 10th term?', NULL, 4, 'High', 'Rule: Square of the term position (n × n); 10th term is 100', 'Rule: Add 3, add 5, add 7; 10th term is 81', 'Rule: Multiply by 4; 10th term is 40', 'Rule: Add 10; 10th term is 100', 'A', 'active', 'These are square numbers: 1² = 1, 2² = 4, 3² = 9... The 10th term is 10² = 10 × 10 = 100.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"81 is the 9th term (9² = 81).\", \"remediation\": \"The 10th term is 10 × 10 = 100.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The pattern does not multiply by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"The pattern is n², so the 10th term is 100.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The difference increases each time, not a constant +10.\", \"remediation\": \"Term n is n²: 10² = 100.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2750, 6, 2, 8, 'Unknowns', 195, 4, 'In the equation 3 × (m + 7) = 48, what is the value of m?', NULL, 4, 'High', '11', '16', '9', '7', 'C', 'active', 'Step 1: Divide both sides by 3: m + 7 = 48 ÷ 3 = 16. Step 2: Subtract 7: m = 16 - 7 = 9.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"3 × (11 + 7) = 3 × 18 = 54.\", \"remediation\": \"48 / 3 = 16; 16 - 7 = 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"16 is the value of (m + 7), not m.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 7 from 16 to find m: m = 16 - 7 = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"3 × (7 + 7) = 3 × 14 = 42, not 48.\", \"remediation\": \"m = 16 - 7 = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2751, 6, 2, 8, 'Relationships', 194, 4, 'A store sold 18 laptops. They sold 3 times as many tablets as laptops, and half as many desktop computers as laptops. How many total devices were sold?', NULL, 4, 'High', '72 devices', '90 devices', '63 devices', '81 devices', 'D', 'active', 'Laptops = 18. Tablets = 18 × 3 = 54. Desktops = 18 ÷ 2 = 9. Total devices = 18 + 54 + 9 = 81 devices.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student omitted desktops (18 + 54 = 72).\", \"remediation\": \"Add desktops: 18 / 2 = 9 desktops, so 72 + 9 = 81.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated the total.\", \"remediation\": \"18 + 54 + 9 = 81.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted desktops instead of adding.\", \"remediation\": \"All devices are added: 18 + 54 + 9 = 81.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:33:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2752, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'What is the value of the numerical expression:  (6 + 4) × 3?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '13', '30', '18', '42', 'B', 'active', 'Evaluate operations inside parentheses first: 6 + 4 = 10. Then multiply: 10 × 3 = 30.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added all numbers: 6 + 4 + 3 = 13.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow the multiplication symbol: (6 + 4) × 3 = 30.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4 × 3 = 12 first and added 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Operations inside parentheses must always be performed first: (6 + 4) = 10, then 10 × 3 = 30.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"10 × 3 = 30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2753, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Which expression matches the phrase: \"multiply 7 by 4, then add 5\"?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '7 × (4 + 5)', '7 + 4 + 5', '(7 × 4) + 5', '(7 + 5) × 4', 'C', 'active', 'First multiply 7 and 4, then add 5 to that product: (7 × 4) + 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This expression adds 4 and 5 first, then multiplies by 7.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Multiply 7 by 4, then add 5\\\" requires (7 × 4) + 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"This expression only adds the numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Use multiplication for the first step: (7 × 4) + 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This adds 7 and 5 first.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow the order of the sentence: 7 × 4 first, then + 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2754, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Evaluate the expression:  20 - 8 ÷ 2', NULL, 5, 'Low', '16', '14', '6', '10', 'A', 'active', 'According to the order of operations, division comes before subtraction: 8 ÷ 2 = 4. Then 20 - 4 = 16.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"20 - 4 = 16.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 20 - 8 = 12 first, then divided 12 ÷ 2 = 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Perform division before subtraction: 8 ÷ 2 = 4, then 20 - 4 = 16.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student guessed 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Division has higher precedence: 20 - (8 ÷ 2) = 16.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2755, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns & Relationships', 197, 5, 'Pattern A starts at 0 and adds 2: 0, 2, 4, 6... Pattern B starts at 0 and adds 6: 0, 6, 12, 18... How do the terms in Pattern B compare to Pattern A?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'Each term in Pattern B is 4 more than Pattern A', 'Each term in Pattern B is half of Pattern A', 'Each term in Pattern B is 3 times the corresponding term in Pattern A', 'There is no relationship between the terms', 'C', 'active', 'Comparing corresponding terms: 6 = 3 × 2, 12 = 3 × 4, 18 = 3 × 6. Each term in Pattern B is 3 times the corresponding term in Pattern A.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 6 - 2 = 4, but that difference changes for other terms (12 - 4 = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"Notice the multiplicative ratio: 6 ÷ 2 = 3, 12 ÷ 4 = 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Pattern B has larger numbers, not half.\", \"remediation\": \"Pattern B terms are 3 times larger than Pattern A.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A consistent multiplicative pattern exists.\", \"remediation\": \"The ratio of rules is 6 / 2 = 3 times.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2756, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Without calculating, how does the value of 5 × (240 + 68) compare to the value of (240 + 68)?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'It has the same value', 'It is 5 more', 'It is one-fifth as large', 'It is 5 times as large', 'D', 'active', 'Multiplying any non-zero quantity by 5 makes the result 5 times as large.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Multiplying by 5 changes the value.\", \"remediation\": \"The factor 5 scales the value 5 times.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student confused multiplying by 5 with adding 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplying by 5 means 5 times as large, not 5 more.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Multiplying by 5 increases the value, not decreases.\", \"remediation\": \"5 × quantity is 5 times larger.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2757, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns & Relationships', 197, 5, 'Given the ordered pairs (1, 3), (2, 6), (3, 9), and (4, 12), what is the relationship between the x-coordinate and the y-coordinate?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'y is 2 more than x (y = x + 2)', 'y is 3 times x (y = 3x)', 'x is 3 times y', 'y is 4 times x', 'B', 'active', 'For each pair: 1 × 3 = 3, 2 × 3 = 6, 3 × 3 = 9, 4 × 3 = 12. So y = 3x.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"1 + 2 = 3, but 2 + 2 = 4 (not 6).\", \"remediation\": \"The rule must work for every pair: multiply x by 3 to get y.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"y is larger than x, not smaller.\", \"remediation\": \"y is 3 times x.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"1 × 4 = 4, not 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply x by 3: y = 3x.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2758, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'What is the value of:  15 - (3 + 2 × 4)?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '4', '2', '48', '8', 'A', 'active', 'Inside parentheses, multiply first: 2 × 4 = 8. Add: 3 + 8 = 11. Finally subtract: 15 - 11 = 4.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"15 - 11 = 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 2 = 5 first and multiplied 5 × 4 = 20, then subtracted.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply before adding inside parentheses: 2 × 4 = 8, then 3 + 8 = 11. 15 - 11 = 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"15 - 11 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2759, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Which statement correctly describes the expression 4 × (18 - 6)?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'The sum of 4 and 18, minus 6', '4 times the difference of 18 and 6', '18 minus the product of 4 and 6', 'The difference of 4 times 18 and 6', 'B', 'active', 'The parentheses enclose (18 - 6), which is the difference of 18 and 6. Multiplying by 4 gives: 4 times the difference of 18 and 6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This describes (4 + 18) - 6.\", \"remediation\": \"4 is multiplied by the entire difference (18 - 6).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This describes 18 - (4 × 6).\", \"remediation\": \"4 multiplies the difference: 4 × (18 - 6).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This describes (4 × 18) - 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Parentheses around (18 - 6) mean subtract first, then multiply by 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2760, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Evaluate the expression with brackets and parentheses:  2 × [15 - (3 + 4)]', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '8', '24', '14', '16', 'D', 'active', 'Innermost parentheses: 3 + 4 = 7. Brackets: 15 - 7 = 8. Multiply: 2 × 8 = 16.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply by 2 at the end.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the outside multiplier: 2 × 8 = 16.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 15 - 3 = 12 first and added 4 = 16, then... miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Evaluate inner parentheses (3 + 4 = 7) first: 15 - 7 = 8, then 2 × 8 = 16.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"2 × [15 - 7] = 2 × 8 = 16.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2761, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Which expression represents: \"subtract 6 from 20, divide by 2, then add 9\"?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '[(20 - 6) ÷ 2] + 9', '(20 - 6) ÷ (2 + 9)', '20 - (6 ÷ 2) + 9', '20 - [6 ÷ (2 + 9)]', 'A', 'active', 'Subtract 6 from 20: (20 - 6). Divide by 2: [(20 - 6) ÷ 2]. Then add 9: [(20 - 6) ÷ 2] + 9.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"This divides by (2 + 9) = 11.\", \"remediation\": \"The addition of 9 happens after the division: [(20 - 6) ÷ 2] + 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This divides 6 by 2 before subtracting from 20.\", \"remediation\": \"The phrase specifies \\\"subtract 6 from 20\\\" first.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This groups operations incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow the steps in chronological order: (20 - 6) ÷ 2 + 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2762, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns & Relationships', 197, 5, 'Sequence X starts at 1 and uses rule \"add 3\". Sequence Y starts at 2 and uses rule \"add 6\". What is the 4th ordered pair (x, y)?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '(10, 18)', '(7, 14)', '(10, 20)', '(13, 26)', 'C', 'active', 'Sequence X: 1, 4, 7, 10 (4th term is 10). Sequence Y: 2, 8, 14, 20 (4th term is 20). 4th ordered pair: (10, 20).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4 to Y instead of 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Sequence Y: 2 + 6 = 8, 8 + 6 = 14, 14 + 6 = 20.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"(7, 14) is the 3rd ordered pair.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the 4th term of each sequence: X = 10, Y = 20.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"(13, 26) is the 5th ordered pair.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate the 4th term: X: 1, 4, 7, 10 and Y: 2, 8, 14, 20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2763, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Evaluate:  3 × [4 + 2 × (8 - 3)]', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '34', '90', '45', '42', 'D', 'active', 'Innermost: 8 - 3 = 5. Inside brackets: 2 × 5 = 10, then 4 + 10 = 14. Outside: 3 × 14 = 42.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4 + 2 = 6 first and multiplied 6 × 5 = 30, then 30 + ?.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplication inside brackets comes before addition: 2 × 5 = 10, then 4 + 10 = 14. 3 × 14 = 42.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied (4 + 2) × (8 - 3) = 6 × 5 = 30, 30 × 3 = 90.\", \"remediation\": \"Order of operations: 2 × 5 = 10; 4 + 10 = 14; 3 × 14 = 42.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 14 = 42.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2764, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Where should parentheses be placed to make this equation true?  18 - 4 × 3 + 2 = 44', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '18 - (4 × 3 + 2)', '(18 - 4) × 3 + 2', '18 - (4 × 3) + 2', '(18 - 4) × (3 + 2)', 'B', 'active', 'Check (18 - 4) × 3 + 2: 18 - 4 = 14. 14 × 3 = 42. 42 + 2 = 44. True!', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"18 - 14 = 4, not 44.\", \"remediation\": \"(18 - 4) × 3 + 2 = 44.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"18 - 12 + 2 = 8, not 44.\", \"remediation\": \"Test (18 - 4) × 3 + 2: 14 × 3 = 42, 42 + 2 = 44.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"(18 - 4) × (3 + 2) = 14 × 5 = 70.\", \"remediation\": \"Only placing parentheses around (18 - 4) yields 44.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2765, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns & Relationships', 197, 5, 'Points A(2, 6), B(4, 12), and C(6, 18) are plotted on a coordinate grid. If a line is drawn through these points, what will be the y-coordinate when x = 10?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '24', '20', '30', '36', 'C', 'active', 'The rule is y = 3x (since 6 = 3×2, 12 = 3×4, 18 = 3×6). When x = 10, y = 3 × 10 = 30.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated for x = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply x by 3: y = 3 × 10 = 30.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 10 + 10.\", \"remediation\": \"The relationship is multiplicative: y = 3x, so y = 30.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated for x = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"When x = 10, y = 3 × 10 = 30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2766, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Compare Expression P:  (45 + 55) ÷ 5   and Expression Q:  (45 ÷ 5) + (55 ÷ 5). Which statement is true?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'Both expressions are equal to 20', 'Expression P equals 100 and Expression Q equals 20', 'Expression P is larger than Expression Q', 'Expression Q is larger than Expression P', 'A', 'active', 'Expression P: 100 ÷ 5 = 20. Expression Q: 9 + 11 = 20. By the distributive property of division over addition, they are equal.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Expression P includes division by 5: 100 / 5 = 20.\", \"remediation\": \"Evaluate fully: 100 ÷ 5 = 20.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Both equal 20.\", \"remediation\": \"Division distributes over addition: (a + b) / c = a/c + b/c.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Both equal 20.\", \"remediation\": \"100 / 5 = 20 and 9 + 11 = 20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2767, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Evaluate:  {5 + [4 × (6 - 2)]} ÷ 3', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '9', '6', '7', '8', 'C', 'active', 'Parentheses: 6 - 2 = 4. Brackets: 4 × 4 = 16. Braces: 5 + 16 = 21. Division: 21 ÷ 3 = 7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error inside brackets.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow nesting order: (6 - 2) = 4, [4 × 4] = 16, {5 + 16} = 21, 21 ÷ 3 = 7.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 1.\", \"remediation\": \"21 ÷ 3 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 21 ÷ 3.\", \"remediation\": \"21 ÷ 3 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2768, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns & Relationships', 197, 5, 'Two friends start running laps. Adam runs 3 laps each day. Ben runs 5 laps each day. After how many days will Ben have run 14 MORE laps than Adam?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '14 days', '5 days', '6 days', '7 days', 'D', 'active', 'Each day, Ben runs 5 - 3 = 2 more laps than Adam. To gain 14 more laps: 14 ÷ 2 = 7 days.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student took 14 days without dividing by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Daily gain is 2 laps: 14 ÷ 2 = 7 days.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"In 5 days, difference is 5 × 2 = 10 laps.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the desired total difference (14) by daily difference (2): 14 ÷ 2 = 7 days.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"In 6 days, difference is 12 laps.\", \"remediation\": \"14 ÷ 2 = 7 days.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2769, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Evaluate the complex expression:  4 × {18 - [2 × (3 + 1)]} + 12 ÷ 3', NULL, 5, 'High', '40', '44', '52', '48', 'B', 'active', 'Innermost: 3 + 1 = 4. Next: 2 × 4 = 8. Braces: 18 - 8 = 10. Multiply: 4 × 10 = 40. Divide: 12 ÷ 3 = 4. Add: 40 + 4 = 44.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add 12 ÷ 3 = 4 at the end.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 4 to 40: 40 + 4 = 44.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added before dividing (40 + 12) ÷ 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Division has higher precedence than addition: 12 ÷ 3 = 4, then 40 + 4 = 44.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error in the brackets.\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 10 + 4 = 44.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2770, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'A company produces 15 boxes in the morning and 25 boxes in the afternoon. Each box holds 12 items. At the end of the day, 40 items are damaged and discarded. Which expression gives the number of usable items?', NULL, 5, 'High', '[(15 + 25) × 12] - 40', '[(15 × 25) + 12] - 40', '(15 + 25 × 12) - 40', '15 + (25 × 12) - 40', 'A', 'active', 'Total boxes = (15 + 25) = 40. Total items = 40 × 12 = 480. Usable items = 480 - 40 = 440. The expression is [(15 + 25) × 12] - 40.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"This multiplies boxes together.\", \"remediation\": \"Boxes are added, not multiplied: (15 + 25) × 12 - 40.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Without parentheses around (15 + 25), 25 × 12 would be multiplied first.\", \"remediation\": \"Add morning and afternoon boxes first: [(15 + 25) × 12] - 40.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This leaves 15 boxes unmultiplied by 12.\", \"remediation\": \"All boxes contain 12 items: (15 + 25) × 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2771, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns & Relationships', 197, 5, 'Rule A starts at 0 and adds 4: 0, 4, 8, 12, ... Rule B starts at 0 and adds 10: 0, 10, 20, 30, ... If a term in Rule A is 48, what is the corresponding term in Rule B?', NULL, 5, 'High', '100', '120', '140', '480', 'B', 'active', 'Term position in Rule A: 48 ÷ 4 = 12th step. Corresponding term in Rule B: 12 × 10 = 120 (or ratio is 10/4 = 2.5: 48 × 2.5 = 120).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"100 corresponds to 10 × 10 (when A = 40).\", \"remediation\": \"48 is the 12th multiple of 4. The 12th multiple of 10 is 120.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"(48 ÷ 4) × 10 = 12 × 10 = 120.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 48 × 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Scale by ratio 10/4 = 2.5: 48 × 2.5 = 120.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2772, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Which expression has a value of 10?', NULL, 5, 'High', '30 ÷ (2 + 4) + 3', '30 - 2 + 4 × 3 ÷ 2', '(30 - 2) + 4 × 3', '[30 - (2 + 4) × 3] ÷ 1.2', 'D', 'active', 'Inside brackets: 2 + 4 = 6. 6 × 3 = 18. 30 - 18 = 12. Then 12 ÷ 1.2 = 10. (Alternatively in whole numbers: [30 - (2 + 4) × 3] + 2 = 12 - 2 = 10).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"30 ÷ 6 + 3 = 5 + 3 = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Option A evaluates to 10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"30 - 2 + 6 = 34.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate carefully with brackets to reach 10.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"28 + 12 = 40.\", \"remediation\": \"Evaluate operations step by step.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2773, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns & Relationships', 197, 5, 'A sequence of rectangles has length L and width W. The length starts at 2 and increases by 3 each step (2, 5, 8, 11...). The width starts at 1 and increases by 2 each step (1, 3, 5, 7...). What is the AREA of the 4th rectangle in the sequence?', NULL, 5, 'High', '77 square units', '64 square units', '88 square units', '56 square units', 'A', 'active', '4th length: 2, 5, 8, 11 (L = 11). 4th width: 1, 3, 5, 7 (W = 7). Area = L × W = 11 × 7 = 77 square units.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 8 × 8.\", \"remediation\": \"4th length is 11, 4th width is 7. Area = 11 × 7 = 77.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 11 × 8.\", \"remediation\": \"The 4th width is 1 + 3(2) = 7: 11 × 7 = 77.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 3rd area (8 × 5 = 40) or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"11 × 7 = 77 square units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2774, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'If A = 3 × (12 + 8) and B = 2 × [15 + (10 - 2)], what is the difference (A - B)?', NULL, 5, 'High', '16', '60', '14', '46', 'C', 'active', 'A = 3 × 20 = 60. B = 2 × [15 + 8] = 2 × 23 = 46. Difference = A - B = 60 - 46 = 14.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error in subtracting 60 - 46.\", \"remediation\": \"60 - 46 = 14.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"60 is the value of A.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract B from A: 60 - 46 = 14.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"46 is the value of B.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate A - B = 60 - 46 = 14.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2775, 6, 2, 8, 'Patterns & Relationships', 197, 5, 'Pattern 1 follows y = 2x + 1. Which set of ordered pairs correctly represents this pattern for x = 0, 1, 2, 3?', NULL, 5, 'High', '(0, 0), (1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6)', '(0, 3), (1, 5), (2, 7), (3, 9)', '(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4)', '(0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7)', 'D', 'active', 'When x=0, y=1. When x=1, y=2(1)+1=3. When x=2, y=2(2)+1=5. When x=3, y=2(3)+1=7. Points: (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This represents y = 2x without adding 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 1 after multiplying by 2: 2(0)+1 = 1, 2(1)+1 = 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"This starts at y = 3.\", \"remediation\": \"At x = 0, y = 2(0) + 1 = 1.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This represents y = x + 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply x by 2 first, then add 1: (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2776, 6, 2, 8, 'Numerical Expressions', 196, 5, 'Two expressions are given:  X = 8 × (250 + 75)   and   Y = 2 × (250 + 75). Without evaluating the sums, how many times larger is X than Y?', NULL, 5, 'High', '2 times larger', '4 times larger', '6 times larger', '8 times larger', 'B', 'active', 'Both expressions have the identical factor (250 + 75). Comparing the outside factors: 8 ÷ 2 = 4. Therefore, X is 4 times larger than Y.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student picked the factor 2 from Y.\", \"remediation\": \"8 is 4 times larger than 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 8 - 2 = 6.\", \"remediation\": \"To find how many \\\"times larger\\\", divide the multipliers: 8 ÷ 2 = 4 times.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student picked the factor 8 from X.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare the ratio of the factors: 8 / 2 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2777, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'What is the value of 3⁴?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '12', '64', '81', '27', 'C', 'active', '3⁴ means 3 multiplied by itself 4 times: 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 9 × 9 = 81.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied base by exponent: 3 × 4 = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"An exponent indicates repeated multiplication: 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 81, not 3 × 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 4³ = 64.\", \"remediation\": \"The base is 3 and exponent is 4: 3⁴ = 81.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 3³ = 27.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by 3 one more time: 27 × 3 = 81.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2778, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'In the algebraic expression 7x + 5, what is the COEFFICIENT of x?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '7', '12', '5', 'x', 'A', 'active', 'The coefficient is the numerical factor multiplied by the variable. Here, 7 is the coefficient of x.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 7 + 5.\", \"remediation\": \"The coefficient of x is 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"5 is the constant term, not the coefficient.\", \"remediation\": \"The coefficient is the number multiplying the variable: 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"x is the variable, not the coefficient.\", \"remediation\": \"The coefficient is the numerical factor in front of the variable (7).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2779, 6, 2, 8, 'One-Variable Equations', 199, 6, 'Solve for x:  x + 9 = 23', NULL, 6, 'Low', '32', '12', '14', '15', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract 9 from both sides: x = 23 - 9 = 14.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 23 + 9.\", \"remediation\": \"To isolate x, perform the inverse operation: subtract 9 from 23 to get 14.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"23 - 9 = 14.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscounted.\", \"remediation\": \"23 - 9 = 14.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:09', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2780, 6, 2, 8, 'One-Variable Equations', 199, 6, 'Solve for m:  4m = 36', NULL, 6, 'Low', '144', '32', '40', '9', 'D', 'active', 'Divide both sides by 4: m = 36 ÷ 4 = 9.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 36 × 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide both sides by 4 to solve for m: m = 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 36 - 4.\", \"remediation\": \"4m means 4 times m. The inverse operation is division: 36 ÷ 4 = 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 36 + 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 36 by 4: m = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2781, 6, 2, 8, 'Inequalities', 200, 6, 'Which inequality represents the statement: \"The temperature t is greater than 32 degrees\"?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 't < 32', 't > 32', 't ≥ 32', 't ≤ 32', 'B', 'active', '\"Greater than\" is represented by the strict inequality symbol >: t > 32.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This represents \\\"less than\\\".\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Greater than\\\" uses the > symbol: t > 32.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This represents \\\"greater than or equal to\\\".\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Greater than\\\" means strictly greater (>).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This represents \\\"less than or equal to\\\".\", \"remediation\": \"Use > for greater than: t > 32.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2782, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'Evaluate the expression 2y + 3 when y = 5.', NULL, 6, 'Low', '13', '15', '28', '10', 'A', 'active', 'Substitute 5 for y: 2(5) + 3 = 10 + 3 = 13.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 5 + 3 = 8, then multiplied by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply before adding: 2 × 5 = 10, then + 3 = 13.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student concatenated 2 and 5 to make 25, then added 3 = 28.\", \"remediation\": \"2y means 2 multiplied by y: 2 × 5 = 10, then 10 + 3 = 13.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add 3.\", \"remediation\": \"2(5) = 10, then add 3: 10 + 3 = 13.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2783, 6, 2, 8, 'Dependent & Independent Variables', 201, 6, 'In the equation d = 50t, where d is total distance traveled and t is time in hours, which variable is the INDEPENDENT variable?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'd (distance)', 't (time)', '50', 'Both d and t', 'B', 'active', 'Time t is the independent variable (input) because distance depends on how many hours you travel.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Distance d is the dependent variable.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance depends on time, so time t is independent and distance d is dependent.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"50 is a constant rate, not a variable.\", \"remediation\": \"The independent variable is t.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"One is independent and one is dependent.\", \"remediation\": \"t is the independent input variable.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2784, 6, 2, 8, 'Inequalities', 200, 6, 'Which number is a solution to the inequality:  x - 3 < 7?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '15', '10', '12', '8', 'D', 'active', 'Add 3 to both sides: x < 10. Among the choices, 8 is the only number strictly less than 10 (8 - 3 = 5 < 7).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"15 - 3 = 12 > 7.\", \"remediation\": \"8 is less than 10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"10 - 3 = 7, which is equal to 7, not strictly less than 7.\", \"remediation\": \"The inequality is strict (<), so x must be less than 10.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"12 - 3 = 9, which is greater than 7.\", \"remediation\": \"Substitute and check: 8 - 3 = 5 < 7 is true.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2785, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'Simplify the expression by combining like terms:  4x + 7 + 3x - 2', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '7x + 5', '12x + 5', '7x + 9', '12x', 'A', 'active', 'Combine x-terms: 4x + 3x = 7x. Combine constant terms: 7 - 2 = 5. Result: 7x + 5.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4 × 3 = 12x.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the coefficients of like terms: 4x + 3x = 7x.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 7 + 2 = 9 instead of subtracting 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Notice the minus sign in front of 2: 7 - 2 = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student combined unlike terms (7x + 5 = 12x).\", \"remediation\": \"Variables and constants cannot be combined into a single term: 7x + 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2786, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'Which expression is equivalent to 3(4x + 5) using the distributive property?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '12x + 8', '12x + 5', '12x + 15', '7x + 8', 'C', 'active', 'Distribute 3 to both terms inside the parentheses: 3 × 4x + 3 × 5 = 12x + 15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 5 = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 5 = 15, so 12x + 15.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply 3 by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 3 by both terms: 3 × 4x = 12x, and 3 × 5 = 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3 + 4 = 7 and 3 + 5 = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"The distributive property requires multiplication: 3 × 4x = 12x, 3 × 5 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2787, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'Which expression is the factored form of 18x + 24 using the greatest common factor (GCF)?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '3(6x + 8)', '2(9x + 12)', '6(3x + 24)', '6(3x + 4)', 'D', 'active', 'The GCF of 18 and 24 is 6. Factoring out 6 gives: 18x ÷ 6 = 3x and 24 ÷ 6 = 4. Form: 6(3x + 4).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"3 is a common factor, but not the GREATEST common factor.\", \"remediation\": \"Factor out the GCF (6) to completely factor: 6(3x + 4).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"2 is not the greatest common factor.\", \"remediation\": \"The GCF of 18 and 24 is 6: 6(3x + 4).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to divide 24 by 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide both terms by 6: 24 ÷ 6 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2788, 6, 2, 8, 'One-Variable Equations', 199, 6, 'Solve for w:  w / 6 = 4.5', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '0.75', '27', '24', '10.5', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply both sides by 6: w = 4.5 × 6 = 27.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 4.5 ÷ 6 = 0.75.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply both sides by 6 to isolate w: w = 27.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 4 × 6 = 24 and omitted the 0.5.\", \"remediation\": \"4.5 × 6 = 27.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4.5 + 6 = 10.5.\", \"remediation\": \"The inverse of division is multiplication: w = 4.5 × 6 = 27.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2789, 6, 2, 8, 'Inequalities', 200, 6, 'On a number line, which graph correctly represents the inequality  x ≤ -2?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', 'An open circle at -2 with an arrow pointing to the left', 'A closed circle at -2 with an arrow pointing to the right', 'A closed circle at -2 with an arrow pointing to the left', 'An open circle at -2 with an arrow pointing to the right', 'C', 'active', 'The \"≤\" symbol includes the endpoint (closed solid circle) and means values less than -2 (arrow points left).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"An open circle is used for strict inequalities (< or >).\", \"remediation\": \"≤ includes the equal sign, so use a closed/solid circle.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Pointing right represents greater than (≥).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Less than\\\" points to the left towards smaller numbers.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This represents x > -2.\", \"remediation\": \"x ≤ -2 has a closed circle and points left.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2790, 6, 2, 8, 'One-Variable Equations', 199, 6, 'A rectangular garden has an area of 84 square meters. The length of the garden is 12 meters. What is the width w of the garden?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '7 meters', '96 meters', '6 meters', '72 meters', 'A', 'active', 'Area = length × width: 84 = 12 × w. Divide by 12: w = 84 ÷ 12 = 7 meters.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 84 + 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide area by length: 84 ÷ 12 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"12 × 6 = 72, not 84.\", \"remediation\": \"84 ÷ 12 = 7 meters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 84 - 12.\", \"remediation\": \"To find width from area, divide: 84 ÷ 12 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2791, 6, 2, 8, 'Dependent & Independent Variables', 201, 6, 'A movie streaming service charges a $10 monthly base fee plus $3 for each premium movie rented (m). Which equation gives the total monthly cost C?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', 'C = 10m + 3', 'C = 13m', 'C = 3m + 10', 'C = (m + 3) × 10', 'C', 'active', 'The variable cost is $3 per movie (3m) and the flat base fee is $10: C = 3m + 10.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student switched the rate and the flat fee.\", \"remediation\": \"$3 is multiplied by the number of movies m, and $10 is added once: C = 3m + 10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 10 + 3 = 13 and multiplied by m.\", \"remediation\": \"The $10 fee is a one-time flat charge, not per movie: C = 3m + 10.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student set up the operations incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Cost = 3m + 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2792, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'Evaluate the expression  a² + 3b - c  when a = 4, b = 5, and c = 7.', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '26', '16', '32', '24', 'D', 'active', 'Substitute: 4² + 3(5) - 7 = 16 + 15 - 7 = 31 - 7 = 24.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"16 + 15 - 7 = 24.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student calculated 4 × 2 instead of 4² (8 + 15 - 7 = 16).\", \"remediation\": \"4² means 4 × 4 = 16. Then 16 + 15 - 7 = 24.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 7 instead of subtracting 7: 31 + 7 = 38.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract c: 31 - 7 = 24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2793, 6, 2, 8, 'Inequalities', 200, 6, 'A roller coaster requires passengers to be at least 48 inches tall to ride. If h represents height in inches, which inequality represents this rule?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', 'h > 48', 'h ≥ 48', 'h ≤ 48', 'h = 48', 'B', 'active', '\"At least 48\" means 48 inches or taller, which is represented by h ≥ 48.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"\\\"At least\\\" includes 48, so it cannot be strictly greater than.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"At least\\\" means greater than or equal to: h ≥ 48.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This means 48 or shorter.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"At least\\\" sets a minimum, so height must be ≥ 48.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Passengers can be taller than 48 as well.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the inequality h ≥ 48.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2794, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'Which two expressions are EQUIVALENT for all positive values of x?', NULL, 6, 'High', '5(2x + 3) - 4x   and   6x + 15', '4(2x - 1)   and   8x - 1', '3(x + 4)   and   3x + 4', '2x + 3x   and   5x²', 'A', 'active', 'Expand 5(2x + 3) - 4x = 10x + 15 - 4x = 6x + 15. This is identical to 6x + 15.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"4(2x - 1) expands to 8x - 4, not 8x - 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 4 by -1 to get -4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"3(x + 4) expands to 3x + 12, not 3x + 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Distribute 3 to both terms: 3 × 4 = 12.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"2x + 3x = 5x, not 5x².\", \"remediation\": \"When adding like terms, add coefficients: 2 + 3 = 5, keep variable x.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2795, 6, 2, 8, 'One-Variable Equations', 199, 6, 'Solve the equation for x:  (2/3)x = 18', NULL, 6, 'High', '12', '27', '54', '9', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply both sides by the reciprocal of 2/3, which is 3/2: x = 18 × (3/2) = (18 ÷ 2) × 3 = 9 × 3 = 27.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 18 by 2/3 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by the reciprocal (3/2): 18 × 3/2 = 27.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 18 × 3 without dividing by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by 2 after multiplying by 3: 54 / 2 = 27.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 18 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"x = 18 × (3/2) = 27.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2796, 6, 2, 8, 'Inequalities', 200, 6, 'A delivery truck can carry at most 2,500 pounds. It is already loaded with 1,350 pounds of cargo. The driver wants to load crates weighing 50 pounds each. What is the maximum number of crates (c) the driver can load?', NULL, 6, 'High', '50 crates', '25 crates', '22 crates', '23 crates', 'D', 'active', 'Inequality: 1350 + 50c ≤ 2500. Subtract 1350: 50c ≤ 1150. Divide by 50: c ≤ 23 crates.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"50 crates would weigh 2,500 pounds alone.\", \"remediation\": \"Account for the existing 1,350 lbs: (2500 - 1350) / 50 = 23.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"50 × 25 = 1250, and 1350 + 1250 = 2600 > 2500 (overweight).\", \"remediation\": \"1150 ÷ 50 = 23 crates maximum.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"22 crates weighs 1100 lbs, but 23 crates can still fit (1150 lbs).\", \"remediation\": \"50 × 23 = 1150, which matches the remaining capacity exactly.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2797, 6, 2, 8, 'Dependent & Independent Variables', 201, 6, 'A car rental company uses the formula C = 0.25m + 35 to calculate total charge C for driving m miles. If a customer has a budget of $85, what is the maximum number of miles they can drive?', NULL, 6, 'High', '200 miles', '340 miles', '140 miles', '480 miles', 'A', 'active', 'Set up inequality: 0.25m + 35 ≤ 85. Subtract 35: 0.25m ≤ 50. Divide by 0.25 (multiply by 4): m ≤ 200 miles.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 85 by 0.25 without subtracting the $35 base fee.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the $35 flat fee first: 85 - 35 = 50. Then 50 / 0.25 = 200 miles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 50 - 35.\", \"remediation\": \"0.25m = 50, so m = 50 × 4 = 200 miles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"50 ÷ 0.25 = 200 miles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2798, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'The perimeter of an equilateral triangle is represented by the expression 6x + 15. What is the length of ONE side of the triangle?', NULL, 6, 'High', '18x + 45', '3x + 5', '2x + 5', '2x + 15', 'C', 'active', 'An equilateral triangle has 3 equal sides. Divide perimeter by 3: (6x + 15) ÷ 3 = (6x/3) + (15/3) = 2x + 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied by 3 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is divided by 3 to find one side: 2x + 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 6 by 2 instead of 3.\", \"remediation\": \"A triangle has 3 sides: divide both terms by 3: 6x ÷ 3 = 2x, 15 ÷ 3 = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student divided only the variable term by 3 and left 15 unchanged.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide all terms by 3: (6x + 15) ÷ 3 = 2x + 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2799, 6, 2, 8, 'One-Variable Equations', 199, 6, 'Solve for p:  3.4 + p = 9.15', NULL, 6, 'High', '6.75', '12.55', '5.25', '5.75', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract: p = 9.15 - 3.40 = 5.75.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to regroup across the whole number.\", \"remediation\": \"9.15 - 3.40 = 5.75.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 9.15 + 3.4.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 3.4 from 9.15 to isolate p: p = 5.75.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 3.9 instead of 3.4.\", \"remediation\": \"9.15 - 3.40 = 5.75.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2800, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 6, 'Evaluate  x³ - (2y + z²)  when x = 3, y = 4, and z = 2.', NULL, 6, 'High', '11', '15', '19', '9', 'B', 'active', 'x³ = 3³ = 27. Inside parentheses: 2(4) + 2² = 8 + 4 = 12. Evaluate: 27 - 12 = 15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"27 - 12 = 15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student evaluated 2² as 2 × 2 = 4, but subtracted only 8: 27 - 8 = 19.\", \"remediation\": \"Both terms inside parentheses are subtracted: 27 - (8 + 4) = 27 - 12 = 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student evaluated 3³ as 3 × 3 = 9.\", \"remediation\": \"3³ = 3 × 3 × 3 = 27. Then 27 - 12 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2801, 6, 2, 8, 'Dependent & Independent Variables', 201, 6, 'A table shows values: (x=2, y=7), (x=4, y=13), (x=6, y=19). Which equation models this relationship, and what is y when x = 10?', NULL, 6, 'High', 'y = 4x - 1; when x = 10, y = 39', 'y = 3x + 1; when x = 10, y = 30', 'y = 3x + 1; when x = 10, y = 31', 'y = 2x + 3; when x = 10, y = 23', 'C', 'active', 'Check rate of change: (13 - 7) / (4 - 2) = 6 / 2 = 3. Equation: y = 3x + b. Using (2, 7): 7 = 3(2) + b -> b = 1. So y = 3x + 1. When x = 10: y = 3(10) + 1 = 31.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Testing (2, 7): 4(2) - 1 = 7, but for x=4: 4(4) - 1 = 15 (not 13).\", \"remediation\": \"The constant rate of change is 3, giving y = 3x + 1. At x=10, y = 31.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add 1 when evaluating x = 10.\", \"remediation\": \"3(10) + 1 = 31.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Testing x=2: 2(2) + 3 = 7, but for x=4: 2(4) + 3 = 11 (not 13).\", \"remediation\": \"The correct linear rule is y = 3x + 1.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:35:10', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2802, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'Simplify the linear expression:  (3x + 5) + (2x - 1)', NULL, 7, 'Low', '5x + 4', '5x - 4', '5x + 6', '6x + 4', 'A', 'active', 'Combine like terms: 3x + 2x = 5x, and 5 + (-1) = 5 - 1 = 4. The simplified expression is 5x + 4.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student used a minus sign for 4.\", \"remediation\": \"5 - 1 = +4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 5 + 1 instead of 5 - 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Pay close attention to signs: 5 + (-1) = 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied 3 × 2 = 6x.\", \"remediation\": \"When adding polynomials, add coefficients: 3 + 2 = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2803, 6, 2, 8, 'Linear Equations', 203, 7, 'Solve the two-step equation:  3x + 7 = 22', NULL, 7, 'Low', '10', '15', '5', '9', 'C', 'active', 'Step 1: Subtract 7 from both sides: 3x = 22 - 7 = 15. Step 2: Divide by 3: x = 15 ÷ 3 = 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 7 instead of subtracting (3x = 29 -> ~10).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 7 first: 22 - 7 = 15, then divide by 3: x = 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"15 is the value of 3x, not x.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 15 by 3 to isolate x: x = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"3x = 15, so x = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2804, 6, 2, 8, 'Word Problems & Applications', 205, 7, 'Solve the inequality:  x + 8 > 15', NULL, 7, 'Low', 'x ≥ 7', 'x < 7', 'x > 23', 'x > 7', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract 8 from both sides: x > 15 - 8 -> x > 7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student changed > to ≥.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the original strict inequality: x > 7.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student flipped the inequality sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtracting a number from both sides does not flip the inequality sign: x > 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student added 15 + 8 = 23.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 8 from 15: 15 - 8 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2805, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'Expand the expression using the distributive property:  -4(2x - 3)', NULL, 7, 'Low', '-8x - 12', '-8x + 12', '-8x - 3', '8x - 12', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply -4 by both terms: (-4)(2x) + (-4)(-3) = -8x + 12.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied -4 by -3 and wrote -12.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative times negative is positive: (-4) × (-3) = +12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply -4 by -3.\", \"remediation\": \"Distribute -4 to both terms: -4 × 2x = -8x, and -4 × -3 = +12.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot the negative sign on the first term.\", \"remediation\": \"-4 × 2x = -8x.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2806, 6, 2, 8, 'Linear Equations', 203, 7, 'Solve for y:  2(y + 4) = 18', NULL, 7, 'Low', '5', '13', '7', '9', 'A', 'active', 'Divide by 2: y + 4 = 9. Subtract 4: y = 9 - 4 = 5. (Or expand: 2y + 8 = 18 -> 2y = 10 -> y = 5).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 18 - 5.\", \"remediation\": \"y + 4 = 9, so y = 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"2y + 8 = 18 -> 2y = 10 -> y = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"9 is the value of y + 4, not y.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 4 from 9: y = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2807, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'Factor the linear expression:  14x - 21', NULL, 7, 'Low', '7(2x + 3)', '7(2x - 3)', '14(x - 3)', '7(2x - 21)', 'B', 'active', 'The GCF of 14 and 21 is 7. Factoring out 7: 14x ÷ 7 = 2x, -21 ÷ 7 = -3. Result: 7(2x - 3).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student changed the minus sign to plus.\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the original minus sign: 7(2x - 3).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"14 is not a factor of 21.\", \"remediation\": \"The greatest common factor is 7: 7(2x - 3).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to divide 21 by 7.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide both terms by 7: 21 ÷ 7 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2808, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Variable Equations', 204, 7, 'A taxi charges a flat fee of $3.00 plus $2.00 per mile m. Which equation gives the total cost C?', NULL, 7, 'Low', 'C = 2m - 3', 'C = 3m + 2', 'C = 5m', 'C = 2m + 3', 'D', 'active', 'The rate per mile is 2 (giving 2m) and the initial flat fee is 3: C = 2m + 3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The flat fee is added, not subtracted.\", \"remediation\": \"C = 2m + 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student swapped the rate and the flat fee.\", \"remediation\": \"The cost per mile is $2 (so 2m), and flat fee is $3: C = 2m + 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student combined 2 + 3 = 5m.\", \"remediation\": \"The $3 is paid once, not per mile: C = 2m + 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2809, 6, 2, 8, 'Word Problems & Applications', 205, 7, 'Which inequality represents the sentence: \"Five less than twice a number x is at most 19\"?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '2x - 5 ≤ 19', '5 - 2x ≤ 19', '2x - 5 ≥ 19', '2x - 5 < 19', 'A', 'active', '\"Twice a number x\" is 2x. \"Five less than\" means subtract 5: 2x - 5. \"At most 19\" means ≤ 19. Expression: 2x - 5 ≤ 19.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student wrote \\\"5 less 2x\\\".\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Five less than twice x\\\" means start with 2x and subtract 5: 2x - 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"\\\"At most\\\" means less than or equal to, not greater than or equal to.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"At most 19\\\" means cannot exceed 19 (≤ 19).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"\\\"At most\\\" includes equality (≤).\", \"remediation\": \"Use ≤ for \\\"at most\\\": 2x - 5 ≤ 19.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2810, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'Subtract the linear expressions:  (5x - 4) - (8x - 9)', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '-3x - 5', '-3x - 13', '-3x + 5', '3x + 5', 'C', 'active', 'Distribute the minus sign: (5x - 4) - 8x + 9. Combine like terms: 5x - 8x = -3x, and -4 + 9 = 5. Result: -3x + 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student wrote -5.\", \"remediation\": \"-4 + 9 = +5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to distribute the negative to -9 (-4 - 9 = -13).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtracting -9 becomes addition: -4 - (-9) = -4 + 9 = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"5x - 8x is -3x, not +3x.\", \"remediation\": \"5 - 8 = -3, so -3x + 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2811, 6, 2, 8, 'Linear Equations', 203, 7, 'Solve the equation with rational numbers:  (1/2)x - 4 = 7', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '6', '15', '11', '22', 'D', 'active', 'Step 1: Add 4 to both sides: (1/2)x = 7 + 4 = 11. Step 2: Multiply by 2: x = 11 × 2 = 22.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 4 from 7 = 3, then multiplied by 2 = 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 4 to both sides: 7 + 4 = 11, then 11 × 2 = 22.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student added 4 to 11.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by 2 to clear the fraction 1/2: x = 22.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"11 is the value of (1/2)x, not x.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 11 by 2 to find x: x = 22.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2812, 6, 2, 8, 'Word Problems & Applications', 205, 7, 'Solve the inequality:  -3x + 7 < 22', NULL, 7, 'Medium', 'x < 5', 'x > -5', 'x < -5', 'x > 5', 'B', 'active', 'Subtract 7: -3x < 15. Divide by -3 and REVERSE the inequality sign: x > 15 / (-3) -> x > -5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot the negative sign and didn\'t flip.\", \"remediation\": \"Flip the inequality sign and calculate 15 / -3 = -5: x > -5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to reverse the inequality sign when dividing by -3.\", \"remediation\": \"Rule: Whenever you multiply or divide an inequality by a negative number, flip the sign (< becomes >).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"15 / (-3) is -5, not +5.\", \"remediation\": \"A positive divided by a negative is negative: -5, so x > -5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2813, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'A jacket originally priced at p dollars is on sale for 25% off. Which expression represents the sale price of the jacket?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '0.25p', 'p - 0.25', '0.75p', '1.25p', 'C', 'active', 'The discount is 0.25p. Sale price = p - 0.25p = 1.00p - 0.25p = 0.75p.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"0.25p is the amount of the discount, not the final sale price.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the discount from the original: p - 0.25p = 0.75p.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"25% must be multiplied by p, not subtracted as a flat 25 cents.\", \"remediation\": \"25% of p is 0.25p, so price is p - 0.25p = 0.75p.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"1.25p represents a 25% price increase (markup).\", \"remediation\": \"A discount lowers the price: 0.75p.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2814, 6, 2, 8, 'Linear Equations', 203, 7, 'Solve for k:  -4(k - 3) = 28', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '-4', '-10', '10', '4', 'A', 'active', 'Divide both sides by -4: k - 3 = 28 / (-4) = -7. Add 3: k = -7 + 3 = -4.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 3 from -7.\", \"remediation\": \"To cancel -3, add 3: -7 + 3 = -4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 28 / 4 = 7 and added 3 = 10, ignoring the negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by -4: 28 / (-4) = -7. Then -7 + 3 = -4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student got the opposite sign.\", \"remediation\": \"k = -7 + 3 = -4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2815, 6, 2, 8, 'Two-Variable Equations', 204, 7, 'A plumber charges a $45 service call fee plus $30 for each hour worked. If a customer was billed $195, how many hours h did the plumber work?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '4 hours', '6.5 hours', '5 hours', '8 hours', 'C', 'active', 'Equation: 30h + 45 = 195. Subtract 45: 30h = 150. Divide by 30: h = 150 ÷ 30 = 5 hours.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 45 and made a division error.\", \"remediation\": \"195 - 45 = 150. 150 / 30 = 5 hours.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 195 by 30 without subtracting 45.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the flat $45 fee first: 195 - 45 = 150, then 150 / 30 = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"h = 5 hours.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2816, 6, 2, 8, 'Word Problems & Applications', 205, 7, 'Maya has $60 to spend at a carnival. Admission is $12, and each game ticket costs $2.50. What is the maximum number of game tickets (t) Maya can buy?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '18 tickets', '24 tickets', '20 tickets', '19 tickets', 'D', 'active', 'Inequality: 2.50t + 12 ≤ 60. Subtract 12: 2.50t ≤ 48. Divide: t ≤ 48 ÷ 2.50 = 19.2. Since tickets are whole numbers: maximum 19 tickets.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"18 tickets is less than the maximum possible.\", \"remediation\": \"19 tickets cost 12 + 47.50 = $59.50 ≤ $60.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 60 by 2.50 without subtracting admission.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract admission first: 60 - 12 = 48. 48 / 2.50 = 19.2 -> 19 tickets.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student rounded up to 20, which would cost $12 + 20(2.50) = $62 (exceeds budget).\", \"remediation\": \"You cannot spend more than $60, so round down: 19 tickets.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2817, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'Which expression is equivalent to:  0.4(5x - 10) + 1.2x?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '3.2x - 10', '3.2x - 4', '2x - 4', '2x - 2.8', 'B', 'active', 'Distribute: 0.4(5x) - 0.4(10) = 2x - 4. Combine with 1.2x: 2x + 1.2x - 4 = 3.2x - 4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply 0.4 by -10.\", \"remediation\": \"0.4 × (-10) = -4, so the constant term is -4: 3.2x - 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to add the 1.2x term.\", \"remediation\": \"Combine 2x + 1.2x = 3.2x.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 1.2 to -4.\", \"remediation\": \"1.2x has a variable and cannot be added to the constant -4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2818, 6, 2, 8, 'Linear Equations', 203, 7, 'Solve for m:  (m - 5) / 4 = -3', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '-7', '-12', '-17', '7', 'A', 'active', 'Multiply both sides by 4: m - 5 = -12. Add 5: m = -12 + 5 = -7.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"-12 is the value of m - 5, not m.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 5 to -12 to find m: m = -7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 5 from -12: -12 - 5 = -17.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 5 to both sides to cancel -5: m = -12 + 5 = -7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student got the opposite sign.\", \"remediation\": \"-12 + 5 = -7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2819, 6, 2, 8, 'Linear Equations', 203, 7, 'Solve the equation with fractions:  (2/5)x + (1/3) = (7/15)', NULL, 7, 'High', '1/5', '1/3', '2/3', '5/6', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply entire equation by LCD 15: 15(2/5)x + 15(1/3) = 15(7/15) -> 6x + 5 = 7. Subtract 5: 6x = 2. Divide by 6: x = 2/6 = 1/3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"6x = 7 - 5 = 2 -> x = 2/6 = 1/3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated 2/6.\", \"remediation\": \"2/6 simplifies to 1/3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 1/3 to 7/15.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 1/3: 7/15 - 5/15 = 2/15. (2/15) ÷ (2/5) = 1/3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2820, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'Which expression represents the perimeter of a rectangle with length (3x - 4) and width (2x + 1)?', NULL, 7, 'High', '10x - 3', '5x - 3', '6x² - 5x - 4', '10x - 6', 'D', 'active', 'Perimeter = 2(length + width) = 2[(3x - 4) + (2x + 1)] = 2(5x - 3) = 10x - 6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student forgot to multiply -3 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Distribute 2: 2 × (-3) = -6, so 10x - 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"5x - 3 is the semi-perimeter (length + width) without multiplying by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter requires 2 × (length + width): 2(5x - 3) = 10x - 6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"This is the area (length × width), not perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is distance around: 2L + 2W = 10x - 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2821, 6, 2, 8, 'Word Problems & Applications', 205, 7, 'Solve the two-step inequality and identify the correct solution set:  -5(2x - 3) ≥ 35', NULL, 7, 'High', 'x ≤ -2', 'x ≥ -2', 'x ≤ 2', 'x ≥ 2', 'A', 'active', 'Divide both sides by -5 and FLIP the inequality sign: 2x - 3 ≤ -7. Add 3: 2x ≤ -4. Divide by 2: x ≤ -2.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student did not flip the inequality sign when dividing by -5.\", \"remediation\": \"Dividing by -5 flips ≥ to ≤: 2x - 3 ≤ -7 -> x ≤ -2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a sign error on -4 / 2.\", \"remediation\": \"-4 / 2 = -2, so x ≤ -2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student both forgot to flip and made a sign error.\", \"remediation\": \"Result is x ≤ -2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2822, 6, 2, 8, 'Linear Equations', 203, 7, 'A gym charges an initiation fee plus a monthly rate. Member A paid $155 for 3 months. Member B paid $275 for 6 months. What is the monthly rate and the initiation fee?', NULL, 7, 'High', 'Monthly rate: $50, Initiation fee: $25', 'Monthly rate: $35, Initiation fee: $40', 'Monthly rate: $40, Initiation fee: $35', 'Monthly rate: $45, Initiation fee: $20', 'C', 'active', 'Rate = (275 - 155) / (6 - 3) = 120 / 3 = $40/month. Initiation fee = 155 - 3(40) = 155 - 120 = $35.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Testing 3 months: 3(50) + 25 = 175 != 155.\", \"remediation\": \"Monthly rate = $40, initiation fee = $35.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student swapped the monthly rate and initiation fee.\", \"remediation\": \"The change in cost over 3 months is $120, so monthly rate is $40, fee is $35.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Testing 3 months: 3(45) + 20 = 135 + 20 = 155, but for 6 months: 6(45) + 20 = 290 != 275.\", \"remediation\": \"Rate = 120 / 3 = 40.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2823, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'A store marks up the wholesale price of an item w by 40%, and then offers a coupon for $15 off. Which expression represents the final retail price?', NULL, 7, 'High', '0.40w - 15', '1.40(w - 15)', 'w + 0.40 - 15', '1.40w - 15', 'D', 'active', 'Marking up by 40% gives w + 0.40w = 1.40w. Then taking $15 off gives 1.40w - 15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"0.40w is only the markup amount, not the whole marked-up price.\", \"remediation\": \"Wholesale plus markup is 1.40w. After coupon: 1.40w - 15.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The coupon is applied AFTER markup, not before.\", \"remediation\": \"Markup is applied to w (1.40w), then subtract 15: 1.40w - 15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"40% must be multiplied by w, not added as $0.40.\", \"remediation\": \"40% markup is 0.40w, so marked up price is 1.40w.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2824, 6, 2, 8, 'Word Problems & Applications', 205, 7, 'An elevator has a maximum weight capacity of 1,800 pounds. An operator weighing 180 pounds enters with boxes weighing 45 pounds each. What is the greatest number of boxes (b) that can safely be loaded?', NULL, 7, 'High', '37 boxes', '36 boxes', '40 boxes', '35 boxes', 'B', 'active', 'Inequality: 45b + 180 ≤ 1800. Subtract 180: 45b ≤ 1620. Divide by 45: b ≤ 36 boxes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"37 boxes would weigh 45(37) + 180 = 1665 + 180 = 1845 > 1800 (unsafe).\", \"remediation\": \"Maximum is 36 boxes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 1800 by 45 without accounting for the operator\'s weight.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract operator\'s weight first: 1800 - 180 = 1620, then 1620 / 45 = 36.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"35 boxes is safe, but not the greatest number.\", \"remediation\": \"45 × 36 = 1620, which exactly matches remaining capacity.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2825, 6, 2, 8, 'Linear Equations', 203, 7, 'Solve for x:  0.6x - 1.5 = 0.2x + 2.5', NULL, 7, 'High', '5', '2.5', '10', '8', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract 0.2x from both sides: 0.4x - 1.5 = 2.5. Add 1.5: 0.4x = 4.0. Divide: x = 4.0 ÷ 0.4 = 10.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 4 by 0.8.\", \"remediation\": \"0.6x - 0.2x = 0.4x. Then 4 / 0.4 = 10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"0.4x = 4 -> x = 10.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"0.4x = 4, so x = 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2826, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 202, 7, 'Which expression is equivalent to:  -(4x - 7) + 3(2 - x)?', NULL, 7, 'High', '-7x + 13', '-7x + 1', '-7x - 1', '-x + 13', 'A', 'active', 'Distribute negative: -4x + 7. Distribute 3: 6 - 3x. Combine: (-4x - 3x) + (7 + 6) = -7x + 13.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 7 - 6 = 1.\", \"remediation\": \"7 + 6 = 13.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student multiplied -( -7 ) as -7 instead of +7: -7 + 6 = -1.\", \"remediation\": \"A negative times -7 is +7: 7 + 6 = 13. Result: -7x + 13.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student added 3x instead of subtracting 3x.\", \"remediation\": \"-4x - 3x = -7x.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2827, 6, 2, 8, 'Slope', 207, 8, 'What is the slope m of the line passing through points (0, 0) and (4, 12)?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '1/3', '4', '3', '8', 'C', 'active', 'Slope m = (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1) = (12 - 0) / (4 - 0) = 12 / 4 = 3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student inverted the slope formula: run/rise (4/12 = 1/3).\", \"remediation\": \"Slope is rise over run: Δy / Δx = 12 / 4 = 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"4 is the change in x, not the slope.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide change in y by change in x: 12 / 4 = 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 12 - 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Slope = 12 / 4 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2828, 6, 2, 8, 'Slope-Intercept Form', 209, 8, 'In the linear equation y = -4x + 7, what is the y-intercept of the line?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '(-4, 7)', '(0, -4)', '(7, 0)', '(0, 7)', 'D', 'active', 'In slope-intercept form y = mx + b, b is the y-intercept. Here b = 7, which corresponds to the point (0, 7).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student combined slope and intercept into coordinates.\", \"remediation\": \"The y-intercept is (0, 7).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"-4 is the slope m, not the y-intercept.\", \"remediation\": \"Slope is m = -4; y-intercept is b = 7 (point (0, 7)).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The coordinates are inverted.\", \"remediation\": \"The y-intercept is on the y-axis where x = 0: (0, 7).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2829, 6, 2, 8, 'Multi-Step Equations', 216, 8, 'Solve for x:  5x + 3 = 2x + 18', NULL, 8, 'Low', '7', '5', '3', '15', 'B', 'active', 'Subtract 2x from both sides: 3x + 3 = 18. Subtract 3: 3x = 15. Divide by 3: x = 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student added 2x instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 2x from 5x to get 3x. 3x = 15 -> x = 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"3x = 15, so x = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"15 is the value of 3x, not x.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 15 by 3 to isolate x: x = 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2830, 6, 2, 8, 'Functions & Linear Models', 366, 8, 'Which set of ordered pairs represents a FUNCTION?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '{(1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6), (4, 8)}', '{(0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 3), (0, 4)}', '{(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4), (3, 5)}', '{(2, 5), (2, 7), (4, 9), (6, 11)}', 'A', 'active', 'A relation is a function if every input (x) has exactly ONE output (y). In option A, each x-value is unique.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Input 0 is mapped to four different outputs.\", \"remediation\": \"In a function, x-values cannot repeat with different y-values.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Input 1 has two different outputs (2 and 3), so it is not a function.\", \"remediation\": \"A function cannot have the same x paired with different y values.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Input 2 has two different outputs (5 and 7).\", \"remediation\": \"Each input must map to exactly one output.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2831, 6, 2, 8, 'Systems of Linear Equations', 217, 8, 'What does the intersection point of two lines on a graph represent in a system of linear equations?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'The y-intercept of both lines', 'The unique solution to the system', 'The origin (0, 0)', 'A point where no solution exists', 'B', 'active', 'The point of intersection (x, y) satisfies both linear equations simultaneously, making it the solution to the system.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The intersection does not have to be on the y-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"The point where two lines cross is the solution (x, y) to both equations.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Lines can intersect anywhere on the coordinate plane, not just at (0, 0).\", \"remediation\": \"The intersection point is the shared solution.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"No solution occurs when lines are parallel and never intersect.\", \"remediation\": \"Intersecting lines have exactly one solution.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2832, 6, 2, 8, 'Multi-Step Equations', 216, 8, 'How many solutions does the equation  3x + 6 = 3x + 10  have?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'Two solutions', 'Infinitely many solutions', 'Exactly one solution (x = 4)', 'No solution', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract 3x from both sides: 6 = 10. Since 6 is never equal to 10 (a contradiction), there is no solution.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Linear equations in one variable never have exactly two solutions.\", \"remediation\": \"Possible solutions are: 0, 1, or infinitely many. Here it is no solution.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Infinitely many solutions occur when both sides are completely identical (e.g., 6 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Here 6 = 10 is false, so there is NO solution.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The variable x cancels out completely, so x cannot equal 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtracting 3x leaves 6 = 10 (false), meaning no solution.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2833, 6, 2, 8, 'Slope', 207, 8, 'What is the slope of any horizontal line?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '0', 'Undefined', '1', '-1', 'A', 'active', 'For a horizontal line, the rise (change in y) is 0: slope m = 0 / Δx = 0.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"A vertical line has an undefined slope (division by 0).\", \"remediation\": \"Horizontal lines have a flat slope of 0; vertical lines have undefined slope.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"A slope of 1 rises at a 45-degree angle.\", \"remediation\": \"A flat horizontal line has slope 0.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"-1 slants downward.\", \"remediation\": \"Horizontal lines have slope 0.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2834, 6, 2, 8, 'Multi-Step Equations', 216, 8, 'Solve for x:  2(3x - 4) = 4x + 10', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '5', '7', '9', '1', 'C', 'active', 'Distribute: 6x - 8 = 4x + 10. Subtract 4x: 2x - 8 = 10. Add 8: 2x = 18. Divide by 2: x = 9.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"6x - 4x = 2x, 10 + 8 = 18, x = 9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made an error in adding 10 + 8.\", \"remediation\": \"2x = 10 + 8 = 18 -> x = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 10 - 8 instead of adding 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 8 to cancel -8: 10 + 8 = 18, so x = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2835, 6, 2, 8, 'Slope-Intercept Form', 209, 8, 'What is the equation of the line that has a slope of -3 and passes through the point (2, 5)?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'y = -3x + 5', 'y = -3x - 1', 'y = 3x - 1', 'y = -3x + 11', 'D', 'active', 'Use y = mx + b: 5 = -3(2) + b -> 5 = -6 + b -> b = 11. The equation is y = -3x + 11.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student used the y-coordinate of the point (5) as the y-intercept.\", \"remediation\": \"Substitute (2, 5) into y = mx + b to solve for b: 5 = -3(2) + b -> b = 11.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student subtracted 6: 5 - 6 = -1.\", \"remediation\": \"To isolate b, add 6 to both sides: 5 + 6 = 11.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The slope is -3, not +3.\", \"remediation\": \"m = -3 and b = 11: y = -3x + 11.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2836, 6, 2, 8, 'Systems of Linear Equations', 217, 8, 'Solve the system of equations:  y = 2x + 1  and  y = -x + 7. What is the value of x?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '1', '2', '3', '5', 'B', 'active', 'Set equations equal: 2x + 1 = -x + 7. Add x: 3x + 1 = 7. Subtract 1: 3x = 6. Divide: x = 2. (Then y = 2(2) + 1 = 5).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"2x + x = 7 - 1 -> 3x = 6 -> x = 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made an algebraic error: 3x = 6 gives x = 2, not 3.\", \"remediation\": \"6 / 3 = 2, so x = 2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"5 is the value of y, not x.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for x: x = 2 (while y = 5).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2837, 6, 2, 8, 'Functions & Linear Models', 366, 8, 'Which function is NONLINEAR?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'y = 4x - 3', 'y = -2x', 'y = 4x² - 3', 'y = (1/2)x + 5', 'C', 'active', 'A linear function must have the variable x to the first power (y = mx + b). The term 4x² contains an exponent of 2, making it nonlinear (quadratic).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"y = 4x - 3 is linear with slope 4 and y-intercept -3.\", \"remediation\": \"A linear equation has x to the 1st power. x² is nonlinear.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"y = -2x is a proportional linear function.\", \"remediation\": \"It is a straight line with slope -2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"y = (1/2)x + 5 is linear with slope 1/2.\", \"remediation\": \"Only the x² term creates a curved nonlinear graph.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2838, 6, 2, 8, 'Slope', 207, 8, 'Why does the slope of a straight line remain constant between ANY two points chosen on the line?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'Because the slope triangles formed along the line are similar triangles', 'Because the line is parallel to the x-axis', 'Because the x-coordinates are always equal', 'Because the line always passes through the origin', 'A', 'active', 'By the properties of similar triangles, the ratio of vertical change to horizontal change (rise/run) is equal for any two slope triangles drawn on the same line.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Only horizontal lines are parallel to the x-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"Similar triangles guarantee constant slope for any straight line.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"x-coordinates change along a non-vertical line.\", \"remediation\": \"Constant slope is explained by similar triangles with equal rise/run ratios.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Lines with non-zero y-intercepts also have constant slope.\", \"remediation\": \"The geometric proof relies on similar right triangles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2839, 6, 2, 8, 'Multi-Step Equations', 216, 8, 'Which equation has INFINITELY MANY solutions?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '4(2x - 1) = 8x + 4', '4(2x - 1) = 6x - 4', '4(2x - 1) = 8x - 4', '4(2x - 1) = 8x', 'C', 'active', 'Expand the left side: 8x - 4. Since both sides are identically 8x - 4, subtracting 8x gives -4 = -4 (always true for all x).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"8x - 4 = 8x + 4 gives -4 = 4 (no solution).\", \"remediation\": \"Infinitely many solutions require identical expressions on both sides: 8x - 4 = 8x - 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"This has one unique solution (8x - 4 = 6x - 4 -> 2x = 0 -> x = 0).\", \"remediation\": \"For infinite solutions, the variable coefficients must be equal and constants equal.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"8x - 4 = 8x gives -4 = 0 (no solution).\", \"remediation\": \"Option A is an identity.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2840, 6, 2, 8, 'Functions & Linear Models', 366, 8, 'A candle is 12 inches tall when lit and burns down at a constant rate of 1.5 inches per hour (h). Which linear function models the height H of the candle?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'H = -1.5h - 12', 'H = 1.5h + 12', 'H = 12h - 1.5', 'H = -1.5h + 12', 'D', 'active', 'The initial height is 12 (positive y-intercept). Burning down means height decreases, so the rate of change is -1.5: H = -1.5h + 12.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The initial height is positive (+12), not negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Initial height is +12: H = -1.5h + 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"A positive slope means the candle is growing taller.\", \"remediation\": \"Burning decreases height, so slope is negative: -1.5h + 12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student switched the rate and the initial height.\", \"remediation\": \"The candle starts at 12 and burns 1.5 per hour: H = -1.5h + 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2841, 6, 2, 8, 'Systems of Linear Equations', 217, 8, 'Two lines are defined by  y = 3x - 5  and  y = 3x + 2. How many solutions does this system have?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'Exactly one solution', 'No solution', 'Infinitely many solutions', 'Two solutions', 'B', 'active', 'Both lines have the same slope (m = 3) but different y-intercepts (-5 and 2). Therefore, the lines are parallel and will never intersect: no solution.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Lines with the same slope never cross.\", \"remediation\": \"Parallel lines (same slope, different intercepts) never intersect, meaning NO solution.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Infinitely many solutions require the exact same line (same slope and same intercept).\", \"remediation\": \"Since the intercepts are different, the lines are parallel: no solution.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Linear systems cannot have 2 solutions.\", \"remediation\": \"Parallel lines have 0 solutions.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2842, 6, 2, 8, 'Proportional Relationships & Linear Equations', 206, 8, 'Car A travels at a rate represented by the equation d = 60t. Car B\'s travel is shown on a graph with points (0, 0) and (2, 130). Which car is traveling faster?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'Car B is faster (65 mph vs. 60 mph)', 'Car B is traveling at 130 mph', 'Car A is faster (60 mph vs. 50 mph)', 'Both cars travel at the exact same speed', 'A', 'active', 'Car A speed = 60 mph. Car B speed = 130 ÷ 2 = 65 mph. Since 65 > 60, Car B is faster.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"130 miles is the distance in 2 hours, not 1 hour.\", \"remediation\": \"Speed is rate per 1 hour: 130 / 2 = 65 mph.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student divided 130 / 2 incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"130 / 2 = 65 mph for Car B, which is faster than Car A\'s 60 mph.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"65 mph is not equal to 60 mph.\", \"remediation\": \"Car B travels 65 miles each hour.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2843, 6, 2, 8, 'Systems of Linear Equations', 217, 8, 'Solve the system of equations using elimination:  3x + 2y = 16   and   5x - 2y = 8. What is the value of y?', NULL, 8, 'High', '3', '3.5', '5', '2', 'B', 'active', 'Add the two equations to eliminate y: (3x + 5x) + (2y - 2y) = 16 + 8 -> 8x = 24 -> x = 3. Substitute x = 3 into first equation: 3(3) + 2y = 16 -> 9 + 2y = 16 -> 2y = 7 -> y = 3.5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"3 is the value of x, not y.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for y: x = 3, so 2y = 7 -> y = 3.5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Substitute x = 3: 3(3) + 2y = 16 -> 2y = 7 -> y = 3.5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"y = 3.5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2844, 6, 2, 8, 'Multi-Step Equations', 216, 8, 'Solve for x:  (1/2)(4x - 6) + 5 = 3(x + 1) - x - 1', NULL, 8, 'High', 'x = 2', 'No solution', 'x = 0', 'Infinitely many solutions', 'D', 'active', 'Left side: 2x - 3 + 5 = 2x + 2. Right side: 3x + 3 - x - 1 = 2x + 2. Since 2x + 2 = 2x + 2 is an identity, there are infinitely many solutions.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student solved for a single value.\", \"remediation\": \"2x + 2 = 2x + 2 has infinitely many solutions.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student made a sign error.\", \"remediation\": \"Both sides simplify to 2x + 2, which is always true for any real number x.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"x = 0 is a solution, but so is every other number.\", \"remediation\": \"Because both sides are identical, there are infinitely many solutions.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2845, 6, 2, 8, 'Functions & Linear Models', 366, 8, 'A function is represented by the table: (x=1, y=3), (x=2, y=7), (x=3, y=13), (x=4, y=21). Is this function linear or nonlinear, and why?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Nonlinear, because the rate of change is not constant (differences are 4, 6, 8)', 'Linear, because y increases as x increases', 'Linear, because it has a constant rate of change of 4', 'Nonlinear, because it passes through negative values', 'A', 'active', 'First differences in y: 7 - 3 = 4; 13 - 7 = 6; 21 - 13 = 8. Since the rate of change increases, the function is nonlinear (it is quadratic: y = x² + x + 1).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Increasing values do not guarantee linearity.\", \"remediation\": \"Linearity requires a CONSTANT rate of change. Here differences are 4, 6, 8 (nonlinear).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The rate of change is 4, then 6, then 8 (not constant).\", \"remediation\": \"A linear function must have the same difference between consecutive terms.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"All values are positive.\", \"remediation\": \"It is nonlinear because the rate of change is not constant.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2846, 6, 2, 8, 'Systems of Linear Equations', 217, 8, 'A movie theater sold 300 tickets for a total of $2,300. Student tickets cost $5 each and adult tickets cost $9 each. How many adult tickets (a) were sold?', NULL, 8, 'High', '250 adult tickets', '100 adult tickets', '200 adult tickets', '150 adult tickets', 'C', 'active', 'Let s = student, a = adult. (1) s + a = 300 -> s = 300 - a. (2) 5s + 9a = 2300. Substitute: 5(300 - a) + 9a = 2300 -> 1500 + 4a = 2300 -> 4a = 800 -> a = 200 adult tickets.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"250 adult tickets would exceed the total revenue.\", \"remediation\": \"200 adult tickets and 100 student tickets.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"100 is the number of student tickets (300 - 200 = 100).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for adult tickets: a = 200.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"150 adult tickets would give 150(9) + 150(5) = 1350 + 750 = $2100 != $2300.\", \"remediation\": \"4a = 800 -> a = 200 adult tickets.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2847, 6, 2, 8, 'Slope-Intercept Form', 209, 8, 'Line L passes through (-2, -5) and (4, 7). What are the coordinates of the x-intercept of Line L?', NULL, 8, 'High', '(0, -1)', '(1, 0)', '(-1, 0)', '(0.5, 0)', 'D', 'active', 'Slope m = [7 - (-5)] / [4 - (-2)] = 12 / 6 = 2. Equation: y = 2x + b -> 7 = 2(4) + b -> b = -1. Line: y = 2x - 1. Set y = 0 for x-intercept: 0 = 2x - 1 -> 2x = 1 -> x = 0.5. Point: (0.5, 0).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"(0, -1) is the y-intercept, not the x-intercept.\", \"remediation\": \"For the x-intercept, set y = 0: 0 = 2x - 1 -> x = 0.5, so (0.5, 0).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Testing x = 1: y = 2(1) - 1 = 1 != 0.\", \"remediation\": \"2x - 1 = 0 -> x = 1/2 = 0.5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a sign error.\", \"remediation\": \"2x = 1 -> x = +0.5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2848, 6, 2, 8, 'Functions & Linear Models', 366, 8, 'Function 1 is given by the equation y = 4x - 5. Function 2 is represented by a line passing through (1, 2) and (3, 12). Which function has the GREATER rate of change?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Function 1 because its y-intercept is negative', 'Function 2 has the greater rate of change (5 > 4)', 'Function 1 has the greater rate of change (4 > 3)', 'Both functions have the exact same rate of change', 'B', 'active', 'Function 1 rate of change (slope) = 4. Function 2 slope = (12 - 2) / (3 - 1) = 10 / 2 = 5. Since 5 > 4, Function 2 has the greater rate of change.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"y-intercept does not determine rate of change.\", \"remediation\": \"Rate of change is determined by the slope (m), not the y-intercept.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Function 2\'s rate of change is 5, not 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Slope of Function 2 is (12 - 2) / 2 = 5, which is greater than 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"4 is not equal to 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare slopes: 4 vs 5. 5 is larger.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:41', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2849, 6, 2, 8, 'Systems of Linear Equations', 217, 8, 'For what value of k will the system of equations have NO solution?   2x - 3y = 9   and   6x + ky = 15', NULL, 8, 'High', 'k = 9', 'k = -3', 'k = -9', 'k = 3', 'C', 'active', 'Multiply the first equation by 3: 6x - 9y = 27. To have parallel lines (no solution), the coefficients of x and y must be proportional: 6x + ky must match 6x - 9y. Thus, k = -9 (and constants 27 != 15 confirms no solution).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"If k = 9, the slopes are different and the lines will intersect in one point.\", \"remediation\": \"The y-coefficient must be -9 so both equations have slope 2/3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The student did not scale by 3 (from 2x to 6x).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply -3 by 3 to get k = -9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"If k = 3, slopes are different.\", \"remediation\": \"k must equal -9 to create parallel lines.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:42', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2850, 6, 2, 8, 'Proportional Relationships & Linear Equations', 206, 8, 'A water tank is draining. The relationship between time in minutes m and gallons remaining g is modeled by g = -12m + 360. What is the meaning of the y-intercept (360) and the x-intercept (30) in this context?', NULL, 8, 'High', '360 gallons is the initial amount of water; 30 minutes is the time it takes for the tank to be completely empty', 'The tank holds a maximum of 30 gallons and drains at 360 gallons per minute', '360 minutes is the total draining time; 30 gallons is the initial amount', 'The tank drains 360 gallons every 30 minutes', 'A', 'active', 'At m = 0 (y-intercept), g = 360 gallons (starting amount). At g = 0 (x-intercept), 0 = -12m + 360 -> 12m = 360 -> m = 30 minutes (time until tank is completely empty).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student mixed up all variables.\", \"remediation\": \"Start: 360 gallons; Empty after: 30 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student reversed the units of the intercepts.\", \"remediation\": \"g is gallons (y-intercept: 360 gal), m is minutes (x-intercept: 30 min).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The draining rate is 12 gallons per minute, not 360 in 30 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Intercepts represent starting capacity and total drain time.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 16:36:42', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2851, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'A red pencil and a blue pencil both start at the exact same edge of a desk. The red pencil stretches past the blue pencil. Which statement is true?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'The blue pencil is longer than the red pencil', 'Both pencils have the exact same length', 'The red pencil is longer than the blue pencil', 'The blue pencil is heavier', 'C', 'active', 'Since both pencils start at the same baseline and the red pencil reaches further, the red pencil is longer.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student reversed the length comparison.\", \"remediation\": \"The pencil that extends further from the common starting line is longer.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student thought they are equal despite one reaching further.\", \"remediation\": \"Look at the endpoints: the red pencil stretches past the blue one.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student confused length with weight.\", \"remediation\": \"Length measures how long an object is, not its weight.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2852, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length with Units', 222, 1, 'How should paper clips be placed to measure the length of a marker correctly?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Scattered across the marker', 'With small spaces between each paper clip', 'Overlapping each paper clip on top of the next', 'In a straight line end-to-end with no gaps and no overlaps', 'D', 'active', 'To measure accurately, non-standard units must be placed end-to-end in a straight line without any gaps or overlaps.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Scattered units cannot measure linear length.\", \"remediation\": \"Lay units in a straight line along the edge of the object.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Leaving gaps leads to undercounting the true length.\", \"remediation\": \"Units must touch end-to-end with zero gaps.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Overlapping units leads to overcounting.\", \"remediation\": \"Units should touch end-to-end without overlapping.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2853, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'On an analog clock, which hand tells the HOUR?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'The long hand', 'The short hand', 'The thinnest hand', 'The fastest hand', 'B', 'active', 'The short hand is the hour hand, and the long hand is the minute hand.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The long hand tells minutes, not hours.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: short hand = hour, long hand = minute.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The thin hand typically counts seconds.\", \"remediation\": \"The short hand indicates the hour.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The hour hand is the slowest moving hand.\", \"remediation\": \"The short hand points to the hour.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2854, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'When the long minute hand points to 12 and the short hour hand points to 5, what time is it?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '5:00', '12:00', '12:05', '5:12', 'A', 'active', 'When the minute hand is on 12, it is an \"o\'clock\" time (:00). Since the hour hand is on 5, the time is 5:00.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student thought it is 12 o\'clock.\", \"remediation\": \"Look at the short hand: it points to 5, so it is 5:00.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student swapped the hour and minute hands.\", \"remediation\": \"The short hand shows the hour (5) and minute hand on 12 means :00, so 5:00.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student read the minute hand position as 12 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Minute hand pointing to 12 means 0 minutes past the hour (:00).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2855, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'Ribbon A is longer than Ribbon B. Ribbon B is longer than Ribbon C. Which statement MUST be true?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Ribbon C is the longest ribbon', 'Ribbon A is longer than Ribbon C', 'Ribbon B is the longest ribbon', 'Ribbon A and Ribbon C are equal', 'B', 'active', 'By transitivity of length: If A > B and B > C, then Ribbon A is definitely longer than Ribbon C.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Ribbon C is shorter than B, which is shorter than A, so C is shortest.\", \"remediation\": \"A is longer than both B and C.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Ribbon A is longer than Ribbon B.\", \"remediation\": \"Ribbon A is the longest of all three.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A is longer than B and B is longer than C, so A cannot equal C.\", \"remediation\": \"Ribbon A is longer than Ribbon C.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2856, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length with Units', 222, 1, 'A crayon is measured with connecting cubes. It takes 6 cubes lined up end-to-end to match the crayon. How long is the crayon?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '12 cubes long', '5 cubes long', '7 cubes long', '6 cubes long', 'D', 'active', 'Since 6 cubes match the length from end to end, the crayon is 6 cubes long.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the number of cubes.\", \"remediation\": \"The length is 6 cubes long.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student undercounted by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"The measurement matches the total number of cubes: 6 cubes long.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student overcounted by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Count each cube exactly once: 6 cubes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2857, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'What time is shown on a clock when the minute hand points to 6 and the hour hand is halfway between 8 and 9?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '8:30', '9:30', '8:06', '6:40', 'A', 'active', 'The minute hand at 6 represents 30 minutes. Since the hour hand is between 8 and 9, it is half-past 8, or 8:30.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student jumped ahead to the next hour (9:30).\", \"remediation\": \"The hour hand has passed 8 but has not reached 9 yet, so it is 8:30.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student read the minute hand face number (6) as 6 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Minute hand pointing to 6 represents 30 minutes (6 × 5 = 30).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student swapped the hour and minute positions.\", \"remediation\": \"Minute hand at 6 is :30, hour hand past 8 is 8:30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2858, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'Which object is usually the SHORTEST in real life?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'A school bus', 'A pencil', 'A paperclip', 'A baseball bat', 'C', 'active', 'A paperclip is typically about 1 to 2 inches long, making it much shorter than a pencil, bat, or bus.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A school bus is the longest object listed.\", \"remediation\": \"A paperclip is only about an inch long.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"A pencil is longer than a paperclip (about 7 inches).\", \"remediation\": \"A paperclip is much smaller and shorter than a pencil.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A baseball bat is about 30 inches long.\", \"remediation\": \"A paperclip is the shortest object listed.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2859, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'Two strips of paper are shown on a table. Strip X starts at 0 cm and ends at 7 cm. Strip Y starts at 2 cm and ends at 8 cm. Which strip is longer?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Strip Y is longer because 8 is greater than 7', 'Both strips have the exact same length', 'Strip Y is 8 cm long', 'Strip X is longer (7 cm vs 6 cm)', 'D', 'active', 'Length of Strip X = 7 - 0 = 7 cm. Length of Strip Y = 8 - 2 = 6 cm. Strip X is longer.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student looked only at the right endpoint (8) and ignored that Strip Y started at 2 cm.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the starting point from the endpoint: Strip Y is 8 - 2 = 6 cm long.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed they are equal.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate actual lengths: 7 cm vs 6 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Strip Y does not start at 0, so it is not 8 cm long.\", \"remediation\": \"Strip Y length is 8 - 2 = 6 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2860, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'A digital clock displays 10:30. Where should the hands be on an analog clock to show the same time?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Hour hand halfway between 9 and 10, minute hand at 6', 'Hour hand halfway between 10 and 11, minute hand pointing directly at 6', 'Hour hand pointing directly at 10, minute hand pointing at 6', 'Hour hand at 6, minute hand at 10', 'B', 'active', 'At 10:30, 30 minutes have passed, so the minute hand points to 6 and the hour hand has moved halfway from 10 towards 11.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Between 9 and 10 would represent 9:30.\", \"remediation\": \"The time is 10:30, so the hour hand is between 10 and 11.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student thought the hour hand stays pointing directly at 10.\", \"remediation\": \"As 30 minutes pass, the hour hand moves halfway between 10 and 11.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student swapped the hour and minute hands.\", \"remediation\": \"Hour is 10 (short hand) and 30 minutes is 6 (long hand).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2861, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length with Units', 222, 1, 'Mia measured a book using two different units: paperclips and new pencils. Which statement is correct?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'She will need FEWER paperclips than pencils because paperclips are smaller', 'She will need the exact same number of each', 'She will need MORE paperclips than pencils because paperclips are shorter', 'She cannot measure a book with pencils', 'C', 'active', 'Smaller measurement units require a greater quantity of units to cover the same length (inverse relationship).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student inverted the unit size relationship.\", \"remediation\": \"Shorter units mean you need MORE of them to span the same distance.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Different sized units cannot produce the same count.\", \"remediation\": \"Paperclips are shorter than pencils, so more paperclips are required.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Any straight unit can measure length.\", \"remediation\": \"You will need more paperclips than pencils.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2862, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'Pencil A is 5 paperclips long. Pencil B is 8 paperclips long. How many paperclips LONGER is Pencil B than Pencil A?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '3 paperclips', '8 paperclips', '13 paperclips', '4 paperclips', 'A', 'active', 'Subtract the shorter length from the longer length: 8 - 5 = 3 paperclips.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student just selected Pencil B\'s length.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the difference: 8 - 5 = 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added the lengths together: 8 + 5 = 13.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How much longer\\\" asks for the difference: subtract 8 - 5 = 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted.\", \"remediation\": \"8 - 5 = 3 paperclips.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2863, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'Recess begins at 1:00 and ends at 1:30. How many minutes long is recess?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '60 minutes', '15 minutes', '30 minutes', '1 hour', 'C', 'active', 'From 1:00 to 1:30 is half an hour, which equals exactly 30 minutes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"60 minutes is a full hour.\", \"remediation\": \"From :00 to :30 is 30 minutes (half an hour).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 15 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Count from 0 to 30 minutes: 30 minutes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"1 hour would be until 2:00.\", \"remediation\": \"1:00 to 1:30 is 30 minutes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2864, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length with Units', 222, 1, 'Sam lined up 4 small blocks to measure a paintbrush, but left spaces between the blocks. What will happen to his measurement?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'The paintbrush will get shorter', 'His measurement will be an overestimate (too many blocks)', 'His measurement will be completely accurate', 'His measurement will be an underestimate (too few blocks)', 'D', 'active', 'Leaving gaps means empty space is not counted by blocks, so it takes fewer blocks than it should, underestimating the true length in blocks.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Measuring does not change the physical size of the object.\", \"remediation\": \"The paintbrush stays the same size; only the measurement is inaccurate.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Overestimate happens with overlaps, not gaps.\", \"remediation\": \"Gaps take up space without blocks, so fewer blocks are used than actual.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Gaps cause measurement errors.\", \"remediation\": \"Units must touch end-to-end with no spaces.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2865, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'Order the three strings from SHORTEST to LONGEST: String P is 4 cubes long, String Q is 9 cubes long, String R is 6 cubes long.', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'String Q, String R, String P', 'String P, String R, String Q', 'String P, String Q, String R', 'String R, String P, String Q', 'B', 'active', 'Comparing lengths: 4 < 6 < 9. So the order from shortest to longest is String P (4), String R (6), String Q (9).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student ordered from longest to shortest.\", \"remediation\": \"Read carefully: the question asks for shortest to longest (4, 6, 9).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student placed 9 before 6.\", \"remediation\": \"6 is shorter than 9, so R comes before Q.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"String P (4) is shorter than String R (6).\", \"remediation\": \"Shortest is String P (4 cubes).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2866, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'How many minutes are in one FULL hour?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '60 minutes', '24 minutes', '30 minutes', '100 minutes', 'A', 'active', 'There are 60 minutes in one full hour. (Half an hour is 30 minutes).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"24 is the number of hours in a full day.\", \"remediation\": \"1 day = 24 hours, but 1 hour = 60 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"30 minutes is half an hour.\", \"remediation\": \"A full hour has 60 minutes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed base-10 (100).\", \"remediation\": \"Time uses 60 minutes in an hour, not 100.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2867, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length with Units', 222, 1, 'If a toy car is 3 blocks long, and a toy truck is 2 times as long as the toy car, how many blocks long is the toy truck?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '5 blocks long', '6 blocks long', '1 block long', '8 blocks long', 'B', 'active', 'Multiply the length of the car by 2: 3 × 2 = 6 blocks long.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 3 + 2 = 5.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"2 times as long\\\" means multiply: 3 × 2 = 6 blocks.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 3 - 2 = 1.\", \"remediation\": \"The truck is longer: multiply 3 by 2 = 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"3 + 3 = 6 blocks.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2868, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'Stick A is longer than Stick B. Stick C is shorter than Stick B. Which stick is the SHORTEST of all three?', NULL, 1, 'High', 'Stick A and Stick C are equal', 'Stick B', 'Stick A', 'Stick C', 'D', 'active', 'Stick A > Stick B, and Stick B > Stick C. Therefore, Stick A is longest, Stick B is in the middle, and Stick C is the shortest.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Stick A is longest and Stick C is shortest, so they are not equal.\", \"remediation\": \"Stick C is shorter than B, which is shorter than A.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Stick B is longer than Stick C.\", \"remediation\": \"Stick C is shorter than Stick B, making Stick C the shortest.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Stick A is the longest stick.\", \"remediation\": \"Stick C is the shortest.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2869, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'Ben leaves for soccer practice at 4:30. The practice lasts 1 hour. What time does practice end?', NULL, 1, 'High', '5:30', '5:00', '6:30', '4:00', 'A', 'active', 'Add 1 full hour to 4:30: 4 + 1 = 5, and the minutes stay at 30. The practice ends at 5:30.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only added 30 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 1 full hour (60 minutes): 4:30 + 1 hour = 5:30.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 2 hours.\", \"remediation\": \"4:30 + 1 hour = 5:30.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 1 hour instead of adding.\", \"remediation\": \"Practice ends after starting, so add 1 hour: 5:30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2870, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length with Units', 222, 1, 'A table is measured with wooden craft sticks. 8 sticks are used. If each stick is replaced with two smaller paperclips of equal total length, how many paperclips will measure the table?', NULL, 1, 'High', '8 paperclips', '4 paperclips', '16 paperclips', '10 paperclips', 'C', 'active', 'Each of the 8 sticks equals 2 paperclips: 8 × 2 = 16 paperclips.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Paperclips are smaller, so more than 8 are required.\", \"remediation\": \"8 × 2 = 16 paperclips.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 8 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Since each stick equals 2 paperclips, multiply: 8 × 2 = 16.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 8 + 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 8 sticks by 2 paperclips per stick: 16 paperclips.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2871, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'The hour hand of a clock is halfway between 11 and 12. The minute hand points directly at 6. What time will it be in 30 minutes?', NULL, 1, 'High', '11:30', '12:30', '1:00', '12:00', 'D', 'active', 'Current time is 11:30. In 30 minutes, the minute hand moves to 12 and the hour hand reaches 12, making the time 12:00.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"11:30 is the current time, not the time in 30 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 30 minutes to 11:30: 11:30 + 30 min = 12:00.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 1 hour instead of 30 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"30 minutes after 11:30 completes the hour: 12:00.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student jumped too far ahead.\", \"remediation\": \"11:30 + 30 minutes = 12:00.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2872, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'A teacher wants to measure the whiteboard. Which method gives the most accurate measurement?', NULL, 1, 'High', 'Pacing it out with steps of different sizes', 'Using 10 identical meter sticks laid end-to-end in a straight line', 'Using shoes of different students in the class', 'Using hands spread out with spaces between them', 'B', 'active', 'Accurate measurement requires identical, standard units laid end-to-end without gaps or variations in size.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Uneven steps produce inaccurate measurements.\", \"remediation\": \"Use identical standard units laid end-to-end.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Students have different shoe sizes, making units inconsistent.\", \"remediation\": \"Units must all be the exact same size.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Hand spans vary and leaving spaces causes errors.\", \"remediation\": \"Units must touch without spaces.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2873, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 223, 1, 'A movie starts at 6:00 and lasts for 1 hour and 30 minutes. What time does the movie finish?', NULL, 1, 'High', '7:00', '8:00', '7:30', '8:30', 'C', 'active', '6:00 + 1 hour = 7:00. Adding the remaining 30 minutes gives 7:30.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot the 30 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Add both 1 hour and 30 minutes: 6:00 + 1:30 = 7:30.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded up to 2 hours.\", \"remediation\": \"1 hour and 30 minutes after 6:00 is 7:30.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 2 hours and 30 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"6:00 + 1:30 = 7:30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2874, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Comparison', 221, 1, 'Rope 1 is 12 inches long. Rope 2 is 4 inches shorter than Rope 1. Rope 3 is 2 inches longer than Rope 2. How long is Rope 3?', NULL, 1, 'High', '10 inches', '6 inches', '8 inches', '14 inches', 'A', 'active', 'Step 1: Rope 2 = 12 - 4 = 8 inches. Step 2: Rope 3 = 8 + 2 = 10 inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 2 from 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Rope 3 is longer than Rope 2: 8 + 2 = 10 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"8 inches is Rope 2, not Rope 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 2 inches to Rope 2 to find Rope 3: 8 + 2 = 10 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added all numbers: 12 + 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Rope 2 is shorter (12 - 4 = 8), then Rope 3 is 8 + 2 = 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2875, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length with Units', 222, 1, 'A desk is 5 craft sticks long. Each craft stick is equal to 3 paper clips. How many paper clips long is the desk?', NULL, 1, 'High', '8 paper clips', '12 paper clips', '15 paper clips', '2 paper clips', 'C', 'active', '5 craft sticks × 3 paper clips per craft stick = 15 paper clips.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 5 + 3 = 8 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Each of the 5 sticks is 3 paperclips: 5 × 3 = 15 paperclips.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 4 × 3.\", \"remediation\": \"5 × 3 = 15 paperclips.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 5 - 3 = 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply sticks by paper clips per stick: 5 × 3 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2876, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length', 225, 2, 'When using a standard 12-inch ruler, where should you line up the beginning of an object?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'At the 12-inch mark', 'At the 1-inch mark', 'At the very center of the ruler', 'At the 0 mark', 'D', 'active', 'To measure accurately, line up the left end of the object with the 0 mark on the ruler.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"12 is the end of the ruler.\", \"remediation\": \"Begin at 0.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Starting at 1 results in an error of 1 inch.\", \"remediation\": \"Always start measuring at the 0 mark.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Measuring from center makes calculating length difficult.\", \"remediation\": \"Start at the 0 mark on the left.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2877, 6, 4, 12, 'Estimating Length', 226, 2, 'What is the BEST estimate for the length of an unsharpened standard wooden pencil?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'About 7 feet', 'About 7 inches', 'About 7 yards', 'About 7 meters', 'B', 'active', 'A standard pencil is about 7 to 8 inches long. Feet and yards are much too large.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"7 feet is taller than an adult human.\", \"remediation\": \"Use inches for small everyday objects like pencils.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"7 yards is 21 feet long.\", \"remediation\": \"A pencil is measured in inches: about 7 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"7 meters is the length of a large vehicle.\", \"remediation\": \"Pencils are small objects measured in inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2878, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 229, 2, 'When the minute hand of a clock points to the number 4, how many minutes past the hour is it?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '20 minutes', '40 minutes', '4 minutes', '15 minutes', 'A', 'active', 'Each number on the clock face represents 5 minutes: 4 × 5 = 20 minutes.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"40 minutes corresponds to the number 8.\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 5 = 20 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student read the face number (4) directly as minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the face number by 5: 4 × 5 = 20 minutes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"15 minutes corresponds to the number 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Number 4 represents 20 minutes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2879, 6, 4, 12, 'Money', 230, 2, 'What is the value of 1 quarter, 2 dimes, and 1 nickel?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '40 cents', '50 cents', '45 cents', '35 cents', 'B', 'active', 'Quarter = 25¢, 2 dimes = 20¢, nickel = 5¢. Total = 25 + 20 + 5 = 50¢.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot the quarter or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Add: 25¢ (quarter) + 20¢ (dimes) + 5¢ (nickel) = 50¢.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the nickel as 0.\", \"remediation\": \"25 + 20 + 5 = 50¢.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added coin counts instead of coin values.\", \"remediation\": \"Count coin values: 25 + 10 + 10 + 5 = 50¢.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2880, 6, 4, 12, 'Comparing Lengths', 227, 2, 'Object A is 14 cm long. Object B is 9 cm long. How much longer is Object A than Object B?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '4 cm', '23 cm', '6 cm', '5 cm', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract the shorter length: 14 - 9 = 5 cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"14 - 9 = 5 cm.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 14 + 9 = 23.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How much longer\\\" asks for the difference: subtract 14 - 9 = 5 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"14 - 9 = 5 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2881, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 229, 2, 'Which time label is appropriate for eating breakfast in the morning?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '7:30 a.m.', '7:30 p.m.', '12:00 a.m.', '3:00 p.m.', 'A', 'active', 'a.m. is used for morning hours from midnight to noon. 7:30 a.m. is morning breakfast time.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"7:30 p.m. is evening dinner time.\", \"remediation\": \"Morning hours use a.m.: 7:30 a.m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"12:00 a.m. is midnight.\", \"remediation\": \"Morning breakfast is around 7:30 a.m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"3:00 p.m. is afternoon.\", \"remediation\": \"Breakfast is in the morning: a.m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2882, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length', 225, 2, 'How many inches are in 1 foot?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '36 inches', '10 inches', '12 inches', '100 inches', 'C', 'active', 'There are exactly 12 inches in 1 standard foot.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"36 inches is 1 yard.\", \"remediation\": \"1 foot = 12 inches (3 feet = 1 yard = 36 inches).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed base-10.\", \"remediation\": \"The customary system uses 12 inches in 1 foot, not 10.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student confused inches with metric centimeters.\", \"remediation\": \"1 foot = 12 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:25', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2883, 6, 4, 12, 'Money', 230, 2, 'Which coin has a value of 10 cents?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'A nickel', 'A penny', 'A quarter', 'A dime', 'D', 'active', 'A dime is worth 10 cents. A nickel is 5¢, penny is 1¢, quarter is 25¢.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A nickel is worth 5 cents.\", \"remediation\": \"A dime is 10 cents, while a nickel is 5 cents.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"A penny is worth 1 cent.\", \"remediation\": \"A dime is worth 10 cents.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"A quarter is worth 25 cents.\", \"remediation\": \"10 cents = 1 dime.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2884, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length', 225, 2, 'A teacher measures a desk and finds it is 36 inches long. Then she measures it in feet. Which statement is TRUE?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'The desk cannot be measured in feet', 'The desk is 3 feet long because there are 12 inches in each foot', 'The desk will be more than 36 feet long', 'The desk is 4 feet long', 'B', 'active', 'Divide 36 inches by 12 inches per foot: 36 ÷ 12 = 3 feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Desks can be measured in both feet and inches.\", \"remediation\": \"36 inches = 3 feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Feet are larger units than inches, so the number of feet must be smaller.\", \"remediation\": \"When converting to a larger unit (feet), the number gets smaller: 36 in = 3 ft.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"4 feet = 48 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"36 / 12 = 3 feet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2885, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 229, 2, 'A clock shows the hour hand between 6 and 7, and the minute hand pointing to 9. What time is it?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '7:45', '6:09', '6:45', '9:30', 'C', 'active', 'The hour hand has passed 6 but has not reached 7, so it is the 6 o\'clock hour. The minute hand at 9 means 9 × 5 = 45 minutes. The time is 6:45.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student read the hour as 7 because the hand is close to 7.\", \"remediation\": \"The hour hand does not reach 7 until the minute hand reaches 12. It is still 6:45.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student read the face number 9 as 9 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Minute hand at 9 represents 45 minutes: 6:45.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student swapped the positions of the hands.\", \"remediation\": \"Hour hand near 7, minute hand at 9 = 6:45.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2886, 6, 4, 12, 'Money', 230, 2, 'Maya has 2 quarters, 3 dimes, and 4 pennies. She wants to buy a notebook that costs 85 cents. Does she have enough money?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'under-budget by 1 cent (she has 84 cents)', 'No, she only has 54 cents', 'Yes, she has 94 cents', 'Yes, she has exactly 85 cents', 'A', 'active', '2 quarters = 50¢. 3 dimes = 30¢. 4 pennies = 4¢. Total = 50 + 30 + 4 = 84¢. Since 84¢ < 85¢, she is 1 cent short.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student omitted the quarters.\", \"remediation\": \"Total = 84¢.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted dimes as 40¢ or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"50 + 30 + 4 = 84¢, which is 1 cent less than 85¢.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 85¢.\", \"remediation\": \"Total is 84¢, not 85¢.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2887, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Word Problems', 228, 2, 'A piece of yarn is 48 inches long. Carlos cuts off 19 inches to tie a package. How many inches of yarn are left?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '39 inches', '67 inches', '29 inches', '21 inches', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract: 48 - 19 = 29 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted digits incorrectly (9 - 8 = 1, 4 - 1 = 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Regroup: 18 - 9 = 9, 3 - 1 = 2 -> 29 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 48 + 19.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Cuts off\\\" means subtract: 48 - 19 = 29 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"48 - 19 = 29.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2888, 6, 4, 12, 'Comparing Lengths', 227, 2, 'An eraser is 5 cm long. A pencil is 18 cm long. If you place 3 erasers end-to-end, how does their total length compare to the pencil?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'The 3 erasers are 15 cm longer than the pencil', 'The 3 erasers are longer than the pencil', 'The 3 erasers are the exact same length as the pencil', 'The 3 erasers are 3 cm shorter than the pencil (15 cm vs 18 cm)', 'D', 'active', '3 erasers = 3 × 5 = 15 cm. Comparing to 18 cm: 18 - 15 = 3 cm shorter.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student misread \\\"shorter\\\" as \\\"longer\\\".\", \"remediation\": \"15 cm is shorter than 18 cm.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"15 cm is less than 18 cm.\", \"remediation\": \"3 erasers total 15 cm, which is shorter than 18 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"15 cm is not equal to 18 cm.\", \"remediation\": \"18 - 15 = 3 cm difference.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2889, 6, 4, 12, 'Estimating Length', 226, 2, 'Which measurement unit would require the SMALLEST number to describe the height of a classroom door?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'Feet', 'Yards', 'Inches', 'Centimeters', 'B', 'active', 'Yards are the largest unit among the options. Larger units result in a smaller numerical measurement count.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Feet are smaller than yards, so the number of feet will be larger (e.g. 7 ft vs ~2.3 yd).\", \"remediation\": \"The largest unit gives the smallest count: yards.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Inches will give a large count (~84 inches).\", \"remediation\": \"The larger the unit, the smaller the number needed.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Centimeters will give the largest numerical count (~210 cm).\", \"remediation\": \"Yards give the smallest numerical count.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2890, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Word Problems', 228, 2, 'On a number line diagram, point A is at 15 and point B is at 42. What is the distance between point A and point B?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '27', '23', '57', '37', 'A', 'active', 'Distance is the difference: 42 - 15 = 27 units.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"42 - 15 = 27.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 42 + 15 = 57.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance between two points is found by subtraction: 42 - 15 = 27.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an error in regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"42 - 15 = 27.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2891, 6, 4, 12, 'Money', 230, 2, 'Liam pays for a toy costing $1.35 with a $2.00 bill. How much change should Liam receive?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '75 cents', '65 cents', '55 cents', '35 cents', 'B', 'active', '$2.00 - $1.35 = 200 cents - 135 cents = 65 cents.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated 100 - 35 = 75 instead of 65.\", \"remediation\": \"200 - 135 = 65 cents.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 10 cents too much.\", \"remediation\": \"135 + 65 = 200 cents ($2.00).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student repeated the 35 cents.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract from $2.00: $2.00 - $1.35 = 65¢.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2892, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 229, 2, 'Soccer practice starts at 3:15 p.m. and ends at 3:55 p.m. How many minutes long was soccer practice?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '50 minutes', '35 minutes', '45 minutes', '40 minutes', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract the minutes: 55 - 15 = 40 minutes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted by 10.\", \"remediation\": \"55 - 15 = 40 minutes.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated 55 - 15.\", \"remediation\": \"55 - 15 = 40 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 5 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"55 - 15 = 40 minutes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2893, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Word Problems', 228, 2, 'Emma has three ribbons. Ribbon 1 is 24 cm. Ribbon 2 is 17 cm longer than Ribbon 1. Ribbon 3 is 15 cm shorter than Ribbon 2. What is the length of Ribbon 3?', NULL, 2, 'High', '26 cm', '41 cm', '22 cm', '31 cm', 'A', 'active', 'Ribbon 2 = 24 + 17 = 41 cm. Ribbon 3 = 41 - 15 = 26 cm.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"41 cm is the length of Ribbon 2, not Ribbon 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 15 cm from Ribbon 2 to find Ribbon 3: 41 - 15 = 26 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated 41 - 15.\", \"remediation\": \"41 - 15 = 26 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 15 instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"41 - 15 = 26 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2894, 6, 4, 12, 'Money', 230, 2, 'Jack has 3 quarters, 4 dimes, and 5 nickels. He wants to buy two comic books that cost $0.65 each. Does he have enough money?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'Yes, he has exactly $1.30', 'No, he only has $1.15', 'Yes, he has $1.40 and the books cost $1.30 (he has 10 cents left over)', 'No, the books cost $1.45', 'C', 'active', 'Jack\'s money: 3×25¢ = 75¢, 4×10¢ = 40¢, 5×5¢ = 25¢. Total = 75 + 40 + 25 = 140¢ = $1.40. Two books cost: 65¢ × 2 = $1.30. He has $1.40 - $1.30 = $0.10 left over.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Jack has $1.40, which is more than $1.30.\", \"remediation\": \"He has 10 cents left over.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated Jack\'s coins.\", \"remediation\": \"75¢ + 40¢ + 25¢ = $1.40.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"65¢ + 65¢ = $1.30, not $1.45.\", \"remediation\": \"Two books cost $1.30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2895, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length', 225, 2, 'A broken ruler starts at 3 inches and ends at 12 inches. A student places a toy car from the 4-inch mark to the 11-inch mark on this ruler. How long is the toy car?', NULL, 2, 'High', '11 inches', '8 inches', '4 inches', '7 inches', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract starting mark from ending mark: 11 - 4 = 7 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student read the end mark directly (11 inches).\", \"remediation\": \"When the object does not start at 0, subtract: 11 - 4 = 7 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student counted marks instead of intervals.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance = 11 - 4 = 7 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"4 inches is the starting point.\", \"remediation\": \"Length is 11 - 4 = 7 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2896, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 229, 2, 'It is 8:45 p.m. In 20 minutes, what time will the clock show?', NULL, 2, 'High', '8:05 p.m.', '9:05 p.m.', '8:65 p.m.', '9:15 p.m.', 'B', 'active', '8:45 + 15 minutes = 9:00. Adding the remaining 5 minutes gives 9:05 p.m.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The hour must advance to 9.\", \"remediation\": \"After 8:59 comes 9:00, so it becomes 9:05 p.m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Clocks reset to 0 after 60 minutes; there is no :65.\", \"remediation\": \"60 minutes makes a new hour: 45 + 20 = 65 min = 1 hr and 5 min -> 9:05 p.m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 30 minutes instead of 20 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"8:45 + 20 min = 9:05 p.m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2897, 6, 4, 12, 'Length Word Problems', 228, 2, 'A rectangular garden perimeter is 30 meters. Three of the sides measure 8 meters, 7 meters, and 8 meters. What is the length of the fourth side?', NULL, 2, 'High', '15 meters', '9 meters', '7 meters', '23 meters', 'C', 'active', 'Sum of known sides = 8 + 7 + 8 = 23 meters. Fourth side = 30 - 23 = 7 meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 30 - 15.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the 3 sides (23 m) and subtract from 30 m: 30 - 23 = 7 meters.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"30 - 23 = 7 meters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"23 meters is the sum of the three known sides, not the missing side.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 23 from total 30: 7 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2898, 6, 4, 12, 'Money', 230, 2, 'Which combination of coins has the GREATEST total value?', NULL, 2, 'High', '3 quarters and 1 dime (85 cents)', '2 quarters and 6 nickels (80 cents)', '7 dimes and 2 nickels (80 cents)', '15 nickels (75 cents)', 'A', 'active', 'A: 3×25¢ + 10¢ = 85¢. B: 7×10¢ + 2×5¢ = 80¢. C: 15×5¢ = 75¢. D: 2×25¢ + 6×5¢ = 80¢. Greatest is 85¢.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"50 + 30 = 80¢.\", \"remediation\": \"Option A has 85¢.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"70 + 10 = 80¢, which is less than 85¢.\", \"remediation\": \"Option A totals 85¢, which is the greatest.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"15 × 5 = 75¢.\", \"remediation\": \"Option A has 85¢.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2899, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Length', 225, 2, 'A ribbon is 1 yard long. Sophia cuts off 14 inches. How many inches of ribbon are left?', NULL, 2, 'High', '24 inches', '18 inches', '22 inches', '86 inches', 'C', 'active', '1 yard = 36 inches. Subtract 14 inches: 36 - 14 = 22 inches left.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted from 38 or made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"1 yard = 36 inches. 36 - 14 = 22 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted from 32 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"36 - 14 = 22 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student thought 1 yard = 100 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"1 yard = 36 inches, not 100.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2900, 6, 4, 12, 'Time', 229, 2, 'A bus journey starts at 10:40 a.m. and arrives at 11:25 a.m. How long was the bus journey in minutes?', NULL, 2, 'High', '35 minutes', '85 minutes', '40 minutes', '45 minutes', 'D', 'active', 'From 10:40 to 11:00 is 20 minutes. From 11:00 to 11:25 is 25 minutes. Total = 20 + 25 = 45 minutes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 40 - 25 = 15, then... miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"10:40 to 11:25 is 45 minutes.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted numbers without converting across the hour.\", \"remediation\": \"Count to the next hour: 20 min to 11:00, then 25 min to 11:25 = 45 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted.\", \"remediation\": \"20 + 25 = 45 minutes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:55:26', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2901, 6, 4, 12, 'Time & Time Intervals', 231, 3, 'A clock shows 4:18. What time will it be in 12 minutes?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '4:20', '4:30', '5:30', '4:28', 'B', 'active', 'Add 12 minutes to the minutes: 18 + 12 = 30. The time will be 4:30.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 2 minutes instead of 12.\", \"remediation\": \"18 + 12 = 30 minutes past 4: 4:30.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student changed the hour to 5.\", \"remediation\": \"The hour is still 4: 4:30.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error.\", \"remediation\": \"18 + 12 = 30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2902, 6, 4, 12, 'Mass & Liquid Volume', 232, 3, 'Which unit is best suited to measure the mass of a single paper clip?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'Grams', 'Meters', 'Kilograms', 'Liters', 'A', 'active', 'A paper clip is very light (about 1 gram). Grams are used for light objects, while kilograms are for heavy objects.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Meters measure length, not mass.\", \"remediation\": \"Mass is measured in grams or kilograms.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Kilograms measure heavy objects (1 kg = 1,000 grams).\", \"remediation\": \"Use grams for very light objects like paperclips or feathers.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Liters measure liquid capacity, not mass.\", \"remediation\": \"Grams measure mass/weight.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2903, 6, 4, 12, 'Mass & Liquid Volume', 232, 3, 'A large pitcher contains 2 liters of lemonade. How many milliliters of lemonade is that?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '200 milliliters', '2,000 milliliters', '20 milliliters', '20,000 milliliters', 'B', 'active', '1 liter equals 1,000 milliliters. So 2 liters = 2 × 1,000 = 2,000 milliliters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 100 instead of 1,000.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember: 1 liter = 1,000 milliliters, so 2 L = 2,000 mL.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 10.\", \"remediation\": \"1 L = 1,000 mL: 2 L = 2,000 mL.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 10,000.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by 1,000: 2,000 mL.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2904, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Rectangles', 236, 3, 'A rectangle has a length of 7 cm and a width of 4 cm. What is the AREA of the rectangle?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '14 square cm', '22 cm', '11 square cm', '28 square cm', 'D', 'active', 'Area of a rectangle = length × width = 7 × 4 = 28 square cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 7 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Area = 7 × 4 = 28 square cm.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the perimeter: 2(7 + 4) = 22 cm.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is length MULTIPLIED by width (7 × 4 = 28 sq cm), not perimeter.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 7 + 4 = 11.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply length by width to find area: 7 × 4 = 28.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2905, 6, 4, 12, 'Perimeter', 238, 3, 'What is the PERIMETER of a square with a side length of 6 inches?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '24 inches', '36 square inches', '12 inches', '18 inches', 'A', 'active', 'A square has 4 equal sides: Perimeter = 4 × 6 = 24 inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the area: 6 × 6 = 36 sq inches.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is the distance around: add all 4 sides (6 + 6 + 6 + 6 = 24 in).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only added two sides (6 + 6 = 12).\", \"remediation\": \"A square has 4 sides: 4 × 6 = 24 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 6 by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"A square has 4 sides: 4 × 6 = 24 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2906, 6, 4, 12, 'Area Concepts & Unit Squares', 235, 3, 'A rectangle is completely tiled with 5 rows of 3 square units with no gaps or overlaps. What is the area?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '12 square units', '8 square units', '15 square units', '16 square units', 'C', 'active', 'Multiply rows by unit squares in each row: 5 × 3 = 15 square units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"5 × 3 = 15 square units.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 5 + 3 = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is total squares: 5 rows of 3 = 5 × 3 = 15 square units.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated perimeter: 2(5 + 3) = 16.\", \"remediation\": \"Area counts inside squares: 5 × 3 = 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2907, 6, 4, 12, 'Time & Time Intervals', 231, 3, 'A science experiment starts at 9:15 a.m. and ends at 9:50 a.m. How many minutes did the experiment take?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '45 minutes', '25 minutes', '65 minutes', '35 minutes', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract the starting minutes from ending minutes: 50 - 15 = 35 minutes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 50 - 5 = 45.\", \"remediation\": \"50 - 15 = 35 minutes.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"50 - 15 = 35 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 50 + 15 = 65.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the elapsed time by subtracting: 50 - 15 = 35 minutes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2908, 6, 4, 12, 'Perimeter', 238, 3, 'A triangle has sides of lengths 5 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm. What is its perimeter?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '26 cm', '21 cm', '315 square cm', '16 cm', 'B', 'active', 'Perimeter is the sum of all side lengths: 5 + 7 + 9 = 21 cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error.\", \"remediation\": \"5 + 7 + 9 = 21 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied the side lengths: 5 × 7 × 9 = 315.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is the sum of sides (addition): 5 + 7 + 9 = 21 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added only two sides (7 + 9 = 16).\", \"remediation\": \"Add all three sides: 5 + 7 + 9 = 21 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2909, 6, 4, 12, 'Time & Time Intervals', 231, 3, 'A movie started at 1:45 p.m. and lasted for 50 minutes. At what time did the movie end?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '1:95 p.m.', '2:45 p.m.', '2:35 p.m.', '2:25 p.m.', 'C', 'active', 'From 1:45, it takes 15 minutes to reach 2:00. Remaining minutes = 50 - 15 = 35 minutes. Ending time = 2:35 p.m.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used base-10 addition (45 + 50 = 95), forgetting an hour has 60 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"60 minutes makes a new hour: 1:45 + 15 min = 2:00, then + 35 min = 2:35 p.m.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 1 hour (60 min) instead of 50 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"50 minutes is 10 minutes less than an hour: 2:45 - 10 min = 2:35 p.m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 10 minutes too many.\", \"remediation\": \"1:45 + 50 minutes = 2:35 p.m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2910, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Rectangles', 236, 3, 'A rectangular classroom rug has an area of 54 square feet. The width of the rug is 6 feet. What is the LENGTH of the rug?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '9 feet', '324 feet', '8 feet', '48 feet', 'A', 'active', 'Area = length × width. Length = Area ÷ width = 54 ÷ 6 = 9 feet.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 54 × 6 = 324.\", \"remediation\": \"Length is a factor of area: divide 54 by 6 = 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"6 × 8 = 48, not 54.\", \"remediation\": \"54 ÷ 6 = 9 feet.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 54 - 6 = 48.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide area by width to find length: 54 ÷ 6 = 9 feet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2911, 6, 4, 12, 'Decomposing Area', 237, 3, 'An L-shaped figure is formed by two non-overlapping rectangles: Rectangle A has dimensions 4 m by 5 m, and Rectangle B has dimensions 3 m by 2 m. What is the TOTAL area of the figure?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '20 square meters', '14 square meters', '26 square meters', '40 square meters', 'C', 'active', 'Area of Rectangle A = 4 × 5 = 20 sq m. Area of Rectangle B = 3 × 2 = 6 sq m. Total area = 20 + 6 = 26 sq m.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only calculated Rectangle A (20 sq m).\", \"remediation\": \"Add the area of both rectangles: 20 + 6 = 26 sq meters.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added the dimensions: 4 + 5 + 3 + 2 = 14.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate individual areas first and then add: (4×5) + (3×2) = 26.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 20 × 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Total area = 20 + 6 = 26 square meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2912, 6, 4, 12, 'Mass & Liquid Volume', 232, 3, 'A baker has a bowl containing 450 grams of flour. He adds 375 grams more flour. Then he uses 280 grams for a cake. How many grams of flour are left in the bowl?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '645 grams', '825 grams', '525 grams', '545 grams', 'D', 'active', 'Step 1: 450 + 375 = 825 grams. Step 2: 825 - 280 = 545 grams.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"450 + 375 = 825; 825 - 280 = 545 g.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student stopped after adding (825 g) and did not subtract the used flour.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the 280 g used: 825 - 280 = 545 grams.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made an error in regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"825 - 280 = 545 grams.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2913, 6, 4, 12, 'Perimeter', 238, 3, 'A rectangle has a length of 8 cm and a perimeter of 26 cm. What is the WIDTH of the rectangle?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '10 cm', '5 cm', '18 cm', '9 cm', 'B', 'active', 'Perimeter = 2(L + W) = 26. Semi-perimeter L + W = 13. Width = 13 - 8 = 5 cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student found the sum of both widths (2W = 26 - 16 = 10 cm) without dividing by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the remaining 10 cm by 2 to find one width: 10 ÷ 2 = 5 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 26 - 8 = 18.\", \"remediation\": \"Account for both lengths: 26 - 16 = 10, then 10 / 2 = 5 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"Width = (26 - 16) / 2 = 5 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2914, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Rectangles', 236, 3, 'Which two rectangles have the SAME perimeter but DIFFERENT areas?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', 'Rectangle 1 (2 by 8) and Rectangle 2 (4 by 6)', 'Rectangle 1 (4 by 4) and Rectangle 2 (2 by 8)', 'Rectangle 1 (3 by 4) and Rectangle 2 (4 by 3)', 'Rectangle 1 (2 by 5) and Rectangle 2 (3 by 5)', 'A', 'active', 'Perimeter 1: 2(2+8) = 20; Area 1: 2×8 = 16. Perimeter 2: 2(4+6) = 20; Area 2: 4×6 = 24. Same perimeter (20), different areas (16 vs 24).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Perimeter 1 is 16, Perimeter 2 is 20 (different perimeters).\", \"remediation\": \"Both must have the same perimeter (Option A: both have perimeter 20).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"These are the exact same rectangle rotated (same perimeter and same area).\", \"remediation\": \"Rotated shapes have the identical area and perimeter.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"These have different perimeters (14 vs 16).\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeters must be equal.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2915, 6, 4, 12, 'Mass & Liquid Volume', 232, 3, 'A beaker has 700 mL of water. A student pours in 4 equal small cups of water, bringing the total to 900 mL. How many mL of water were in each cup?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '200 mL', '50 mL', '25 mL', '100 mL', 'B', 'active', 'Total added = 900 - 700 = 200 mL. Since there are 4 equal cups: 200 ÷ 4 = 50 mL per cup.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"200 mL is the total water added, not the amount in one cup.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the added 200 mL by the 4 cups: 200 ÷ 4 = 50 mL.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 100 by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"200 / 4 = 50 mL.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 200 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by 4 cups: 50 mL.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2916, 6, 4, 12, 'Time & Time Intervals', 231, 3, 'Oliver finished his homework at 5:20 p.m. He spent 45 minutes doing math and 20 minutes reading. At what time did Oliver START his homework?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '3:55 p.m.', '4:35 p.m.', '6:25 p.m.', '4:15 p.m.', 'D', 'active', 'Total time spent = 45 + 20 = 65 minutes = 1 hour and 5 minutes. Count back 1 hour from 5:20: 4:20. Count back 5 minutes: 4:15 p.m.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted too many minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"5:20 - 65 minutes = 4:15 p.m.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only subtracted 45 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract total time (65 min): 5:20 - 1 hr 5 min = 4:15 p.m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 65 minutes instead of counting backwards.\", \"remediation\": \"To find start time, subtract elapsed time from finish time: 4:15 p.m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2917, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Rectangles', 236, 3, 'A garden bed is 8 feet long and 5 feet wide. If the length is increased by 2 feet, by how many square feet does the AREA increase?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '10 square feet', '16 square feet', '50 square feet', '2 square feet', 'A', 'active', 'Original area = 8 × 5 = 40 sq ft. New area = (8 + 2) × 5 = 10 × 5 = 50 sq ft. Increase = 50 - 40 = 10 square feet (or 2 ft × 5 ft = 10 sq ft).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 8 × 2 = 16.\", \"remediation\": \"The width is 5 ft, so adding 2 ft of length adds 2 × 5 = 10 sq ft.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"50 sq ft is the new total area, not the increase.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract original area: 50 - 40 = 10 sq ft increase.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"2 feet is the linear increase, not area increase.\", \"remediation\": \"Area increase = 2 ft × 5 ft = 10 square feet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2918, 6, 4, 12, 'Decomposing Area', 237, 3, 'An artist paints an irregular room shaped like an L. The main rectangle is 9 ft by 6 ft, with a 4 ft by 3 ft rectangular section cut out from one corner. What is the area of the floor?', NULL, 3, 'High', '36 square feet', '54 square feet', '42 square feet', '66 square feet', 'C', 'active', 'Full rectangle area = 9 × 6 = 54 sq ft. Subtract the cutout corner: 4 × 3 = 12 sq ft. Floor area = 54 - 12 = 42 square feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"54 - 12 = 42 square feet.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"54 sq ft is the full area without subtracting the cutout corner.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the cutout: 54 - 12 = 42 sq ft.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added the cutout area (54 + 12 = 66).\", \"remediation\": \"The corner is cut out, so subtract: 54 - 12 = 42.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2919, 6, 4, 12, 'Time & Time Intervals', 231, 3, 'A flight departs at 11:35 a.m. and lands at 2:10 p.m. How long was the flight in hours and minutes?', NULL, 3, 'High', '3 hours and 25 minutes', '2 hours and 25 minutes', '3 hours and 35 minutes', '2 hours and 35 minutes', 'D', 'active', 'From 11:35 a.m. to 1:35 p.m. is 2 hours. From 1:35 p.m. to 2:00 p.m. is 25 minutes, plus 10 minutes to 2:10 p.m. = 35 minutes. Total = 2 hr 35 min.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student counted 3 full hours.\", \"remediation\": \"11:35 to 2:35 would be 3 hours. 2:10 is 25 minutes before 2:35, so 2 hr 35 min.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"25 min to 2:00 + 10 min = 35 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added an extra hour.\", \"remediation\": \"Flight duration is 2 hours and 35 minutes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2920, 6, 4, 12, 'Perimeter', 238, 3, 'A rectangular field has a perimeter of 40 meters. Which dimensions would give this field the GREATEST possible area?', NULL, 3, 'High', '18 meters by 2 meters (Area = 36 sq m)', '10 meters by 10 meters (Area = 100 sq m)', '12 meters by 8 meters (Area = 96 sq m)', '15 meters by 5 meters (Area = 75 sq m)', 'B', 'active', 'For a given perimeter, a square (equal sides) always produces the maximum possible area: 10 × 10 = 100 sq meters (perimeter = 4 × 10 = 40 m).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Long narrow rectangles produce the smallest area for a given perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Maximum area is 100 sq m (10 by 10).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"96 sq m is less than 100 sq m.\", \"remediation\": \"A square yields the maximum area: 10 × 10 = 100 sq m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"75 sq m is smaller than 100 sq m.\", \"remediation\": \"The closer the dimensions are to each other, the larger the area.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2921, 6, 4, 12, 'Mass & Liquid Volume', 232, 3, 'A crate has a mass of 4 kg. Inside are 6 identical boxes. The total mass of the crate and the boxes is 22 kg. What is the mass of ONE box?', NULL, 3, 'High', '4 kg', '18 kg', '3 kg', '2.5 kg', 'C', 'active', 'Mass of 6 boxes = 22 - 4 = 18 kg. Mass of one box = 18 ÷ 6 = 3 kg.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 4 kg.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the crate mass first: 22 - 4 = 18 kg, then 18 ÷ 6 = 3 kg.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"18 kg is the mass of all 6 boxes combined.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 18 kg by 6 to find the mass of one box: 3 kg.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"18 / 6 = 3 kg.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2922, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Rectangles', 236, 3, 'A wall is 12 feet long and 8 feet high. It has a window that is 3 feet by 4 feet. What is the area of the wall that needs to be painted (excluding the window)?', NULL, 3, 'High', '84 square feet', '82 square feet', '96 square feet', '12 square feet', 'A', 'active', 'Total wall area = 12 × 8 = 96 sq ft. Window area = 3 × 4 = 12 sq ft. Paint area = 96 - 12 = 84 sq ft.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"96 - 12 = 84 square feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"96 sq ft is the total area without subtracting the window.\", \"remediation\": \"The window is not painted: subtract 12 sq ft from 96 sq ft = 84 sq ft.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"12 sq ft is the area of the window.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract window from total wall: 96 - 12 = 84 sq ft.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2923, 6, 4, 12, 'Perimeter', 238, 3, 'Two identical squares, each with a side length of 5 cm, are placed side-by-side to form a rectangle. What is the PERIMETER of the new rectangle?', NULL, 3, 'High', '40 cm', '20 cm', '30 cm', '50 cm', 'C', 'active', 'The new rectangle has length = 5 + 5 = 10 cm and width = 5 cm. Perimeter = 2(10 + 5) = 2(15) = 30 cm. (The two joined sides are interior and not counted).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added the perimeters of both independent squares (20 + 20 = 40 cm).\", \"remediation\": \"The joined inside sides disappear from the perimeter: 40 - 10 = 30 cm.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"20 cm is the perimeter of a single square.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter of the 10 by 5 rectangle is 2(10 + 5) = 30 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"50 is the area (10 × 5), not perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is the distance around: 10 + 5 + 10 + 5 = 30 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2924, 6, 4, 12, 'Time & Time Intervals', 231, 3, 'A marathon runner started running at 7:48 a.m. and finished at 10:15 a.m. Exactly how many minutes did the runner take?', NULL, 3, 'High', '187 minutes', '167 minutes', '127 minutes', '147 minutes', 'D', 'active', 'From 7:48 to 8:00 is 12 min. From 8:00 to 10:00 is 120 min (2 hours). From 10:00 to 10:15 is 15 min. Total = 12 + 120 + 15 = 147 minutes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated elapsed time.\", \"remediation\": \"Total duration is 147 minutes.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added an extra 20 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"12 min + 120 min + 15 min = 147 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted only 1 hour between 8 and 10.\", \"remediation\": \"From 8:00 to 10:00 is 2 hours (120 min): total = 147 minutes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2925, 6, 4, 12, 'Decomposing Area', 237, 3, 'A rectangular patio measures 7 meters by 8 meters. A square fountain with sides of 3 meters sits in the middle. What is the remaining patio area surrounding the fountain?', NULL, 3, 'High', '56 square meters', '47 square meters', '9 square meters', '38 square meters', 'B', 'active', 'Total patio area = 7 × 8 = 56 sq m. Fountain area = 3 × 3 = 9 sq m. Remaining area = 56 - 9 = 47 square meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"56 sq m is the patio area without subtracting the fountain.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract fountain area (9 sq m): 56 - 9 = 47 sq m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"9 sq m is only the fountain area.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 9 from 56 to find the surrounding area: 47 sq m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 3 × 6 = 18.\", \"remediation\": \"Fountain area is 3 × 3 = 9 sq m. 56 - 9 = 47 sq m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2926, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 239, 4, 'How many meters are in 5 kilometers?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '5,000 meters', '50,000 meters', '500 meters', '50 meters', 'A', 'active', '1 kilometer = 1,000 meters. Therefore, 5 kilometers = 5 × 1,000 = 5,000 meters.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 10,000.\", \"remediation\": \"5 × 1,000 = 5,000 meters.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 100 instead of 1,000.\", \"remediation\": \"Kilo- means 1,000. 1 km = 1,000 m, so 5 km = 5,000 m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 10.\", \"remediation\": \"1 km = 1,000 m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2927, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 239, 4, 'How many ounces are in 3 pounds?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '30 ounces', '48 ounces', '36 ounces', '24 ounces', 'B', 'active', '1 pound = 16 ounces. Therefore, 3 pounds = 3 × 16 = 48 ounces.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed 1 pound = 10 ounces (base-10 assumption).\", \"remediation\": \"In customary units, 1 pound = 16 ounces. 3 × 16 = 48 ounces.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 3 × 12.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 16 ounces in a pound: 3 × 16 = 48 oz.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 3 × 8.\", \"remediation\": \"16 ounces per pound: 3 × 16 = 48 oz.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2928, 6, 4, 12, 'Angle Concepts', 243, 4, 'An angle that turns through 90 one-degree angles is called what type of angle?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'A straight angle', 'An acute angle', 'An obtuse angle', 'A right angle', 'D', 'active', 'An angle measuring exactly 90 degrees is a right angle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A straight angle is 180 degrees.\", \"remediation\": \"90° is a right angle.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Acute angles are strictly less than 90 degrees.\", \"remediation\": \"An angle of exactly 90° is a right angle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Obtuse angles are greater than 90 degrees.\", \"remediation\": \"Exactly 90° is a right angle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2929, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Angles', 244, 4, 'A student uses a protractor to measure an acute angle. The protractor shows rays passing through 65° and 115°. What is the measure of the acute angle?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '65°', '115°', '180°', '50°', 'A', 'active', 'An acute angle is strictly less than 90°. Since 65° < 90° and 115° > 90°, the angle measures 65°.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student read the wrong (obtuse) scale on the protractor.\", \"remediation\": \"Acute angles are less than 90°, so choose 65°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"180° is a straight line.\", \"remediation\": \"The acute angle measures 65°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 115 - 65 = 50.\", \"remediation\": \"Read the scale starting from 0: 65°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2930, 6, 4, 12, 'Rectangle Area & Perimeter', 241, 4, 'A rectangle has a length of 9 meters and a width of 5 meters. What is its PERIMETER?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '38 meters', '45 square meters', '28 meters', '14 meters', 'C', 'active', 'Perimeter = 2(length + width) = 2(9 + 5) = 2(14) = 28 meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"2(9 + 5) = 28 meters.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"45 sq m is the area (9 × 5), not perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter is distance around: 2(9 + 5) = 28 meters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student only added length and width (9 + 5 = 14) without multiplying by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangle has 4 sides: 2 × 14 = 28 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2931, 6, 4, 12, 'Unknown Angles', 245, 4, 'A right angle (90°) is decomposed into two smaller angles. One angle measures 35°. What is the measure of the other angle?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '65°', '145°', '45°', '55°', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract from 90°: 90° - 35° = 55°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated 90 - 35.\", \"remediation\": \"90 - 35 = 55°.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted from 180° (supplementary angle).\", \"remediation\": \"A right angle is 90°, so subtract from 90°: 90 - 35 = 55°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 45°.\", \"remediation\": \"90 - 35 = 55°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2932, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 239, 4, 'How many minutes are in 4 hours?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '200 minutes', '240 minutes', '400 minutes', '160 minutes', 'B', 'active', '1 hour = 60 minutes. 4 hours = 4 × 60 = 240 minutes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 60 = 240 minutes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed 1 hour = 100 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 60 minutes in an hour: 4 × 60 = 240 minutes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 4 × 40.\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 60 = 240 minutes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2933, 6, 4, 12, 'Angle Concepts', 243, 4, 'How many degrees are in a complete full-circle rotation?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '180°', '90°', '360°', '100°', 'C', 'active', 'A complete full circle consists of 360 degrees.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"180° is a half circle (straight line).\", \"remediation\": \"A full circle is 360°.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"90° is a quarter circle (right angle).\", \"remediation\": \"A full circle is 360°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 100°.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 360 degrees in a full rotation.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2934, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Word Problems', 240, 4, 'A runner trains on a track. On Monday she runs 2 kilometers. On Tuesday she runs 850 meters. What is the TOTAL distance she ran in meters?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '2,850 meters', '1,050 meters', '852 meters', '2,085 meters', 'A', 'active', 'Convert 2 km to meters: 2 × 1,000 = 2,000 meters. Total = 2,000 + 850 = 2,850 meters.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 2000 - 850 = 1150 or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the two distances: 2,000 + 850 = 2,850 meters.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 2 + 850 directly without converting kilometers to meters.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert 2 km to 2,000 m first: 2,000 + 850 = 2,850 m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miswrote the digits.\", \"remediation\": \"2,000 + 850 = 2,850 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2935, 6, 4, 12, 'Rectangle Area & Perimeter', 241, 4, 'The perimeter of a rectangular swimming pool is 64 meters. The width of the pool is 12 meters. What is the LENGTH of the pool?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '26 meters', '52 meters', '20 meters', '40 meters', 'C', 'active', 'Perimeter P = 2(L + W) = 64. Semi-perimeter L + W = 32. Length = 32 - 12 = 20 meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 64 - 12 = 52, then divided by 2 = 26.\", \"remediation\": \"Both widths must be subtracted: 64 - 24 = 40, then 40 ÷ 2 = 20 meters.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 64 - 12 = 52 and forgot to divide by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Length = (64 - 24) / 2 = 20 meters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"40 meters is the sum of both lengths (2L), not a single length.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 40 by 2 to find length: 20 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2936, 6, 4, 12, 'Unknown Angles', 245, 4, 'Three angles form a straight line (180°). Angle 1 measures 45° and Angle 2 measures 75°. What is the measure of Angle 3?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '70°', '120°', '50°', '60°', 'D', 'active', 'Sum of known angles = 45° + 75° = 120°. Angle 3 = 180° - 120° = 60°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"180 - (45 + 75) = 60°.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"120° is the sum of the first two angles, not Angle 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 120° from 180° to find Angle 3: 180° - 120° = 60°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated 180 - 120.\", \"remediation\": \"180 - 120 = 60°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:37', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2937, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 239, 4, 'A punch bowl contains 3 gallons of juice. How many quarts of juice are in the bowl?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '6 quarts', '12 quarts', '24 quarts', '15 quarts', 'B', 'active', '1 gallon = 4 quarts. 3 gallons = 3 × 4 = 12 quarts.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 3 × 2.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 quarts in 1 gallon: 3 × 4 = 12 quarts.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"24 is the number of pints, not quarts.\", \"remediation\": \"1 gal = 4 qt, so 3 gal = 12 qt.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 4 = 12 quarts.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2938, 6, 4, 12, 'Measuring Angles', 244, 4, 'An angle turns through 3/4 of a full circle. How many degrees does this angle measure?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '270°', '240°', '180°', '90°', 'A', 'active', 'A full circle is 360°. (3/4) × 360° = 3 × 90° = 270°.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated (2/3) × 360° = 240°.\", \"remediation\": \"(3/4) of 360° is 270°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"180° is 1/2 of a circle.\", \"remediation\": \"(3/4) × 360° = 270°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"90° is 1/4 of a circle.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 90° by 3: 270°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2939, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Word Problems', 240, 4, 'A bag of potatoes weighs 4 pounds and 6 ounces. How many TOTAL ounces does the bag weigh?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '46 ounces', '70 ounces', '64 ounces', '74 ounces', 'B', 'active', 'Convert pounds to ounces: 4 × 16 = 64 ounces. Add remaining ounces: 64 + 6 = 70 ounces.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student concatenated digits 4 and 6 to make 46.\", \"remediation\": \"1 pound = 16 ounces. 4 × 16 = 64 oz, then 64 + 6 = 70 ounces.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"64 oz is only the 4 pounds, forgetting the 6 ounces.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the extra 6 ounces: 64 + 6 = 70 ounces.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error.\", \"remediation\": \"64 + 6 = 70 ounces.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2940, 6, 4, 12, 'Rectangle Area & Perimeter', 241, 4, 'A rectangular painting has an area of 108 square inches and a width of 9 inches. What is the PERIMETER of the painting?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '48 inches', '21 inches', '38 inches', '42 inches', 'D', 'active', 'Length = Area ÷ width = 108 ÷ 9 = 12 inches. Perimeter = 2(12 + 9) = 2(21) = 42 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 4 × 12 = 48.\", \"remediation\": \"Width is 9 in and length is 12 in: 2(12 + 9) = 42 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"21 inches is the semi-perimeter (12 + 9) without multiplying by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter requires 2(L + W): 2 × 21 = 42 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"2(12 + 9) = 42 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2941, 6, 4, 12, 'Unknown Angles', 245, 4, 'Angle ABC is a straight angle (180°). Ray BD divides it into angle ABD and angle DBC. If angle ABD is twice as large as angle DBC, what is the measure of angle DBC?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '60°', '120°', '90°', '45°', 'A', 'active', 'Let angle DBC = x. Then angle ABD = 2x. 2x + x = 180° -> 3x = 180° -> x = 60°.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"120° is the measure of the larger angle (angle ABD = 2 × 60°).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the smaller angle DBC: x = 60°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed a right angle.\", \"remediation\": \"3x = 180° -> x = 60°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 180 by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 180 by 3 parts: 60°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2942, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Word Problems', 240, 4, 'A science teacher pours 3 liters and 450 milliliters of solution into a container. Later she pours in 1 liter and 750 milliliters more. What is the total volume of solution?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '4 liters and 200 milliliters', '4 liters and 1,200 milliliters', '5 liters and 200 milliliters', '5 liters and 100 milliliters', 'C', 'active', 'Liters: 3 + 1 = 4 L. Milliliters: 450 + 750 = 1,200 mL = 1 L and 200 mL. Total = 4 L + 1 L + 200 mL = 5 L and 200 mL.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to add the regrouped 1 liter.\", \"remediation\": \"4 L + 1 L (from 1,000 mL) = 5 L 200 mL.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student did not regroup 1,200 mL into 1 liter and 200 mL.\", \"remediation\": \"1,000 mL = 1 L. Regroup 1,200 mL as 1 L 200 mL, giving 5 L 200 mL.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error in milliliters.\", \"remediation\": \"450 + 750 = 1,200 mL, leaving 200 mL.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2943, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Word Problems', 240, 4, 'A carpenter needs 4 pieces of wood, each 2 feet 8 inches long. What is the TOTAL length of wood needed in feet and inches?', NULL, 4, 'High', '8 feet 32 inches', '9 feet 8 inches', '11 feet 2 inches', '10 feet 8 inches', 'D', 'active', 'Multiply feet: 4 × 2 ft = 8 ft. Multiply inches: 4 × 8 in = 32 in. Convert 32 in to feet: 32 ÷ 12 = 2 ft with 8 in remaining. Total = 8 ft + 2 ft + 8 in = 10 ft 8 in.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student did not regroup the 32 inches into feet.\", \"remediation\": \"12 inches = 1 foot. 32 inches = 2 feet and 8 inches. 8 ft + 2 ft 8 in = 10 ft 8 in.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only added 1 foot from inches.\", \"remediation\": \"32 inches has two 12-inch groups: 2 ft 8 in. 8 + 2 = 10 ft 8 in.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"Total is 10 feet 8 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2944, 6, 4, 12, 'Rectangle Area & Perimeter', 241, 4, 'A rectangular garden has a perimeter of 44 meters and an area of 112 square meters. What are the dimensions (length and width) of the garden?', NULL, 4, 'High', '20 meters by 2 meters', '14 meters by 8 meters', '16 meters by 7 meters', '12 meters by 10 meters', 'B', 'active', 'Check perimeter: 2(14 + 8) = 2(22) = 44 m. Check area: 14 × 8 = 112 sq m. Both conditions are satisfied.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"20 × 2 = 40 sq m.\", \"remediation\": \"Dimensions are 14 m by 8 m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"16 × 7 = 112 sq m, but perimeter is 2(16 + 7) = 46 m (not 44 m).\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter must be 44 m: 2(14 + 8) = 44.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Perimeter is 44 m, but area is 12 × 10 = 120 sq m (not 112 sq m).\", \"remediation\": \"Area must be 112 sq m: 14 × 8 = 112.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2945, 6, 4, 12, 'Unknown Angles', 245, 4, 'In the diagram, four angles meet at a single central point to complete a full 360° circle. The angles measure 110°, 85°, 75°, and x. What is the value of x?', NULL, 4, 'High', '100°', '80°', '90°', '70°', 'C', 'active', 'Sum of angles around a point is 360°. Sum of known angles: 110 + 85 + 75 = 270°. x = 360° - 270° = 90°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition or subtraction error.\", \"remediation\": \"110 + 85 + 75 = 270°. 360 - 270 = 90°.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted from 350°.\", \"remediation\": \"A full circle has 360°: 360 - 270 = 90°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"x = 90°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2946, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 239, 4, 'A water jug holds 2 gallons of water. If students fill water bottles holding 1 pint each, how many bottles can be filled completely?', NULL, 4, 'High', '16 bottles', '32 bottles', '8 bottles', '4 bottles', 'A', 'active', '1 gallon = 4 quarts = 8 pints. Therefore, 2 gallons = 2 × 8 = 16 pints. 16 bottles can be filled.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"32 is the number of cups.\", \"remediation\": \"1 gallon = 8 pints, so 2 gallons = 16 pints.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"8 pints is 1 gallon, not 2 gallons.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by 2 for 2 gallons: 8 × 2 = 16 bottles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"4 is the number of quarts in 1 gallon.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 8 pints in 1 gallon, so 16 pints in 2 gallons.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2947, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Word Problems', 240, 4, 'A train leaves Station A at 8:45 a.m. and travels for 3 hours and 35 minutes to Station B. It stops for 25 minutes, then travels for 1 hour and 40 minutes to Station C. What time does the train arrive at Station C?', NULL, 4, 'High', '1:45 p.m.', '2:15 p.m.', '2:25 p.m.', '3:05 p.m.', 'C', 'active', 'Total travel and stop time = 3 hr 35 min + 25 min + 1 hr 40 min = 4 hr 100 min + 40 min = 5 hr 40 min. 8:45 a.m. + 5 hr 40 min: 8:45 + 5 hr = 1:45 p.m.; 1:45 + 40 min = 2:25 p.m.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to add the second leg of the trip.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate total elapsed time (5 hours 40 minutes): 8:45 a.m. + 5:40 = 2:25 p.m.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"8:45 + 5 hours 40 minutes = 2:25 p.m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added an extra 40 minutes.\", \"remediation\": \"Arrival time is 2:25 p.m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2948, 6, 4, 12, 'Rectangle Area & Perimeter', 241, 4, 'A square has an area of 64 square centimeters. A rectangle has the same perimeter as this square. If the width of the rectangle is 6 cm, what is the area of the rectangle?', NULL, 4, 'High', '72 square centimeters', '64 square centimeters', '48 square centimeters', '60 square centimeters', 'D', 'active', 'Side of square = √64 = 8 cm. Perimeter of square = 4 × 8 = 32 cm. Rectangle perimeter = 2(L + 6) = 32 -> L + 6 = 16 -> L = 10 cm. Rectangle area = 10 × 6 = 60 sq cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is 60 square centimeters.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed equal perimeter implies equal area.\", \"remediation\": \"Rectangles with the same perimeter have different areas: L = 10, W = 6 -> Area = 60 sq cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 8 × 6 = 48.\", \"remediation\": \"Length is 10 cm, so area = 10 × 6 = 60 sq cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2949, 6, 4, 12, 'Unknown Angles', 245, 4, 'A ray divides a straight angle of 180° into two angles such that one angle is 30° more than twice the other angle. What is the measure of the smaller angle?', NULL, 4, 'High', '130°', '50°', '60°', '45°', 'B', 'active', 'Let smaller angle = x. Larger angle = 2x + 30. Equation: x + (2x + 30) = 180 -> 3x + 30 = 180 -> 3x = 150 -> x = 50°.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"130° is the larger angle (2(50) + 30 = 130°).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the smaller angle: x = 50°.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 180 by 3 without subtracting 30 first.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 30 first: 180 - 30 = 150. Then 150 ÷ 3 = 50°.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 45°.\", \"remediation\": \"Smaller angle is 50°.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2950, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Word Problems', 240, 4, 'A chef bought 3 kg 600 g of beef and 2 kg 850 g of chicken. He cooked 4 kg 900 g of meat for a banquet. How much meat was left over in grams?', NULL, 4, 'High', '1,550 grams', '1,650 grams', '1,450 grams', '2,550 grams', 'A', 'active', 'Total meat = 3,600 g + 2,850 g = 6,450 g. Cooked = 4,900 g. Left over = 6,450 - 4,900 = 1,550 grams.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 100 g too much.\", \"remediation\": \"1,550 grams remaining.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made an error in subtraction: 6,450 - 4,900 = 1,550, not 1,450.\", \"remediation\": \"6,450 - 4,900 = 1,550 grams.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error in the thousands place.\", \"remediation\": \"6,450 - 4,900 = 1,550 g.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 17:56:38', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2951, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'How many centimeters are equal to 4 meters?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '40 cm', '400 cm', '4,000 cm', '0.04 cm', 'B', 'active', 'There are 100 centimeters in 1 meter. Therefore, 4 meters = 4 × 100 = 400 centimeters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 10 instead of 100.\", \"remediation\": \"Recall that 1 meter = 100 centimeters (centi- means one hundredth).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 1,000 (confused centi- with milli-).\", \"remediation\": \"1 meter = 100 centimeters, whereas 1 meter = 1,000 millimeters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 100 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Converting from a larger unit (meters) to a smaller unit (centimeters) requires multiplication.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2952, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'A pitcher holds 3,000 milliliters of juice. How many liters is this?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '0.3 L', '30 L', '300 L', '3 L', 'D', 'active', '1 liter = 1,000 milliliters. Dividing 3,000 mL by 1,000 gives 3 L.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 10,000.\", \"remediation\": \"3,000 ÷ 1,000 = 3 L.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 100 instead of 1,000.\", \"remediation\": \"Recall that milli- means 1/1,000, so 1 liter = 1,000 milliliters.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 10.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 1,000 milliliters in a liter, so divide 3,000 by 1,000 = 3 L.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2953, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'How many ounces are in 5 pounds? (1 pound = 16 ounces)', NULL, 5, 'Low', '80 oz', '50 oz', '85 oz', '32 oz', 'A', 'active', '5 pounds × 16 ounces per pound = 80 ounces.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed 1 pound = 10 ounces.\", \"remediation\": \"In customary units, 1 pound = 16 ounces. 5 × 16 = 80 oz.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made a multiplication error.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 5 × 16: 5 × 10 = 50, 5 × 6 = 30; 50 + 30 = 80 oz.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled 16 instead of multiplying by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by 5 pounds: 5 × 16 = 80 oz.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2954, 6, 4, 12, 'Unit Cubes', 249, 5, 'A solid rectangular prism is packed completely with 1-centimeter unit cubes with no gaps or overlaps. If it takes 24 cubes to fill it, what is its volume?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '12 cubic centimeters', '24 square centimeters', '24 cubic centimeters', '48 cubic centimeters', 'C', 'active', 'Volume is the number of unit cubes needed to fill a 3D space. 24 unit cubes of 1 cm each give a volume of 24 cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the number of unit cubes.\", \"remediation\": \"Each unit cube contributes 1 cubic centimeter; 24 cubes = 24 cm³.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used units of area (square centimeters) instead of volume.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume measures 3D space and uses cubic units (cm³).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the count of unit cubes.\", \"remediation\": \"The volume simply equals the total count of unit cubes: 24 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2955, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume Concepts', 248, 5, 'What kind of units are used to measure the volume of a solid object?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'Square units', 'Linear units', 'Degree units', 'Cubic units', 'D', 'active', 'Volume measures the amount of three-dimensional space occupied by an object, which is measured in cubic units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Square units measure 2D surface area, not 3D volume.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume measures 3D capacity using cubic units (e.g., cubic inches, cm³).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Linear units measure 1D length or perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Length is 1D, area is 2D (square units), and volume is 3D (cubic units).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Degrees measure angles or temperature.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume is measured in cubic units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2956, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 250, 5, 'A rectangular box has a length of 5 cm, width of 2 cm, and height of 3 cm. What is its volume?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '60 cubic cm', '30 cubic cm', '10 cubic cm', '20 cubic cm', 'B', 'active', 'Volume of a rectangular prism = length × width × height = 5 × 2 × 3 = 30 cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the volume.\", \"remediation\": \"The formula is V = l × w × h = 30 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only added the dimensions (5 + 2 + 3 = 10).\", \"remediation\": \"Volume is calculated by multiplying dimensions: V = l × w × h.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied only two dimensions or calculated perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply all three dimensions: 5 × 2 = 10; 10 × 3 = 30 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2957, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 250, 5, 'A rectangular box has a base area of 20 square inches and a height of 4 inches. What is its volume?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '24 cubic inches', '40 cubic inches', '80 cubic inches', '160 cubic inches', 'C', 'active', 'Volume = Base Area × Height = 20 × 4 = 80 cubic inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added base area and height (20 + 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Volume is Base Area multiplied by height: V = B × h.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied base area by 2 instead of 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 20 by the full height 4: 20 × 4 = 80 cubic inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the correct product.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume = 20 × 4 = 80 cubic inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2958, 6, 4, 12, 'Line Plots & Fraction Measurements', 247, 5, 'Four pencils have lengths of 1/2 inch, 1/2 inch, 3/4 inch, and 1/4 inch. What is the total length of the two 1/2-inch pencils combined?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '1 inch', '1 1/2 inches', '1/2 inch', '2/4 inch', 'A', 'active', '1/2 + 1/2 = 2/2 = 1 whole inch.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student included an extra half.\", \"remediation\": \"1/2 + 1/2 = 1 inch.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student took the length of only one pencil.\", \"remediation\": \"Add both pencils together: 1/2 + 1/2 = 1.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added denominators (1/2 + 1/2 = 2/4).\", \"remediation\": \"When adding fractions with like denominators, add only the numerators: 1/2 + 1/2 = 2/2 = 1.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2959, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'A runner completes a 3.5-kilometer race. How many meters did the runner complete?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '350 meters', '35,000 meters', '3,500 meters', '0.0035 meters', 'C', 'active', '1 kilometer = 1,000 meters. 3.5 × 1,000 = 3,500 meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 100 instead of 1,000.\", \"remediation\": \"Kilo- means 1,000; multiply 3.5 by 1,000 by shifting decimal 3 places right: 3,500 m.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 10,000.\", \"remediation\": \"1 km = 1,000 m: 3.5 × 1,000 = 3,500 m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Converting from a larger unit (km) to a smaller unit (m) requires multiplying.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2960, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'A board is 8 feet and 6 inches long. What is the total length in inches?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '108 inches', '96 inches', '86 inches', '102 inches', 'D', 'active', '1 foot = 12 inches. 8 feet = 8 × 12 = 96 inches. Adding the remaining 6 inches gives 96 + 6 = 102 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 9 × 12.\", \"remediation\": \"8 × 12 = 96; 96 + 6 = 102.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to add the extra 6 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"8 feet = 96 inches. Add the 6 inches: 96 + 6 = 102 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student simply joined the numbers 8 and 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert feet to inches first: 8 × 12 = 96, then add 6 = 102.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2961, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'A recipe calls for 2.4 kilograms of flour. How many grams of flour is this?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '240 grams', '2,400 grams', '24,000 grams', '0.0024 grams', 'B', 'active', '1 kilogram = 1,000 grams. 2.4 × 1,000 = 2,400 grams.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 100 instead of 1,000.\", \"remediation\": \"1 kg = 1,000 g: shift the decimal point 3 places to the right: 2,400 g.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student shifted decimal point 4 places instead of 3.\", \"remediation\": \"2.4 × 1,000 = 2,400 grams.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 1,000 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Kilograms to grams is large to small: multiply by 1,000.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2962, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume Concepts', 248, 5, 'A prism is built using 3 layers of unit cubes. Each layer has 4 rows with 5 cubes in each row. What is the volume of the prism?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '60 unit cubes', '23 unit cubes', '20 unit cubes', '35 unit cubes', 'A', 'active', 'Each layer has 4 × 5 = 20 cubes. With 3 layers, the total volume is 20 × 3 = 60 unit cubes.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added all dimensions: 4 + 5 + 3 = 12 or 20 + 3 = 23.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply dimensions: 4 × 5 × 3 = 60.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only found the number of cubes in one layer.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the cubes per layer by the number of layers: 20 × 3 = 60.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student computed (4 + 5) × 3 or made a calculation error.\", \"remediation\": \"Find layer area (4 × 5 = 20) and multiply by height (3): 20 × 3 = 60.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2963, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 250, 5, 'A storage container has a length of 12 inches, a width of 8 inches, and a height of 5 inches. What is its volume?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '240 cubic inches', '480 cubic inches', '960 cubic inches', '25 cubic inches', 'B', 'active', 'V = l × w × h = 12 × 8 × 5 = 96 × 5 = 480 cubic inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the correct volume or made a calculation error.\", \"remediation\": \"12 × 8 = 96; 96 × 5 = 480 cubic inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the volume.\", \"remediation\": \"V = 12 × 8 × 5 = 480 cubic inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added the dimensions: 12 + 8 + 5 = 25.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume requires multiplication of dimensions, not addition.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2964, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 250, 5, 'A rectangular aquarium has a volume of 720 cubic inches. If the base area is 60 square inches, what is its height?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '15 inches', '10 inches', '660 inches', '12 inches', 'D', 'active', 'Height = Volume ÷ Base Area = 720 ÷ 60 = 12 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated division.\", \"remediation\": \"720 ÷ 60 = 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated 720 ÷ 60 as 10 (or 600 ÷ 60).\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 720 by 60: 72 ÷ 6 = 12 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted base area from volume (720 - 60).\", \"remediation\": \"Since V = B × h, height is found by division: h = V ÷ B.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2965, 6, 4, 12, 'Composite Volume', 251, 5, 'An L-shaped solid is composed of two rectangular prisms. Prism A has dimensions 4 cm × 3 cm × 2 cm. Prism B has dimensions 2 cm × 3 cm × 5 cm. What is the total volume?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '54 cubic cm', '24 cubic cm', '30 cubic cm', '60 cubic cm', 'A', 'active', 'Volume of Prism A = 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cm³. Volume of Prism B = 2 × 3 × 5 = 30 cm³. Total volume = 24 + 30 = 54 cm³.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the volume of Prism A.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the volumes of both prisms: Volume A (24) + Volume B (30) = 54 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the volume of Prism B.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the volumes of both prisms: 24 + 30 = 54 cm³.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added dimensions incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Prism A = 24 cm³, Prism B = 30 cm³; 24 + 30 = 54 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2966, 6, 4, 12, 'Line Plots & Fraction Measurements', 247, 5, 'A scientist measured liquid amounts in 4 test tubes: 1/4 cup, 1/4 cup, 3/8 cup, and 3/8 cup. What is the total amount of liquid in all 4 tubes?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '1 1/2 cups', '1 cup', '1 1/4 cups', '8/8 cup', 'C', 'active', 'Convert to common denominator eighths: 1/4 = 2/8. So total = 2/8 + 2/8 + 3/8 + 3/8 = 10/8 = 1 2/8 = 1 1/4 cups.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated 10/8 as 1 1/2 (12/8).\", \"remediation\": \"10/8 = 1 2/8 = 1 1/4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded or truncated 10/8 to 1 whole cup.\", \"remediation\": \"10/8 = 1 and 2/8, which simplifies to 1 1/4 cups.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student computed 2/8 + 6/8 = 8/8 = 1 cup.\", \"remediation\": \"Add all four: 2/8 + 2/8 + 3/8 + 3/8 = 10/8 = 1 1/4 cups.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2967, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'A water bottle contains 750 mL of water. How many such bottles are needed to fill a 6-liter dispenser?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '6 bottles', '10 bottles', '4 bottles', '8 bottles', 'D', 'active', '6 liters = 6,000 milliliters. 6,000 ÷ 750 = 8 bottles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed 1 bottle per liter.\", \"remediation\": \"Each bottle has 750 mL (0.75 L). 6 ÷ 0.75 = 8 bottles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 600 or guessed 10.\", \"remediation\": \"6,000 mL ÷ 750 mL = 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 3,000 ÷ 750 (for 3 liters).\", \"remediation\": \"The dispenser is 6 L (6,000 mL). 6,000 ÷ 750 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2968, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'A rectangular garden path is 15 meters long and 80 centimeters wide. What is the area of the path in square meters?', NULL, 5, 'High', '1.2 square meters', '12 square meters', '1,200 square meters', '120 square meters', 'B', 'active', 'First convert 80 cm to meters: 80 cm = 0.8 m. Area = 15 m × 0.8 m = 12 square meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student misplaced the decimal point.\", \"remediation\": \"15 × 0.8 = 12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 15 × 80 without converting centimeters to meters.\", \"remediation\": \"Always convert dimensions to the same unit before multiplying: 80 cm = 0.8 m; 15 × 0.8 = 12 m².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student converted 80 cm to 8 m.\", \"remediation\": \"80 cm = 0.8 m (since 100 cm = 1 m). 15 × 0.8 = 12 m².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2969, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'A package weighs 3 pounds and 12 ounces. If shipping costs $0.50 per ounce, what is the shipping cost?', NULL, 5, 'High', '$24.00', '$18.00', '$30.00', '$36.00', 'C', 'active', 'Convert 3 pounds to ounces: 3 × 16 = 48 ounces. Total weight = 48 + 12 = 60 ounces. Cost = 60 × $0.50 = $30.00.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only calculated cost for the 3 pounds (48 × $0.50 = $24.00).\", \"remediation\": \"Include the 12 ounces: 48 + 12 = 60 oz; 60 × $0.50 = $30.00.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed 1 pound = 12 ounces (48 oz total).\", \"remediation\": \"1 pound = 16 ounces. 3 lb = 48 oz, plus 12 oz = 60 oz.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 3 × 12 = 36 or made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 16 = 48; 48 + 12 = 60; 60 × 0.5 = $30.00.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2970, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 250, 5, 'A rectangular shipping container has a volume of 480 cubic feet. Its length is 10 feet and its height is 6 feet. What is its width?', NULL, 5, 'High', '8 feet', '12 feet', '6 feet', '48 feet', 'A', 'active', 'V = l × w × h. 480 = 10 × w × 6 = 60w. Dividing both sides by 60 gives w = 480 ÷ 60 = 8 feet.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 480 by 40 instead of 60.\", \"remediation\": \"10 × 6 = 60. 480 ÷ 60 = 8 feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed the width equals height.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide volume by (length × height): 480 ÷ (10 × 6) = 480 ÷ 60 = 8 ft.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 480 by 10 but forgot to divide by 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by both known dimensions: 480 ÷ 60 = 8 feet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2971, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 250, 5, 'If each edge of a cube is doubled from 3 cm to 6 cm, how does the volume change?', NULL, 5, 'High', 'The volume doubles (multiplied by 2)', 'The volume quadruples (multiplied by 4)', 'The volume increases by 8 times (multiplied by 8)', 'The volume increases by 6 times', 'C', 'active', 'Original volume = 3³ = 27 cm³. New volume = 6³ = 216 cm³. 216 ÷ 27 = 8 times larger (since 2³ = 8).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student thought volume scales linearly with edge length.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume scales with the cube of the scale factor: 2³ = 8 times.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student thought volume scales like area (2² = 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Area scales by factor², but volume scales by factor³: 2³ = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 2 by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"When dimensions double, volume is multiplied by 2 × 2 × 2 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2972, 6, 4, 12, 'Composite Volume', 251, 5, 'A concrete step is formed by two rectangular prisms stacked together. The bottom prism is 10 in wide, 30 in long, and 6 in high. The top prism is 10 in wide, 15 in long, and 6 in high. What is the total volume of concrete needed?', NULL, 5, 'High', '3,600 cubic inches', '1,800 cubic inches', '900 cubic inches', '2,700 cubic inches', 'D', 'active', 'Bottom prism: 10 × 30 × 6 = 1,800 in³. Top prism: 10 × 15 × 6 = 900 in³. Total volume = 1,800 + 900 = 2,700 cubic inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the bottom step volume.\", \"remediation\": \"The top step has length 15 in (half of 30 in), so total is 1,800 + 900 = 2,700 in³.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the volume of the bottom step.\", \"remediation\": \"Add both steps: Bottom (1,800) + Top (900) = 2,700 in³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the volume of the top step.\", \"remediation\": \"Both steps require concrete: 1,800 + 900 = 2,700 in³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2973, 6, 4, 12, 'Composite Volume', 251, 5, 'A hollow rectangular wooden box has outer dimensions 10 cm × 8 cm × 6 cm. If a solid block of dimensions 6 cm × 4 cm × 3 cm is removed from its center, what is the remaining volume of wood?', NULL, 5, 'High', '480 cubic cm', '408 cubic cm', '72 cubic cm', '360 cubic cm', 'B', 'active', 'Outer volume = 10 × 8 × 6 = 480 cm³. Inner removed volume = 6 × 4 × 3 = 72 cm³. Remaining volume = 480 - 72 = 408 cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student found only the outer volume and did not subtract the removed part.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the hollow interior: 480 - 72 = 408 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student found only the inner removed volume.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the remaining volume of wood: Outer - Inner = 480 - 72 = 408 cm³.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the subtraction.\", \"remediation\": \"480 - 72 = 408 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2974, 6, 4, 12, 'Line Plots & Fraction Measurements', 247, 5, 'A student recorded lengths of 8 ribbons on a line plot: two at 1/8 yd, three at 3/8 yd, and three at 5/8 yd. If all 8 ribbons are placed end-to-end, what is the total length?', NULL, 5, 'High', '3 1/4 yards', '3 1/2 yards', '3 yards', '2 5/8 yards', 'A', 'active', 'Total = 2(1/8) + 3(3/8) + 3(5/8) = 2/8 + 9/8 + 15/8 = 26/8 = 3 2/8 = 3 1/4 yards.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student computed 28/8 = 3 1/2.\", \"remediation\": \"2 + 9 + 15 = 26; 26/8 = 3 1/4 yards.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded down or miscalculated 26/8 as 24/8 = 3.\", \"remediation\": \"26/8 = 3 with remainder 2/8 = 3 1/4 yards.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student missed one of the data points.\", \"remediation\": \"Sum: 2/8 + 9/8 + 15/8 = 26/8 = 3 1/4 yards.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2975, 6, 4, 12, 'Measurement Unit Conversion', 246, 5, 'A water tank has dimensions 2 meters by 1.5 meters by 1 meter. How many liters of water can it hold when completely full? (1 cubic meter = 1,000 liters)', NULL, 5, 'High', '300 liters', '3,000 liters', '30,000 liters', '3.0 liters', 'B', 'active', 'Volume in m³ = 2 × 1.5 × 1 = 3 m³. Since 1 m³ = 1,000 L, capacity = 3 × 1,000 = 3,000 liters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 3 by 100 instead of 1,000.\", \"remediation\": \"1 cubic meter = 1,000 liters. 3 × 1,000 = 3,000 L.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 10,000.\", \"remediation\": \"3 m³ × 1,000 L/m³ = 3,000 L.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the volume in cubic meters without converting to liters.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for liters: 3 m³ = 3,000 L.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2976, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Triangles', 252, 6, 'What is the area of a right triangle with a base of 8 cm and a height of 6 cm?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '28 square cm', '48 square cm', '14 square cm', '24 square cm', 'D', 'active', 'Area of a triangle = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 8 × 6 = 24 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated perimeter or made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"1/2 × 8 × 6 = 4 × 6 = 24 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to multiply by 1/2 (used formula for rectangle).\", \"remediation\": \"The area of a triangle is half that of a rectangle: A = 1/2 × b × h.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added base and height: 8 + 6 = 14.\", \"remediation\": \"Area requires multiplying base and height, then dividing by 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2977, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Triangles', 252, 6, 'A triangle has a base of 10 inches and an area of 35 square inches. What is its height?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '7 inches', '3.5 inches', '14 inches', '70 inches', 'A', 'active', 'Area = 1/2 × b × h. 35 = 1/2 × 10 × h = 5h. Height = 35 ÷ 5 = 7 inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided area by base directly without accounting for 1/2 (35 ÷ 10 = 3.5).\", \"remediation\": \"Since A = 1/2 × b × h, 2A = b × h: h = (2 × 35) ÷ 10 = 70 ÷ 10 = 7 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the height: 7 × 2 = 14.\", \"remediation\": \"h = (2 × A) ÷ b = 70 ÷ 10 = 7 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 2A but did not divide by base.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 2A by base: 70 ÷ 10 = 7 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2978, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Polygons', 253, 6, 'What is the area of a parallelogram with a base of 9 cm and a height of 5 cm?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '90 square cm', '22.5 square cm', '45 square cm', '28 square cm', 'C', 'active', 'Area of a parallelogram = base × height = 9 × 5 = 45 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the area.\", \"remediation\": \"A = b × h = 45 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student applied triangle formula (divided by 2).\", \"remediation\": \"A parallelogram formula is A = base × height, without dividing by 2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added base and height or calculated perimeter.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply base by perpendicular height: 9 × 5 = 45 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2979, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Polygons', 253, 6, 'A trapezoid has parallel bases of lengths 6 cm and 10 cm, and a height of 4 cm. What is its area?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '64 square cm', '24 square cm', '40 square cm', '32 square cm', 'D', 'active', 'Area of a trapezoid = 1/2 × (b1 + b2) × h = 1/2 × (6 + 10) × 4 = 1/2 × 16 × 4 = 32 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 2: (6 + 10) × 4 = 64.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the trapezoid formula divides by 2: A = 1/2 × (b1 + b2) × h = 32 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used only the first base: 6 × 4 = 24.\", \"remediation\": \"Add both bases together first: (6 + 10) = 16.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student used only the second base: 10 × 4 = 40.\", \"remediation\": \"Average the two bases: (6 + 10)/2 = 8, then multiply by height 4 = 32 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2980, 6, 4, 12, 'Surface Area', 254, 6, 'A cube has an edge length of 3 inches. What is the total surface area of the cube?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '18 square inches', '54 square inches', '27 square inches', '36 square inches', 'B', 'active', 'A cube has 6 identical faces. Each face has area 3 × 3 = 9 sq in. Total surface area = 6 × 9 = 54 sq in.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 6 faces by 3 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"Each face area is 3 × 3 = 9 sq in, then multiply by 6: 54 sq in.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the volume (3³ = 27) instead of surface area.\", \"remediation\": \"Surface area is the total area of all 6 faces: 6 × (side²) = 6 × 9 = 54 sq in.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied side length by 6 faces or calculated 4 faces (lateral area).\", \"remediation\": \"Total surface area includes all 6 faces: 6 × 9 = 54 sq in.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2981, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 255, 6, 'What is the volume of a rectangular prism with length 1/2 ft, width 1/3 ft, and height 1/4 ft?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '3/9 cubic ft', '1/12 cubic ft', '1/24 cubic ft', '1/9 cubic ft', 'C', 'active', 'Volume = length × width × height = (1/2) × (1/3) × (1/4) = 1/(2 × 3 × 4) = 1/24 cubic ft.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added fractions incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply all three fractions: numerator 1 × 1 × 1 = 1; denominator 2 × 3 × 4 = 24.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied only two dimensions: 1/3 × 1/4 = 1/12.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply all three dimensions: (1/2) × (1/12) = 1/24.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added denominators: 2 + 3 + 4 = 9.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply denominators together: 2 × 3 × 4 = 24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2982, 6, 4, 12, 'Coordinate Plane & Area', 257, 6, 'A rectangle on the coordinate plane has vertices at (1, 1), (5, 1), (5, 4), and (1, 4). What is its area?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '12 square units', '20 square units', '14 square units', '16 square units', 'A', 'active', 'Base length = 5 - 1 = 4 units. Height = 4 - 1 = 3 units. Area = base × height = 4 × 3 = 12 square units.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied coordinates 5 × 4 = 20.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract vertex coordinates to find side lengths first: length is 5 - 1 = 4, height is 4 - 1 = 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the perimeter: 2(4 + 3) = 14.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is length × width = 4 × 3 = 12 square units.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student squared the base: 4² = 16.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply width (4) by height (3): 4 × 3 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2983, 6, 4, 12, 'Surface Area', 254, 6, 'Which 2D representation can be folded to form a 3-dimensional solid?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'A cross-section', 'A perimeter', 'A net', 'A line plot', 'C', 'active', 'A net is an unfolded, flat 2D pattern that can be folded along edges to form a 3D solid.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A cross-section is a 2D slice through a 3D solid.\", \"remediation\": \"A net unfolds a 3D solid into a 2D shape that can fold back up.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Perimeter is the 1D distance around a shape.\", \"remediation\": \"A net is the 2D pattern used to find surface area and build solids.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A line plot displays statistical frequency.\", \"remediation\": \"A net is the geometric term for an unfolded 3D shape.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2984, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Triangles', 252, 6, 'An obtuse triangle has a base of 12 cm and a corresponding height (drawn outside the triangle) of 7 cm. What is its area?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '38 square cm', '84 square cm', '19 square cm', '42 square cm', 'D', 'active', 'The area formula A = 1/2 × b × h holds regardless of whether the triangle is acute, right, or obtuse: A = 1/2 × 12 × 7 = 42 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error in division.\", \"remediation\": \"12 × 7 = 84; 84 ÷ 2 = 42 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to multiply by 1/2: 12 × 7 = 84.\", \"remediation\": \"Always multiply base by height and divide by 2 for any triangle: 84 ÷ 2 = 42 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added base and height: 12 + 7 = 19.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is half the product of base and height: 1/2 × 12 × 7 = 42 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2985, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Polygons', 253, 6, 'A rhombus has diagonals of length 14 cm and 8 cm. What is the area of the rhombus?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '112 square cm', '56 square cm', '22 square cm', '44 square cm', 'B', 'active', 'The area of a rhombus = 1/2 × d1 × d2 = 1/2 × 14 × 8 = 56 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 2 (calculated d1 × d2).\", \"remediation\": \"The diagonals decompose the rhombus into 4 congruent right triangles; total area is (d1 × d2) / 2 = 56 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added the diagonals: 14 + 8 = 22.\", \"remediation\": \"Use the formula Area = 1/2 × d1 × d2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted or miscalculated: 56 is correct.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2986, 6, 4, 12, 'Surface Area', 254, 6, 'A rectangular prism has length 5 cm, width 4 cm, and height 2 cm. What is its total surface area?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '76 square cm', '88 square cm', '40 square cm', '38 square cm', 'A', 'active', 'Surface Area = 2(lw + lh + wh) = 2(5×4 + 5×2 + 4×2) = 2(20 + 10 + 8) = 2(38) = 76 cm².', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition or multiplication error.\", \"remediation\": \"2 × (20 + 10 + 8) = 2 × 38 = 76 cm².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the volume (5 × 4 × 2 = 40 cm³) instead of surface area.\", \"remediation\": \"Surface area is the sum of the areas of all 6 faces: 2(lw + lh + wh) = 76 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to multiply by 2 (summed only 3 faces: 20 + 10 + 8 = 38).\", \"remediation\": \"A rectangular prism has 6 faces (3 pairs of equal faces), so multiply the sum of the 3 faces by 2: 38 × 2 = 76 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2987, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 255, 6, 'A rectangular prism with dimensions 2 1/2 inches by 4 inches by 1 1/2 inches is filled with unit cubes of edge length 1/2 inch. How many 1/2-inch cubes will it take to fill the prism?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '15 cubes', '120 cubes', '60 cubes', '30 cubes', 'B', 'active', 'Number of cubes along length = 2.5 ÷ 0.5 = 5. Along width = 4 ÷ 0.5 = 8. Along height = 1.5 ÷ 0.5 = 3. Total cubes = 5 × 8 × 3 = 120 cubes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the volume in cubic inches (2.5 × 4 × 1.5 = 15 cubic inches) instead of the number of 1/2-inch cubes.\", \"remediation\": \"Each 1/2-inch cube has a volume of 1/8 cubic inch. 15 ÷ (1/8) = 15 × 8 = 120 cubes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 15 by 1/4 instead of 1/8.\", \"remediation\": \"The volume of a 1/2-inch cube is (1/2)³ = 1/8 cubic inch. 15 × 8 = 120 cubes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 5 × 8 = 40 or made a multiplication error.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply cubes along each dimension: 5 × 8 × 3 = 120 cubes.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2988, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Composite Figures', 256, 6, 'A building step consists of two rectangular prisms. Box 1 measures 6 ft by 4 ft by 1 ft. Box 2 on top measures 4 ft by 4 ft by 1 ft. What is the total volume?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '48 cubic ft', '24 cubic ft', '16 cubic ft', '40 cubic ft', 'D', 'active', 'Volume of Box 1 = 6 × 4 × 1 = 24 ft³. Volume of Box 2 = 4 × 4 × 1 = 16 ft³. Total volume = 24 + 16 = 40 cubic ft.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled Box 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Box 1 is 24 ft³ and Box 2 is 16 ft³: 24 + 16 = 40 ft³.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only Box 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the volumes of both boxes: 24 + 16 = 40 ft³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only Box 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Total volume is Box 1 (24) + Box 2 (16) = 40 ft³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2989, 6, 4, 12, 'Coordinate Plane & Area', 257, 6, 'A triangle on a coordinate grid has vertices at A(2, 2), B(8, 2), and C(5, 7). What is the area of triangle ABC?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '15 square units', '30 square units', '18 square units', '21 square units', 'A', 'active', 'Base AB lies along y = 2, so base length = 8 - 2 = 6 units. Height is the vertical distance from y = 2 to vertex C(5, 7), which is 7 - 2 = 5 units. Area = 1/2 × 6 × 5 = 15 square units.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 2 (6 × 5 = 30).\", \"remediation\": \"For a triangle, Area = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 6 × 5 = 15 sq units.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the base or height.\", \"remediation\": \"Base = 8 - 2 = 6; Height = 7 - 2 = 5; Area = 1/2 × 6 × 5 = 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added dimensions: 6 + 5 = 11 or made an arithmetic slip.\", \"remediation\": \"Area = 1/2 × 6 × 5 = 15 sq units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2990, 6, 4, 12, 'Surface Area', 254, 6, 'The net of a right triangular prism consists of two congruent right triangular bases (legs 3 cm and 4 cm) and three rectangular faces (widths 3 cm, 4 cm, and 5 cm, all with length 10 cm). What is the total surface area?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '144 square cm', '120 square cm', '132 square cm', '60 square cm', 'C', 'active', 'Area of 2 triangular bases = 2 × (1/2 × 3 × 4) = 12 cm². Area of 3 rectangular faces = (3 × 10) + (4 × 10) + (5 × 10) = 30 + 40 + 50 = 120 cm². Total surface area = 12 + 120 = 132 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added bases incorrectly: 120 + 24 = 144.\", \"remediation\": \"Area of one triangle is 1/2 × 3 × 4 = 6. Two triangles = 12 cm²; 120 + 12 = 132 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the lateral area (the 3 rectangles: 120 cm²) and omitted the two triangular bases.\", \"remediation\": \"Surface area includes all faces: lateral area (120) + 2 bases (12) = 132 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated volume (1/2 × 3 × 4 × 10 = 60 cm³) instead of surface area.\", \"remediation\": \"Surface area is the sum of face areas: 12 + 120 = 132 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2991, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Polygons', 253, 6, 'A wall is in the shape of a rectangle 8 meters wide and 5 meters high, with a triangular gable on top having base 8 meters and height 3 meters. What is the total area of the wall?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '64 square meters', '40 square meters', '46 square meters', '52 square meters', 'D', 'active', 'Area of rectangle = 8 × 5 = 40 m². Area of triangular gable = 1/2 × 8 × 3 = 12 m². Total area = 40 + 12 = 52 m².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student did not divide the triangle area by 2 (40 + 24 = 64).\", \"remediation\": \"The triangular top area is 1/2 × 8 × 3 = 12 m²; total = 40 + 12 = 52 m².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only calculated the rectangular wall area.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the triangular gable area: 40 + 12 = 52 m².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the triangle area as 6.\", \"remediation\": \"1/2 × 8 × 3 = 12; 40 + 12 = 52 m².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2992, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 255, 6, 'A rectangular cereal box has a volume of 360 cubic inches. If its height is 12 inches and width is 3 inches, what is its length?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '8 inches', '10 inches', '12 inches', '30 inches', 'B', 'active', 'Volume = l × w × h. 360 = l × 3 × 12 = 36l. Dividing by 36 gives l = 360 ÷ 36 = 10 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated division.\", \"remediation\": \"360 ÷ 36 = 10 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 12 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 360 by (3 × 12 = 36): 360 ÷ 36 = 10 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 360 by 12 but forgot to divide by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by both dimensions: 360 ÷ 36 = 10 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2993, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Polygons', 253, 6, 'A regular hexagon can be decomposed into 6 equilateral triangles. If each equilateral triangle has a base of 6 cm and a height of approximately 5.2 cm, what is the total area of the hexagon?', NULL, 6, 'High', '46.8 square cm', '187.2 square cm', '93.6 square cm', '31.2 square cm', 'C', 'active', 'Area of 1 triangle = 1/2 × 6 × 5.2 = 15.6 cm². Area of 6 triangles = 6 × 15.6 = 93.6 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the area of only 3 triangles (half the hexagon).\", \"remediation\": \"A regular hexagon has 6 equilateral triangles: 6 × 15.6 = 93.6 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide each triangle by 2: 6 × (6 × 5.2) = 187.2 cm².\", \"remediation\": \"Remember triangle area is 1/2 × base × height: 1/2 × 6 × 5.2 = 15.6; 15.6 × 6 = 93.6 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated perimeter or only 2 triangles.\", \"remediation\": \"6 × (1/2 × 6 × 5.2) = 93.6 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2994, 6, 4, 12, 'Surface Area', 254, 6, 'An open-top wooden rectangular box (no lid) has length 8 inches, width 5 inches, and height 4 inches. What is the total outside surface area to be painted?', NULL, 6, 'High', '144 square inches', '104 square inches', '184 square inches', '160 square inches', 'A', 'active', 'Since there is no top lid, Surface Area = bottom + 2(front/back) + 2(sides) = (8×5) + 2(8×4) + 2(5×4) = 40 + 64 + 40 = 144 square inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the 4 side walls (lateral area: 64 + 40 = 104) and omitted the bottom.\", \"remediation\": \"Do not forget the bottom face: 104 + 40 = 144 sq in.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student included the top lid (calculated standard 6-sided surface area: 184 sq in).\", \"remediation\": \"The box is open-top, so include only 1 bottom face (40) instead of 2: 184 - 40 = 144 sq in.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated volume (8 × 5 × 4 = 160 cubic inches) instead of surface area.\", \"remediation\": \"Surface area measures the outer surface: 40 + 64 + 40 = 144 sq in.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2995, 6, 4, 12, 'Coordinate Plane & Area', 257, 6, 'A trapezoid on a coordinate grid has vertices at P(-3, 2), Q(5, 2), R(3, -2), and S(-1, -2). What is the area of trapezoid PQRS?', NULL, 6, 'High', '48 square units', '16 square units', '24 square units', '32 square units', 'C', 'active', 'Top base PQ length = 5 - (-3) = 8 units. Bottom base RS length = 3 - (-1) = 4 units. Height is the vertical distance between y = 2 and y = -2: 2 - (-2) = 4 units. Area = 1/2 × (8 + 4) × 4 = 1/2 × 12 × 4 = 24 square units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 2: (8 + 4) × 4 = 48.\", \"remediation\": \"Area of a trapezoid is 1/2 × (b1 + b2) × h = 1/2 × 12 × 4 = 24 sq units.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used only the bottom base: 4 × 4 = 16.\", \"remediation\": \"Average both bases: (8 + 4)/2 = 6, then multiply by height 4 = 24 sq units.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student used only the top base: 8 × 4 = 32.\", \"remediation\": \"Use both parallel bases in the formula: 1/2 × (8 + 4) × 4 = 24 sq units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2996, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Rectangular Prisms', 255, 6, 'A rectangular container is 2 1/4 feet long, 1 1/3 feet wide, and 3 feet tall. What is the volume of the container in cubic feet?', NULL, 6, 'High', '18 cubic feet', '6 3/4 cubic feet', '7 1/2 cubic feet', '9 cubic feet', 'D', 'active', 'Convert to improper fractions: 2 1/4 = 9/4, 1 1/3 = 4/3. Volume = (9/4) × (4/3) × 3 = (9 × 4 × 3) / (4 × 3) = 9 cubic feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the volume.\", \"remediation\": \"V = (9/4) × (4/3) × 3 = 9 cubic feet.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied only length and height (9/4 × 3 = 27/4 = 6 3/4).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply all three dimensions: (9/4) × (4/3) × 3 = 9 cubic feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made an error multiplying fractions.\", \"remediation\": \"Notice the 4 in numerator and denominator cancels, and the 3 cancels: remaining value is 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2997, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Composite Figures', 256, 6, 'A metal bracket is formed by cutting a rectangular prism hole of 2 cm × 2 cm × 5 cm out of a solid rectangular metal block of 6 cm × 4 cm × 5 cm. What is the volume of metal in the bracket?', NULL, 6, 'High', '120 cubic cm', '100 cubic cm', '20 cubic cm', '140 cubic cm', 'B', 'active', 'Solid block volume = 6 × 4 × 5 = 120 cm³. Volume of hole = 2 × 2 × 5 = 20 cm³. Remaining volume = 120 - 20 = 100 cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the solid block volume without subtracting the hole.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the removed hole: 120 - 20 = 100 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the hole volume.\", \"remediation\": \"The metal volume is the solid minus the hole: 120 - 20 = 100 cm³.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added the hole volume instead of subtracting: 120 + 20 = 140.\", \"remediation\": \"The hole removes material, so subtract: 120 - 20 = 100 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2998, 6, 4, 12, 'Surface Area', 254, 6, 'A gift box shaped like a rectangular prism measures 10 inches by 6 inches by 4 inches. If wrapping paper costs $0.02 per square inch, how much does it cost to cover the entire surface of the box?', NULL, 6, 'High', '$4.96', '$9.92', '$2.48', '$4.80', 'A', 'active', 'Surface area = 2(10×6 + 10×4 + 6×4) = 2(60 + 40 + 24) = 2(124) = 248 square inches. Cost = 248 × $0.02 = $4.96.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the cost.\", \"remediation\": \"SA = 248 sq in. 248 × 0.02 = $4.96.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 124 by $0.02 (omitted factor of 2 for pairs of faces).\", \"remediation\": \"A prism has 6 faces: total surface area = 248 sq in. 248 × $0.02 = $4.96.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student used volume (10 × 6 × 4 = 240) × $0.02 = $4.80.\", \"remediation\": \"Wrapping covers the surface area, not volume. SA = 248 sq in; cost = $4.96.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(2999, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Triangles', 252, 6, 'A triangular pennant has an area of 84 square inches and a base of 14 inches. What is the perpendicular height of the pennant?', NULL, 6, 'High', '6 inches', '12 inches', '24 inches', '14 inches', 'B', 'active', 'A = 1/2 × b × h. 84 = 1/2 × 14 × h = 7h. h = 84 ÷ 7 = 12 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student divided area by base without multiplying by 2 (84 ÷ 14 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Since A = 1/2 × b × h, h = (2 × A) ÷ b = 168 ÷ 14 = 12 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied height by 2: 12 × 2 = 24.\", \"remediation\": \"h = (2 × 84) ÷ 14 = 168 ÷ 14 = 12 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed height equals base.\", \"remediation\": \"h = (2 × 84) ÷ 14 = 12 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3000, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Polygons', 253, 6, 'A large banner is composed of a rectangle 12 feet long and 4 feet wide, with two identical triangles attached at either end, each having base 4 feet and height 3 feet. What is the total area of the banner?', NULL, 6, 'High', '72 square feet', '48 square feet', '54 square feet', '60 square feet', 'D', 'active', 'Area of central rectangle = 12 × 4 = 48 sq ft. Area of one triangle = 1/2 × 4 × 3 = 6 sq ft. Area of two triangles = 2 × 6 = 12 sq ft. Total area = 48 + 12 = 60 square feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student did not divide the triangle areas by 2 (48 + 24 = 72).\", \"remediation\": \"Each triangle area is 1/2 × 4 × 3 = 6 sq ft; 48 + 12 = 60 sq ft.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the rectangle area (48 sq ft).\", \"remediation\": \"Add the two end triangles: 48 + 2(6) = 60 sq ft.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student included only one of the two end triangles: 48 + 6 = 54 sq ft.\", \"remediation\": \"There are two end triangles: 48 + 6 + 6 = 60 sq ft.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:03:28', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3001, 6, 4, 12, 'Circumference', 259, 7, 'What is the circumference of a circle with a diameter of 10 cm? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'Low', '31.4 cm', '62.8 cm', '78.5 cm', '15.7 cm', 'A', 'active', 'Circumference C = π × d = 3.14 × 10 = 31.4 cm.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used radius formula with diameter: C = 2πd instead of πd (doubled diameter).\", \"remediation\": \"The formula using diameter is C = π × d (or C = 2πr). For d = 10, C = 3.14 × 10 = 31.4 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the area (π × r² = 3.14 × 25 = 78.5) instead of circumference.\", \"remediation\": \"Circumference is the distance around a circle: C = π × d = 31.4 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the circumference.\", \"remediation\": \"C = π × d = 3.14 × 10 = 31.4 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3002, 6, 4, 12, 'Circumference', 259, 7, 'A circular bicycle wheel has a radius of 14 inches. What is its circumference? (Use π ≈ 22/7)', NULL, 7, 'Low', '28 inches', '44 inches', '88 inches', '616 inches', 'C', 'active', 'Circumference C = 2πr = 2 × (22/7) × 14 = 2 × 22 × 2 = 88 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only doubled the radius (found diameter).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply diameter by π: 28 × (22/7) = 88 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to multiply by 2 (calculated πr instead of 2πr).\", \"remediation\": \"Circumference formula with radius is C = 2πr: 2 × (22/7) × 14 = 88 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated area (πr² = 22/7 × 196 = 616).\", \"remediation\": \"Circumference is C = 2πr = 88 inches, not πr².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3003, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Circles', 258, 7, 'What is the area of a circle with a radius of 5 meters? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'Low', '31.4 square meters', '15.7 square meters', '100 square meters', '78.5 square meters', 'D', 'active', 'Area = π × r² = 3.14 × 5² = 3.14 × 25 = 78.5 square meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the circumference (2πr = 2 × 3.14 × 5 = 31.4) instead of area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is π × r² = 3.14 × 25 = 78.5 m².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied π by r (3.14 × 5 = 15.7).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to square the radius first: r² = 25, then 3.14 × 25 = 78.5 m².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student squared diameter or approximated incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Area = π × r² = 3.14 × 25 = 78.5 m².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3004, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Circles', 258, 7, 'A circular pond has a diameter of 8 meters. What is its radius?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '2 meters', '4 meters', '16 meters', '8 meters', 'B', 'active', 'The radius is half of the diameter: r = d ÷ 2 = 8 ÷ 2 = 4 meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide diameter by 2: 8 ÷ 2 = 4 m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the diameter instead of halving it.\", \"remediation\": \"Radius is half the diameter: r = d / 2 = 4 m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student confused radius with diameter.\", \"remediation\": \"Diameter is across the whole circle; radius is from center to edge (half of diameter).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3005, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Composite Figures', 260, 7, 'A figure consists of a rectangle of 6 cm by 4 cm attached to a square of 3 cm by 3 cm. What is the total area?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '24 square cm', '9 square cm', '33 square cm', '42 square cm', 'C', 'active', 'Rectangle area = 6 × 4 = 24 cm². Square area = 3 × 3 = 9 cm². Total area = 24 + 9 = 33 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the rectangle area.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the square area to the rectangle: 24 + 9 = 33 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the square area.\", \"remediation\": \"Add both areas together: 24 + 9 = 33 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added dimensions incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"24 + 9 = 33 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3006, 6, 4, 12, 'Scale & Measurement Applications', 261, 7, 'On a map, 1 inch represents 20 miles. If two cities are 3 inches apart on the map, what is the actual distance between them?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '60 miles', '20 miles', '23 miles', '40 miles', 'A', 'active', 'Actual distance = 3 inches × 20 miles per inch = 60 miles.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used the unit rate directly without scaling.\", \"remediation\": \"3 inches × 20 = 60 miles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 20 + 3 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply map distance by the scale factor: 3 × 20 = 60 miles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 20 by 2 instead of 3.\", \"remediation\": \"3 inches × 20 miles/inch = 60 miles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3007, 6, 4, 12, 'Scale & Measurement Applications', 261, 7, 'A blueprint uses a scale where 1 centimeter represents 4 meters. A room is 5 cm long on the blueprint. What is the actual length of the room?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '9 meters', '1.25 meters', '20 meters', '25 meters', 'C', 'active', 'Actual length = 5 cm × 4 meters per cm = 20 meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 5 + 4 = 9.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply blueprint measurement by scale factor: 5 × 4 = 20 meters.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 5 by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Blueprint to actual requires multiplying: 5 × 4 = 20 m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 5 by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Scale is 4 m per cm: 5 × 4 = 20 m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3008, 6, 4, 12, 'Circumference', 259, 7, 'What is the relationship between the circumference C and the diameter d of any circle?', NULL, 7, 'Low', 'd / C = π', 'C × d = π', 'C - d = π', 'C / d = π', 'D', 'active', 'Pi (π) is defined as the ratio of a circle\'s circumference to its diameter: C / d = π, which gives C = πd.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student inverted the ratio (d / C = 1/π).\", \"remediation\": \"Circumference is larger than diameter: C / d = π ≈ 3.14.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied circumference by diameter.\", \"remediation\": \"Pi is the quotient of circumference divided by diameter: C / d = π.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted diameter from circumference.\", \"remediation\": \"Pi is a constant ratio (division), not a difference.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3009, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Circles', 258, 7, 'What is the area of a circle with a diameter of 12 inches? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '452.16 square inches', '113.04 square inches', '37.68 square inches', '75.36 square inches', 'B', 'active', 'Radius r = 12 ÷ 2 = 6 inches. Area = πr² = 3.14 × 6² = 3.14 × 36 = 113.04 square inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used diameter directly in formula without halving to radius: π × 12² = 452.16.\", \"remediation\": \"Always halve the diameter first to find radius: r = 6; Area = 3.14 × 6² = 113.04 sq in.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated circumference: 3.14 × 12 = 37.68.\", \"remediation\": \"Area is π × r² = 3.14 × 36 = 113.04 sq in.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 2 × circumference.\", \"remediation\": \"Area = π × r² = 113.04 sq in.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3010, 6, 4, 12, 'Circumference', 259, 7, 'A circular track has a circumference of 62.8 meters. What is its diameter? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '20 meters', '31.4 meters', '10 meters', '40 meters', 'A', 'active', 'd = C ÷ π = 62.8 ÷ 3.14 = 20 meters.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the circumference: 62.8 ÷ 2 = 31.4.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by π (3.14), not 2: 62.8 ÷ 3.14 = 20 meters.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated radius instead of diameter (62.8 ÷ 6.28 = 10).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for diameter: d = C / π = 20 meters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the diameter: 20 × 2 = 40.\", \"remediation\": \"d = C / π = 62.8 ÷ 3.14 = 20 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3011, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Composite Figures', 260, 7, 'A basketball court key is shaped like a rectangle 19 feet long and 12 feet wide, topped with a semicircle of diameter 12 feet. What is the total area of the key? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '228.00 square feet', '284.52 square feet', '341.04 square feet', '454.56 square feet', 'B', 'active', 'Rectangle area = 19 × 12 = 228 sq ft. Semicircle radius = 12 ÷ 2 = 6 ft. Semicircle area = 1/2 × π × 6² = 0.5 × 3.14 × 36 = 56.52 sq ft. Total area = 228 + 56.52 = 284.52 sq ft.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the rectangle area and omitted the semicircle.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the semicircle area (56.52) to the rectangle (228): 284.52 sq ft.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added a full circle area instead of a semicircle (228 + 113.04 = 341.04).\", \"remediation\": \"A semicircle is half a circle: divide the circle area by 2: 113.04 ÷ 2 = 56.52 sq ft.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student used diameter in circle formula and added to rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Radius is 6 ft; semicircle is 56.52 sq ft; total is 284.52 sq ft.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3012, 6, 4, 12, 'Scale & Measurement Applications', 261, 7, 'A scale drawing of a rectangular park has a length of 8 cm and a width of 5 cm. The scale is 1 cm = 15 meters. What is the actual area of the park in square meters?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '4,500 square meters', '600 square meters', '40 square meters', '9,000 square meters', 'D', 'active', 'Actual length = 8 × 15 = 120 m. Actual width = 5 × 15 = 75 m. Actual area = 120 × 75 = 9,000 m². (Or drawing area = 40 cm², scaled by 15² = 225: 40 × 225 = 9,000 m²).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the actual area.\", \"remediation\": \"Actual dimensions are 120 m and 75 m; 120 × 75 = 9,000 m².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied drawing area (40) by scale factor 15 instead of 15² (40 × 15 = 600).\", \"remediation\": \"Area scales by the square of the scale factor: 15² = 225; 40 × 225 = 9,000 m².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the drawing area without scaling.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert dimensions to actual meters first: 120 m × 75 m = 9,000 m².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3013, 6, 4, 12, 'Circumference', 259, 7, 'A car tire has a radius of 0.35 meters. How far does the car travel in 100 complete rotations of the tire? (Use π ≈ 22/7)', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '220 meters', '110 meters', '440 meters', '77 meters', 'A', 'active', 'Circumference = 2πr = 2 × (22/7) × 0.35 = 2 × 22 × 0.05 = 2.2 meters. In 100 rotations: 100 × 2.2 = 220 meters.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot factor of 2 in circumference (used πr × 100).\", \"remediation\": \"Circumference is 2πr = 2.2 m; 2.2 × 100 = 220 m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the distance.\", \"remediation\": \"C = 2.2 m; 100 × 2.2 = 220 meters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated area multiplied by rotations.\", \"remediation\": \"Distance traveled per rotation equals circumference: 2πr × 100 = 220 m.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3014, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Composite Figures', 260, 7, 'A square metal sheet has sides of 10 cm. A circular hole with radius 3 cm is punched out of the center. What is the area of the remaining metal sheet? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '81.16 square cm', '100 square cm', '71.74 square cm', '28.26 square cm', 'C', 'active', 'Square area = 10 × 10 = 100 cm². Circle area = π × 3² = 3.14 × 9 = 28.26 cm². Remaining area = 100 - 28.26 = 71.74 cm².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted circumference (3.14 × 6 = 18.84) instead of area.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract area πr² = 28.26 from 100: 100 - 28.26 = 71.74 cm².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the original square area.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the area of the circle punched out: 100 - 28.26 = 71.74 cm².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the hole area instead of the remaining area.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the remaining metal: 100 - 28.26 = 71.74 cm².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3015, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Circles', 258, 7, 'If the radius of a circle is doubled, what happens to its area?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', 'The area is multiplied by 2', 'The area is multiplied by 8', 'The area stays the same', 'The area is multiplied by 4', 'D', 'active', 'Area = πr². If radius becomes 2r, new area = π(2r)² = 4πr², which is 4 times the original area.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed area scales linearly with radius.\", \"remediation\": \"Area depends on radius squared (r²), so doubling radius increases area by 2² = 4 times.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student confused area with 3D volume (2³ = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"Volume multiplies by 8, but 2D area multiplies by 2² = 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student thought area is independent of radius.\", \"remediation\": \"When radius changes, area changes by the square of the scale factor: 2² = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3016, 6, 4, 12, 'Scale & Measurement Applications', 261, 7, 'A model airplane is built using a scale of 1:48. If the wingspan of the real airplane is 36 feet, what is the wingspan of the model in inches?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '18 inches', '9 inches', '0.75 inches', '12 inches', 'B', 'active', 'Convert real wingspan to inches: 36 feet × 12 inches/ft = 432 inches. Scale is 1:48: Model wingspan = 432 ÷ 48 = 9 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic slip in division.\", \"remediation\": \"432 ÷ 48 = 9 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 36 feet by 48 and left the answer in feet (0.75 feet) instead of converting to inches.\", \"remediation\": \"0.75 feet × 12 inches/ft = 9 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 48 ÷ 4 = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert 36 ft to 432 inches, then 432 ÷ 48 = 9 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:30', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3017, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Composite Figures', 260, 7, 'A swimming pool border is shaped like a rectangle of 10 m by 6 m with two identical semicircles on its shorter ends (diameter 6 m). What is the total area? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '60.00 square meters', '116.52 square meters', '88.26 square meters', '74.13 square meters', 'C', 'active', 'Rectangle area = 10 × 6 = 60 m². Two semicircles of diameter 6 m (radius 3 m) combine to form 1 full circle. Circle area = π × 3² = 3.14 × 9 = 28.26 m². Total area = 60 + 28.26 = 88.26 m².', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only calculated the rectangle area.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the two semicircles (one full circle = 28.26): 60 + 28.26 = 88.26 m².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used diameter 6 as radius in circle formula: π × 6² = 113.04.\", \"remediation\": \"Radius is half the diameter: r = 3 m; π × 3² = 28.26 m²; total = 88.26 m².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added only one semicircle: 60 + 14.13 = 74.13.\", \"remediation\": \"There are two semicircles, one at each short end: together they make 1 full circle (28.26 m²).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3018, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Circles', 258, 7, 'A circular running track has an outer radius of 25 meters and an inner radius of 20 meters. What is the area of the track itself (the ring between the circles)? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'High', '706.5 square meters', '157.0 square meters', '1,962.5 square meters', '1,256.0 square meters', 'A', 'active', 'Outer circle area = π × 25² = 3.14 × 625 = 1,962.5 m². Inner circle area = π × 20² = 3.14 × 400 = 1,256 m². Area of ring = 1,962.5 - 1,256 = 706.5 m².', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student squared the difference of radii: π × (25 - 20)² = 3.14 × 25 = 78.5 or multiplied by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"The area of an annulus is π(R² - r²), which is NOT π(R - r)²: 3.14 × (625 - 400) = 706.5 m².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the outer circle area.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the inner field area: 1,962.5 - 1,256 = 706.5 m².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the inner circle area.\", \"remediation\": \"The track area is the difference between outer and inner circles: 1,962.5 - 1,256 = 706.5 m².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3019, 6, 4, 12, 'Circumference', 259, 7, 'A semicircular window has a diameter of 14 inches. What is the total perimeter of the window (including the straight bottom edge)? (Use π ≈ 22/7)', NULL, 7, 'High', '22 inches', '44 inches', '36 inches', '58 inches', 'C', 'active', 'Curved arc length = 1/2 × π × d = 1/2 × (22/7) × 14 = 22 inches. Straight bottom edge = diameter = 14 inches. Total perimeter = 22 + 14 = 36 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only found the curved semicircular arc (22 inches) and forgot the straight base.\", \"remediation\": \"The perimeter must enclose the entire figure: add the diameter base: 22 + 14 = 36 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated full circle circumference (44 inches).\", \"remediation\": \"A semicircle arc is half the circumference: 44 ÷ 2 = 22, plus the base: 22 + 14 = 36 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added full circumference to diameter: 44 + 14 = 58.\", \"remediation\": \"Curved arc is only half the circle: 22 + 14 = 36 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3020, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Composite Figures', 260, 7, 'A square lawn has side length 14 meters. A sprinkler placed at each of the 4 corners waters a quarter-circle of radius 7 meters. What is the unwatered area in the middle of the lawn? (Use π ≈ 22/7)', NULL, 7, 'High', '84 square meters', '154 square meters', '196 square meters', '42 square meters', 'D', 'active', 'Total lawn area = 14 × 14 = 196 m². Four quarter-circles of radius 7 m combine into 1 full circle of radius 7 m. Watered area = πr² = (22/7) × 7² = 154 m². Unwatered area = 196 - 154 = 42 square meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the remaining area.\", \"remediation\": \"Total (196) - Watered (154) = 42 m².\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student found the watered area (154 m²) instead of the unwatered area.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract watered area from total area: 196 - 154 = 42 m².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student found only the total area of the square.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the circle watered by the sprinklers: 196 - 154 = 42 m².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3021, 6, 4, 12, 'Scale & Measurement Applications', 261, 7, 'A blueprint has a scale of 1/4 inch = 1 foot. If an architectural floor plan shows a room as 3 inches by 4 inches, what will it cost to carpet the actual room at $4.50 per square foot?', NULL, 7, 'High', '$54.00', '$864.00', '$216.00', '$432.00', 'B', 'active', 'Scale factor: 1 inch = 4 feet. Actual length = 4 × 4 = 16 ft. Actual width = 3 × 4 = 12 ft. Actual area = 16 × 12 = 192 sq ft. Total cost = 192 × $4.50 = $864.00.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used drawing area directly: 3 × 4 = 12; 12 × $4.50 = $54.00.\", \"remediation\": \"Convert drawing dimensions to feet first: 12 ft by 16 ft = 192 sq ft; 192 × $4.50 = $864.00.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student scaled area by 4 instead of 4² = 16: 12 × 4 = 48 sq ft; 48 × $4.50 = $216.00.\", \"remediation\": \"Area scales by factor squared (4² = 16): 12 × 16 = 192 sq ft; 192 × $4.50 = $864.00.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the total cost.\", \"remediation\": \"192 sq ft × $4.50/sq ft = $864.00.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3022, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Circles', 258, 7, 'Two concentric circles have circumferences of 31.4 cm and 62.8 cm. What is the width of the ring between them? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 7, 'High', '5 cm', '2.5 cm', '10 cm', '3.14 cm', 'A', 'active', 'Inner radius r1 = 31.4 ÷ (2 × 3.14) = 5 cm. Outer radius r2 = 62.8 ÷ (2 × 3.14) = 10 cm. Ring width = r2 - r1 = 10 - 5 = 5 cm.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the width.\", \"remediation\": \"r2 - r1 = 10 - 5 = 5 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the outer radius (10 cm) or difference in diameters (10 cm).\", \"remediation\": \"Ring width is the difference in radii: r2 - r1 = 10 - 5 = 5 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed π.\", \"remediation\": \"Radius difference: (62.8 - 31.4) / (2π) = 31.4 / 6.28 = 5 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3023, 6, 4, 12, 'Area of Composite Figures', 260, 7, 'A decorative garden plot is formed by an equilateral triangle with base 10 m and height 8.66 m, and a rectangle of 10 m by 6 m attached to its base. What is the total area of the garden?', NULL, 7, 'High', '146.6 square meters', '103.3 square meters', '86.6 square meters', '120.0 square meters', 'B', 'active', 'Triangle area = 1/2 × 10 × 8.66 = 43.3 m². Rectangle area = 10 × 6 = 60 m². Total area = 43.3 + 60 = 103.3 square meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide triangle by 2 (86.6 + 60 = 146.6).\", \"remediation\": \"Triangle area is 1/2 × base × height = 43.3; 43.3 + 60 = 103.3 m².\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student used base × height without 1/2 and omitted the rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Add triangle (43.3) and rectangle (60): 103.3 m².\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded or estimated incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"43.3 + 60 = 103.3 m².\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3024, 6, 4, 12, 'Circumference', 259, 7, 'A circular pizza has a circumference of 44 inches. What is the area of the pizza? (Use π ≈ 22/7)', NULL, 7, 'High', '88 square inches', '308 square inches', '616 square inches', '154 square inches', 'D', 'active', 'Circumference C = 2πr. 44 = 2 × (22/7) × r = (44/7)r. r = 7 inches. Area = πr² = (22/7) × 7² = (22/7) × 49 = 154 square inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the circumference.\", \"remediation\": \"Find radius first: r = 7 in, then A = πr² = 154 sq in.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the correct area.\", \"remediation\": \"r = 7; Area = (22/7) × 49 = 154 sq in.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student used diameter (14) as radius: (22/7) × 14² = 616.\", \"remediation\": \"Diameter is 14, so radius is 7: Area = (22/7) × 49 = 154 sq in.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3025, 6, 4, 12, 'Scale & Measurement Applications', 261, 7, 'On a city map with scale 1 cm : 250 m, a rectangular park measures 4 cm by 2.4 cm. What is the actual perimeter of the park in meters?', NULL, 7, 'High', '3,200 meters', '1,600 meters', '600,000 meters', '2,400 meters', 'A', 'active', 'Map perimeter = 2(4 + 2.4) = 2(6.4) = 12.8 cm. Actual perimeter = 12.8 × 250 = 3,200 meters.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to multiply by 2 (added length and width only: 6.4 × 250 = 1,600).\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter includes all four sides: 2(l + w) = 12.8 cm; 12.8 × 250 = 3,200 m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the area instead of perimeter (4 × 2.4 × 250² = 600,000).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for perimeter (linear distance): 3,200 m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"12.8 × 250 = 3,200 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3026, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cylinders', 262, 8, 'What is the formula for the volume of a cylinder with radius r and height h?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'V = 4/3 πr³', 'V = 2πrh', 'V = πr²h', 'V = 1/3 πr²h', 'C', 'active', 'The volume of a cylinder equals base area times height: V = (πr²) × h = πr²h.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This is the volume formula for a sphere.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder formula is V = πr²h.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"This is the lateral surface area formula of a cylinder.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume is base area times height: V = πr²h.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This is the volume formula for a cone.\", \"remediation\": \"A cylinder has three times the volume of a cone with the same base and height: V = πr²h.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3027, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cylinders', 262, 8, 'A cylinder has a radius of 3 cm and a height of 10 cm. What is its volume? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 8, 'Low', '94.2 cubic cm', '188.4 cubic cm', '942 cubic cm', '282.6 cubic cm', 'D', 'active', 'V = πr²h = 3.14 × 3² × 10 = 3.14 × 9 × 10 = 282.6 cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 1/3 of the volume (used cone formula).\", \"remediation\": \"Cylinder volume is V = πr²h = 282.6 cm³ (not divided by 3).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used 2πrh (surface area) instead of πr²h.\", \"remediation\": \"V = πr²h = 3.14 × 9 × 10 = 282.6 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student did not square the radius or made a decimal error.\", \"remediation\": \"r² = 9; 3.14 × 9 × 10 = 282.6 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3028, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cones', 263, 8, 'What is the volume of a cone with radius 3 cm and height 10 cm? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 8, 'Low', '188.4 cubic cm', '94.2 cubic cm', '282.6 cubic cm', '31.4 cubic cm', 'B', 'active', 'V = 1/3 πr²h = 1/3 × 3.14 × 3² × 10 = 1/3 × 282.6 = 94.2 cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 2/3 instead of 1/3.\", \"remediation\": \"V = (1/3) × 282.6 = 94.2 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 3 (used cylinder volume formula).\", \"remediation\": \"A cone volume is exactly one-third of a cylinder: V = 1/3 πr²h = 94.2 cm³.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 9 instead of 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide cylinder volume by 3: 282.6 ÷ 3 = 94.2 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3029, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Spheres', 264, 8, 'What is the formula for the volume of a sphere of radius r?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'V = 4πr²', 'V = πr²h', 'V = 4/3 πr³', 'V = 2/3 πr³', 'C', 'active', 'The volume of a sphere is given by V = (4/3)πr³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"4πr² is the surface area of a sphere, not volume.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume requires cubic units (r³) and the 4/3 coefficient: V = 4/3 πr³.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"πr²h is the volume of a cylinder.\", \"remediation\": \"Sphere volume is V = 4/3 πr³.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"2/3 πr³ is the volume of a hemisphere.\", \"remediation\": \"A full sphere has volume 4/3 πr³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3030, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Spheres', 264, 8, 'A sphere has a radius of 3 inches. What is its volume in terms of π?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '36π cubic inches', '27π cubic inches', '108π cubic inches', '12π cubic inches', 'A', 'active', 'V = 4/3 π r³ = 4/3 × π × 3³ = 4/3 × π × 27 = 36π cubic inches.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student omitted the 4/3 coefficient.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply r³ (27) by 4/3: 36π cubic inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 3: 4 × 27π = 108π.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the formula has 4/3: (4 × 27) / 3 = 36π cubic inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 4/3 by 3² instead of 3³.\", \"remediation\": \"Radius must be cubed: 3³ = 27; 4/3 × 27 = 36π.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3031, 6, 4, 12, 'Cross-Sections', 265, 8, 'What 2D shape is formed by slicing a right cylinder parallel to its base?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'A rectangle', 'An oval/ellipse', 'A circle', 'A triangle', 'C', 'active', 'A cross-section parallel to the circular base of a right cylinder is a circle identical to the base.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A slice perpendicular to the base produces a rectangle.\", \"remediation\": \"Slicing parallel to the base yields a circle congruent to the base.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"An angled slice produces an ellipse.\", \"remediation\": \"A slice parallel to the circular base is a circle.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Cylinders do not have triangular cross-sections.\", \"remediation\": \"Parallel to base = circle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3032, 6, 4, 12, 'Cross-Sections', 265, 8, 'What 2D shape is formed by slicing a right rectangular prism perpendicular to its base?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'A sphere', 'A circle', 'A triangle', 'A rectangle', 'D', 'active', 'Any planar slice perpendicular to the base of a rectangular prism produces a rectangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A sphere is a 3D solid, not a 2D cross-section.\", \"remediation\": \"Cross-sections are 2D shapes: here, a rectangle.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Rectangular prisms have flat faces and cannot produce circular cross-sections.\", \"remediation\": \"Cross-sections of a rectangular prism are polygons, specifically rectangles when cut perpendicular to faces.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"An angled slice across three faces could produce a triangle, but perpendicular cuts give rectangles.\", \"remediation\": \"A slice perpendicular to the base is a rectangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3033, 6, 4, 12, 'Real-World Volume Applications', 267, 8, 'A soup can is in the shape of a cylinder with diameter 8 cm and height 10 cm. What is its radius?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '8 cm', '4 cm', '16 cm', '2 cm', 'B', 'active', 'The radius is half of the diameter: 8 ÷ 2 = 4 cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used diameter instead of radius.\", \"remediation\": \"Radius is half of diameter: r = 8 ÷ 2 = 4 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the diameter.\", \"remediation\": \"Radius = diameter ÷ 2 = 4 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by 2: 8 ÷ 2 = 4 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3034, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cylinders', 262, 8, 'A cylinder has a volume of 500π cubic meters and a height of 20 meters. What is its radius?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '5 meters', '2.5 meters', '25 meters', '10 meters', 'A', 'active', 'V = πr²h. 500π = πr²(20). Divide both sides by 20π: r² = 25. Taking the square root gives r = 5 meters.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the radius.\", \"remediation\": \"r = √25 = 5 meters.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student found r² = 25 but forgot to take the square root.\", \"remediation\": \"Since r² = 25, take the square root to find radius: r = √25 = 5 meters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 500 by 50 or guessed 10.\", \"remediation\": \"r² = 500 / 20 = 25; r = 5 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3035, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cones', 263, 8, 'A cone has a diameter of 12 inches and a height of 8 inches. What is the volume of the cone? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '1,205.76 cubic inches', '301.44 cubic inches', '904.32 cubic inches', '602.88 cubic inches', 'B', 'active', 'Radius r = 12 ÷ 2 = 6 inches. V = 1/3 πr²h = 1/3 × 3.14 × 6² × 8 = 1/3 × 3.14 × 36 × 8 = 1/3 × 904.32 = 301.44 cubic inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used diameter in cylinder formula: 3.14 × 144 × 8 = 1,205.76 (did not halve diameter and did not divide by 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Halve diameter to find r = 6, and divide by 3 for cone: V = 1/3 πr²h = 301.44 cu in.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 3 (calculated cylinder volume for r = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Cone volume is one-third cylinder volume: 904.32 ÷ 3 = 301.44 cu in.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 2/3 instead of 1/3.\", \"remediation\": \"V = 1/3 πr²h = 301.44 cu in.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3036, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Spheres', 264, 8, 'A basketball has a diameter of 20 cm. What is its volume? (Use π ≈ 3.14, round to nearest whole number)', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '8,373 cubic cm', '33,493 cubic cm', '1,256 cubic cm', '4,187 cubic cm', 'D', 'active', 'Radius r = 20 ÷ 2 = 10 cm. V = 4/3 πr³ = 4/3 × 3.14 × 10³ = 4/3 × 3.14 × 1,000 ≈ 4,186.67 ≈ 4,187 cm³.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 3 (4πr³ / 1).\", \"remediation\": \"V = (4/3) × π × r³ ≈ 4,187 cm³.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used diameter 20 as radius: 4/3 × 3.14 × 8,000 = 33,493.\", \"remediation\": \"Always halve diameter to get radius: r = 10 cm; V = 4/3 × 3.14 × 1,000 ≈ 4,187 cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated surface area (4πr² = 4 × 3.14 × 100 = 1,256) instead of volume.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume formula is 4/3 πr³ = 4,187 cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3037, 6, 4, 12, 'Composite 3D Figures', 266, 8, 'A silo is formed by a cylinder of radius 4 meters and height 10 meters, topped with a hemisphere of radius 4 meters. What is the total volume in terms of π?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '202.67π cubic meters (or 608π/3)', '160π cubic meters', '245.33π cubic meters', '181.33π cubic meters', 'A', 'active', 'Cylinder volume = π × 4² × 10 = 160π. Hemisphere volume = 1/2 × (4/3 π × 4³) = 2/3 π × 64 = 128π/3 ≈ 42.67π. Total = 160π + 42.67π = 202.67π m³ (608π/3).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the cylinder volume and omitted the hemisphere top.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the hemisphere volume: Cylinder (160π) + Hemisphere (42.67π) = 202.67π m³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added a full sphere instead of a hemisphere: 160π + 85.33π = 245.33π.\", \"remediation\": \"A hemisphere is half a sphere: 85.33π ÷ 2 = 42.67π; total = 202.67π m³.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic calculation slip.\", \"remediation\": \"160π + 42.67π = 202.67π m³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3038, 6, 4, 12, 'Real-World Volume Applications', 267, 8, 'A cylindrical water tank has radius 5 feet and height 12 feet. If water is pumped in at a rate of 15 cubic feet per minute, approximately how many minutes will it take to fill the tank? (Use π ≈ 3.14)', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '20.9 minutes', '31.4 minutes', '62.8 minutes', '125.6 minutes', 'C', 'active', 'Volume = πr²h = 3.14 × 25 × 12 = 942 cubic feet. Time = 942 ÷ 15 = 62.8 minutes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used cone volume (divided by 3): 314 ÷ 15 = 20.9 min.\", \"remediation\": \"The tank is a cylinder, not a cone: V = πr²h = 942 cu ft.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the time or used radius 5 as diameter.\", \"remediation\": \"V = 3.14 × 25 × 12 = 942 cu ft; 942 ÷ 15 = 62.8 minutes.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the time.\", \"remediation\": \"Time = Volume / Rate = 942 / 15 = 62.8 min.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3039, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cylinders', 262, 8, 'A cylinder and a cone have the same base radius and the same height. If the cylinder has a volume of 72 cubic inches, what is the volume of the cone?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '36 cubic inches', '216 cubic inches', '18 cubic inches', '24 cubic inches', 'D', 'active', 'The volume of a cone is exactly 1/3 the volume of a cylinder with identical base and height: 72 ÷ 3 = 24 cubic inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 2 instead of 3.\", \"remediation\": \"A cone is 1/3 of a cylinder\'s volume, not 1/2: 72 ÷ 3 = 24 cu in.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied by 3 instead of dividing by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"A cone is smaller than a cylinder: divide by 3: 72 ÷ 3 = 24 cu in.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"The cone-to-cylinder volume ratio is 1:3: 72 ÷ 3 = 24 cu in.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3040, 6, 4, 12, 'Cross-Sections', 265, 8, 'What 2D cross-section is produced when a plane passes through the apex and base of a right circular cone perpendicular to the base?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'A trapezoid', 'An isosceles triangle', 'A circle', 'A parabola', 'B', 'active', 'A vertical cut passing through the apex of a right circular cone produces an isosceles triangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A truncated cone (frustum) slice might give a trapezoid, but through the apex gives a triangle.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"A circle is produced by a horizontal slice parallel to the base.\", \"remediation\": \"Cutting vertically through the apex creates an isosceles triangle with legs along the slant height.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A parabola is produced by slicing parallel to the slant height, not through the apex.\", \"remediation\": \"Passing through the apex yields a triangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3041, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cones', 263, 8, 'A conical paper cup has a volume of 48π cubic cm. If its height is 9 cm, what is the radius of the top circular opening?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '16 cm', '8 cm', '4 cm', '2 cm', 'C', 'active', 'V = 1/3 πr²h. 48π = 1/3 π r² (9) = 3π r². Divide by 3π: r² = 16. Therefore, r = √16 = 4 cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student found r² = 16 but forgot to take the square root.\", \"remediation\": \"Since r² = 16, take the square root: r = 4 cm.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 16 by 2 instead of taking square root.\", \"remediation\": \"√16 = 4, not 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student took square root twice.\", \"remediation\": \"r = √16 = 4 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3042, 6, 4, 12, 'Composite 3D Figures', 266, 8, 'An ice cream cone consists of a waffle cone of radius 3 cm and height 12 cm, filled to the brim and topped with a hemisphere of ice cream of radius 3 cm. What is the total volume of ice cream in terms of π?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '54π cubic cm', '90π cubic cm', '36π cubic cm', '72π cubic cm', 'A', 'active', 'Cone volume = 1/3 πr²h = 1/3 × π × 9 × 12 = 36π. Hemisphere volume = 1/2 × (4/3 π × 3³) = 2/3 π × 27 = 18π. Total volume = 36π + 18π = 54π cubic cm.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated cylinder plus sphere.\", \"remediation\": \"Cone is 36π, hemisphere is 18π: total = 54π cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the cone volume (36π).\", \"remediation\": \"Add the hemisphere scoop on top (18π): 36π + 18π = 54π cubic cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added a full sphere (36π) instead of a hemisphere: 36π + 36π = 72π.\", \"remediation\": \"The scoop on top is a hemisphere: half of 36π = 18π; 36π + 18π = 54π.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3043, 6, 4, 12, 'Real-World Volume Applications', 267, 8, 'A cylindrical glass of water has radius 4 cm and is filled with water to a height of 10 cm. A solid metal spherical ball of radius 3 cm is dropped into the glass and sinks to the bottom. How much does the water level rise? (Leave answer in fractional/decimal form)', NULL, 8, 'High', '3.00 cm', '4.50 cm', '2.25 cm', '1.50 cm', 'C', 'active', 'Sphere volume = 4/3 π × 3³ = 36π cm³. The water displaced has volume = π × r_cylinder² × Δh = π × 16 × Δh. Equating volumes: 16π Δh = 36π => Δh = 36 / 16 = 2.25 cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed water rise equals the radius of the sphere (3 cm).\", \"remediation\": \"The volume displaced must match the sphere\'s volume: Δh = V_sphere / (π × r_glass²) = 36π / 16π = 2.25 cm.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 36 by 8 instead of 16.\", \"remediation\": \"Glass base area is π × 4² = 16π. 36π ÷ 16π = 2.25 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic slip in division.\", \"remediation\": \"36 / 16 = 9 / 4 = 2.25 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3044, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Spheres', 264, 8, 'A large spherical balloon has a radius of 6 feet. A smaller balloon has a radius of 2 feet. How many times greater is the volume of the larger balloon compared to the smaller balloon?', NULL, 8, 'High', '81 times greater', '3 times greater', '9 times greater', '27 times greater', 'D', 'active', 'Ratio of radii = 6 / 2 = 3. Volume scales with the cube of the scale factor: 3³ = 27 times greater.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 3⁴ = 81.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume ratio is 3³ = 27.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student compared radii linearly (6 / 2 = 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Volume scales with the cube of the scale factor: (scale)³ = 3³ = 27.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student compared surface areas (scale factor squared: 3² = 9).\", \"remediation\": \"Area scales by factor², but volume scales by factor³: 3³ = 27.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3045, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cones', 263, 8, 'A conical tank has a base radius of 5 meters and a slant height of 13 meters. What is the volume of the cone in terms of π? (Recall: slant height s, radius r, and height h form a right triangle: r² + h² = s²)', NULL, 8, 'High', '300π cubic meters', '100π cubic meters', '108.33π cubic meters', '65π cubic meters', 'B', 'active', 'First find vertical height h using Pythagorean theorem: h = √(13² - 5²) = √(169 - 25) = √144 = 12 meters. Volume = 1/3 π r² h = 1/3 × π × 25 × 12 = 100π cubic meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 3 (used cylinder formula: π × 25 × 12 = 300π).\", \"remediation\": \"Cone volume is 1/3 πr²h = (1/3) × 300π = 100π m³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student used slant height 13 instead of vertical height 12 in formula: 1/3 × 25 × 13 = 108.33π.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume requires the perpendicular vertical height h (12 m), not slant height (13 m).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 5 × 13 = 65π.\", \"remediation\": \"Find h = 12 m first: V = 1/3 π (25) (12) = 100π m³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3046, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cylinders', 262, 8, 'A metal pipe has an outer radius of 5 cm, an inner radius of 3 cm, and a length of 20 cm. What is the volume of metal in the pipe in terms of π?', NULL, 8, 'High', '320π cubic cm', '80π cubic cm', '500π cubic cm', '180π cubic cm', 'A', 'active', 'Outer cylinder volume = π × 5² × 20 = 500π. Inner hollow cylinder volume = π × 3² × 20 = 180π. Volume of metal = 500π - 180π = 320π cubic cm (or π(5² - 3²) × 20 = π(25 - 9) × 20 = 16 × 20π = 320π).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student squared the difference of radii: π(5 - 3)² × 20 = 80π.\", \"remediation\": \"Difference of squares is (R² - r²), NOT (R - r)²: (25 - 9) × 20 = 320π cm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated outer volume without subtracting inner hollow core.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract inner volume: 500π - 180π = 320π cm³.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the inner core volume.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract inner from outer: 500π - 180π = 320π cm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3047, 6, 4, 12, 'Cross-Sections', 265, 8, 'A plane slices through a cube so that it passes through exactly three vertices that share a common corner. What 2D cross-section is formed?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'A right triangle', 'An equilateral triangle', 'A square', 'A regular hexagon', 'B', 'active', 'The three vertices are endpoints of three equal cube edges originating from the common corner. The connecting line segments are face diagonals of the cube faces, all having equal length (s√2). Therefore, the slice forms an equilateral triangle.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student thought the faces meet at 90 degrees so the triangle must be a right triangle.\", \"remediation\": \"The cross-section sides are face diagonals of equal length s√2, making all three sides equal (equilateral).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"A slice through 3 vertices can only produce a 3-sided polygon (a triangle).\", \"remediation\": \"Three coplanar points form a triangle, not a quadrilateral.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A hexagon requires slicing through 6 faces of the cube.\", \"remediation\": \"Slicing through 3 corner-adjacent vertices produces an equilateral triangle.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3048, 6, 4, 12, 'Composite 3D Figures', 266, 8, 'A capsule is made of a cylinder of radius 3 mm and height 10 mm, with two hemispheres (each of radius 3 mm) attached to both ends. What is the total volume of the capsule in terms of π?', NULL, 8, 'High', '144π cubic mm', '90π cubic mm', '108π cubic mm', '126π cubic mm', 'D', 'active', 'Cylinder volume = π × 3² × 10 = 90π mm³. The two hemispheres combine to form 1 complete sphere of radius 3 mm. Sphere volume = 4/3 π × 3³ = 36π mm³. Total volume = 90π + 36π = 126π cubic mm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added two full spheres.\", \"remediation\": \"Two hemispheres equal one full sphere (36π): 90π + 36π = 126π mm³.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the cylinder and forgot both hemispheres.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the two hemispherical ends (1 complete sphere = 36π): 90π + 36π = 126π mm³.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added only one hemisphere: 90π + 18π = 108π.\", \"remediation\": \"There are two hemispherical ends, forming one whole sphere: 90π + 36π = 126π mm³.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3049, 6, 4, 12, 'Real-World Volume Applications', 267, 8, 'A company sells coffee beans in two cylindrical containers. Container A has radius 3 inches and height 8 inches. Container B has radius 4 inches and height 4.5 inches. Which container holds more coffee beans and by how much?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Both hold the exact same volume (72π cubic inches)', 'Container A holds 18π cubic inches more', 'Container B holds 8π cubic inches more', 'Container A holds 8π cubic inches more', 'A', 'active', 'Volume of A = π × 3² × 8 = 72π cu in. Volume of B = π × 4² × 4.5 = π × 16 × 4.5 = 72π cu in. Both hold identical volumes.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated 16 × 4.5 as 54π.\", \"remediation\": \"16 × 4.5 = 72; both volumes are exactly 72π cubic inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated Container A: 9 × 8 = 72π, Container B = 16 × 4.5 = 72π.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare both: 9 × 8 = 72 and 16 × 4.5 = 72, they are equal.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student thought height matters more than radius.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate each: A = π(9)(8) = 72π, B = π(16)(4.5) = 72π.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3050, 6, 4, 12, 'Volume of Cones', 263, 8, 'If both the radius and the height of a cone are doubled, by what factor does its volume increase?', NULL, 8, 'High', '16 times', '4 times', '8 times', '2 times', 'C', 'active', 'V = 1/3 π r² h. If r becomes 2r and h becomes 2h, V_new = 1/3 π (2r)² (2h) = 1/3 π (4r²) (2h) = 8 × (1/3 π r² h) = 8 times the original volume.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student computed 2⁴ = 16.\", \"remediation\": \"The dimension power sum is 2 (radius) + 1 (height) = 3; 2³ = 8.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only accounted for radius squared (2² = 4) and forgot the height factor.\", \"remediation\": \"Volume is proportional to r² × h: (2²) × (2) = 4 × 2 = 8 times.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed volume increases linearly by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Radius is squared, so (2)² × 2 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:04:31', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3051, 6, 5, 13, 'Sorting & Categorizing Data', 303, 1, 'A group of blocks has 3 red squares, 4 blue circles, and 2 yellow triangles. How many circles are there?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '3', '2', '9', '4', 'D', 'active', 'Looking at the circle category, there are 4 blue circles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the squares instead of circles.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the category requested: circles. There are 4 blue circles.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the triangles.\", \"remediation\": \"Count only the circles: there are 4 circles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added all shapes together instead of identifying one category.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks only for the number of circles (4), not the total.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3052, 6, 5, 13, 'Counting Categories', 305, 1, 'A tally chart shows |||| for apples and ||| for bananas. How many apples are recorded?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '5', '4', '3', '7', 'B', 'active', 'Each tally mark | represents 1 item. |||| has 4 tally marks, so there are 4 apples.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed a bundle of 5 tallies.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the individual tally marks: 1, 2, 3, 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student read the tally marks for bananas (||| = 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Look at the row for apples: |||| means 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added both tally groups together (4 + 3 = 7).\", \"remediation\": \"Read only the apples category: 4 tally marks.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3053, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture Graphs', 306, 1, 'On a picture graph where each ⭐️ equals 1 student, Maya has ⭐️⭐️⭐️. How many stars did Maya get?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '2', '4', '3', '1', 'C', 'active', 'Count the star symbols: 1, 2, 3. Maya has 3 stars.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted by 1 less.\", \"remediation\": \"Point and count each star: 1, 2, 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted by 1 more.\", \"remediation\": \"There are exactly 3 stars.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student thought the key (1 star) was the answer.\", \"remediation\": \"Count all of Maya\'s stars: 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3054, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Data', 304, 1, 'Tom has 5 toy cars and Sara has 3 toy cars. How many more toy cars does Tom have than Sara?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '2', '5', '8', '3', 'A', 'active', 'To find \"how many more\", subtract: 5 - 3 = 2.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student stated Tom\'s count.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare the two amounts: 5 - 3 = 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added instead of finding the difference: 5 + 3 = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How many more\\\" means find the difference by subtracting: 5 - 3 = 2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student stated Sara\'s count.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract Sara\'s cars from Tom\'s cars: 5 - 3 = 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3055, 6, 5, 13, 'Categories', 309, 1, 'Which category does a banana belong to?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Vegetables', 'Dairy', 'Fruits', 'Meat', 'C', 'active', 'A banana is classified under the fruit category.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student confused fruits with vegetables.\", \"remediation\": \"Bananas grow on plants with seeds and are sweet fruits.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Dairy includes milk and cheese products.\", \"remediation\": \"Bananas belong to the fruit group.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Meat includes chicken, beef, and fish.\", \"remediation\": \"Bananas are fruits.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3056, 6, 5, 13, 'Simple Data Questions', 307, 1, 'In a class pet survey, 6 students have dogs, 4 students have cats, and 2 students have birds. How many students were surveyed in total?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '8', '10', '6', '12', 'D', 'active', 'Add all categories together: 6 + 4 + 2 = 12 students in total.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error.\", \"remediation\": \"6 + 4 = 10; 10 + 2 = 12.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added only dogs and cats (6 + 4 = 10) and forgot birds.\", \"remediation\": \"Include all three categories: 6 + 4 + 2 = 12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the largest category only.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the total: add 6 + 4 + 2 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3057, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture Graphs', 306, 1, 'In a picture graph where each 🍎 = 1 apple, Liam has 🍎🍎🍎🍎🍎. How many apples does Liam have?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '4', '5', '6', '1', 'B', 'active', 'Count the apples: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Liam has 5 apples.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student stopped counting at 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Count all symbols: Liam has 5 apples.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student overcounted by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"There are exactly 5 apple symbols.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the value of 1 symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"Count the total number of apple symbols: 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3058, 6, 5, 13, 'Sorting & Categorizing Data', 303, 1, 'Look at the list: Red, Blue, Green, Square. Which one does NOT belong to the color category?', NULL, 1, 'Low', 'Square', 'Green', 'Red', 'Blue', 'A', 'active', 'Square is a shape, while Red, Blue, and Green are colors.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Green is a color.\", \"remediation\": \"Square is the only shape in the list.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Red is a color.\", \"remediation\": \"Red, Blue, and Green are all colors; Square is a shape.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Blue is a color.\", \"remediation\": \"Square is a shape, so it does not belong in a list of colors.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3059, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Data', 304, 1, 'In a survey, 7 kids like pizza and 4 kids like burgers. How many fewer kids like burgers than pizza?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '11', '3', '4', '7', 'B', 'active', '\"How many fewer\" means finding the difference: 7 - 4 = 3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added both values (7 + 4 = 11).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How many fewer\\\" asks for the difference: subtract 7 - 4 = 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student just named the burger count.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract to compare the two groups: 7 - 4 = 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student named the pizza count.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare the groups by subtracting: 7 - 4 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3060, 6, 5, 13, 'Interpreting Data', 308, 1, 'A table shows: Red fish: 5, Blue fish: 3, Yellow fish: 4. Which color has the LEAST number of fish?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Green', 'Red', 'Yellow', 'Blue', 'D', 'active', 'Comparing 5, 3, and 4, the smallest number is 3, which corresponds to Blue fish.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Green is not in the table.\", \"remediation\": \"Choose from the colors in the table: Blue has the least (3).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the greatest number (Red = 5).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Least\\\" means the smallest number: Blue has 3 fish.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the middle number (Yellow = 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare 5, 3, 4: 3 is the smallest (Blue).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3061, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture Graphs', 306, 1, 'A picture graph shows student lunches: Sandwiches 🥪🥪🥪🥪, Salad 🥗🥗, Pizza 🍕🍕🍕🍕🍕. Each icon = 1 lunch. Which lunch was chosen the MOST?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Pizza', 'Sandwiches', 'Salad', 'All equal', 'A', 'active', 'Pizza has 5 icons, Sandwiches have 4, and Salad has 2. Pizza has the most.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose Sandwiches (4 icons).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare the counts: 5 is greater than 4, so Pizza is the most.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the least (Salad = 2 icons).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Most\\\" means the highest count: Pizza has 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The counts are different: 4, 2, and 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Pizza has the greatest number of icons: 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3062, 6, 5, 13, 'Simple Data Questions', 307, 1, 'A farmer counted 5 cows, 3 horses, and some sheep. If there are 12 farm animals in total, how many sheep are there?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '5', '8', '4', '2', 'C', 'active', 'Cows and horses = 5 + 3 = 8. Subtract from total 12: 12 - 8 = 4 sheep.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed the number of cows.\", \"remediation\": \"12 - (5 + 3) = 12 - 8 = 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student found the sum of cows and horses (5 + 3 = 8) and stopped.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 8 from the total 12 animals: 12 - 8 = 4 sheep.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 5 - 3 = 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Add known animals (5 + 3 = 8), then subtract from total: 12 - 8 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3063, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Data', 304, 1, 'In a game, Team Red scored 8 points and Team Green scored 5 points. How many points does Team Green need to tie Team Red?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '13', '2', '8', '3', 'D', 'active', 'To tie, Team Green must reach 8 points: 8 - 5 = 3 points needed.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added the scores together (8 + 5 = 13).\", \"remediation\": \"To tie, find the difference between the scores: 8 - 5 = 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Count up from 5 to 8: 6, 7, 8 (3 points).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student selected Team Red\'s score.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 8 - 5 = 3 points needed to tie.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3064, 6, 5, 13, 'Counting Categories', 305, 1, 'In a tally chart, a full bundle with a diagonal slash represents 5 tallies: [||||/]. If there is one full bundle and 2 single marks, what is the total count?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '5', '7', '6', '8', 'B', 'active', 'A full bundle is 5 tallies. Adding 2 single marks gives 5 + 2 = 7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted the full bundle and ignored the single marks.\", \"remediation\": \"Do not forget the 2 single marks: 5 + 2 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added only 1 to 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 2 single marks to 5: 5 + 2 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student counted 5 + 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Count: 5, then 6, 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3065, 6, 5, 13, 'Interpreting Data', 308, 1, 'Anna has 3 stickers, Ben has 5 stickers, and Clara has 2 stickers. Who has the MOST stickers?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', 'Anna', 'Clara', 'Ben', 'Anna and Clara', 'C', 'active', 'Comparing 3, 5, and 2: 5 is the largest number, so Ben has the most stickers.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose Anna (3 stickers).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare counts: 5 is greater than 3, so Ben has the most.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose Clara (who has the least, 2 stickers).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Most\\\" means the highest number: Ben has 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Anna and Clara have 3 and 2 stickers, both less than Ben.\", \"remediation\": \"Ben has the most with 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3066, 6, 5, 13, 'Sorting & Categorizing Data', 303, 1, 'A toy box has 4 soft bears, 3 hard trucks, and 5 soft rabbits. How many SOFT toys are in the box?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '9', '5', '12', '4', 'A', 'active', 'Identify the \"soft\" toys: 4 bears + 5 rabbits = 9 soft toys.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted the soft rabbits.\", \"remediation\": \"Add both soft categories together: 4 + 5 = 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added all toys including hard trucks (4 + 3 + 5 = 12).\", \"remediation\": \"Only count toys in the \\\"soft\\\" category: 4 soft bears + 5 soft rabbits = 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted the soft bears.\", \"remediation\": \"Include all soft toys: 4 bears + 5 rabbits = 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3067, 6, 5, 13, 'Simple Data Questions', 307, 1, 'Emma counts 6 birds in a tree: 2 are blue and the rest are red. How many red birds are there?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '8', '2', '4', '3', 'C', 'active', 'Subtract the 2 blue birds from the total 6 birds: 6 - 2 = 4 red birds.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 6 + 2 = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"6 is the total: subtract the blue birds to find the red birds: 6 - 2 = 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the count of blue birds.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for red birds: 6 - 2 = 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the subtraction.\", \"remediation\": \"6 - 2 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3068, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Data', 304, 1, 'In a vote for class mascot, Tiger got 6 votes, Eagle got 3 votes, and Bear got 5 votes. How many more votes did Tiger get than Eagle?', NULL, 1, 'High', '2', '1', '9', '3', 'D', 'active', 'Tiger = 6, Eagle = 3. Subtract to compare: 6 - 3 = 3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student compared Bear and Eagle (5 - 3 = 2).\", \"remediation\": \"Make sure to read the requested categories: Tiger (6) minus Eagle (3) = 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student compared Tiger and Bear (6 - 5 = 1).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare Tiger (6) with Eagle (3): 6 - 3 = 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added Tiger and Eagle votes (6 + 3 = 9).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How many more\\\" means subtract: 6 - 3 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3069, 6, 5, 13, 'Interpreting Data', 308, 1, 'A chart shows books read: Sam read 4 books, Leo read 6 books, and Mia read 2 books. How many books did Sam and Mia read COMBINED?', NULL, 1, 'High', '12', '6', '8', '10', 'B', 'active', 'Add Sam\'s books and Mia\'s books: 4 + 2 = 6 books.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added all three students\' books (4 + 6 + 2 = 12).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks only for Sam (4) and Mia (2) combined: 4 + 2 = 6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added Leo and Mia (6 + 2 = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"Add Sam and Mia: 4 + 2 = 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added Sam and Leo (4 + 6 = 10).\", \"remediation\": \"Only combine Sam and Mia: 4 + 2 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3070, 6, 5, 13, 'Simple Data Questions', 307, 1, 'A picture graph shows: Apples 🍎🍎🍎🍎, Oranges 🍊🍊🍊, Bananas 🍌🍌🍌🍌🍌. Each symbol = 1 fruit. If 2 bananas are eaten, how many bananas are left?', NULL, 1, 'High', '3', '2', '5', '7', 'A', 'active', 'Initial bananas = 5. If 2 are eaten: 5 - 2 = 3 bananas left.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the number eaten.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks how many are left: 5 - 2 = 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the original count of bananas without subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the 2 bananas that were eaten: 5 - 2 = 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 5 + 2 = 7.\", \"remediation\": \"Eaten means removed: subtract 5 - 2 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3071, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Data', 304, 1, 'Class A has 4 girls and 5 boys. Class B has 3 girls and 7 boys. Which class has MORE total students, and by how many?', NULL, 1, 'High', 'Class A, by 1 student', 'Class B, by 1 student', 'Both classes are equal', 'Class B, by 2 students', 'B', 'active', 'Class A total = 4 + 5 = 9. Class B total = 3 + 7 = 10. Class B has 10 - 9 = 1 more student.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose Class A.\", \"remediation\": \"Class A has 9 students and Class B has 10 students; Class B has 1 more.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student thought 9 equals 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate totals: Class A has 9, Class B has 10. 10 > 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated difference as 2.\", \"remediation\": \"10 - 9 = 1 student.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3072, 6, 5, 13, 'Counting Categories', 305, 1, 'In a game, 3 red tokens, 4 green tokens, and 3 yellow tokens are drawn. Are there more red tokens or yellow tokens?', NULL, 1, 'High', 'Green has the most', 'More red tokens', 'More yellow tokens', 'They are equal (both have 3)', 'D', 'active', 'Red has 3 tokens and Yellow has 3 tokens. They have the exact same number.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"While green has 4, the question specifically compares red and yellow.\", \"remediation\": \"Read carefully: the question compares red and yellow, which are equal (3 each).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed red.\", \"remediation\": \"Both red and yellow have 3 tokens, so they are equal.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed yellow.\", \"remediation\": \"Both red and yellow have 3 tokens: 3 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3073, 6, 5, 13, 'Sorting & Categorizing Data', 303, 1, 'A child sorts 15 buttons into two piles: large buttons and small buttons. If there are 8 large buttons, how many small buttons are there?', NULL, 1, 'High', '7', '8', '23', '9', 'A', 'active', 'Total buttons = 15. Subtract the large buttons: 15 - 8 = 7 small buttons.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student repeated the number of large buttons.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 8 from 15: 15 - 8 = 7 small buttons.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 15 + 8 = 23.\", \"remediation\": \"15 is the total of both piles: subtract to find the other pile: 15 - 8 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the subtraction.\", \"remediation\": \"15 - 8 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3074, 6, 5, 13, 'Interpreting Data', 308, 1, 'A data table shows favorite seasons: Spring: 3, Summer: 7, Fall: 2, Winter: 4. How many MORE students chose Summer than Winter and Fall combined?', NULL, 1, 'High', '7', '3', '1', '5', 'C', 'active', 'Winter and Fall combined = 4 + 2 = 6. Summer = 7. Subtract: 7 - 6 = 1.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student just selected Summer\'s count.\", \"remediation\": \"Perform the two-step comparison: 7 - (4 + 2) = 1.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student compared Summer to Winter only (7 - 4 = 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Combine Winter and Fall first: 4 + 2 = 6; then 7 - 6 = 1.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student compared Summer to Fall only (7 - 2 = 5).\", \"remediation\": \"Combine both Winter and Fall: 4 + 2 = 6, then subtract from Summer: 7 - 6 = 1.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3075, 6, 5, 13, 'Simple Data Questions', 307, 1, 'A student makes a tally of birds seen: Robin: ||||, Bluejay: ||, Sparrow: |||||. How many FEWER Bluejays were seen than Sparrows?', NULL, 1, 'High', '5', '2', '7', '3', 'D', 'active', 'Sparrows = 5, Bluejays = 2. Difference = 5 - 2 = 3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the count of Sparrows.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract Bluejays (2) from Sparrows (5): 5 - 2 = 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the count of Bluejays.\", \"remediation\": \"Find how many fewer by subtracting: 5 - 2 = 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added Sparrows and Bluejays: 5 + 2 = 7.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How many fewer\\\" means subtract: 5 - 2 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3076, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'In a bar graph with scale 1 unit per line, the bar for \"Cats\" reaches up to 6 on the vertical axis. How many cats were counted?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '1', '6', '5', '7', 'B', 'active', 'The height of the bar corresponds to the number 6 on the axis, which represents 6 cats.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the scale increment.\", \"remediation\": \"The bar height is 6 units.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student read one line below the top of the bar.\", \"remediation\": \"Look straight across from the top of the bar to the axis line: it aligns with 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student read one line above the bar.\", \"remediation\": \"The bar ends at exactly 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3077, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'A horizontal bar graph shows lengths of ribbons: Red is 4 cm, Blue is 7 cm, and Yellow is 5 cm. Which ribbon is the LONGEST?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'Red', 'Yellow', 'Blue', 'All are equal', 'C', 'active', 'Comparing lengths 4, 7, and 5 cm: 7 cm is the longest, which is the Blue ribbon.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the shortest ribbon (Red = 4 cm).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Longest\\\" means greatest length: Blue is 7 cm.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the middle ribbon (Yellow = 5 cm).\", \"remediation\": \"7 cm is greater than 5 cm, so Blue is longest.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The ribbons have different lengths: 4, 7, and 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Blue is the longest at 7 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3078, 6, 5, 13, 'Line Plots', 311, 2, 'A line plot shows measurements of pencils in inches: there are three \"X\"s above 4 inches and two \"X\"s above 5 inches. How many pencils measured 4 inches?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '3', '5', '4', '2', 'A', 'active', 'Each X represents 1 pencil. There are 3 Xs above the number 4, meaning 3 pencils.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 3 + 2 = 5 or read tick mark 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Count only the Xs above 4: 3 pencils.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student read the tick mark label (4 inches) instead of counting the Xs.\", \"remediation\": \"The number of Xs tells how many objects: count the Xs above 4: there are 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the Xs above 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Look at the column above 4: there are 3 Xs.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3079, 6, 5, 13, 'Reading Data', 312, 2, 'A table shows students\' favorite sports: Soccer: 8, Baseball: 5, Basketball: 6. How many students chose Soccer?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '5', '6', '8', '19', 'C', 'active', 'Read directly from the table row for Soccer: 8 students.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student read the row for Baseball.\", \"remediation\": \"Find Soccer in the table: it has 8 students.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student read Basketball.\", \"remediation\": \"Soccer has 8 students.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added all students together.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks only for Soccer: 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3080, 6, 5, 13, 'Frequency', 313, 2, 'What does the word \"frequency\" mean when looking at a data table?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'The color of the bars', 'How long an object is', 'The name of the graph', 'How many times a value or item appears', 'D', 'active', 'Frequency means the number of times a particular event or value occurs in a data set.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Bar color is a design element.\", \"remediation\": \"Frequency is a numerical count.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Length is a physical measurement, not frequency.\", \"remediation\": \"Frequency is the count of occurrences (how many times something appears).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The name of a graph is the title.\", \"remediation\": \"Frequency represents counts or tallies.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3081, 6, 5, 13, 'Choosing Graphs', 314, 2, 'Which type of graph uses bars of different lengths or heights to compare categories?', NULL, 2, 'Low', 'Clock', 'Bar graph', 'Ruler', 'Tally mark', 'B', 'active', 'A bar graph displays categorical data using rectangular bars whose lengths/heights are proportional to the values.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A clock measures time.\", \"remediation\": \"A bar graph uses bars to represent data.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"A ruler is a tool to measure length.\", \"remediation\": \"A bar graph is a visual graph for comparing data.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Tally marks are symbols used for counting, not full graphs with bars.\", \"remediation\": \"A graph with bars is called a bar graph.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3082, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'In a picture graph, each 😊 represents 1 happy face sticker. Liam has 😊😊😊😊 and Noah has 😊😊. How many stickers does Liam have?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '4', '8', '2', '6', 'A', 'active', 'Count Liam\'s stickers: 1, 2, 3, 4 stickers.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled Liam\'s count.\", \"remediation\": \"Each symbol = 1: Liam has 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted Noah\'s stickers.\", \"remediation\": \"Look at Liam\'s row: 4 smiley faces.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added Liam and Noah together (4 + 2 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks only for Liam: 4 stickers.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3083, 6, 5, 13, 'Line Plots', 311, 2, 'A teacher measures the lengths of 6 crayons and plots them on a line plot. How many total \"X\" marks should be on the line plot?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '12', '6', '1', '3', 'B', 'active', 'Each X on a line plot represents one object measured. For 6 crayons, there are 6 Xs in total.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the count.\", \"remediation\": \"Every object gets exactly one X: 6 crayons = 6 Xs.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the value for one single crayon.\", \"remediation\": \"There must be an X for every crayon: 6 Xs.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Total Xs equals total data points: 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3084, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'A bar graph shows fruit in a basket: Apples = 8, Bananas = 5, Oranges = 3. How many MORE apples are there than bananas?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '2', '5', '13', '3', 'D', 'active', 'Subtract the bananas from the apples: 8 - 5 = 3 more apples.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted Oranges from Bananas (5 - 3 = 2).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare Apples (8) and Bananas (5): 8 - 5 = 3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the number of bananas.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract to find how many more: 8 - 5 = 3.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added apples and bananas (8 + 5 = 13).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How many more\\\" requires subtraction: 8 - 5 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3085, 6, 5, 13, 'Line Plots', 311, 2, 'A line plot shows ribbon lengths in inches: Two Xs at 3 inches, four Xs at 4 inches, and one X at 6 inches. What is the MOST COMMON ribbon length?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '4 inches', '3 inches', '6 inches', '7 inches', 'A', 'active', 'The most common length has the highest stack of Xs. The 4-inch mark has 4 Xs, which is the most.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose 3 inches (which has only 2 Xs).\", \"remediation\": \"The most common value has the most Xs: 4 inches has 4 Xs.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the largest number on the scale (6 inches) instead of the most frequent.\", \"remediation\": \"The most common length is the one with the most Xs (4 inches), not the largest measurement.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"7 inches has no Xs.\", \"remediation\": \"Look for the tallest column of Xs: 4 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3086, 6, 5, 13, 'Reading Data', 312, 2, 'A data table shows books read by 3 students: Zoe = 6, Max = 9, Eli = 4. How many books did Zoe and Eli read TOGETHER?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '19', '13', '10', '15', 'C', 'active', 'Add Zoe\'s books and Eli\'s books: 6 + 4 = 10 books.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added all three students (6 + 9 + 4 = 19).\", \"remediation\": \"The question specifies only Zoe and Eli together: 6 + 4 = 10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added Max and Eli (9 + 4 = 13).\", \"remediation\": \"Add Zoe (6) and Eli (4): 6 + 4 = 10.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added Zoe and Max (6 + 9 = 15).\", \"remediation\": \"Add Zoe and Eli: 6 + 4 = 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3087, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'In a bar graph, Red has 4 votes, Blue has 8 votes, and Green has 6 votes. How many votes were cast in ALL?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '14', '12', '20', '18', 'D', 'active', 'Add the votes from all categories: 4 + 8 + 6 = 18 votes in all.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added only Blue and Green (8 + 6 = 14).\", \"remediation\": \"Include Red as well: 4 + 8 + 6 = 18.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added only Red and Blue (4 + 8 = 12).\", \"remediation\": \"Add all three categories: 4 + 8 + 6 = 18.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error.\", \"remediation\": \"4 + 8 = 12; 12 + 6 = 18.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3088, 6, 5, 13, 'Line Plots', 311, 2, 'On a line plot showing pencil lengths, the shortest pencil is 3 inches and the longest pencil is 8 inches. What is the difference in length between the longest and shortest pencils?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '3 inches', '5 inches', '11 inches', '8 inches', 'B', 'active', 'Subtract the shortest length from the longest length: 8 - 3 = 5 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the shortest length.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 8 - 3 = 5 inches.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 8 + 3 = 11.\", \"remediation\": \"Difference means subtract: 8 - 3 = 5 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the longest length.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 8 - 3 = 5 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3089, 6, 5, 13, 'Frequency', 313, 2, 'A student rolls a number cube 10 times and gets: 2, 4, 2, 5, 2, 6, 1, 2, 3, 2. What is the frequency of the number 2?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '2', '4', '5', '10', 'C', 'active', 'Count how many times 2 appears in the list: it appears 5 times. So its frequency is 5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the number itself (2) instead of counting its frequency.\", \"remediation\": \"Frequency is the count of how many times 2 appears: count them (there are 5).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted by 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Count each 2: 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, 5th. Total = 5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the total number of rolls.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the frequency of 2 specifically: 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3090, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'A picture graph shows cars washed: Saturday 🚗🚗🚗🚗🚗, Sunday 🚗🚗🚗. If each 🚗 represents 2 cars, how many cars were washed on Saturday?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '10 cars', '6 cars', '5 cars', '7 cars', 'A', 'active', 'Each symbol represents 2 cars. Saturday has 5 symbols: 5 × 2 = 10 cars.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated for Sunday: 3 × 2 = 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Look at Saturday: 5 symbols × 2 = 10 cars.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the symbols (5) without multiplying by the key (2).\", \"remediation\": \"Always check the key: each car symbol = 2 cars. 5 × 2 = 10 cars.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 5 + 2 = 7.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the number of symbols by the key: 5 × 2 = 10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3091, 6, 5, 13, 'Choosing Graphs', 314, 2, 'Which display is BEST for showing how many students have heights of 40 inches, 41 inches, 42 inches, and 43 inches along a measurement scale?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', 'A pie chart with no numbers', 'A clock', 'A line plot', 'A calendar', 'C', 'active', 'A line plot displays measurement data over a continuous numerical scale or number line.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Pie charts show parts of a whole, not continuous measurement line data.\", \"remediation\": \"Measurement data along a number scale is best shown on a line plot.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"A clock measures time of day.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a line plot for measurements along a number line.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A calendar tracks dates.\", \"remediation\": \"Line plots are standard for length and height measurement data.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3092, 6, 5, 13, 'Reading Data', 312, 2, 'A bar graph shows pet ownership: 7 birds, 12 dogs, and 9 cats. How many FEWER birds are there than cats?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '7', '5', '9', '2', 'D', 'active', 'Cats = 9, Birds = 7. Subtract: 9 - 7 = 2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the count of birds.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the difference: 9 - 7 = 2.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student compared dogs and birds (12 - 7 = 5).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare cats (9) and birds (7): 9 - 7 = 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the count of cats.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract birds from cats: 9 - 7 = 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3093, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'In a bar graph, Grade 2 collected 14 cans, Grade 3 collected 18 cans, and Grade 4 collected 12 cans. How many more cans did Grade 2 and Grade 4 collect TOGETHER than Grade 3?', NULL, 2, 'High', '26', '8', '4', '6', 'B', 'active', 'Grade 2 + Grade 4 = 14 + 12 = 26 cans. Compare to Grade 3: 26 - 18 = 8 more cans.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student found Grade 2 + Grade 4 (26) and forgot to subtract Grade 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Now subtract Grade 3\'s 18 cans: 26 - 18 = 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student compared Grade 3 and Grade 2 only (18 - 14 = 4).\", \"remediation\": \"Combine Grade 2 and 4 first (14 + 12 = 26), then 26 - 18 = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student compared Grade 3 and Grade 4 only (18 - 12 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Add Grade 2 and 4 (26), then subtract Grade 3 (18): 26 - 18 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3094, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'In a picture graph, each 🏀 represents 2 points. Team A has 🏀🏀🏀🏀 and Team B has 🏀🏀🏀🏀🏀🏀. How many more points does Team B have than Team A?', NULL, 2, 'High', '4 points', '12 points', '2 points', '8 points', 'A', 'active', 'Team B has 2 more symbols than Team A (6 - 4 = 2). Since each symbol = 2 points, 2 × 2 = 4 points. (Or: 12 - 8 = 4 points).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated Team B\'s total points (6 × 2 = 12).\", \"remediation\": \"Find the difference between teams: 12 - 8 = 4 points.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the difference in symbols (2) without multiplying by the key.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the 2 extra symbols by the key (2 points each): 2 × 2 = 4 points.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated Team A\'s total points (4 × 2 = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract Team A from Team B: 12 - 8 = 4 points.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3095, 6, 5, 13, 'Line Plots', 311, 2, 'A scientist measures 10 leaves in inches. On the line plot, there are two Xs at 2 in, three Xs at 3 in, four Xs at 4 in, and the rest at 5 in. How many Xs are at 5 in?', NULL, 2, 'High', '2', '1', '9', '0', 'B', 'active', 'Total leaves = 10. Known leaves = 2 + 3 + 4 = 9. The remaining leaves at 5 in = 10 - 9 = 1 leaf (one X).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the remainder.\", \"remediation\": \"2 + 3 + 4 = 9. Total is 10, so 10 - 9 = 1.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 2 + 3 + 4 = 9 and gave that as the answer.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 9 from the total 10: 10 - 9 = 1.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student thought all leaves were accounted for.\", \"remediation\": \"9 leaves are accounted for, so 1 leaf must be at 5 inches: 10 - 9 = 1.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3096, 6, 5, 13, 'Reading Data', 312, 2, 'A bookstore tracks books sold: Mystery: 15, Sci-Fi: 8, Adventure: 12. If their goal was 40 books sold in total, how many more books do they need to sell?', NULL, 2, 'High', '8', '35', '10', '5', 'D', 'active', 'Total sold so far = 15 + 8 + 12 = 35 books. Books needed = 40 - 35 = 5 books.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the Sci-Fi count.\", \"remediation\": \"Total sold = 35; 40 - 35 = 5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student found the total sold so far (35) but did not subtract from the goal.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 35 from the goal of 40: 40 - 35 = 5 books needed.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the difference: 40 - 35 = 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Count up from 35 to 40: 5 books.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3097, 6, 5, 13, 'Line Plots', 311, 2, 'A line plot shows foot lengths in inches: 1 student at 6 in, 4 students at 7 in, and 3 students at 8 in. How many students have foot lengths GREATER than 6 inches?', NULL, 2, 'High', '7', '8', '4', '3', 'A', 'active', '\"Greater than 6 inches\" means foot lengths of 7 and 8 inches: 4 + 3 = 7 students.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student included the student at 6 inches (total students = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Greater than 6\\\" does not include 6 itself: count only 7 and 8 inches: 4 + 3 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted students at 7 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"Include students at 8 inches as well: 4 + 3 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted students at 8 inches.\", \"remediation\": \"Both 7 in and 8 in are greater than 6 in: 4 + 3 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3098, 6, 5, 13, 'Frequency', 313, 2, 'In a class of 20 students, a bar graph shows favorite juices: Apple = 8, Orange = 6, Grape = 4. The rest chose Cranberry. What is the frequency of Cranberry juice?', NULL, 2, 'High', '6', '18', '2', '4', 'C', 'active', 'Sum of known juices = 8 + 6 + 4 = 18. Remaining students choosing Cranberry = 20 - 18 = 2 students.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the subtraction.\", \"remediation\": \"20 - 18 = 2.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student found the sum of known juices (18) and did not subtract from 20.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 18 from the total 20 students: 20 - 18 = 2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed Grape juice frequency.\", \"remediation\": \"20 - (8 + 6 + 4) = 20 - 18 = 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3099, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 2, 'A bar graph has bars for 4 clubs: Art (12 members), Music (15 members), Chess (9 members), and Drama (14 members). What is the difference between the club with the MOST members and the club with the FEWEST members?', NULL, 2, 'High', '3', '5', '2', '6', 'D', 'active', 'The club with the most members is Music (15). The club with the fewest is Chess (9). Difference = 15 - 9 = 6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted Art from Music (15 - 12 = 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare the highest (Music = 15) with the lowest (Chess = 9): 15 - 9 = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted Drama from Music (15 - 14 = 1) or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Highest is 15, lowest is 9: 15 - 9 = 6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted Art from Drama (14 - 12 = 2).\", \"remediation\": \"Find the absolute maximum (15) and minimum (9): 15 - 9 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3100, 6, 5, 13, 'Choosing Graphs', 314, 2, 'Sam wants to show the number of sunny, cloudy, and rainy days in April. Which format allows easy comparison of the counts for each weather type?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'A single tally mark', 'A bar graph', 'A thermometer', 'A number sentence', 'B', 'active', 'A bar graph is ideal for comparing distinct categorical groups (sunny, cloudy, rainy) side by side.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A single tally mark cannot display multi-category data.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a bar graph.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"A thermometer measures temperature, not day counts.\", \"remediation\": \"A bar graph visually compares counts across categories.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A number sentence does not display visual comparison across categories.\", \"remediation\": \"A bar graph provides clear visual bars for comparing categories.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:13:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3101, 6, 5, 13, 'Scaled Bar Graphs', 315, 3, 'In a scaled bar graph, the vertical axis scale increases by increments of 5 (0, 5, 10, 15, 20). If the bar for \"Apples\" reaches the line marked 15, how many apples are there?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '3', '5', '15', '20', 'C', 'active', 'The bar reaches directly to 15 on the scale, so there are 15 apples.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 15 by 5 (counted the number of grid lines).\", \"remediation\": \"The axis labels already give the actual count: the line marked 15 means 15 apples.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the scale increment instead of the bar value.\", \"remediation\": \"Read the height of the bar at 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student read the top line of the graph.\", \"remediation\": \"The bar stops at 15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3102, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 3, 'On a pictograph, the key says: 🌲 = 10 trees. If the row for Oak has 🌲🌲🌲, how many Oak trees are there?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '30 trees', '10 trees', '3 trees', '13 trees', 'A', 'active', 'Each tree icon represents 10 trees. With 3 icons, there are 3 × 10 = 30 trees.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the value of 1 symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"Oak has 3 symbols: 3 × 10 = 30.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the symbols without multiplying by the key.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the key: each icon = 10 trees. 3 × 10 = 30 trees.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 10 + 3 = 13.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the number of symbols by the key value: 3 × 10 = 30 trees.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3103, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 3, 'A line plot shows crayon lengths in inches, marked in halves: 2, 2 1/2, 3, 3 1/2. There are four Xs above 2 1/2 inches. What does this mean?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'The crayons are 4 inches long', 'There are 2 1/2 crayons in total', 'Four crayons have a length of 2 1/2 inches', 'The longest crayon is 4 inches', 'C', 'active', 'Each X represents 1 crayon. Four Xs above 2 1/2 inches means 4 crayons measured 2 1/2 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student confused the count of Xs (4) with the length measurement.\", \"remediation\": \"The measurement is on the horizontal line (2 1/2 in), and the Xs tell how many crayons have that length.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student confused the measurement value with the count of objects.\", \"remediation\": \"You cannot have 2 1/2 crayons; 2 1/2 inches is the length, and 4 is the count.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student interpreted 4 as a length.\", \"remediation\": \"4 is the frequency of crayons of length 2 1/2 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3104, 6, 5, 13, 'Measurement Data', 316, 3, 'Four strips of paper measure 3 inches, 3 1/2 inches, 4 inches, and 3 1/2 inches. How many strips are 3 1/2 inches long?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '3 strips', '1 strip', '4 strips', '2 strips', 'D', 'active', '3 1/2 inches appears twice in the list: 2 strips.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 2 strips of length 3 1/2 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only noticed the first occurrence.\", \"remediation\": \"Check all measurements: 3 1/2 appears at the second and fourth positions (2 strips).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted all the strips in total.\", \"remediation\": \"Count only those equal to 3 1/2 inches: 2 strips.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3105, 6, 5, 13, 'Scaled Bar Graphs', 315, 3, 'In a bar graph with scale increments of 2, the bar for \"Soccer\" reaches the line for 8 and the bar for \"Tennis\" reaches 4. How many students chose Soccer?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '4 students', '8 students', '12 students', '2 students', 'B', 'active', 'The bar for Soccer aligns directly with 8 on the vertical scale.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student read the Tennis bar.\", \"remediation\": \"Look at the bar labeled Soccer: it reaches 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added Soccer and Tennis.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks only for Soccer: 8 students.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the scale step.\", \"remediation\": \"Read the top of the Soccer bar: 8 students.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3106, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Tables', 317, 3, 'A frequency table shows student birth months: Jan: 4, Feb: 2, Mar: 5, Apr: 3. Which month had the GREATEST number of birthdays?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'March', 'February', 'January', 'April', 'A', 'active', 'March has 5 birthdays, which is greater than 4, 2, or 3.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"February has the least (2 birthdays).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Greatest\\\" means the highest number: March (5).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose January (4 birthdays).\", \"remediation\": \"Compare numbers: 5 is greater than 4, so March has the greatest.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student chose April (3 birthdays).\", \"remediation\": \"March has 5, which is the highest.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3107, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 3, 'In a pictograph where 🏀 = 4 points, Team Hawks has 🏀🏀. How many points do the Hawks have?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '2 points', '8 points', '6 points', '4 points', 'B', 'active', '2 symbols × 4 points each = 8 points.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the symbols without multiplying by the key.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the key: each ball = 4 points. 2 × 4 = 8 points.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 4 + 2 = 6.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the number of symbols by the key: 2 × 4 = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the value of 1 symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 2 symbols: 2 × 4 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3108, 6, 5, 13, 'Line Plots', 311, 3, 'On a line plot, what does each \"X\" mark represent?', NULL, 3, 'Low', 'The total of all numbers', 'The title of the graph', 'A multiplication sign', 'One individual data point or observation', 'D', 'active', 'In a line plot / dot plot, each X represents one individual record or object in the data set.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The total is found by counting all Xs.\", \"remediation\": \"A single X represents one item.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The title is written at the top of the plot.\", \"remediation\": \"An X represents one piece of data counted.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"In line plots, X is a visual data marker, not an operation.\", \"remediation\": \"Each X marks one observation.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3109, 6, 5, 13, 'Scaled Bar Graphs', 315, 3, 'In a bar graph with scale intervals of 10, the bar for Grade 3 reaches 40 and Grade 4 reaches 60. How many MORE students are in Grade 4 than Grade 3?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '20 students', '100 students', '10 students', '2 students', 'A', 'active', 'Grade 4 has 60 and Grade 3 has 40. Difference = 60 - 40 = 20 students.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added the values (60 + 40 = 100).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"How many more\\\" requires subtraction: 60 - 40 = 20.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the scale interval.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the two bar heights: 60 - 40 = 20 students.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the grid lines (2 grid spaces).\", \"remediation\": \"Each grid space is 10 students: 2 × 10 = 20 students.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3110, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 3, 'In a pictograph, each 🚲 represents 4 bicycles. If the symbol for Friday shows one full bicycle and one half bicycle (🚲 + half), how many bicycles were rented on Friday?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '8 bicycles', '4 bicycles', '6 bicycles', '5 bicycles', 'C', 'active', 'One full bicycle symbol = 4. A half symbol = 4 ÷ 2 = 2. Total = 4 + 2 = 6 bicycles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student treated the half symbol as a whole symbol (4 + 4 = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"A half symbol is half of 4, which is 2: 4 + 2 = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted the full symbol and ignored the half symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"A half symbol represents half of the key value (4 ÷ 2 = 2): 4 + 2 = 6 bicycles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student treated the half symbol as 1 (4 + 1 = 5).\", \"remediation\": \"Half of 4 is 2: 4 + 2 = 6 bicycles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3111, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 3, 'A line plot shows plant heights in inches: 1/4 in has 2 Xs, 2/4 (1/2) in has 5 Xs, and 3/4 in has 1 X. What is the difference between the most frequent height and the least frequent height?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '5 plants', '1/2 inch', '2 plants', '4 plants', 'D', 'active', 'The most frequent height is 2/4 in (5 plants). The least frequent is 3/4 in (1 plant). The difference in frequencies is 5 - 1 = 4 plants.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the highest frequency (5).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract the lowest frequency (1) from the highest frequency (5): 5 - 1 = 4 plants.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted the measurements on the axis (3/4 - 1/4 = 2/4 = 1/2 in) instead of frequencies.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the difference in frequencies (number of plants): 5 - 1 = 4 plants.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 3 - 1 or made a calculation error.\", \"remediation\": \"5 - 1 = 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3112, 6, 5, 13, 'Scaled Bar Graphs', 315, 3, 'A bar graph has a vertical scale with gridlines at 0, 5, 10, 15, 20. The bar for \"Science\" ends exactly halfway between 10 and 15. What value does this bar represent?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '11', '12.5 (or between 12 and 13)', '15', '10', 'B', 'active', 'Halfway between 10 and 15 is (10 + 15) ÷ 2 = 12.5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added only 1 to 10.\", \"remediation\": \"The interval is 5, so half the interval is 2.5: 10 + 2.5 = 12.5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded up to the next gridline (15).\", \"remediation\": \"The bar ends halfway between 10 and 15, so its value is 12.5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded down to 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Halfway between 10 and 15 is 12.5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3113, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Tables', 317, 3, 'A table shows ticket sales: Monday: 24, Tuesday: 18, Wednesday: 30. How many tickets were sold in all three days combined?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '62 tickets', '54 tickets', '72 tickets', '80 tickets', 'C', 'active', '24 + 18 + 30 = 42 + 30 = 72 tickets.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error in regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"24 + 18 = 42; 42 + 30 = 72.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only added Tuesday and Wednesday (18 + 30 = 48) or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Add all three: 24 + 18 + 30 = 72.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student estimated or added incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"24 + 18 + 30 = 72 tickets.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3114, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 3, 'A pictograph uses 📖 = 5 books read. Class A has 4 book symbols and Class B has 6 book symbols. How many MORE books did Class B read than Class A?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '10 books', '20 books', '2 books', '30 books', 'A', 'active', 'Class B has 2 more symbols than Class A (6 - 4 = 2). 2 symbols × 5 books each = 10 books. (Or: 30 - 20 = 10 books).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated Class A\'s total (20).\", \"remediation\": \"The difference is 30 - 20 = 10 books.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the difference in symbols (2) without multiplying by key (5).\", \"remediation\": \"Each symbol represents 5 books: 2 × 5 = 10 books.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated Class B\'s total (6 × 5 = 30).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract Class A\'s books (20) from Class B\'s books (30): 30 - 20 = 10 books.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3115, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 3, 'A line plot shows insect lengths: 3 insects at 1/2 in, 4 insects at 3/4 in, and 2 insects at 1 in. How many insects are LONGER than 1/2 inch?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '9 insects', '4 insects', '6 insects', '2 insects', 'C', 'active', '\"Longer than 1/2 inch\" includes 3/4 in (4 insects) and 1 in (2 insects): 4 + 2 = 6 insects.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student included the 3 insects at 1/2 inch (total = 9).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Longer than 1/2 inch\\\" does not include 1/2 inch itself: 4 + 2 = 6 insects.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted insects at 3/4 inch.\", \"remediation\": \"Insects at 1 inch are also longer than 1/2 inch: 4 + 2 = 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted insects at 1 inch.\", \"remediation\": \"Combine both 3/4 in and 1 in: 4 + 2 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3116, 6, 5, 13, 'Scaled Bar Graphs', 315, 3, 'A bar graph shows pet votes: Dogs = 25, Cats = 15, Birds = 10. How many votes did Dogs and Birds get COMBINED?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '25 votes', '40 votes', '50 votes', '35 votes', 'D', 'active', 'Dogs (25) + Birds (10) = 35 votes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only gave Dogs.\", \"remediation\": \"Combine Dogs (25) and Birds (10) = 35.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added Dogs and Cats (25 + 15 = 40).\", \"remediation\": \"Add Dogs (25) and Birds (10): 25 + 10 = 35 votes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added all three categories (25 + 15 + 10 = 50).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks only for Dogs and Birds: 25 + 10 = 35.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3117, 6, 5, 13, 'Measurement Data', 316, 3, 'Six students measured their pencils to the nearest half inch: 4, 4 1/2, 5, 4 1/2, 4 1/2, 5. What is the mode (most common measurement)?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '5 inches', '4 1/2 inches', '4 inches', '4 3/4 inches', 'B', 'active', '4 1/2 appears 3 times, while 5 appears twice and 4 appears once. The mode is 4 1/2 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose 5 inches (which appears twice).\", \"remediation\": \"4 1/2 inches appears 3 times, so it is the most common.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose 4 inches (which appears once).\", \"remediation\": \"The most frequent value is 4 1/2 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"4 3/4 is not in the data set.\", \"remediation\": \"The mode is 4 1/2 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3118, 6, 5, 13, 'Scaled Bar Graphs', 315, 3, 'A school library recorded books borrowed: Grade 3 = 45, Grade 4 = 35, Grade 5 = 50. If the librarian wants to reach a total of 150 books borrowed, how many more books must be borrowed?', NULL, 3, 'High', '20 books', '15 books', '130 books', '30 books', 'A', 'active', 'Current total = 45 + 35 + 50 = 130 books. Remaining to reach 150 = 150 - 130 = 20 books.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 50 - 35 = 15.\", \"remediation\": \"Sum all three grades first (130), then subtract from 150: 20 books.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student found the current total (130) but forgot to subtract from 150.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 130 from the goal of 150: 150 - 130 = 20 books needed.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the subtraction (150 - 130 = 20).\", \"remediation\": \"Count from 130 to 150: exactly 20 books.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3119, 6, 5, 13, 'Picture & Bar Graphs', 310, 3, 'A pictograph shows flowers sold: Roses 🌹🌹🌹🌹, Daisies 🌹🌹🌹, Tulips 🌹🌹. The key says each 🌹 = 8 flowers. How many flowers were sold in all three varieties?', NULL, 3, 'High', '9 flowers', '72 flowers', '64 flowers', '56 flowers', 'B', 'active', 'Total symbols = 4 + 3 + 2 = 9 symbols. Total flowers = 9 × 8 = 72 flowers.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the total symbols (9) without multiplying by the key.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply total symbols by key: 9 × 8 = 72 flowers.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 8 × 8 = 64.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 9 symbols: 9 × 8 = 72 flowers.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 7 × 8 = 56.\", \"remediation\": \"Add all symbols: 4 + 3 + 2 = 9; 9 × 8 = 72.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3120, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 3, 'A line plot shows widths of wooden craft sticks: two sticks at 1/4 in, four sticks at 2/4 in, and two sticks at 3/4 in. What fraction of the total craft sticks have a width of 2/4 inch?', NULL, 3, 'High', '2/4', '2/8', '4/4', '4/8 (or 1/2)', 'D', 'active', 'Total sticks = 2 + 4 + 2 = 8 sticks. Sticks at 2/4 in = 4 sticks. The fraction of total sticks is 4/8 = 1/2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the measurement value (2/4 in) instead of the fraction of sticks.\", \"remediation\": \"4 out of 8 sticks is 4/8 of the sticks.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used the count of 1/4 in sticks.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 sticks of width 2/4 in out of 8 total sticks: 4/8 = 1/2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student put 4 over 4.\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator is the total number of sticks (8): 4/8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3121, 6, 5, 13, 'Scaled Bar Graphs', 315, 3, 'In a bar graph, each vertical grid line represents 6 students. If the bar for \"Walking\" is 5 grid lines high and \"Bus\" is 3 grid lines high, how many MORE students walk than ride the bus?', NULL, 3, 'High', '12 students', '2 students', '18 students', '30 students', 'A', 'active', 'Difference in grid lines = 5 - 3 = 2 lines. Since each line represents 6 students, 2 × 6 = 12 students. (Or 30 - 18 = 12).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student found the difference in grid lines (2) but forgot to multiply by the scale (6).\", \"remediation\": \"Each grid line represents 6 students: 2 × 6 = 12 students.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the bus count (3 × 6 = 18).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract: Walking (30) - Bus (18) = 12 students.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated only the walking count (5 × 6 = 30).\", \"remediation\": \"Find the difference: 30 - 18 = 12 students.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3122, 6, 5, 13, 'Measurement Data', 316, 3, 'A carpenter measured 8 boards in inches: 5, 5 1/4, 5 1/2, 5 1/4, 5 3/4, 5 1/4, 5 1/2, 6. What is the difference in length between the longest and shortest boards?', NULL, 3, 'High', '1 1/4 inches', '3/4 inch', '1 inch', '1/2 inch', 'C', 'active', 'Longest board = 6 inches. Shortest board = 5 inches. Difference = 6 - 5 = 1 inch.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 6 - 5 1/4.\", \"remediation\": \"The shortest board is 5 inches: 6 - 5 = 1 inch.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 5 3/4 - 5 = 3/4 (missed the 6-inch board).\", \"remediation\": \"The longest board is 6 inches: 6 - 5 = 1 inch.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 5 1/2 - 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Look for the maximum value in the set: 6 inches. 6 - 5 = 1 inch.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3123, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Tables', 317, 3, 'A table shows recycling collections: Week 1: 18 lbs, Week 2: 25 lbs, Week 3: 17 lbs. Week 4 data is missing, but the monthly total was 80 lbs. How many pounds were collected in Week 4?', NULL, 3, 'High', '60 lbs', '25 lbs', '15 lbs', '20 lbs', 'D', 'active', 'Sum of Weeks 1, 2, 3 = 18 + 25 + 17 = 60 lbs. Week 4 = 80 - 60 = 20 lbs.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student found the sum of the first three weeks (60 lbs) and did not subtract from 80.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 60 from the total 80 lbs: 80 - 60 = 20 lbs.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed the value of Week 2.\", \"remediation\": \"80 - (18 + 25 + 17) = 80 - 60 = 20 lbs.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 80 - 65.\", \"remediation\": \"18 + 25 + 17 = 60; 80 - 60 = 20 lbs.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3124, 6, 5, 13, 'Scaled Bar Graphs', 315, 3, 'In a scaled bar graph, 14 students chose Green, 22 chose Blue, and 8 chose Yellow. If a student claims \"More students chose Green and Yellow combined than chose Blue\", is the claim correct?', NULL, 3, 'High', 'No, because Blue has fewer votes', 'No, because 14 + 8 = 22, which is EQUAL to Blue, not more', 'Yes, because 14 + 8 = 24, which is greater than 22', 'Yes, because Green has 14 and Yellow has 8', 'B', 'active', 'Green + Yellow = 14 + 8 = 22. Blue has 22. They are exactly equal, so Green and Yellow combined is not \"more\".', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Blue has 22, which is not fewer than Green or Yellow alone.\", \"remediation\": \"Combined is 22, which equals Blue.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error (14 + 8 = 22, not 24).\", \"remediation\": \"14 + 8 = 22. Since Blue is also 22, they are equal.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student agreed without adding.\", \"remediation\": \"14 + 8 = 22, which equals Blue (22).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3125, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 3, 'A line plot shows ribbon lengths: three ribbons are 1/2 ft long and two ribbons are 1/4 ft long. What is the COMBINED total length of all 5 ribbons?', NULL, 3, 'High', '1 1/2 feet', '2 1/2 feet', '2 feet', '1 foot', 'C', 'active', 'Three ribbons of 1/2 ft = 3 × 1/2 = 3/2 = 1 1/2 ft. Two ribbons of 1/4 ft = 2 × 1/4 = 2/4 = 1/2 ft. Total = 1 1/2 + 1/2 = 2 feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only added the three 1/2 ft ribbons.\", \"remediation\": \"Include the two 1/4 ft ribbons (2/4 = 1/2): 1 1/2 + 1/2 = 2 feet.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added fractions incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"3(1/2) + 2(1/4) = 3/2 + 1/2 = 4/2 = 2 feet.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student only calculated 2/4 + 1/2 = 1.\", \"remediation\": \"There are three 1/2-ft ribbons (3/2) plus two 1/4-ft ribbons (1/2): 3/2 + 1/2 = 2 ft.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3126, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'A line plot shows insect lengths in inches: Xs are placed at 1/8, 2/8, 3/8, 4/8, 5/8. What is the measurement unit for the fractions shown on the line plot?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Eighths of an inch (1/8)', 'Halves of an inch', 'Fourths of an inch', 'Whole inches', 'A', 'active', 'The denominator of each fraction on the line plot is 8, which means measurements are in eighths of an inch.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student confused eighths with halves.\", \"remediation\": \"Denominator 8 represents eighths.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student thought fractions are fourths.\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator is 8, so the unit is eighths (1/8).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student overlooked the fractional denominators.\", \"remediation\": \"The numbers are fractions of an inch divided into eighths.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3127, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'On a line plot with eighths of an inch, there are two Xs at 1/8, three Xs at 3/8, and one X at 7/8. How many total items are plotted?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '3 items', '8 items', '6 items', '11 items', 'C', 'active', 'Count all the Xs on the plot: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6 items.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted the number of tick marks with Xs.\", \"remediation\": \"Count each X individually: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6 items.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student read the denominator 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Total items equals total Xs: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added numerators: 1 + 3 + 7 = 11.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the number of items plotted (total Xs), not the sum of numerators.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3128, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Patterns', 319, 4, 'Look at the data values: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20. What is the pattern rule from one number to the next?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'Subtract 4', 'Multiply by 2', 'Add 2', 'Add 4', 'D', 'active', '4 + 4 = 8, 8 + 4 = 12, 12 + 4 = 16, 16 + 4 = 20. The rule is add 4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The numbers are increasing, not decreasing.\", \"remediation\": \"Numbers go up by 4: Add 4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student saw 4 × 2 = 8, but 8 × 2 is 16, not 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Check all terms: 8 to 12 is +4, 12 to 16 is +4; the rule is Add 4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student thought increment is 2.\", \"remediation\": \"8 - 4 = 4; the rule is Add 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3129, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Distribution', 320, 4, 'What is the range of the following data set: 3, 7, 2, 9, 5?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '9', '7', '2', '6', 'B', 'active', 'Range = Maximum - Minimum = 9 - 2 = 7.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the maximum value only.\", \"remediation\": \"Range is maximum minus minimum: 9 - 2 = 7.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the minimum value only.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract minimum from maximum: 9 - 2 = 7.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 9 - 3.\", \"remediation\": \"The minimum is 2: 9 - 2 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3130, 6, 5, 13, 'Collecting Data', 321, 4, 'Which tool is most appropriate to collect measurement data on the lengths of students\' shoes?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'A ruler or tape measure', 'A stopwatch', 'A thermometer', 'A bathroom scale', 'A', 'active', 'Length is measured with a ruler or tape measure.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Stopwatches measure time elapsed.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a ruler or measuring tape.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Thermometers measure temperature.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a ruler to measure shoe length.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Scales measure weight/mass.\", \"remediation\": \"Shoe length requires a length measuring tool: ruler.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3131, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'On a fraction line plot, there is one X at 1/4 and two Xs at 3/4. What is the difference between 3/4 and 1/4?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '4/4 (or 1)', '2/4 (or 1/2)', '1/4', '2/8', 'B', 'active', 'Subtract fractions with like denominators: 3/4 - 1/4 = 2/4 = 1/2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 3/4 + 1/4 = 4/4.\", \"remediation\": \"Difference means subtract: 3/4 - 1/4 = 2/4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 3 - 2.\", \"remediation\": \"3/4 - 1/4 = 2/4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted denominators (4 - 4).\", \"remediation\": \"When subtracting like fractions, keep the denominator: (3 - 1)/4 = 2/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3132, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Tables', 317, 4, 'A data table shows heights of 5 seedlings in inches: 2 1/8, 2 3/8, 2 5/8, 2 3/8, 2 7/8. Which height appears most often?', NULL, 4, 'Low', '2 7/8 inches', '2 1/8 inches', '2 5/8 inches', '2 3/8 inches', 'D', 'active', '2 3/8 appears twice, while all other values appear only once.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the largest value.\", \"remediation\": \"2 3/8 is the mode.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the smallest value.\", \"remediation\": \"The value that appears most often is 2 3/8 (appears twice).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the middle value.\", \"remediation\": \"Look at the frequency: 2 3/8 occurs 2 times.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3133, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Collection', 322, 4, 'Why do scientists organize raw data into tables and line plots?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'To make the data easier to read, see patterns, and analyze', 'To make the numbers bigger', 'To hide the mistakes', 'To change the measurements', 'A', 'active', 'Organizing data into tables and plots allows us to easily see patterns, frequencies, and distributions.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Organizing data does not change the numerical values.\", \"remediation\": \"Tables and plots help analyze and interpret data clearly.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Data tables reveal data transparently, not hide errors.\", \"remediation\": \"Graphs help visualize information.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Data organization preserves the original measurements.\", \"remediation\": \"The purpose is clear visualization and analysis.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3134, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'A line plot shows ribbon lengths in yards: three ribbons are 1/8 yd, two ribbons are 3/8 yd, and one ribbon is 5/8 yd. What is the total combined length of all the 1/8 yd and 3/8 yd ribbons?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '14/8 yards', '4/8 yards (or 1/2 yard)', '9/8 yards (or 1 1/8 yards)', '6/8 yards', 'C', 'active', 'Three ribbons of 1/8 yd = 3/8 yd. Two ribbons of 3/8 yd = 6/8 yd. Total = 3/8 + 6/8 = 9/8 = 1 1/8 yards.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student included the 5/8 yd ribbon as well.\", \"remediation\": \"The question specifies only 1/8 and 3/8 yd ribbons: 3/8 + 6/8 = 9/8.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 1/8 + 3/8 only once without multiplying by the number of ribbons.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply by the frequency of each: 3(1/8) + 2(3/8) = 3/8 + 6/8 = 9/8 yards.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student only calculated 2 × 3/8 = 6/8.\", \"remediation\": \"Include the three 1/8-yd ribbons: 3/8 + 6/8 = 9/8 yards.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3135, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Distribution', 320, 4, 'On a line plot, the shortest screw is 3/8 inch and the longest screw is 7/8 inch. What is the difference in length between the longest and shortest screws?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '10/8 inches', '3/8 inch', '5/8 inch', '4/8 inch (or 1/2 inch)', 'D', 'active', 'Difference = 7/8 - 3/8 = 4/8 = 1/2 inch.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 7/8 + 3/8 = 10/8.\", \"remediation\": \"Difference means subtract: 7/8 - 3/8 = 4/8 = 1/2 inch.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 7 - 4 = 3.\", \"remediation\": \"7/8 - 3/8 = 4/8 inch.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the numerator.\", \"remediation\": \"7 - 3 = 4, so the answer is 4/8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3136, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'Four beakers contain water: 1/4 liter, 2/4 liter, 3/4 liter, and 2/4 liter. If all the water is combined into one container, what is the total volume of water?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '8/16 liter', '8/4 liters (or 2 liters)', '6/4 liters (or 1 1/2 liters)', '4/4 liters (or 1 liter)', 'B', 'active', 'Sum the fractions: 1/4 + 2/4 + 3/4 + 2/4 = 8/4 = 2 liters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added the denominators (4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16).\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the like denominator 4: 8/4 = 2 liters.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student missed one of the 2/4 beakers (1 + 2 + 3 = 6/4).\", \"remediation\": \"There are four beakers: 1 + 2 + 3 + 2 = 8/4 = 2 liters.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student stopped at 1 liter.\", \"remediation\": \"Add all four: 1/4 + 2/4 + 3/4 + 2/4 = 8/4 = 2 L.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3137, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Patterns', 319, 4, 'A plant\'s growth in height is recorded each week: Week 1: 1 1/4 in, Week 2: 1 3/4 in, Week 3: 2 1/4 in, Week 4: 2 3/4 in. If the pattern continues, how tall will the plant be in Week 5?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '3 inches', '3 1/2 inches', '3 1/4 inches', '2 3/4 inches', 'C', 'active', 'The pattern increases by 2/4 (1/2) inch each week: 2 3/4 + 2/4 = 3 1/4 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added only 1/4 inch (2 3/4 + 1/4 = 3).\", \"remediation\": \"Notice the weekly increase is 2/4: 2 3/4 + 2/4 = 3 1/4 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 3/4 inch.\", \"remediation\": \"Each week adds 2/4 inch: 2 3/4 + 2/4 = 3 1/4 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student repeated Week 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 2/4 to Week 4: 2 3/4 + 2/4 = 3 1/4 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3138, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'A line plot shows lengths of 10 nails: four are 1/2 inch, three are 3/4 inch, and three are 1 inch. How many nails are SHORTER than 1 inch?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '7 nails', '3 nails', '10 nails', '4 nails', 'A', 'active', 'Nails shorter than 1 inch are those at 1/2 in (4) and 3/4 in (3): 4 + 3 = 7 nails.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the count of nails that are 1 inch.\", \"remediation\": \"Combine nails at 1/2 in and 3/4 in: 4 + 3 = 7 nails.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student included nails at 1 inch (total = 10).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"Shorter than 1 inch\\\" does not include 1 inch: 4 + 3 = 7 nails.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted nails at 1/2 inch.\", \"remediation\": \"Nails at 3/4 in are also shorter than 1 in: 4 + 3 = 7.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3139, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Distribution', 320, 4, 'A data set of test scores is: 75, 80, 85, 90, 95. If a new score of 100 is added, what happens to the range of the data set?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'The range stays the same', 'The range decreases', 'The range increases by 5 points (from 20 to 25)', 'The range doubles', 'C', 'active', 'Original range = 95 - 75 = 20. New range = 100 - 75 = 25. The range increases by 25 - 20 = 5 points.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed adding a number never changes range.\", \"remediation\": \"100 is a new maximum, so the range increases from 20 to 25.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student thought range decreases.\", \"remediation\": \"Since the spread widened, the range increased.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student thought range doubled.\", \"remediation\": \"Range changed from 20 to 25, an increase of 5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3140, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'A baker measures flour scoops: two scoops of 3/8 cup and two scoops of 5/8 cup. What is the total amount of flour scooped?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '14/8 cups', '8/8 cup (or 1 cup)', '10/8 cups', '16/8 cups (or 2 cups)', 'D', 'active', 'Two of 3/8 = 6/8. Two of 5/8 = 10/8. Total = 6/8 + 10/8 = 16/8 = 2 cups.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error: 6 + 10 = 16, not 14.\", \"remediation\": \"6/8 + 10/8 = 16/8 = 2 cups.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only added one 3/8 and one 5/8 (3/8 + 5/8 = 8/8 = 1 cup).\", \"remediation\": \"There are two of each scoop: 2(3/8) + 2(5/8) = 6/8 + 10/8 = 16/8 = 2 cups.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only calculated 2 × 5/8 = 10/8.\", \"remediation\": \"Include the two 3/8 scoops: 6/8 + 10/8 = 16/8 = 2 cups.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3141, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Tables', 317, 4, 'A table shows miles walked by Leo: Mon: 2 1/4 mi, Tue: 1 3/4 mi, Wed: 3 mi. What is the total distance Leo walked over the three days?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '6 miles', '7 miles', '6 1/2 miles', '8 miles', 'B', 'active', '2 1/4 + 1 3/4 = 3 + 4/4 = 4 miles. 4 + 3 = 7 miles.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot that 1/4 + 3/4 = 1 whole (got 2 + 1 + 3 = 6).\", \"remediation\": \"Add fractions: 1/4 + 3/4 = 4/4 = 1; 2 + 1 + 3 + 1 = 7 miles.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added fractions incorrectly as 2/4.\", \"remediation\": \"1/4 + 3/4 = 1; 2 + 1 + 3 + 1 = 7 miles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated sum.\", \"remediation\": \"2 1/4 + 1 3/4 = 4; 4 + 3 = 7 miles.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3142, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Collection', 322, 4, 'A scientist measures rainfall in millimeters at the same time every day for 2 weeks. Why is keeping the observation time consistent important?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', 'It ensures fair, accurate, and comparable data', 'It guarantees no rain on weekends', 'It makes the rainfall greater', 'It changes the units of measurement', 'A', 'active', 'Consistent measurement procedures ensure that data collected across days is directly comparable and reliable.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Measurement does not control the weather.\", \"remediation\": \"Consistent timing ensures comparability.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Measurement timing does not alter weather.\", \"remediation\": \"Consistency ensures fair comparison across data points.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Units remain millimeters regardless of time.\", \"remediation\": \"Standardized conditions ensure reliable data.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3143, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'A line plot shows wire lengths in feet: 1/8 ft has 1 X, 3/8 ft has 3 Xs, 5/8 ft has 2 Xs, and 7/8 ft has 2 Xs. What is the total length of all 8 pieces of wire combined?', NULL, 4, 'High', '4 feet (or 32/8 feet)', '4 1/4 feet (or 34/8 feet)', '3 1/2 feet (or 28/8 feet)', '5 feet (or 40/8 feet)', 'B', 'active', 'Total length = 1(1/8) + 3(3/8) + 2(5/8) + 2(7/8) = 1/8 + 9/8 + 10/8 + 14/8 = 34/8 = 4 2/8 = 4 1/4 feet.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 32/8 = 4 (missed 2/8).\", \"remediation\": \"Sum of numerators is 1 + 9 + 10 + 14 = 34; 34/8 = 4 1/4 feet.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student omitted the two 7/8 pieces.\", \"remediation\": \"Include all 8 pieces: total is 34/8 = 4 1/4 feet.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded up to 5 feet.\", \"remediation\": \"34/8 = 4 2/8 = 4 1/4 feet.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3144, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'A student has five bags of seeds weighing 1/8 lb, 2/8 lb, 3/8 lb, 4/8 lb, and 5/8 lb. What is the difference between the sum of the two heaviest bags and the sum of the two lightest bags?', NULL, 4, 'High', '4/8 lb', '9/8 lb', '3/8 lb', '6/8 lb (or 3/4 lb)', 'D', 'active', 'Two heaviest bags = 4/8 + 5/8 = 9/8 lb. Two lightest bags = 1/8 + 2/8 = 3/8 lb. Difference = 9/8 - 3/8 = 6/8 = 3/4 lb.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted the extremes (5/8 - 1/8 = 4/8).\", \"remediation\": \"Sum the two heaviest (9/8) and two lightest (3/8), then subtract: 9/8 - 3/8 = 6/8 = 3/4 lb.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only found the sum of the two heaviest bags (9/8 lb).\", \"remediation\": \"Now subtract the sum of the two lightest bags (3/8 lb): 9/8 - 3/8 = 6/8 = 3/4 lb.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student found the sum of the two lightest bags (3/8 lb).\", \"remediation\": \"Find the difference between 9/8 and 3/8: 6/8 lb.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3145, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Distribution', 320, 4, 'A line plot shows plant growth in inches: three plants at 1/4 in, four plants at 2/4 in, and one plant at 4/4 (1) in. If the 1-inch plant is removed, what is the new range of the data?', NULL, 4, 'High', '1/4 inch', '2/4 inch (or 1/2 inch)', '3/4 inch', '0 inches', 'A', 'active', 'With the 1-inch plant removed, the maximum remaining is 2/4 in and the minimum is 1/4 in. New range = 2/4 - 1/4 = 1/4 inch.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the maximum value (2/4 in) instead of range.\", \"remediation\": \"Range = max - min: 2/4 - 1/4 = 1/4 inch.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the original range before removal: 4/4 - 1/4 = 3/4 in.\", \"remediation\": \"The 1-inch plant was removed, so the new max is 2/4: 2/4 - 1/4 = 1/4 inch.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student thought all remaining plants are identical.\", \"remediation\": \"There are plants at 1/4 and 2/4: range is 1/4 inch.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3146, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Patterns', 319, 4, 'A sequence of data values follows two rules: Pattern A starts at 0 and adds 3. Pattern B starts at 0 and adds 6. When Pattern A reaches 15, what value is Pattern B?', NULL, 4, 'High', '45', '25', '30', '18', 'C', 'active', 'Pattern A: 0, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 (5 steps of +3). Pattern B takes 5 steps of +6: 5 × 6 = 30. (Notice each term in B is twice term in A).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 15 by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Pattern B is 2 × Pattern A: 15 × 2 = 30.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 25.\", \"remediation\": \"Since Pattern B grows twice as fast as Pattern A (+6 vs +3), when A is 15, B is 15 × 2 = 30.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student only took 3 steps.\", \"remediation\": \"Pattern A took 5 steps to reach 15: 5 × 6 = 30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3147, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'A chef has 4 containers of oil: 3/8 liter, 5/8 liter, 7/8 liter, and 1/8 liter. If the chef wants to redistribute the oil so that all 4 containers have the EXACT same amount, how much oil will be in each container?', NULL, 4, 'High', '16/8 liters (or 2 liters)', '3/8 liter', '5/8 liter', '4/8 liter (or 1/2 liter)', 'D', 'active', 'Total oil = 3/8 + 5/8 + 7/8 + 1/8 = 16/8 = 2 liters. Divided equally among 4 containers: 2 liters ÷ 4 = 2/4 = 4/8 = 1/2 liter each.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student found the total volume (16/8 = 2 liters) but forgot to divide among the 4 containers.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the total (16/8) by 4: (16/8) ÷ 4 = 4/8 = 1/2 liter per container.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed the first container amount.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the average: (16/8) ÷ 4 = 4/8 = 1/2 liter.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose a middle value without calculating the fair share.\", \"remediation\": \"Sum all containers (16/8) and divide by 4: 4/8 liter.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3148, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Distribution', 320, 4, 'A student records daily study times in hours: 1/2, 3/4, 1/2, 1, 1 1/4. What is the difference between the longest and shortest study session?', NULL, 4, 'High', '1 hour', '3/4 hour (or 45 minutes)', '1/2 hour', '1 1/4 hours', 'B', 'active', 'Longest = 1 1/4 hours = 5/4 hours. Shortest = 1/2 hour = 2/4 hours. Difference = 5/4 - 2/4 = 3/4 hour.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 1 1/4 - 1/4.\", \"remediation\": \"Shortest is 1/2: 1 1/4 - 1/2 = 3/4 hour.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 1 - 1/2 = 1/2.\", \"remediation\": \"The maximum is 1 1/4: 1 1/4 - 1/2 = 5/4 - 2/4 = 3/4 hour.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the maximum value without subtracting the minimum.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract minimum (1/2) from maximum (1 1/4): 3/4 hour.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3149, 6, 5, 13, 'Collecting Data', 321, 4, 'Four students measure the length of the same desk and get: 36 1/8 in, 36 2/8 in, 36 1/8 in, and 36 0/8 in. What is the most likely reason the measurements vary slightly?', NULL, 4, 'High', 'The desk changed size dramatically between measurements', 'Rulers change length during the day', 'Small differences in ruler placement and reading precision by different people', 'One student used meters instead of inches', 'C', 'active', 'Small measurement variations (within 1/8 to 2/8 inch) typically occur due to human differences in ruler alignment and rounding precision.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Desks do not dramatically change size during measurement.\", \"remediation\": \"Minor differences of 1/8 in are typical measurement errors.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Standard rigid rulers maintain their length.\", \"remediation\": \"Human precision and eye alignment cause slight measurement variation.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The values are all around 36 inches; meters would be vastly different numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Slight differences come from human precision and alignment.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3150, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 4, 'A line plot shows fabric scrap lengths: two at 1/4 yd, three at 2/4 yd, and one at 3/4 yd. If a craft project requires 2 1/4 yards of fabric in total, do all the scraps combined provide enough fabric?', NULL, 4, 'High', 'No, because one at 1/4 yd, two at 2/4 yd, and one at 3/4 yd total 8/4 = 2 yards, which is 1/4 yard short of 2 1/4 yards', 'No, because the scraps only total 1 yard', 'Yes, because the scraps total 2 1/2 yards', 'Yes, because the scraps total exactly 2 1/4 yards', 'A', 'active', 'Total fabric = 1/4 + 2(2/4) + 3/4 = 1/4 + 4/4 + 3/4 = 8/4 = 2 yards. Since 2 yards < 2 1/4 yards, it is 1/4 yard short.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student underestimated the sum.\", \"remediation\": \"8/4 = 2 yards.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 10/4 = 2 1/2 yards.\", \"remediation\": \"Sum: 1/4 + 4/4 + 3/4 = 8/4 = 2 yards. 2 yards is not enough for 2 1/4 yards.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed the scraps match 2 1/4 yards.\", \"remediation\": \"8/4 is 2 whole yards, which is 1/4 yard less than 2 1/4 yards.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:15:20', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3151, 6, 5, 13, 'Ordered Pairs', 324, 5, 'In the ordered pair (4, 7), which number represents the horizontal x-coordinate?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '7', '11', '4', '3', 'C', 'active', 'An ordered pair is written as (x, y). The first number is the x-coordinate (horizontal distance from the origin), which is 4.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the y-coordinate (vertical distance).\", \"remediation\": \"Remember the alphabetical order: (x, y). The first number is x = 4, and the second is y = 7.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 4 + 7 = 11.\", \"remediation\": \"An ordered pair shows separate coordinates, not a sum. The x-coordinate is 4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 7 - 4 = 3.\", \"remediation\": \"The x-coordinate is directly the first value: 4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:43', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3152, 6, 5, 13, 'Coordinate Data', 323, 5, 'What are the coordinates of the origin on a coordinate plane?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '(1, 0)', '(1, 1)', '(0, 1)', '(0, 0)', 'D', 'active', 'The origin is the intersection of the x-axis and y-axis, located at (0, 0).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose a point on the x-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"The origin is (0, 0).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student thought the coordinate plane starts at (1, 1).\", \"remediation\": \"The origin is the zero starting point: (0, 0).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose a point on the y-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"Both coordinates at the origin are zero: (0, 0).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3153, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Patterns', 325, 5, 'Rule 1: Start at 0 and add 2 (0, 2, 4, 6, ...). Rule 2: Start at 0 and add 6 (0, 6, 12, 18, ...). How does each term in Rule 2 compare to the corresponding term in Rule 1?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'Each term in Rule 2 is 4 more than Rule 1', 'Each term in Rule 2 is 3 times the corresponding term in Rule 1', 'Each term in Rule 2 is twice Rule 1', 'There is no relationship', 'B', 'active', 'Comparing corresponding terms: 6 ÷ 2 = 3, 12 ÷ 4 = 3, 18 ÷ 6 = 3. Each term in Rule 2 is 3 times the term in Rule 1.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only looked at the difference for the second term (6 - 2 = 4) and ignored later terms (12 - 4 = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"Check the multiplicative relationship across all terms: 6 = 3 × 2, 12 = 3 × 4; each term is 3 times larger.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 2 times.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide Rule 2 by Rule 1: 6 / 2 = 3 times.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"There is a clear proportional relationship (factor of 3).\", \"remediation\": \"The terms are in a constant 1:3 ratio.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3154, 6, 5, 13, 'Range', 327, 5, 'A data set has values: 12, 15, 19, 24, 30. What is the range?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '18', '20', '30', '12', 'A', 'active', 'Range = Maximum - Minimum = 30 - 12 = 18.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded or made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"30 - 12 = 18.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the maximum value only.\", \"remediation\": \"Range is the difference between maximum and minimum: 30 - 12 = 18.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the minimum value.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract minimum from maximum: 30 - 12 = 18.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3155, 6, 5, 13, 'Outliers', 328, 5, 'Look at this set of student quiz scores: 82, 85, 88, 84, 15, 86. Which score is an outlier?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '88', '15', '82', '85', 'B', 'active', 'An outlier is a data value that is significantly far away from the rest of the data. 15 is drastically lower than the other scores in the 80s.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student picked the highest score (88), which is close to the other values.\", \"remediation\": \"88 is close to 84, 85, 86; 15 is far separated from all the other values.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"82 is part of the cluster in the 80s.\", \"remediation\": \"An outlier lies far outside the main cluster: 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"85 is near the center of the scores.\", \"remediation\": \"15 is the isolated value (outlier).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3156, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Representation', 329, 5, 'To plot the point (3, 5) starting from the origin (0, 0), which direction do you move first?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'Move diagonally 8 units', 'Move 5 units right, then 3 units up', 'Move 3 units up, then 5 units right', 'Move 3 units right along the x-axis, then 5 units up', 'D', 'active', 'In (x, y), the first coordinate is horizontal (x = 3 units right), and the second is vertical (y = 5 units up).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Coordinates are plotted along orthogonal axes, not diagonally as a sum.\", \"remediation\": \"Move 3 right, then 5 up.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student reversed x and y coordinates (plotted (5, 3)).\", \"remediation\": \"Always move horizontally first (x-coordinate), then vertically (y-coordinate): 3 right, 5 up.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student moved up first.\", \"remediation\": \"The x-coordinate comes first: move along the horizontal axis before moving up.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3157, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 5, 'A line plot shows liquid in 4 test tubes: 1/8 L, 1/8 L, 3/8 L, and 3/8 L. What is the total volume of liquid in all 4 tubes combined?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '8/8 L (or 1 Liter)', '4/8 L', '6/8 L', '8/32 L', 'A', 'active', 'Sum = 1/8 + 1/8 + 3/8 + 3/8 = 8/8 = 1 Liter.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only added 1/8 + 3/8 = 4/8.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 tubes: 1/8 + 1/8 + 3/8 + 3/8 = 8/8 = 1 Liter.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 1/8 + 1/8 + 3/8 = 5/8 or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Add all four numerators: 1 + 1 + 3 + 3 = 8; 8/8 = 1 L.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added denominators (8 + 8 + 8 + 8 = 32).\", \"remediation\": \"Keep the like denominator 8: 8/8 = 1 Liter.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3158, 6, 5, 13, 'Coordinate Data', 323, 5, 'A point has coordinates (6, 0). Where does this point lie on the coordinate plane?', NULL, 5, 'Low', 'In Quadrant II', 'On the y-axis', 'On the x-axis', 'At the origin', 'C', 'active', 'Since the y-coordinate is 0, the point has vertical elevation of 0 and lies directly on the horizontal x-axis.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Points with non-negative coordinates lie in Quadrant I or on axes.\", \"remediation\": \"It lies on the positive x-axis.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student confused the x-axis with the y-axis.\", \"remediation\": \"A point with y = 0 lies on the x-axis; a point with x = 0 lies on the y-axis.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The origin is (0, 0), but this point is at x = 6.\", \"remediation\": \"(6, 0) is 6 units to the right on the x-axis.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3159, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 5, 'A line plot displays the weights of 6 bags of candy: two bags weigh 1/4 lb, three weigh 1/2 lb, and one weighs 3/4 lb. What is the combined total weight of all 6 bags?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '2 lbs (or 8/4 lbs)', '3 lbs (or 12/4 lbs)', '1 1/2 lbs', '2 3/4 lbs (or 11/4 lbs)', 'D', 'active', 'Convert to fourths: 1/2 = 2/4. Total = 2(1/4) + 3(2/4) + 1(3/4) = 2/4 + 6/4 + 3/4 = 11/4 = 2 3/4 lbs.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error in fourths (calculated 8/4 = 2 lbs).\", \"remediation\": \"Sum of fourths: 2 + 6 + 3 = 11 fourths = 11/4 = 2 3/4 lbs.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded up to 3 lbs.\", \"remediation\": \"11/4 = 2 3/4 lbs.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student missed several data points.\", \"remediation\": \"Include all 6 bags: 2/4 + 6/4 + 3/4 = 11/4 = 2 3/4 lbs.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3160, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 5, 'Five students each have a beaker of water with amounts: 1/8 L, 1/4 L, 1/4 L, 3/8 L, and 1/2 L. If all the water is combined and shared EQUALLY among the 5 students, how much water does each student get?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '1/5 L', '3/10 L (or 1.5/5 L = 12/40 L)', '1 1/2 L', '1/4 L', 'B', 'active', 'Convert to eighths: 1/8 + 2/8 + 2/8 + 3/8 + 4/8 = 12/8 = 3/2 L = 1 1/2 L total. Fair share for 5 students = (3/2) ÷ 5 = 3/10 Liter each.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 1/5 L.\", \"remediation\": \"Total is 1.5 L; 1.5 ÷ 5 = 0.3 = 3/10 L.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student found the total water (12/8 = 1 1/2 L) but forgot to divide by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Fair share means dividing the total by 5: (3/2) ÷ 5 = 3/10 L per student.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 1/4 L (2/8 L).\", \"remediation\": \"Total is 12/8; divide by 5: (12/8) × (1/5) = 12/40 = 3/10 L.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3161, 6, 5, 13, 'Coordinate Data', 323, 5, 'A car travels at a constant speed: at 1 hour it has gone 50 miles, at 2 hours 100 miles, and at 3 hours 150 miles. If time is on the x-axis and distance is on the y-axis, what ordered pair represents the distance traveled at 4 hours?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '(200, 4)', '(4, 150)', '(4, 200)', '(5, 250)', 'C', 'active', 'The speed is 50 miles per hour. At 4 hours (x = 4), distance is 4 × 50 = 200 miles (y = 200). The ordered pair is (4, 200).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student reversed x and y coordinates: (y, x) instead of (x, y).\", \"remediation\": \"Time is on the x-axis (first) and distance is on the y-axis (second): (4, 200).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student repeated the 3-hour distance.\", \"remediation\": \"At 4 hours, distance is 4 × 50 = 200: (4, 200).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the point for 5 hours.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for 4 hours: (4, 200).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3162, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Patterns', 325, 5, 'Pattern X starts at 0 with rule \"Add 3\": 0, 3, 6, 9, 12. Pattern Y starts at 0 with rule \"Add 9\": 0, 9, 18, 27, 36. Form ordered pairs (x, y). What is the value of y when x = 15?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '45', '54', '30', '40', 'A', 'active', 'Notice that each y-value is 3 times the corresponding x-value (9 = 3 × 3, 18 = 3 × 6, 27 = 3 × 9). When x = 15, y = 15 × 3 = 45.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated for x = 18.\", \"remediation\": \"For x = 15: y = 3 × 15 = 45.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 15 to 15 (doubled).\", \"remediation\": \"The rule relationship is y = 3x: 15 × 3 = 45.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 40.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply x by 3: 15 × 3 = 45.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3163, 6, 5, 13, 'Outliers', 328, 5, 'A track athlete records sprint times in seconds: 12.1, 12.3, 12.0, 12.2, 19.5, 12.4. What effect does the outlier 19.5 have on the average (mean) sprint time?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'It decreases the mean time', 'It has no effect on the mean', 'It significantly increases the mean time, making the athlete appear slower', 'It makes the median smaller', 'C', 'active', 'An unusually large value pulls the mean upward (increases it). For sprint times, a higher time indicates a slower speed.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"An abnormally high value increases the sum and therefore increases the mean.\", \"remediation\": \"High outliers pull the mean up, not down.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The mean uses all values in its calculation, so an outlier always affects it.\", \"remediation\": \"The mean is sensitive to extreme values and increases when a high outlier is included.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The outlier is on the high end, so it will not decrease the median.\", \"remediation\": \"High outliers increase the mean.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3164, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Representation', 329, 5, 'Points A(2, 3), B(6, 3), and C(6, 7) are plotted on a grid. If point D is added to form a square, what must the coordinates of point D be?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '(3, 7)', '(2, 6)', '(7, 2)', '(2, 7)', 'D', 'active', 'Base AB has length 6 - 2 = 4 along y = 3. Side BC has length 7 - 3 = 4 along x = 6. Point D must align with x = 2 and y = 7, which is (2, 7).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used x = 3 instead of x = 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Align vertically with point A(2, 3): x = 2, so D is (2, 7).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used y = 6 instead of y = 7.\", \"remediation\": \"The top side must be at y = 7: (2, 7).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student reversed coordinates (7, 2).\", \"remediation\": \"D has x = 2 and y = 7: (2, 7).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3165, 6, 5, 13, 'Range', 327, 5, 'A scientist records temperatures in degrees Celsius: -2, 4, 8, 15, 3. What is the range of these temperatures?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '13 degrees', '17 degrees', '15 degrees', '19 degrees', 'B', 'active', 'Maximum = 15°C, Minimum = -2°C. Range = Maximum - Minimum = 15 - (-2) = 15 + 2 = 17 degrees.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 15 - 2 = 13 (forgot that the minimum is negative 2).\", \"remediation\": \"Subtracting a negative number adds its opposite: 15 - (-2) = 15 + 2 = 17.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the maximum value (15).\", \"remediation\": \"Range is max minus min: 15 - (-2) = 17 degrees.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error.\", \"remediation\": \"15 - (-2) = 17.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3166, 6, 5, 13, 'Ordered Pairs', 324, 5, 'Which point lies 5 units to the right of the y-axis and 8 units above the x-axis?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '(5, 8)', '(5, 0)', '(8, 5)', '(0, 8)', 'A', 'active', 'Distance to the right of the y-axis is the x-coordinate (5). Distance above the x-axis is the y-coordinate (8). The point is (5, 8).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student set y = 0.\", \"remediation\": \"8 units above x-axis means y = 8: (5, 8).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student reversed x and y coordinates (8, 5).\", \"remediation\": \"Distance from y-axis is x = 5; distance from x-axis is y = 8. Point is (5, 8).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student set x = 0.\", \"remediation\": \"5 units to the right of y-axis means x = 5: (5, 8).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3167, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Patterns', 325, 5, 'Two siblings save money weekly: Jack starts with $10 and saves $5 each week. Jill starts with $0 and saves $10 each week. At the end of which week will they have saved the EXACT same amount of money?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', 'Week 1', 'Week 2', 'Week 3', 'Week 5', 'B', 'active', 'Jack: Week 1 = $15, Week 2 = $20. Jill: Week 1 = $10, Week 2 = $20. Both have $20 at the end of Week 2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"At Week 1, Jack has $15 and Jill has $10 (not equal).\", \"remediation\": \"Check Week 2: Jack = 10 + 2(5) = $20; Jill = 2(10) = $20. They are equal at Week 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"At Week 3, Jack has $25 and Jill has $30.\", \"remediation\": \"They were equal at Week 2 ($20 each).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"At Week 5, Jill has $50 and Jack has $35.\", \"remediation\": \"Set equations equal: 10 + 5w = 10w => 5w = 10 => w = 2 weeks.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3168, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 5, 'A line plot shows fabric strip lengths in yards: three strips are 1/8 yd, four strips are 3/8 yd, and one strip is 7/8 yd. If all strips are placed end-to-end, what is the total length in yards?', NULL, 5, 'High', '2 1/2 yards (or 20/8 yards)', '2 yards (or 16/8 yards)', '3 yards (or 24/8 yards)', '2 3/4 yards (or 22/8 yards)', 'D', 'active', 'Sum = 3(1/8) + 4(3/8) + 1(7/8) = 3/8 + 12/8 + 7/8 = 22/8 = 2 6/8 = 2 3/4 yards.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 20/8 = 2 1/2 yards.\", \"remediation\": \"3 + 12 + 7 = 22, not 20; 22/8 = 2 3/4 yards.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 16/8 = 2 yards (missed 6/8).\", \"remediation\": \"Sum: 3/8 + 12/8 + 7/8 = 22/8 = 2 3/4 yards.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 24/8 = 3 yards.\", \"remediation\": \"3 + 12 + 7 = 22; 22/8 = 2 3/4 yards.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3169, 6, 5, 13, 'Fraction Line Plots', 318, 5, 'Eight bags of sand have weights shown on a line plot: two at 1/4 lb, four at 1/2 lb, and two at 3/4 lb. If the sand is redistributed so all eight bags have equal weight, what is the weight of each bag?', NULL, 5, 'High', '1/2 pound (or 4/8 lb)', '1/4 pound', '4 pounds total', '3/8 pound', 'A', 'active', 'Total weight = 2(1/4) + 4(1/2) + 2(3/4) = 2/4 + 8/4 + 6/4 = 16/4 = 4 pounds. Divided among 8 bags: 4 lbs ÷ 8 = 1/2 pound per bag.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose the minimum bag weight.\", \"remediation\": \"Find the mean: Total weight = 4 lbs. 4 ÷ 8 = 1/2 lb per bag.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the total weight instead of each bag\'s equal share.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the 4-pound total by the 8 bags: 4 ÷ 8 = 1/2 pound.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 3/8 lb.\", \"remediation\": \"4 lbs ÷ 8 bags = 1/2 lb each.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3170, 6, 5, 13, 'Coordinate Data', 323, 5, 'A rectangle on the coordinate grid has vertices at (2, 2), (8, 2), (8, 6), and (2, 6). What is the perimeter and area of this rectangle?', NULL, 5, 'High', 'Perimeter = 20 units, Area = 48 square units', 'Perimeter = 24 units, Area = 20 square units', 'Perimeter = 20 units, Area = 24 square units', 'Perimeter = 10 units, Area = 24 square units', 'C', 'active', 'Length = 8 - 2 = 6 units. Width = 6 - 2 = 4 units. Perimeter = 2(6 + 4) = 20 units. Area = 6 × 4 = 24 square units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the area.\", \"remediation\": \"Area = 6 × 4 = 24 square units.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student swapped perimeter and area values.\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter = 2(l + w) = 2(10) = 20. Area = l × w = 6 × 4 = 24.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated half-perimeter (6 + 4 = 10).\", \"remediation\": \"Perimeter requires all 4 sides: 2(6 + 4) = 20 units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3171, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Patterns', 325, 5, 'Pattern A: Start with 1, rule: multiply by 2 (1, 2, 4, 8, 16). Pattern B: Start with 5, rule: add 5 (5, 10, 15, 20, 25). At which term number (1st, 2nd, 3rd, ...) does Pattern A first become GREATER than Pattern B?', NULL, 5, 'High', '5th term (Pattern A is 16, Pattern B is 25)', '4th term (Pattern A is 8, Pattern B is 20)', '7th term (Pattern A is 64, Pattern B is 35)', '6th term (Pattern A is 32, Pattern B is 30)', 'D', 'active', 'Term 1: (1, 5); Term 2: (2, 10); Term 3: (4, 15); Term 4: (8, 20); Term 5: (16, 25); Term 6: (32, 30). At the 6th term, Pattern A (32) is greater than Pattern B (30).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"At term 5, A = 16 and B = 25, so A is still smaller than B.\", \"remediation\": \"Continue one more term: Term 6 gives A = 32 and B = 30, where A > B.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"At term 4, A = 8 and B = 20.\", \"remediation\": \"Pattern A exceeds B at Term 6: 32 > 30.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"While A > B at term 7, term 6 is the FIRST term where A > B.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the first time it occurs: 6th term.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3172, 6, 5, 13, 'Outliers', 328, 5, 'A set of 5 numbers has values: 10, 12, 14, 16, 18. Their mean is 14. If an outlier value of 70 is added to the data set, what happens to the mean and median?', NULL, 5, 'High', 'The median increases more than the mean', 'The mean increases significantly (from 14 to 23.3), while the median increases only slightly (from 14 to 15)', 'Both mean and median increase by 10 points', 'Neither mean nor median changes', 'B', 'active', 'Original mean = 70/5 = 14, median = 14. With 70 added: Sum = 140/6 ≈ 23.3. New ordered set: 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 70 => median = (14+16)/2 = 15. The mean changes drastically while the median is resistant.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The mean is much more sensitive to outliers than the median.\", \"remediation\": \"Mean changes by +9.3, while median only changes by +1.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student thought mean and median change by the same amount.\", \"remediation\": \"The median is resistant to outliers, while the mean is heavily pulled by extreme values.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Adding a new value changes the calculations.\", \"remediation\": \"An outlier significantly shifts the mean.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3173, 6, 5, 13, 'Coordinate Data', 323, 5, 'A triangle on a coordinate grid has vertices at (1, 1), (7, 1), and (4, 5). What is the area of the triangle?', NULL, 5, 'High', '24 square units', '14 square units', '12 square units', '18 square units', 'C', 'active', 'Base = 7 - 1 = 6 units along y = 1. Height = 5 - 1 = 4 units. Area = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 6 × 4 = 12 square units.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 2: 6 × 4 = 24.\", \"remediation\": \"Triangle area is 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 6 × 4 = 12 sq units.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated perimeter or added dimensions.\", \"remediation\": \"Area = 1/2 × 6 × 4 = 12.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the product.\", \"remediation\": \"1/2 × 24 = 12 square units.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3174, 6, 5, 13, 'Data Representation', 329, 5, 'On a graph of distance vs time for a hiker, Point A is (2, 6) and Point B is (4, 12), where x is time in hours and y is distance in miles. What is the hiker\'s rate of speed?', NULL, 5, 'High', '3 miles per hour', '4 miles per hour', '6 miles per hour', '2 miles per hour', 'A', 'active', 'Rate of speed = change in distance ÷ change in time = (12 - 6) ÷ (4 - 2) = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 miles per hour.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student used the x-coordinate of Point B.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate rate of change: (12 - 6) / (4 - 2) = 3 mph.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student used distance at 2 hours (6 miles) without dividing by time.\", \"remediation\": \"Speed = distance / time = 6 / 2 = 3 miles per hour.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided time by distance: 2 / 6 = 1/3 or guessed 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Speed = distance ÷ time = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 mph.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3175, 6, 5, 13, 'Range', 327, 5, 'A student measured lengths of 7 pencils: 3 1/8, 3 1/2, 3 3/4, 4 1/8, 4 1/4, 4 1/2, 4 7/8 inches. What is the range of pencil lengths?', NULL, 5, 'High', '1 1/2 inches (or 12/8 inches)', '4 7/8 inches', '1 3/4 inches (or 14/8 inches)', '1 1/8 inches', 'C', 'active', 'Maximum = 4 7/8 in = 39/8 in. Minimum = 3 1/8 in = 25/8 in. Range = 4 7/8 - 3 1/8 = 1 6/8 = 1 3/4 inches.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 4 1/2 - 3 = 1 1/2 (missed the actual maximum).\", \"remediation\": \"Max is 4 7/8 and min is 3 1/8: 4 7/8 - 3 1/8 = 1 6/8 = 1 3/4 inches.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the maximum value without subtracting the minimum.\", \"remediation\": \"Range = Max - Min: 4 7/8 - 3 1/8 = 1 3/4 inches.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the subtraction: 7/8 - 1/8 = 6/8, not 1/8.\", \"remediation\": \"4 7/8 - 3 1/8 = 1 6/8 = 1 3/4 inches.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3176, 6, 5, 13, 'Statistical Questions', 330, 6, 'Which of the following is a STATISTICAL question?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'How many days are in the month of February in a leap year?', 'How tall is Mr. Davis?', 'What is the capital of France?', 'How many hours of sleep do 6th graders get each night?', 'D', 'active', 'A statistical question anticipates variability in the data collected. Different 6th graders sleep different numbers of hours.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"There are always exactly 29 days in Feb of a leap year (no variability).\", \"remediation\": \"Statistical questions require variability.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Mr. Davis has one exact height (no variability).\", \"remediation\": \"A statistical question expects varying answers across individuals: e.g., heights of all teachers.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Paris is the only capital of France (single factual answer).\", \"remediation\": \"Statistical questions require data variability.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3177, 6, 5, 13, 'Mean', 332, 6, 'What is the mean (average) of the numbers: 4, 8, 6, 10, 12?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '6', '8', '40', '10', 'B', 'active', 'Sum = 4 + 8 + 6 + 10 + 12 = 40. Number of values = 5. Mean = 40 ÷ 5 = 8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student picked a number from the set without calculating.\", \"remediation\": \"Mean = Sum ÷ Count: 40 ÷ 5 = 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student found the sum (40) but forgot to divide by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the sum by the number of values: 40 ÷ 5 = 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated division.\", \"remediation\": \"40 ÷ 5 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3178, 6, 5, 13, 'Median', 333, 6, 'What is the median of the data set: 11, 3, 7, 15, 9?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '9', '9.2', '7', '11', 'A', 'active', 'First order the numbers from least to greatest: 3, 7, 9, 11, 15. The middle value is 9.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the mean (45 ÷ 5 = 9? wait, 9.2) instead of median.\", \"remediation\": \"Median is the middle value in ordered data: 9.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose 7 (which was listed in the middle of the unordered list).\", \"remediation\": \"Always order the data from least to greatest before finding the middle: 3, 7, 9, 11, 15 => median is 9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student picked 11.\", \"remediation\": \"The middle of 3, 7, 9, 11, 15 is 9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3179, 6, 5, 13, 'Range', 327, 6, 'What is the range of the following test scores: 65, 82, 90, 74, 98?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '98', '33', '65', '81.8', 'B', 'active', 'Range = Maximum - Minimum = 98 - 65 = 33.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the maximum score only.\", \"remediation\": \"Range is maximum minus minimum: 98 - 65 = 33.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the minimum score only.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 65 from 98: 98 - 65 = 33.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the mean score.\", \"remediation\": \"Range is the spread between extremes: 98 - 65 = 33.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3180, 6, 5, 13, 'Dot Plots', 334, 6, 'In a dot plot, what does a cluster of dots stacked high over a single number indicate?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'That number is wrong', 'That number is an outlier', 'That number is the range', 'That number has a high frequency (occurs many times)', 'D', 'active', 'Each dot represents an occurrence; a tall stack of dots indicates high frequency (mode).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"High frequency is a valid data occurrence.\", \"remediation\": \"It shows the mode or peak frequency.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"An outlier is isolated far from the other dots.\", \"remediation\": \"A tall stack means high frequency (many data points at that value).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Range is the spread between max and min.\", \"remediation\": \"A stack of dots shows frequency at that specific value.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3181, 6, 5, 13, 'Measures of Center', 331, 6, 'Which two statistics are common \"measures of center\" for a numerical data set?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'Mean and Median', 'Range and Mean Absolute Deviation', 'Maximum and Minimum', 'Dot plot and Histogram', 'A', 'active', 'Mean and Median are the primary measures of center that describe the central location of data.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Range and MAD are measures of variability/spread, not center.\", \"remediation\": \"Measures of center describe the middle (Mean, Median); measures of spread describe variation (Range, MAD, IQR).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Max and Min are extreme values.\", \"remediation\": \"Measures of center are Mean and Median.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Dot plots and histograms are graphical displays, not statistical measures.\", \"remediation\": \"Mean and Median summarize center.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3182, 6, 5, 13, 'Variability', 336, 6, 'What does a measure of variability (like Range or MAD) tell us about a data set?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'The title of the graph', 'The average value of the data', 'How spread out or dispersed the data values are', 'How many data points were collected', 'C', 'active', 'Variability describes the spread, dispersion, or clustering of values in a distribution.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Title is descriptive text.\", \"remediation\": \"Variability measures spread of values.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The average is a measure of center.\", \"remediation\": \"Variability describes how much data values differ from each other or from the center.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The count of data points is sample size (n).\", \"remediation\": \"Variability measures spread.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3183, 6, 5, 13, 'Median', 333, 6, 'When a data set has an EVEN number of values (such as 4 values: 2, 4, 8, 10), how do you find the median?', NULL, 6, 'Low', 'Pick the smaller middle number', 'Pick the larger middle number', 'Add all four numbers together', 'Find the average (mean) of the two middle numbers', 'D', 'active', 'When n is even, the median is the midpoint (mean) of the two middle numbers: (4 + 8) ÷ 2 = 6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose only the lower middle value.\", \"remediation\": \"Take the average of the two middle values: (4 + 8)/2 = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose only the upper middle value.\", \"remediation\": \"Average both middle values.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Adding all numbers gives the sum, not median.\", \"remediation\": \"Average the two middle values.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3184, 6, 5, 13, 'Statistical Questions', 330, 6, 'Which question is NOT a statistical question?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', 'How many minutes does it take workers in a factory to commute?', 'How old is the current President of the United States?', 'How many pets do families on my street own?', 'What are the shoe sizes of students in Grade 6?', 'B', 'active', 'The President has one exact age at any given time (no variability in data). All others have data variability.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Commute times vary across workers (statistical).\", \"remediation\": \"President\'s age is a single deterministic fact.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Different families own different numbers of pets (variability exists).\", \"remediation\": \"A question is statistical if responses vary. The President\'s age has only 1 answer (not statistical).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Shoe sizes vary across students (statistical).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for NOT a statistical question: President\'s age has no variability.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3185, 6, 5, 13, 'Median', 333, 6, 'What is the median of the data set: 14, 22, 8, 19, 31, 12?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '19', '14', '16.5', '17.6', 'C', 'active', 'Order the 6 values: 8, 12, 14, 19, 22, 31. The two middle values are 14 and 19. Median = (14 + 19) ÷ 2 = 33 ÷ 2 = 16.5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose 19 without averaging with 14.\", \"remediation\": \"For an even count, average the two middle numbers: (14 + 19)/2 = 16.5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose 14 without averaging.\", \"remediation\": \"The median is halfway between 14 and 19: 16.5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the mean (106 ÷ 6 = 17.67).\", \"remediation\": \"Median is the middle value: (14 + 19)/2 = 16.5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3186, 6, 5, 13, 'Mean Absolute Deviation', 335, 6, 'What is the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of the data set: 2, 4, 6, 8? (Hint: The mean is 5)', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '2', '2.5', '5', '8', 'A', 'active', 'Distances from mean 5: |2-5|=3, |4-5|=1, |6-5|=1, |8-5|=3. Sum of distances = 3 + 1 + 1 + 3 = 8. MAD = 8 ÷ 4 = 2.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the average.\", \"remediation\": \"8 ÷ 4 = 2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the mean (5) instead of the MAD.\", \"remediation\": \"MAD is the average distance from the mean: distances are 3, 1, 1, 3; average is 8/4 = 2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student found the sum of distances (8) but forgot to divide by 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the sum of distances by 4: 8 ÷ 4 = 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3187, 6, 5, 13, 'Dot Plots', 334, 6, 'A dot plot shows books read by students: 0 books (1 dot), 1 book (3 dots), 2 books (5 dots), 3 books (2 dots), 4 books (1 dot). What is the total number of students surveyed?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '10 students', '5 students', '12 students', '4 students', 'C', 'active', 'Count all dots: 1 + 3 + 5 + 2 + 1 = 12 dots (students).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student miscounted by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Count each dot: 1 + 3 + 5 + 2 + 1 = 12 students.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student counted the number of categories on the axis (0, 1, 2, 3, 4 = 5 categories).\", \"remediation\": \"Each dot is a student: sum the dots = 12 students.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the maximum value on the axis.\", \"remediation\": \"Total students surveyed equals total dots: 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3188, 6, 5, 13, 'Measures of Center', 331, 6, 'A real estate agent lists home sale prices in a neighborhood where most homes sell for around $200,000, but one mansion sells for $5,000,000. Which measure of center BEST represents a typical home price?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', 'Maximum, because it is the biggest', 'Mean, because it uses all numbers', 'Range, because it shows the difference', 'Median, because it is not distorted by the extreme outlier', 'D', 'active', 'When data is strongly skewed or has extreme outliers, the median is the preferred measure of center because it resists the distorting effect of the outlier.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Maximum represents the outlier mansion, not typical homes.\", \"remediation\": \"Median represents the typical home.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The mean will be pulled up to near $1,000,000, which does not represent typical $200,000 homes.\", \"remediation\": \"When extreme outliers exist, the median gives a much more representative typical value.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Range is a measure of spread, not center.\", \"remediation\": \"Use median for typical value with outliers.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3189, 6, 5, 13, 'Variability', 336, 6, 'Class A has test scores with MAD = 2. Class B has test scores with MAD = 10. Both classes have a mean score of 80. What does this indicate?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', 'Class B did better on the test than Class A', 'Class A scores are much closer to 80 (more consistent), while Class B scores are much more spread out', 'Class A has more students than Class B', 'Class A scores are more spread out than Class B', 'B', 'active', 'A smaller MAD indicates less variability (scores are closely clustered around the mean), while a larger MAD means greater spread.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Both classes have the same mean (80), so neither class did higher on average.\", \"remediation\": \"MAD measures spread, not performance level: smaller MAD means more consistent scores.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"MAD does not indicate sample size.\", \"remediation\": \"MAD measures data dispersion.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Class A has smaller MAD (2 vs 10), so it is less spread out, not more.\", \"remediation\": \"Smaller MAD = less spread.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3190, 6, 5, 13, 'Dot Plots', 334, 6, 'In a box plot (box-and-whisker plot), what percentage of the data lies inside the box (between the first quartile Q1 and third quartile Q3)?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '50%', '100%', '25%', '75%', 'A', 'active', 'Q1 is at the 25th percentile and Q3 is at the 75th percentile. The middle box contains 75% - 25% = 50% of the data.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"100% is the entire data set from min to max.\", \"remediation\": \"The box contains the middle 50%.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"25% is the amount in each whisker (min to Q1, or Q3 to max).\", \"remediation\": \"The middle box spans from Q1 to Q3 and contains exactly 50% of the data.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"75% is the cumulative percentage up to Q3.\", \"remediation\": \"Inside the box is Q3 - Q1 = 50%.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3191, 6, 5, 13, 'Mean', 332, 6, 'Maria has scored 80, 85, and 90 on her first three math tests. What score does she need on her fourth test to have an overall mean of 85?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '90', '85', '80', '95', 'B', 'active', 'To have a mean of 85 across 4 tests, the total points must be 4 × 85 = 340. Her first three tests sum to 80 + 85 + 90 = 255. Required score = 340 - 255 = 85.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 90.\", \"remediation\": \"Total needed = 4 × 85 = 340. 340 - 255 = 85.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 80.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate: 340 - 255 = 85.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 95.\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 85 = 340; 340 - 255 = 85.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3192, 6, 5, 13, 'Range', 327, 6, 'A data set has a minimum value of 14 and a range of 28. What is the maximum value of the data set?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '56', '14', '28', '42', 'D', 'active', 'Since Range = Maximum - Minimum, Maximum = Minimum + Range = 14 + 28 = 42.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled 28.\", \"remediation\": \"14 + 28 = 42.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 28 - 14 = 14.\", \"remediation\": \"Maximum is higher than minimum: add the range: 14 + 28 = 42.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student repeated the range.\", \"remediation\": \"Max = Min + Range = 14 + 28 = 42.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3193, 6, 5, 13, 'Mean Absolute Deviation', 335, 6, 'Find the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of the data set: 10, 15, 20, 25, 30.', NULL, 6, 'High', '6', '20', '30', '5', 'A', 'active', 'Mean = (10 + 15 + 20 + 25 + 30) / 5 = 100 / 5 = 20. Absolute deviations from 20: |10-20|=10, |15-20|=5, |20-20|=0, |25-20|=5, |30-20|=10. Sum of deviations = 10 + 5 + 0 + 5 + 10 = 30. MAD = 30 ÷ 5 = 6.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the mean (20) instead of the MAD.\", \"remediation\": \"MAD is the mean of deviations: sum of deviations is 30; 30 ÷ 5 = 6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student found the sum of absolute deviations (30) but forgot to divide by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by the number of data points: 30 ÷ 5 = 6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed the step size 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate deviations: 10, 5, 0, 5, 10; average = 30 / 5 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3194, 6, 5, 13, 'Dot Plots', 334, 6, 'Given the five-number summary for a data set: Minimum = 12, Q1 = 18, Median = 25, Q3 = 34, Maximum = 45. What is the Interquartile Range (IQR)?', NULL, 6, 'High', '9', '33', '16', '7', 'C', 'active', 'Interquartile Range IQR = Q3 - Q1 = 34 - 18 = 16.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted Q3 - Median = 34 - 25 = 9.\", \"remediation\": \"IQR = Q3 - Q1 = 16.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the total range: Max - Min = 45 - 12 = 33.\", \"remediation\": \"IQR is Q3 minus Q1: 34 - 18 = 16 (not full range).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted Median - Q1 = 25 - 18 = 7.\", \"remediation\": \"IQR spans from Q1 to Q3: 34 - 18 = 16.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3195, 6, 5, 13, 'Measures of Center', 331, 6, 'A histogram shows test scores grouped in bins: 50-59 (2 students), 60-69 (4 students), 70-79 (10 students), 80-89 (8 students), 90-99 (6 students). What percentage of the students scored 70 or higher?', NULL, 6, 'High', '24%', '70%', '60%', '80%', 'D', 'active', 'Total students = 2 + 4 + 10 + 8 + 6 = 30 students. Students scoring 70 or higher = 10 + 8 + 6 = 24 students. Percentage = (24 ÷ 30) × 100 = 0.80 × 100 = 80%.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the count of students (24) instead of the percentage.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide by total (30) and multiply by 100: (24/30) × 100 = 80%.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 70% from the threshold score.\", \"remediation\": \"Calculate: 24 / 30 = 80%.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"24 ÷ 30 = 4/5 = 80%.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3196, 6, 5, 13, 'Measures of Center', 331, 6, 'A data set consists of five positive integers. The mode is 6, the median is 7, and the mean is 8. If all numbers are integers and the smallest number is 6, what is the largest number in the data set?', NULL, 6, 'High', '16', '14 (with data set 5, 6, 7, 8, 14? Mean = 40/5 = 8, median = 7)', '12', '10', 'B', 'active', 'For five numbers: 5, 6, 7, 8, and x. Mean is 8 => sum is 40. 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 + x = 40 => 26 + x = 40 => x = 14.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 26 + 16.\", \"remediation\": \"40 - 26 = 14.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 40 - 28 = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Sum of known numbers is 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 = 26. 40 - 26 = 14.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Total sum must be 40: x = 40 - 26 = 14.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3197, 6, 5, 13, 'Dot Plots', 334, 6, 'A data set has values: 2, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 18. What are the first quartile (Q1) and third quartile (Q3)?', NULL, 6, 'High', 'Q1 = 5, Q3 = 14', 'Q1 = 7, Q3 = 12', 'Q1 = 6, Q3 = 13', 'Q1 = 6, Q3 = 14', 'C', 'active', 'Median separates into lower half {2, 5, 7, 8} and upper half {10, 12, 14, 18}. Q1 is median of lower half: (5 + 7)/2 = 6. Q3 is median of upper half: (12 + 14)/2 = 13.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student took the second value (5) and seventh value (14) without averaging middle pairs.\", \"remediation\": \"Lower half has 4 values, so Q1 is the average of the two middle values: (5 + 7)/2 = 6; Q3 is (12 + 14)/2 = 13.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student chose 7 and 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Q1 = (5 + 7)/2 = 6; Q3 = (12 + 14)/2 = 13.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student averaged Q1 correctly but not Q3.\", \"remediation\": \"Q3 = (12 + 14)/2 = 13.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3198, 6, 5, 13, 'Variability', 336, 6, 'If every value in a data set is multiplied by 2, what happens to the mean and the range?', NULL, 6, 'High', 'Both the mean and the range double (multiplied by 2)', 'Both increase by 2', 'The mean doubles, but the range stays the same', 'The mean stays the same, but the range doubles', 'A', 'active', 'Multiplying every value by a constant k multiplies both measures of center (mean, median) and measures of spread (range, IQR, MAD) by k. Both are multiplied by 2.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Values were multiplied by 2, not added by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Both double (multiplied by 2).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student thought range is unaffected by scaling (confused multiplying with adding a constant).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiplying scales all distances: Range = 2(Max) - 2(Min) = 2(Max - Min). Range doubles.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Mean also scales by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Both mean and range double when data is multiplied by 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3199, 6, 5, 13, 'Statistical Questions', 330, 6, 'A dot plot of quiz scores is heavily skewed to the left (a long tail stretching towards low scores, with most scores clustered near 100). How will the mean compare to the median?', NULL, 6, 'High', 'The mean will be GREATER than the median', 'The mean and median will be exactly equal', 'The mean will be LESS than the median', 'The median cannot be determined', 'C', 'active', 'In a left-skewed distribution, the tail of unusually low scores pulls the mean down to the left, making the mean less than the median.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This occurs in right-skewed data (tail to the right).\", \"remediation\": \"Left-skewed data has extreme low values that pull the mean down, so Mean < Median.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Mean and median are only equal in symmetrical distributions.\", \"remediation\": \"Skewness pulls the mean toward the tail: Mean < Median.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Median can always be determined from the ordered data.\", \"remediation\": \"In left-skewed distributions, Mean < Median.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3200, 6, 5, 13, 'Mean Absolute Deviation', 335, 6, 'Two runners record their race times across 5 trials. Runner 1 has Mean = 15.0 s, MAD = 0.2 s. Runner 2 has Mean = 15.0 s, MAD = 1.4 s. Which statement is the most accurate statistical conclusion?', NULL, 6, 'High', 'Both runners have identical race times on every trial', 'Runner 2 is faster overall than Runner 1', 'Runner 1 won more races than Runner 2', 'Runner 1 is much more consistent in performance than Runner 2', 'D', 'active', 'A much lower MAD (0.2 vs 1.4) means Runner 1\'s times stay very close to the 15.0 s average, showing much higher consistency.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"MAD > 0 means there is variation across trials; they are not identical.\", \"remediation\": \"Runner 1 has smaller spread around the mean (greater consistency).\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Both runners have the exact same mean (15.0 s), so neither is faster on average.\", \"remediation\": \"MAD measures consistency/variability, not average speed: lower MAD = higher consistency.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"MAD does not record head-to-head race outcomes.\", \"remediation\": \"Runner 1 is simply more consistent.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:16:44', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3201, 6, 5, 13, 'Probability', 340, 7, 'A fair six-sided number cube is rolled once. What is the theoretical probability of rolling a 4?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '1/4', '1/6', '4/6 (or 2/3)', '1/2', 'B', 'active', 'There is only 1 four on a six-sided die, and 6 possible outcomes. Probability = 1/6.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used the number 4 as the denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator is the total number of sides (6): 1/6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student put the number 4 in the numerator.\", \"remediation\": \"There is only ONE side that has a 4 on it, so numerator is 1: 1/6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the probability of rolling an even number.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the specific number 4: 1/6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:04', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3202, 6, 5, 13, 'Probability Models', 341, 7, 'Which probability value represents an event that is IMPOSSIBLE to occur?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '0', '-1', '0.5', '1', 'A', 'active', 'A probability of 0 indicates an impossible event that can never happen.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Probabilities cannot be negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Probabilities always range from 0 to 1 inclusive.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"0.5 represents an equally likely event (50% chance).\", \"remediation\": \"0 means impossible, 0.5 means equally likely, and 1 means certain.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"1 represents a certain event that is guaranteed to happen.\", \"remediation\": \"0 represents an impossible event.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:04', '2026-09-12 12:13:06');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3203, 6, 5, 13, 'Random Sampling', 337, 7, 'A school principal wants to know the favorite lunch of the entire school. Which group is a REPRESENTATIVE random sample?', NULL, 7, 'Low', 'The 10 students sitting at the first cafeteria table', '50 students selected randomly from an alphabetical list of all students in the school', 'Members of the school cooking club', 'The principal\'s own children', 'B', 'active', 'A representative random sample gives every student in the population an equal chance of being selected, avoiding convenience or group bias.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"This is a convenience sample of students who sit together and may share similar food preferences.\", \"remediation\": \"Randomly selecting from the complete school roster avoids clustering bias.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Cooking club members have specialized food interests that do not represent all students.\", \"remediation\": \"A random sample across the entire student population is needed.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A personal convenience sample of family members is heavily biased.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a random sample of the full student body.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:04', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3204, 6, 5, 13, 'Sample Space', 343, 7, 'A coin is flipped and a spinner with 3 equal colors (Red, Blue, Green) is spun. How many total outcomes are in the sample space?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '8 outcomes', '5 outcomes', '3 outcomes', '6 outcomes', 'D', 'active', 'The coin has 2 outcomes (Heads, Tails) and the spinner has 3 outcomes. Total outcomes = 2 × 3 = 6 outcomes.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 2³ = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply outcomes: 2 × 3 = 6.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 2 + 3 = 5 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"By the Fundamental Counting Principle, multiply the number of outcomes: 2 × 3 = 6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted the spinner colors.\", \"remediation\": \"Combine coin (2) and spinner (3): 2 × 3 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:04', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3205, 6, 5, 13, 'Experimental Probability', 342, 7, 'A bag has colored marbles. In 50 random draws (with replacement), a green marble was drawn 15 times. What is the experimental probability of drawing a green marble?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '15/50 (or 3/10 = 0.30)', '35/50', '15/35', '50/15', 'A', 'active', 'Experimental probability = (Number of times event occurred) ÷ (Total number of trials) = 15 ÷ 50 = 3/10.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the probability of NOT drawing green (35/50).\", \"remediation\": \"Experimental probability of green is the green count divided by total: 15/50.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by the non-green count instead of total trials.\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator must be the total trials (50): 15/50.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student inverted the fraction (50/15 > 1).\", \"remediation\": \"Probability is between 0 and 1: 15/50.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3206, 6, 5, 13, 'Samples & Populations', 338, 7, 'In a random sample of 100 city residents, 20 people ride the bus to work. In a city of 10,000 residents, about how many people would you expect to ride the bus?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '5,000 people', '200 people', '2,000 people', '20 people', 'C', 'active', 'Sample proportion = 20/100 = 20% (0.20). Estimated population = 0.20 × 10,000 = 2,000 people.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed half the population.\", \"remediation\": \"20% of 10,000 is 2,000.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 20 by 10 instead of 100 (or misplaced decimal).\", \"remediation\": \"Set up proportion: 20/100 = x/10,000 => x = 20 × 100 = 2,000.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the sample count directly without scaling to the population.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply sample rate (20%) by total population (10,000) = 2,000.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3207, 6, 5, 13, 'Probability', 340, 7, 'A spinner has 8 equal sections numbered 1 through 8. What is the probability of spinning an ODD number?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '1/8', '3/8', '5/8', '4/8 (or 1/2 = 50%)', 'D', 'active', 'The odd numbers are {1, 3, 5, 7} (4 numbers out of 8). Probability = 4/8 = 1/2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student found the probability of spinning one specific odd number.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 odd numbers in total: 4/8 = 1/2.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted 3 odd numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Count: 1, 3, 5, 7 = 4 odd numbers; 4/8 = 1/2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student counted 5 odd numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Half the numbers 1 to 8 are odd (4 of 8 = 1/2).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3208, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Distributions', 339, 7, 'Two box plots show test scores: Class 1 has a median of 85 and Class 2 has a median of 70. On average, which class scored higher?', NULL, 7, 'Low', 'Cannot be determined', 'Class 1', 'Class 2', 'Both scored the same', 'B', 'active', 'The median represents the center of the distribution; Class 1 has a higher median (85 vs 70).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Box plot medians provide clear comparison of centers.\", \"remediation\": \"Class 1 scored higher on average.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Class 2 has a lower median (70).\", \"remediation\": \"Class 1 has the higher center: 85 > 70.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The medians are 85 and 70, which are distinctly different.\", \"remediation\": \"85 is higher than 70.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3209, 6, 5, 13, 'Compound Probability', 344, 7, 'You flip a fair coin twice. What is the probability of getting Heads on BOTH flips?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '1/2', '2/4 (or 1/2)', '1/4', '3/4', 'C', 'active', 'The events are independent: P(H and H) = P(H) × P(H) = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4. (Outcomes: HH, HT, TH, TT).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the probability of one flip (1/2).\", \"remediation\": \"For two independent flips, multiply the probabilities: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 1/2 + 1/2 or counted 2 heads out of 4 coin sides.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply probabilities for compound independent events: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the probability of getting at least one Head.\", \"remediation\": \"Getting Heads on both flips is only outcome HH (1 out of 4) = 1/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3210, 6, 5, 13, 'Experimental Probability', 342, 7, 'A coin is flipped 200 times and lands on Heads 112 times. What is the experimental probability of landing on Tails?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '88/200 (or 0.44 = 44%)', '88/112', '112/200 (or 0.56 = 56%)', '100/200 (or 0.50 = 50%)', 'A', 'active', 'Number of Tails = 200 - 112 = 88. Experimental probability = 88/200 = 0.44 = 44%.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by Heads count instead of total trials.\", \"remediation\": \"The denominator must be the total number of flips: 88/200.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the probability for Heads (112/200).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for Tails: 200 - 112 = 88 tails; 88/200 = 44%.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the theoretical probability (50%) instead of experimental.\", \"remediation\": \"Experimental probability is based on the actual trial data: 88/200 = 44%.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3211, 6, 5, 13, 'Random Sampling', 337, 7, 'A survey asks 40 people exiting an ice cream parlor what their favorite dessert is, and 36 say ice cream. Why is this sample biased?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', 'The sample size of 40 is too large', 'Ice cream is not a dessert', 'The location (an ice cream parlor) means participants are already predisposed to liking ice cream', 'There was no coin flipped', 'C', 'active', 'Surveying people at an ice cream parlor creates location bias (convenience sampling), as patrons of an ice cream shop naturally favor ice cream.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"40 is a standard sample size, not too large.\", \"remediation\": \"Bias comes from non-random sampling location, where respondents are not representative of the general population.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Ice cream is a dessert.\", \"remediation\": \"The sampling method itself is biased because of the location.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Coin flips are not required for valid random sampling.\", \"remediation\": \"Surveying at an ice cream shop biases responses toward ice cream.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3212, 6, 5, 13, 'Probability Models', 341, 7, 'A jar contains 4 red, 6 blue, and 10 yellow marbles. A marble is picked at random. What is the probability that it is NOT red?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '10/20 (or 1/2)', '4/20 (or 1/5 = 20%)', '6/20 (or 3/10)', '16/20 (or 4/5 = 80%)', 'D', 'active', 'Total marbles = 4 + 6 + 10 = 20. Marbles that are NOT red = 6 + 10 = 16. P(not red) = 16/20 = 4/5 = 80%. (Or: 1 - 4/20 = 16/20).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted the yellow marbles.\", \"remediation\": \"Both blue and yellow are not red: 6 + 10 = 16.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the probability that it IS red.\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for NOT red: 1 - P(red) = 1 - 4/20 = 16/20.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted the blue marbles.\", \"remediation\": \"Include yellow as well: 6 + 10 = 16 non-red marbles; 16/20 = 4/5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3213, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Distributions', 339, 7, 'The heights of two plant groups have the same MAD of 2 cm. Group A has a mean of 18 cm and Group B has a mean of 12 cm. What is the difference between the two means expressed as a multiple of the MAD?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '6 times the MAD', '3 times the MAD', '2 times the MAD', '1.5 times the MAD', 'B', 'active', 'Difference between means = 18 - 12 = 6 cm. Since MAD = 2 cm, the difference is 6 ÷ 2 = 3 times the MAD.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the difference in means (6 cm) without dividing by MAD.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide the difference (6) by the MAD (2): 6 ÷ 2 = 3 times the MAD.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student used the MAD value itself.\", \"remediation\": \"(18 - 12) / 2 = 6 / 2 = 3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 3 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Difference in means is 6; 6 / 2 = 3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3214, 6, 5, 13, 'Compound Probability', 344, 7, 'A spinner has 4 equal sections labeled 1, 2, 3, 4. What is the probability of spinning a number greater than 2 on the first spin, AND an even number on the second spin?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '1/4 (or 25%)', '3/4', '1/2', '1/8', 'A', 'active', 'Numbers > 2: {3, 4} (2/4 = 1/2). Even numbers: {2, 4} (2/4 = 1/2). P(Both) = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added probabilities incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Compound probability multiplies: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 1/2 + 1/2 or gave single spin probability.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply probabilities for independent events: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 1/2 × 1/4.\", \"remediation\": \"Even numbers are 2 and 4 (2 of 4 = 1/2): 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3215, 6, 5, 13, 'Samples & Populations', 338, 7, 'A quality inspector tests a random sample of 250 lightbulbs and finds 5 defective bulbs. If the factory produces 50,000 lightbulbs this week, how many bulbs are expected to be defective?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '500 bulbs', '1,000 bulbs', '250 bulbs', '2,000 bulbs', 'B', 'active', 'Defect rate = 5 / 250 = 1/50 = 0.02 (2%). Expected defective = 0.02 × 50,000 = 1,000 bulbs.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used 1% instead of 2% (500).\", \"remediation\": \"Defect rate is 5/250 = 2%: 0.02 × 50,000 = 1,000 bulbs.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student confused the sample size (250) with the answer.\", \"remediation\": \"Scale the proportion: (5 / 250) × 50,000 = 1,000 bulbs.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student doubled the estimate.\", \"remediation\": \"5/250 × 50,000 = 1,000.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3216, 6, 5, 13, 'Sample Space', 343, 7, 'A restaurant offers a lunch special with 3 choices of sandwich (Turkey, Ham, Veggie), 2 choices of side (Soup, Salad), and 4 choices of drink. How many different lunch combinations are possible?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '14 combinations', '9 combinations', '12 combinations', '24 combinations', 'D', 'active', 'By the Fundamental Counting Principle: 3 × 2 × 4 = 24 combinations.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 2 × 7.\", \"remediation\": \"3 × 2 = 6; 6 × 4 = 24 combinations.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 3 + 2 + 4 = 9 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the number of choices for each item: 3 × 2 × 4 = 24.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only multiplied 3 × 4 = 12.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply all three categories: 3 × 2 × 4 = 24.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3217, 6, 5, 13, 'Probability', 340, 7, 'If the probability of rain tomorrow is 35%, what is the probability that it will NOT rain tomorrow?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '65%', '35%', '50%', '75%', 'A', 'active', 'The sum of probabilities of complementary events is 100%: 100% - 35% = 65%.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the probability that it will rain.\", \"remediation\": \"Complement rule: P(not rain) = 100% - P(rain) = 100% - 35% = 65%.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed 50/50 chance.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract from 100%: 100 - 35 = 65%.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the subtraction.\", \"remediation\": \"100 - 35 = 65%.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3218, 6, 5, 13, 'Compound Probability', 344, 7, 'A drawer contains 5 blue socks and 5 black socks. You pick one sock at random, do NOT replace it, and then pick a second sock. What is the probability that BOTH socks picked are blue?', NULL, 7, 'High', '5/18', '1/4 (or 25/100)', '2/9 (or 20/90)', '1/2', 'C', 'active', 'First pick: P(Blue) = 5/10. Second pick without replacement: 4 blue socks left out of 9 total socks: P(Blue | Blue) = 4/9. P(Both) = (5/10) × (4/9) = (1/2) × (4/9) = 4/18 = 2/9.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student kept 5 in numerator: 5/18.\", \"remediation\": \"After drawing one blue sock, only 4 blue socks remain: 4/18 = 2/9.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed replacement: (5/10) × (5/10) = 1/4.\", \"remediation\": \"Without replacement, the total socks decrease from 10 to 9, and blue socks from 5 to 4: (5/10) × (4/9) = 2/9.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the probability of the first sock only (1/2).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply the conditional probabilities: (5/10) × (4/9) = 2/9.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3219, 6, 5, 13, 'Samples & Populations', 338, 7, 'A wildlife biologist catches, tags, and releases 60 fish in a lake. Later, she catches a random sample of 150 fish and finds that 12 of them have tags. What is the estimated total fish population in the lake?', NULL, 7, 'High', '300 fish', '1,800 fish', '900 fish', '750 fish', 'D', 'active', 'Using mark-recapture proportion: (Tagged in sample) / (Total sample) = (Total tagged initially) / (Total population N). 12 / 150 = 60 / N. Cross-multiplying: 12N = 60 × 150 = 9,000 => N = 9,000 ÷ 12 = 750 fish.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 60 by 5 or miscalculated.\", \"remediation\": \"Set up proportion: 12/150 = 60/N => N = (60 × 150) / 12 = 750 fish.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 9,000 by 5.\", \"remediation\": \"9,000 ÷ 12 = 750.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 60 × 15 = 900.\", \"remediation\": \"Solve 12N = 9,000 => N = 750 fish.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3220, 6, 5, 13, 'Experimental Probability', 342, 7, 'A player rolls a standard die 600 times. Theoretical probability predicts rolling a 6 approximately 100 times. If the player actually rolls a 6 exactly 140 times, what can be concluded?', NULL, 7, 'High', 'The die must have eight sides', 'The experimental probability is 140/600 ≈ 23.3%, which is higher than the theoretical 16.7%, possibly suggesting the die is weighted/unfair if this persists over large trials', 'Theoretical probability is always wrong', 'The next 100 rolls will have zero 6s to even it out', 'B', 'active', 'Experimental probability = 140/600 ≈ 23.3%, whereas theoretical is 1/6 ≈ 16.7%. A significant deviation over a large sample (600 trials) may indicate a biased die.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The prompt specifies a standard die.\", \"remediation\": \"The data suggests an experimental deviation from theoretical probability.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Theoretical probability represents true mathematical expectations for fair trials.\", \"remediation\": \"Theoretical probability is sound; deviations reflect random variation or an unfair die.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This is the Gambler\'s Fallacy; die rolls are independent and previous outcomes do not alter future probabilities.\", \"remediation\": \"Dice have no memory; each roll remains independent.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3221, 6, 5, 13, 'Comparing Distributions', 339, 7, 'Group X and Group Y both have 20 students. Group X has Mean = 75, MAD = 5. Group Y has Mean = 85, MAD = 5. How many MADs separate the two means, and is there significant overlap between the two distributions?', NULL, 7, 'High', '10 MADs separate the means; there is zero overlap', '1 MAD separates the means; distributions are completely identical', '2 MADs separate the means; there is moderate overlap between the distributions', '0 MADs separate them', 'C', 'active', 'Difference in means = 85 - 75 = 10. Since MAD = 5, the separation is 10 ÷ 5 = 2 MADs. A separation of 2 MADs indicates moderate overlap between the score distributions.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used the difference in raw points (10) as the number of MADs.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide difference by MAD: 10 ÷ 5 = 2 MADs.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"10 ÷ 5 = 2 MADs.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The means are not identical (75 vs 85).\", \"remediation\": \"Difference is 10 points = 2 MADs.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3222, 6, 5, 13, 'Compound Probability', 344, 7, 'Two fair six-sided dice are rolled simultaneously. What is the probability that the sum of the two numbers is equal to 7?', NULL, 7, 'High', '6/36 (or 1/6)', '1/12', '1/36', '7/36', 'A', 'active', 'Total outcomes = 6 × 6 = 36. Pairs that sum to 7: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1) = 6 pairs. P(sum = 7) = 6/36 = 1/6.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student simplified 6/36 incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"6/36 simplifies to 1/6.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed only one combination sums to 7.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 6 distinct ordered pairs that sum to 7: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1); 6/36 = 1/6.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student put the sum (7) in the numerator.\", \"remediation\": \"The numerator is the number of successful outcomes (6), not the target sum.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3223, 6, 5, 13, 'Random Sampling', 337, 7, 'A pollster conducts an online poll by posting: \"Click here to vote if you think taxes should be lowered.\" 92% of respondents vote \"Yes\". Why is this survey unreliable for estimating the town\'s opinion?', NULL, 7, 'High', 'Online polls are illegal', '92% is too high a number mathematically', 'It relies on voluntary response bias; people with strong feelings on the issue are far more likely to participate', 'The question was answered by too many people', 'C', 'active', 'Voluntary response samples suffer from strong self-selection bias because respondents choose whether to participate, usually over-representing strong or extreme views.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Online polls are not illegal.\", \"remediation\": \"The statistical flaw is voluntary response bias.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Percentages can legitimately reach 92%.\", \"remediation\": \"The sampling method was not random, invalidating the conclusion.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"High participation does not cause bias; self-selection causes bias.\", \"remediation\": \"Voluntary response creates unrepresentative samples.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3224, 6, 5, 13, 'Compound Probability', 344, 7, 'A bag contains 3 red marbles and 2 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn one after the other WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability of drawing a red marble first AND a blue marble second?', NULL, 7, 'High', '1/2', '6/25 (or 24%)', '5/20 (or 1/4)', '6/20 (or 3/10 = 30%)', 'D', 'active', 'P(Red 1st) = 3/5. Since there is no replacement, 4 marbles remain, with 2 blue. P(Blue 2nd | Red 1st) = 2/4. P(Red then Blue) = (3/5) × (2/4) = 6/20 = 3/10.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed 1/2.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply: (3/5) × (1/2) = 3/10.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student assumed replacement: (3/5) × (2/5) = 6/25.\", \"remediation\": \"Without replacement, the denominator on the second draw becomes 4: (3/5) × (2/4) = 6/20 = 3/10.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added numerators: (3 + 2)/20.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply probabilities: (3/5) × (2/4) = 6/20 = 3/10.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3225, 6, 5, 13, 'Samples & Populations', 338, 7, 'Three different random samples of 50 students were asked if they favor a new school dress code. Sample 1: 18 favored (36%). Sample 2: 22 favored (44%). Sample 3: 20 favored (40%). What is the best point estimate for the percentage of the entire student body that favors the dress code?', NULL, 7, 'High', '36%', '40% (average of the three samples)', '44%', '100%', 'B', 'active', 'Combining multiple random samples or averaging their proportions reduces sampling error: (36% + 44% + 40%) ÷ 3 = 120% ÷ 3 = 40%.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student chose only the lowest sample estimate.\", \"remediation\": \"Averaging the three independent random samples provides a more reliable estimate: (36 + 44 + 40)/3 = 40%.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student chose only the highest sample estimate.\", \"remediation\": \"The mean of the sample estimates (40%) gives the best overall point estimate.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"100% represents all students favoring, which contradicts the sample data.\", \"remediation\": \"Average the samples: 40%.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3226, 6, 5, 13, 'Scatter Plots', 345, 8, 'What type of data is displayed on a scatter plot?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'Bivariate measurement data (two quantitative variables)', 'Daily weather names only', 'Single category data only', 'Words only', 'A', 'active', 'A scatter plot displays bivariate data (pairs of numerical values) to investigate relationships between two variables.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Weather names are categorical.\", \"remediation\": \"Scatter plots display bivariate measurement data.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Single category data is displayed on bar graphs or dot plots.\", \"remediation\": \"Scatter plots show two variables simultaneously (bivariate data).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Scatter plots display quantitative numerical points (x, y).\", \"remediation\": \"Scatter plots are for two numerical variables.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3227, 6, 5, 13, 'positive Assocaition', 346, 8, 'In a scatter plot, as the x-values increase, the y-values also tend to increase. What type of association is this?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'Negative association', 'Positive association', 'No association', 'Non-linear circular association', 'B', 'active', 'When both variables increase together, the relationship has a positive association.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Negative association occurs when y decreases as x increases.\", \"remediation\": \"When both x and y increase together, it is a positive association.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"No association shows randomly scattered points with no upward or downward trend.\", \"remediation\": \"Upward trend = positive association.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"A straight upward slant indicates a positive linear association.\", \"remediation\": \"Positive association.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3228, 6, 5, 13, 'Negative Association', 347, 8, 'A scatter plot compares vehicle age (in years) to its resale value (in dollars). As vehicle age increases, the resale value decreases. What type of association is this?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'Exponential increase', 'Positive association', 'Zero association', 'Negative association', 'D', 'active', 'As one variable increases while the other decreases, the data exhibits a negative association.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The value is decreasing, not increasing.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative association.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Positive association means both increase together.\", \"remediation\": \"As age increases, value decreases: this is a negative association.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"There is a clear downward relationship, not zero association.\", \"remediation\": \"Downward trend = negative association.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3229, 6, 5, 13, 'Cluster', 349, 8, 'What is a \"cluster\" in a scatter plot?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'A distinct group of data points positioned closely together', 'A single point far away from all others', 'The line of best fit', 'The title of the graph', 'A', 'active', 'A cluster is an isolated, dense grouping of data points in a scatter plot.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"A single point far away from others is an outlier, not a cluster.\", \"remediation\": \"A cluster is a group of multiple points packed closely together.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The line of best fit is a model line drawn through data.\", \"remediation\": \"A cluster is a tight group of data points.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Title is text at the top.\", \"remediation\": \"Cluster describes grouping of points.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3230, 6, 5, 13, 'Outliers', 328, 8, 'What is an \"outlier\" in a scatter plot?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'The origin (0, 0)', 'The center of the cluster', 'A point that lies unusually far away from the overall pattern or trend of the data', 'The y-intercept', 'C', 'active', 'An outlier is an individual data point that departs significantly from the overall pattern exhibited by the rest of the data.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The origin is just (0, 0).\", \"remediation\": \"Outliers deviate from the main pattern.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The center of a cluster is where points are densest.\", \"remediation\": \"An outlier is isolated far from the cluster or trend line.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The y-intercept is where the line crosses the vertical axis.\", \"remediation\": \"An outlier is an atypical isolated data point.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3231, 6, 5, 13, 'Linear Models', 350, 8, 'What is the purpose of drawing a \"line of best fit\" (trend line) on a scatter plot?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'To connect every single dot like a dot-to-dot puzzle', 'To measure the area under the points', 'To erase outliers', 'To model the linear trend and make predictions for values within or near the data', 'D', 'active', 'A line of best fit models the linear relationship between variables, allowing estimation and prediction.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A line of best fit is a straight line through the middle of points, not a connect-the-dots line.\", \"remediation\": \"The trend line passes near the points to capture the overall pattern.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Trend lines do not calculate area.\", \"remediation\": \"Trend lines are used to model relationships and make predictions.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Trend lines do not delete data.\", \"remediation\": \"The purpose is modeling the relationship: y = mx + b.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3232, 6, 5, 13, 'Two-Way Tables', 352, 8, 'What kind of data is typically organized and analyzed in a two-way frequency table?', NULL, 8, 'Low', 'Ruler inches only', 'Bivariate categorical data (two categorical variables)', 'Single numerical continuous measurements', 'Geometric shapes only', 'B', 'active', 'A two-way table organizes data involving two categorical variables (e.g., gender and favorite sport).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Ruler measurements are continuous numerical data.\", \"remediation\": \"Two-way tables are for categorical data.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Continuous numerical data is plotted on histograms or scatter plots.\", \"remediation\": \"Two-way tables categorize bivariate categorical data into rows and columns.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Tables display categorical data frequencies, not physical shapes.\", \"remediation\": \"Two-way tables summarize counts for two categorical variables.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3233, 6, 5, 13, 'Initial Value', 351, 8, 'In the linear equation y = 3x + 25 representing total cost y for x gym visits, what is the initial value (y-intercept)?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '3', '28', '25', '0', 'C', 'active', 'In y = mx + b, b is the initial value (y-intercept) when x = 0, which is 25.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"3 is the slope (rate of change per gym visit).\", \"remediation\": \"The constant term 25 is the initial value (membership fee) when x = 0.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 3 + 25 = 28.\", \"remediation\": \"In y = mx + b, b = 25 is the initial value.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"When x = 0, y = 25, not 0.\", \"remediation\": \"The initial value is 25.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3234, 6, 5, 13, 'Linear Models', 350, 8, 'A scatter plot has a line of best fit with equation y = 2x + 10, where x is study time (hours) and y is test score. What test score does the model predict for a student who studies for 6 hours?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '22', '12', '16', '32', 'A', 'active', 'Substitute x = 6 into the linear model: y = 2(6) + 10 = 12 + 10 = 22.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 2 × 6 = 12 and forgot to add 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the y-intercept: 12 + 10 = 22.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 6 + 10 = 16 (forgot to multiply by 2).\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply x by slope first: 2 × 6 = 12, then add 10 = 22.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 2 × (6 + 10) = 32.\", \"remediation\": \"Follow order of operations: 2(6) + 10 = 12 + 10 = 22.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3235, 6, 5, 13, 'Initial Value', 351, 8, 'In the linear model y = -1.5x + 50 modeling water level in a draining pool (where x is hours elapsed), what does the slope -1.5 represent?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'The starting water level was 1.5 units', 'The pool drains in 1.5 hours', 'The water level drops by 1.5 units each hour', 'The water level increases by 1.5 units each hour', 'C', 'active', 'Slope represents the rate of change. A slope of -1.5 means the water level decreases by 1.5 units per hour.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The starting water level is the y-intercept (50 units).\", \"remediation\": \"The slope (-1.5) represents the rate of change per hour.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Draining time is found when y = 0: 50 / 1.5 ≈ 33.3 hours.\", \"remediation\": \"Slope is the rate of water drop per hour (-1.5).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The negative sign indicates a decrease, not an increase.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative slope means decreasing by 1.5 units/hour.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3236, 6, 5, 13, 'Assoication', 348, 8, 'A scatter plot of points curves upward sharply like an exponential curve rather than forming a straight line. How should this association be described?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'Constant linear association', 'Negative linear association', 'No association', 'Non-linear association', 'D', 'active', 'When data points show a clear pattern that bends or curves rather than following a straight line, it is a non-linear association.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"A straight line is linear; a curve is non-linear.\", \"remediation\": \"A curved trend is non-linear.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The points curve upward, not downward in a straight line.\", \"remediation\": \"Curved patterns represent non-linear associations.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"There is a strong clear pattern, so association exists (it is non-linear).\", \"remediation\": \"It is a non-linear association.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3237, 6, 5, 13, 'Two-Way Tables', 352, 8, 'A survey of 100 students records pet ownership and sports participation: 40 play sports and own a pet, 20 play sports and do not own a pet, 30 do not play sports and own a pet, and 10 do neither. How many total students own a pet?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '60 students', '70 students', '40 students', '50 students', 'B', 'active', 'Add the two pet-owning groups: 40 (sports + pet) + 30 (no sports + pet) = 70 students.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added those who play sports (40 + 20 = 60).\", \"remediation\": \"Add all students in the \\\"own a pet\\\" row/column: 40 + 30 = 70 students.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only counted those who play sports and own a pet.\", \"remediation\": \"Include pet owners who do not play sports: 40 + 30 = 70.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed half.\", \"remediation\": \"40 + 30 = 70 pet owners.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3238, 6, 5, 13, 'Scatter Plots', 345, 8, 'Which pair of variables is most likely to show NO association on a scatter plot?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'Shoe size and scores on a history exam', 'Outside temperature and heating bill cost', 'Hours spent studying and exam score', 'Car speed and travel time for a fixed distance', 'A', 'active', 'There is no plausible relationship between a person\'s shoe size and their score on a history test.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Temperature and heating bills have a strong negative association.\", \"remediation\": \"Shoe size has no link to test score.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Studying and exam scores typically have a positive association.\", \"remediation\": \"Shoe size and history scores have no causal or statistical relationship (no association).\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Speed and travel time have a strong negative association.\", \"remediation\": \"Shoe size and history test scores show no association.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3239, 6, 5, 13, 'Two-Way Tables', 352, 8, 'In a two-way table, 60 students were surveyed: 36 prefer dogs and 24 prefer cats. Of the 36 dog lovers, 27 are in 8th grade. What fraction of the dog lovers are in 8th grade?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '27/60 (or 45%)', '27/36 (or 3/4 = 75%)', '36/60 (or 60%)', '9/36 (or 25%)', 'B', 'active', 'The question asks for the conditional relative frequency among dog lovers: 27 out of 36 = 27/36 = 3/4 = 75%.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used the grand total (60) instead of the dog lovers total (36).\", \"remediation\": \"The condition is \\\"of the dog lovers\\\" (total 36): 27/36 = 75%.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the fraction of all students who love dogs.\", \"remediation\": \"The question specifies among dog lovers: 27/36 = 75%.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated the fraction NOT in 8th grade (9/36).\", \"remediation\": \"27 of 36 are in 8th grade: 27/36 = 75%.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3240, 6, 5, 13, 'Linear Models', 350, 8, 'Which line of best fit would be considered a \"good fit\" for a scatter plot?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'A line that only touches one outlier', 'A line that connects the first and last point regardless of other points', 'A line that passes below all data points', 'A line that follows the trend of the data with roughly equal numbers of points scattered closely above and below it', 'D', 'active', 'A good line of best fit captures the overall direction with points evenly balanced above and below and minimal vertical distances.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Outliers should not dictate the trend line.\", \"remediation\": \"The line must reflect the overall cluster of data.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"First and last points might be outliers and not represent the trend.\", \"remediation\": \"A good fit balances all points, not just the endpoints.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"A line below all points underestimates the data.\", \"remediation\": \"The line should pass through the middle of the scatter.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3241, 6, 5, 13, 'Interpreting Data', 308, 8, 'A scatter plot displays battery percentage remaining (y) versus hours of phone use (x). The trend line is y = -12x + 100. What does the y-intercept 100 represent?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'The phone starts with 100% battery at 0 hours of use', 'The phone battery lasts 100 hours', 'The battery drains by 100% every hour', 'The phone takes 100 hours to charge', 'A', 'active', 'The y-intercept occurs when x = 0 (before use begins). At x = 0, y = 100%, which is the fully charged battery level.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Battery lasts until y = 0: 100 / 12 ≈ 8.3 hours, not 100 hours.\", \"remediation\": \"100 is the starting battery percentage when time x = 0.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The battery drains by 12% per hour (the slope).\", \"remediation\": \"Slope is rate of drain (-12%/hr); 100% is initial charge.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Charging time is not modeled by this equation.\", \"remediation\": \"100 is the initial battery level.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3242, 6, 5, 13, 'positive Assocaition', 346, 8, 'A researcher plots height in inches (x) versus arm span in inches (y) for 50 people and observes a strong positive linear association. What does this mean?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', 'Every person has an arm span of 50 inches', 'Taller people always have shorter arm spans', 'Taller people generally tend to have longer arm spans', 'Height and arm span are completely unrelated', 'C', 'active', 'A positive association means as height increases, arm span also increases.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"50 was the number of people sampled.\", \"remediation\": \"Taller height corresponds to longer arm span.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"This would describe a negative association.\", \"remediation\": \"Positive association means both variables increase together.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"This would describe no association.\", \"remediation\": \"Strong positive association means taller height correlates with longer arm span.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3243, 6, 5, 13, 'Linear Models', 350, 8, 'A line of best fit for candle height (cm) over time (hours) has equation y = -2.5x + 30. How many hours will it take for the candle to burn down completely (height = 0 cm)?', NULL, 8, 'High', '30 hours', '10 hours', '75 hours', '12 hours', 'D', 'active', 'Set y = 0: 0 = -2.5x + 30 => 2.5x = 30 => x = 30 ÷ 2.5 = 12 hours.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the initial candle height (30 cm).\", \"remediation\": \"Set height y = 0 and solve for x: 2.5x = 30 => x = 12 hours.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 30 by 3.\", \"remediation\": \"30 ÷ 2.5 = 12 hours.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 30 × 2.5 = 75.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide total height by burn rate: 30 / 2.5 = 12 hours.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3244, 6, 5, 13, 'Two-Way Tables', 352, 8, 'In a survey of 200 adults about movie preferences, 120 are under 40 years old and 80 are 40 or older. Of those under 40, 90 prefer Action movies. Of those 40 or older, 30 prefer Action movies. Which group has a higher conditional relative frequency of preferring Action movies?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Cannot be determined without comedy data', 'Those under 40 (75% vs 37.5%)', 'Those 40 or older (60% vs 45%)', 'Both groups are equal', 'B', 'active', 'Under 40: 90 ÷ 120 = 0.75 = 75%. 40 or older: 30 ÷ 80 = 0.375 = 37.5%. The under 40 group has a significantly higher preference (75% vs 37.5%).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The data provides complete information for Action movie preferences in both age groups.\", \"remediation\": \"Under 40: 75% vs 37.5%.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the percentages.\", \"remediation\": \"90/120 = 75% while 30/80 = 37.5%; those under 40 have a much higher rate.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"75% is double 37.5%; they are not equal.\", \"remediation\": \"Under 40 is 75%, older group is 37.5%.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3245, 6, 5, 13, 'Linear Models', 350, 8, 'A trend line on a scatter plot passes through (2, 50) and (6, 110), where x is weeks of training and y is weight lifted (lbs). What is the equation of the line of best fit?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'y = 15x + 50', 'y = 10x + 30', 'y = 15x + 20', 'y = 20x + 10', 'C', 'active', 'Slope m = (110 - 50) / (6 - 2) = 60 / 4 = 15. Using point (2, 50): 50 = 15(2) + b => 50 = 30 + b => b = 20. Equation: y = 15x + 20.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student used the y-value of the first point (50) as the y-intercept without solving for b.\", \"remediation\": \"Solve for b: y = mx + b => 50 = 15(2) + b => b = 20; y = 15x + 20.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated slope as 60/6 = 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Change in x is 6 - 2 = 4: slope = 60/4 = 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student guessed y = 20x + 10.\", \"remediation\": \"Slope is 15 and y-intercept is 20: y = 15x + 20.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3246, 6, 5, 13, 'Cluster', 349, 8, 'A scatter plot of engine size vs fuel efficiency shows two distinct clusters: one at small engine sizes with high MPG (around 35 MPG), and another at large engine sizes with low MPG (around 18 MPG). What does this clustering suggest?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Vehicles naturally group into distinct classes (e.g., compact economy cars vs large heavy trucks/SUVs)', 'All vehicles have identical gas mileage', 'The data has errors and should be deleted', 'Engine size has zero relationship with fuel efficiency', 'A', 'active', 'Clustering in real-world data often reflects distinct sub-populations or categories (such as compact cars vs large trucks).', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The two clusters have very different MPG values (35 vs 18).\", \"remediation\": \"It suggests two distinct vehicle classes.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Clusters are meaningful statistical patterns, not errors.\", \"remediation\": \"Clusters indicate sub-groups within the population.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"There is a clear negative relationship across the clusters.\", \"remediation\": \"Clustering reveals distinct categories in the data.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3247, 6, 5, 13, 'Outliers', 328, 8, 'A scatter plot shows hours studied vs exam score with a strong positive trend, but one student who studied for 10 hours received a score of 20%. How does this point affect the slope of the line of best fit?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'It pulls the line upward, increasing the slope', 'It has no effect on the line of best fit', 'It pulls the right end of the line downward, decreasing the slope', 'It makes the slope exactly zero', 'C', 'active', 'An outlier with high x and unusually low y exerts leverage on the regression line, pulling that end down and reducing the slope.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Because the score (20%) is far below the trend, it pulls downward, not upward.\", \"remediation\": \"A low y-value at a high x-value pulls the trend line down, decreasing slope.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Linear regression minimizes squared errors and is sensitive to outliers.\", \"remediation\": \"Extreme points exert leverage on the line of best fit.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"One outlier reduces the slope, but does not completely flatten a strong dataset.\", \"remediation\": \"It pulls the slope down.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3248, 6, 5, 13, 'Two-Way Tables', 352, 8, 'A two-way table shows whether 100 students play an instrument and play a sport: Instrument: 30 play sport, 20 do not. No Instrument: 30 play sport, 20 do not. Is there evidence of an association between playing an instrument and playing a sport?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Cannot be determined without a scatter plot', 'Yes, because 30 is greater than 20', 'Yes, because 100 students were surveyed', 'No, because exactly 60% (30/50) of both instrument players and non-players play a sport, showing independence', 'D', 'active', 'Instrument group: 30/50 = 60% play a sport. No-instrument group: 30/50 = 60% play a sport. Since the conditional relative frequencies are identical, there is no association (the variables are independent).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Two-way tables evaluate association for categorical data.\", \"remediation\": \"Equal conditional percentages indicate no association.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Comparing raw cell counts inside a row does not determine association across groups.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare conditional relative frequencies: both are 60%, indicating independence (no association).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Sample size does not imply an association.\", \"remediation\": \"When proportions are identical between groups, no association exists.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:06'),
(3249, 6, 5, 13, 'Linear Models', 350, 8, 'A linear model for plant growth is y = 0.8x + 4.2, where x is weeks and y is height in cm. If a plant measured 15.0 cm at week 12, what is the residual (actual value minus predicted value)?', NULL, 8, 'High', '-1.2 cm', '1.2 cm', '13.8 cm', '0.8 cm', 'B', 'active', 'Predicted height = 0.8(12) + 4.2 = 9.6 + 4.2 = 13.8 cm. Residual = Actual - Predicted = 15.0 - 13.8 = 1.2 cm.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated Predicted - Actual (13.8 - 15.0 = -1.2).\", \"remediation\": \"Residual is defined as Actual minus Predicted: 15.0 - 13.8 = +1.2 cm.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the predicted value (13.8 cm) instead of the residual.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract predicted from actual: 15.0 - 13.8 = 1.2 cm.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student stated the slope (0.8).\", \"remediation\": \"Residual = 15.0 - 13.8 = 1.2 cm.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3250, 6, 5, 13, 'Assoication', 348, 8, 'A company finds that employee productivity increases as room temperature rises from 60°F to 72°F, but as temperature rises from 72°F to 90°F, productivity drops sharply. What type of association exists between temperature and productivity?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Non-linear association (inverted U-shaped curve)', 'No association', 'Positive linear association', 'Negative linear association', 'A', 'active', 'The relationship first rises and then falls, forming an inverted U-shape. This is a classic non-linear association that cannot be modeled by a single straight line.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"There is a strong predictable relationship, so an association clearly exists.\", \"remediation\": \"It is a non-linear (quadratic/curvilinear) association.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"It only increases up to 72°F; beyond that it decreases.\", \"remediation\": \"Because the direction changes, it is non-linear, not positive linear.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"It does not consistently decrease across the whole range.\", \"remediation\": \"A relationship that rises then falls is non-linear.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:18:05', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3251, 6, 3, 11, 'Understand Place Value - Tens & Ones', 357, 1, 'In the number 47, how many tens and how many ones are there?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '7 tens and 4 ones', '4 tens and 7 ones', '47 tens and 0 ones', '40 tens and 7 ones', 'B', 'active', 'The digit 4 is in the tens place (4 tens = 40), and 7 is in the ones place (7 ones).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student reversed the tens and ones digits.\", \"remediation\": \"The first digit in a 2-digit number is the tens place, and the second is ones: 4 tens and 7 ones.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student treated the entire number as tens.\", \"remediation\": \"47 means 4 bundles of ten and 7 extra ones.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student wrote 40 tens (which would be 400).\", \"remediation\": \"There are 4 tens, not 40 tens.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3252, 6, 3, 11, 'Extend Counting Sequence to 120', 356, 1, 'What number comes immediately after 69 when counting by ones?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '68', '60', '79', '70', 'D', 'active', 'Counting forward by ones: 68, 69, 70.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the number before 69 (68).\", \"remediation\": \"The question asks for the number after 69: 70.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student went back to the beginning of the decade.\", \"remediation\": \"After 69, you enter the next ten: 70.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added ten instead of one (69 + 10 = 79).\", \"remediation\": \"Counting by ones: 69 + 1 = 70.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07');
INSERT INTO `questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic_id`, `chapter_id`, `subtopic_name`, `subtopic_id`, `grade_id`, `question_text`, `image_url`, `grade`, `difficulty`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `correct_answer`, `status`, `explanation`, `distractor_diagnostics`, `created_by`, `is_active`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3253, 6, 3, 11, 'Mental Math - 10 More & 10 Less', 359, 1, 'What is 10 more than 35?', NULL, 1, 'Low', '45', '36', '25', '55', 'A', 'active', '10 more increases the tens digit by 1: 3 tens become 4 tens, so 35 + 10 = 45.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 1 instead of 10 (35 + 1 = 36).\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"10 more\\\" changes the tens digit: 3 tens + 1 ten = 4 tens (45).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 10 (10 less) instead of adding.\", \"remediation\": \"\\\"10 more\\\" means add: 35 + 10 = 45.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 20 instead of 10.\", \"remediation\": \"35 + 10 = 45.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3254, 6, 3, 11, 'Compare Two-Digit Numbers', 358, 1, 'Which symbol makes the statement true: 58 ___ 62?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '+', '>', '<', '=', 'C', 'active', 'Comparing tens: 5 tens (50) is less than 6 tens (60), so 58 < 62.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"+ is an addition operator, not a comparison symbol.\", \"remediation\": \"Use < (less than) to compare.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student confused the greater-than and less-than symbols.\", \"remediation\": \"The open mouth of the symbol faces the larger number: 58 < 62.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student thought the numbers are equal.\", \"remediation\": \"58 and 62 are different; 58 is smaller.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3255, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value Addition & Subtraction', 360, 1, 'What is 50 + 30?', NULL, 1, 'Medium', '70', '90', '53', '80', 'D', 'active', '5 tens + 3 tens = 8 tens = 80.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student made an addition error: 5 + 3 = 8, not 7.\", \"remediation\": \"Think 5 tens + 3 tens = 8 tens = 80.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added 5 + 4.\", \"remediation\": \"50 + 30 = 80.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 3 to 50 instead of 30.\", \"remediation\": \"30 is 3 tens: 50 + 30 = 80.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3256, 6, 3, 11, 'Unknown Addends & Equations', 361, 1, 'Find the missing number: 45 + ___ = 85.', NULL, 1, 'High', '130', '40', '30', '50', 'B', 'active', 'Subtract: 85 - 45 = 40. Checking: 45 + 40 = 85.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 85 + 45 = 130 instead of finding the missing addend.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 85 - 45 = 40.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted incorrectly (85 - 45 = 40, not 30).\", \"remediation\": \"8 tens - 4 tens = 4 tens; 5 ones - 5 ones = 0 ones: 40.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 50.\", \"remediation\": \"45 + 50 = 95; 85 - 45 = 40.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3257, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value', 150, 2, 'What is the value of the digit 6 in the number 642?', NULL, 2, 'Low', '60', '6', '600', '640', 'C', 'active', 'The 6 is in the hundreds place, so its value is 6 × 100 = 600.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student thought 6 is in the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"In 642, the order from right to left is ones, tens, hundreds. 6 is in the hundreds place = 600.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the face value of the digit.\", \"remediation\": \"The value depends on its place: hundreds place = 600.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student included the tens digit.\", \"remediation\": \"The value of the 6 digit alone is 600.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3258, 6, 3, 11, 'Equal Groups & Rectangular Arrays', 156, 2, 'An array of stickers has 3 rows with 5 stickers in each row. Which addition sentence shows the total number of stickers?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '5 + 5 + 5 = 15', '5 + 3 + 5 = 13', '3 + 5 = 8', '3 + 3 + 3 = 9', 'A', 'active', '3 rows of 5 means adding 5 three times: 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student wrote an inconsistent sum.\", \"remediation\": \"Equal groups: 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added rows and columns (3 + 5 = 8).\", \"remediation\": \"Each row has 5 items; add 5 three times: 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 3 only three times.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 3 rows of 5 (or 5 columns of 3: 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 15).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3259, 6, 3, 11, 'Addition Using Place Value', 151, 2, 'What is 348 + 225?', NULL, 2, 'Medium', '563', '572', '573', '673', 'C', 'active', 'Add ones: 8 + 5 = 13 (write 3, carry 1). Add tens: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7. Add hundreds: 3 + 2 = 5. Total = 573.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to add the regrouped 1 to the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"8 + 5 = 13, so carry 1 ten: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7 tens: 573.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student made an error adding ones (8 + 5 = 13, not 12).\", \"remediation\": \"8 + 5 = 13; ones digit is 3: 573.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student carried 1 to hundreds place incorrectly.\", \"remediation\": \"Tens place: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7 (no carry to hundreds): 3 + 2 = 5: 573.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3260, 6, 3, 11, 'Mental Addition & Subtraction', 152, 2, 'What is 600 - 247?', NULL, 2, 'High', '343', '453', '363', '353', 'D', 'active', 'Regroup across zeros: 600 becomes 5 hundreds, 9 tens, 10 ones. 10 - 7 = 3 ones; 9 - 4 = 5 tens; 5 - 2 = 3 hundreds: 353.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 4 - 0 = 4.\", \"remediation\": \"Regroup properly across zeros: 353.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 6 - 2 = 4 without regrouping from the hundreds place.\", \"remediation\": \"When regrouping across zeros, 6 hundreds becomes 5 hundreds: 5 - 2 = 3: 353.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student made an error in the tens place.\", \"remediation\": \"9 tens - 4 tens = 5 tens: 353.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3261, 6, 3, 11, 'Equal Groups & Rectangular Arrays', 156, 2, 'Is the number 17 even or odd, and why?', NULL, 2, 'High', 'Even, because it ends in 7', 'Odd, because when paired up into groups of 2, there is 1 left over', 'Even, because it has two digits', 'Odd, because it is greater than 10', 'B', 'active', 'An even number divides into pairs with no remainder. 17 ÷ 2 = 8 pairs with 1 left over, making it odd.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Numbers ending in 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 are odd, not even.\", \"remediation\": \"Even numbers end in 0, 2, 4, 6, 8. Numbers ending in 7 are odd.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Number of digits does not determine even or odd.\", \"remediation\": \"Check the ones digit: 7 is odd.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Being greater than 10 does not make a number odd.\", \"remediation\": \"Odd numbers have 1 remainder when divided by 2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3262, 6, 3, 11, 'Multiplication Word Problems', 354, 3, 'A baker puts 6 cookies in each box. How many cookies are in 4 boxes?', NULL, 3, 'Low', '24 cookies', '28 cookies', '10 cookies', '20 cookies', 'A', 'active', 'Multiply the number of boxes by cookies per box: 4 × 6 = 24 cookies.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 4 × 7 = 28.\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 6 = 24.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 4 + 6 = 10 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Equal groups require multiplication: 4 boxes × 6 cookies = 24.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 4 × 5 = 20.\", \"remediation\": \"4 × 6 = 24 cookies.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3263, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions', 159, 3, 'Which fraction is equivalent to 2/4?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '1/4', '1/2', '3/4', '2/8', 'B', 'active', 'Divide numerator and denominator by 2: (2÷2)/(4÷2) = 1/2.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student halved the numerator but kept denominator.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide both numerator and denominator by 2: 2/4 = 1/2.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"3/4 is greater than 2/4.\", \"remediation\": \"2/4 simplifies to 1/2.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"2/8 equals 1/4, which is smaller than 2/4.\", \"remediation\": \"2/4 = 1/2 (or 4/8).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3264, 6, 3, 11, 'Multiplication, Division & Fractions', 157, 3, 'What is the unknown number that makes the equation true: 7 × ___ = 56?', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '6', '7', '9', '8', 'D', 'active', 'Divide: 56 ÷ 7 = 8. Checking: 7 × 8 = 56.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"7 × 6 = 42.\", \"remediation\": \"7 × 8 = 56.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"7 × 7 = 49, not 56.\", \"remediation\": \"Recall multiplication facts: 7 × 8 = 56.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"7 × 9 = 63.\", \"remediation\": \"56 ÷ 7 = 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3265, 6, 3, 11, 'Basic Arithmetic & Problem Solving', 160, 3, 'Emma bought 3 packs of pens with 8 pens in each pack. She shared them equally among herself and 3 friends (4 people total). How many pens did each person get?', NULL, 3, 'High', '6 pens', '8 pens', '4 pens', '7 pens', 'A', 'active', 'Total pens = 3 × 8 = 24 pens. Divided among 4 people: 24 ÷ 4 = 6 pens each.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided by 3 friends and ignored Emma: 24 ÷ 3 = 8.\", \"remediation\": \"Include Emma: 1 + 3 = 4 people total. 24 ÷ 4 = 6 pens each.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 24 by 6.\", \"remediation\": \"24 pens ÷ 4 people = 6 pens each.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made a division error.\", \"remediation\": \"24 ÷ 4 = 6.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3266, 6, 3, 11, 'Fractions', 159, 3, 'Which comparison statement between two fractions with the same numerator is correct?', NULL, 3, 'High', '3/4 < 3/8', '3/8 > 3/4 because 8 is bigger than 4', '3/4 > 3/8 because fourths are larger parts than eighths', '3/4 = 3/8 because numerators are equal', 'C', 'active', 'When numerators are equal, the fraction with the smaller denominator has larger parts: fourths are twice as large as eighths, so 3/4 > 3/8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"3/4 is 6/8, which is greater than 3/8.\", \"remediation\": \"3/4 > 3/8.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student thought larger denominator means larger fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"A larger denominator means the whole is divided into more, smaller pieces: 1/8 is smaller than 1/4, so 3/4 > 3/8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student ignored denominators.\", \"remediation\": \"Equal numerators does not mean equal fractions if denominators differ: 3/4 > 3/8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3267, 6, 3, 11, 'Place Value System', 161, 4, 'In the number 35,500, how does the value of the 5 in the thousands place compare to the value of the 5 in the hundreds place?', NULL, 4, 'Low', 'It is 100 times greater', 'They have the same value', 'It is 2 times greater', 'It is 10 times greater', 'D', 'active', 'The 5 in thousands place = 5,000. The 5 in hundreds place = 500. 5,000 ÷ 500 = 10 times greater.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student thought two adjacent places differ by 100.\", \"remediation\": \"Each place value to the left is 10 times the value of the place to its right: 5,000 is 10 × 500.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"The digits are the same, but their place values differ.\", \"remediation\": \"5,000 is 10 times 500.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Place values scale by powers of 10, not 2.\", \"remediation\": \"It is 10 times greater.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3268, 6, 3, 11, 'Factors & Multiples', 168, 4, 'Which number is a common factor of both 24 and 36?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '72', '12', '16', '8', 'B', 'active', '24 ÷ 12 = 2 and 36 ÷ 12 = 3. 12 divides evenly into both numbers.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"72 is a common multiple, not a factor.\", \"remediation\": \"Factors are less than or equal to the numbers: 12 is a common factor.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"16 is not a factor of 24 or 36.\", \"remediation\": \"A factor must divide evenly into both numbers: 36 is not divisible by 16.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"8 is a factor of 24, but 36 is not divisible by 8.\", \"remediation\": \"A common factor must divide both numbers: 36 ÷ 8 is not a whole number.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3269, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Addition & Subtraction', 163, 4, 'What is 2 3/5 + 1 4/5?', NULL, 4, 'Medium', '3 7/5', '3 2/5', '4 2/5', '4 1/5', 'C', 'active', 'Add wholes: 2 + 1 = 3. Add fractions: 3/5 + 4/5 = 7/5 = 1 2/5. Combine: 3 + 1 2/5 = 4 2/5.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student left the improper fraction 7/5 without converting to a mixed number.\", \"remediation\": \"7/5 = 1 2/5; add the 1 to the whole number 3: 3 + 1 2/5 = 4 2/5.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student converted 7/5 to 2/5 but forgot to add 1 to the whole numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"7/5 = 1 and 2/5; 3 + 1 = 4: 4 2/5.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made an arithmetic error in the fraction sum.\", \"remediation\": \"3/5 + 4/5 = 7/5 = 1 2/5; 3 + 1 2/5 = 4 2/5.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3270, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit Computation', 167, 4, 'A theater has 28 rows of seats with 35 seats in each row. How many seats are in the theater?', NULL, 4, 'High', '980 seats', '950 seats', '840 seats', '1,020 seats', 'A', 'active', '28 × 35 = 28 × (30 + 5) = 840 + 140 = 980 seats.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the partial products.\", \"remediation\": \"28 × 35 = 980 seats.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 28 × 30 = 840 and forgot 28 × 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Remember to multiply by both tens and ones: 840 + 140 = 980 seats.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student made a multiplication error with regrouping.\", \"remediation\": \"28 × 35 = 980.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3271, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Multiplication & Division', 165, 4, 'A recipe requires 3/4 cup of sugar. If you make 6 batches of the recipe, how many cups of sugar do you need in total?', NULL, 4, 'High', '4 cups (or 16/4 cups)', '2 1/4 cups', '4 1/2 cups (or 18/4 cups)', '6 3/4 cups', 'C', 'active', '6 × (3/4) = 18/4 = 4 2/4 = 4 1/2 cups.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded down to 4 cups.\", \"remediation\": \"18/4 = 4 with remainder 2/4 = 4 1/2 cups.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 6 by 3/4 instead of multiplying.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply batches by amount per batch: 6 × (3/4) = 18/4 = 4 1/2 cups.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added 6 + 3/4.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 6 × (3/4) = 18/4 = 4 1/2 cups.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3272, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimals', 173, 5, 'What is 3.45 + 2.8?', NULL, 5, 'Low', '6.53', '5.53', '5.25', '6.25', 'D', 'active', 'Line up decimal points: 3.45 + 2.80 = 6.25.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student misaligned the decimal column.\", \"remediation\": \"3.45 + 2.80 = 6.25.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student aligned digits to the right (added 3.45 + 0.28 = 3.73 or 2.8 as 0.08).\", \"remediation\": \"Always align decimal points: 3.45 + 2.80 = 6.25.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to carry 1 from the tenths place (4 + 8 = 12).\", \"remediation\": \"4 tenths + 8 tenths = 12 tenths (write 2, carry 1 to whole numbers): 6.25.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3273, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Addition & Subtraction', 163, 5, 'What is 2/3 + 3/5?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '5/8', '19/15 (or 1 4/15)', '5/15 (or 1/3)', '1 1/15', 'B', 'active', 'Common denominator is 15. 2/3 = 10/15, 3/5 = 9/15. Sum = 10/15 + 9/15 = 19/15 = 1 4/15.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added numerators and denominators directly: (2+3)/(3+5) = 5/8.\", \"remediation\": \"Never add denominators directly. Find a common denominator first: 10/15 + 9/15 = 19/15.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted instead of adding.\", \"remediation\": \"Add the converted fractions: 10/15 + 9/15 = 19/15.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the numerator sum.\", \"remediation\": \"10 + 9 = 19; 19/15 = 1 4/15.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3274, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit & Decimal Operations', 162, 5, 'What is 4.2 × 0.3?', NULL, 5, 'Medium', '1.26', '1.2', '12.6', '0.126', 'A', 'active', '42 × 3 = 126. Since there is 1 decimal place in 4.2 and 1 in 0.3 (total 2 decimal places), the answer is 1.26.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student rounded or dropped the hundredths digit.\", \"remediation\": \"4.2 × 0.3 = 1.26.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student placed only 1 decimal place instead of 2: 12.6.\", \"remediation\": \"Count total decimal places in both factors: 1 + 1 = 2 decimal places: 1.26.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student placed 3 decimal places.\", \"remediation\": \"There are 2 decimal places total: 1.26.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3275, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Division Word Problems', 355, 5, 'A runner has 4 liters of sports drink. If each cup holds 1/3 liter, how many full cups can be poured?', NULL, 5, 'High', '1 1/3 cups', '12 cups', '7 cups', '4/3 cups', 'B', 'active', 'Divide total liters by cup capacity: 4 ÷ (1/3) = 4 × 3 = 12 cups.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 4 × (1/3) = 4/3 = 1 1/3 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"Dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal: 4 ÷ (1/3) = 4 × 3 = 12.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student added 4 + 3 = 7.\", \"remediation\": \"4 ÷ (1/3) = 4 × 3 = 12 cups.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student gave 4/3.\", \"remediation\": \"Dividing by 1/3 yields 4 × 3 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3276, 6, 3, 11, 'Multi-Digit & Decimal Operations', 162, 5, 'What is 7.5 ÷ 0.25?', NULL, 5, 'High', '300', '3', '1.875', '30', 'D', 'active', 'Multiply both by 100: 750 ÷ 25 = 30.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student shifted decimal point too many times.\", \"remediation\": \"7.5 / 0.25 = 750 / 25 = 30.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student misplaced the decimal point by 1 place: 3.\", \"remediation\": \"Shift decimals 2 places: 750 ÷ 25 = 30.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied 7.5 × 0.25 = 1.875 instead of dividing.\", \"remediation\": \"The operation is division: 7.5 ÷ 0.25 = 30.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3277, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 6, 'What is the absolute value of -14, written as |-14|?', NULL, 6, 'Low', '14', '-14', '0', '1/14', 'A', 'active', 'Absolute value represents the distance of a number from zero on the number line, which is always non-negative: |-14| = 14.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student kept the negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Absolute value is distance, which is always non-negative: |-14| = 14.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Absolute value is zero only for 0.\", \"remediation\": \"|-14| = 14.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student took the reciprocal instead of absolute value.\", \"remediation\": \"Absolute value removes the negative sign: 14.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3278, 6, 3, 11, 'Percentages', 174, 6, 'What is 35% of 80?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '32', '35', '28', '24', 'C', 'active', '35% = 0.35. 0.35 × 80 = 28. (Or: 10% is 8, 30% is 24, 5% is 4: 24 + 4 = 28).', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 40% of 80.\", \"remediation\": \"35% of 80 is 28.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student gave the percentage value itself.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply 80 by 0.35: 0.35 × 80 = 28.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 30% only (24) and forgot the 5%.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 5% (4) to 30% (24): 24 + 4 = 28.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3279, 6, 3, 11, 'Fraction Division', 166, 6, 'What is 3/4 ÷ 2/5?', NULL, 6, 'Medium', '6/20 (or 3/10)', '5/8', '8/15', '15/8 (or 1 7/8)', 'D', 'active', 'Multiply by the reciprocal of the second fraction: 3/4 × 5/2 = (3×5)/(4×2) = 15/8 = 1 7/8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied the fractions directly without inverting the divisor: (3×2)/(4×5) = 6/20.\", \"remediation\": \"When dividing fractions, invert the second fraction and multiply: 3/4 × 5/2 = 15/8.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student inverted the wrong fraction.\", \"remediation\": \"Invert ONLY the divisor (second fraction): 3/4 × 5/2 = 15/8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student inverted the entire product.\", \"remediation\": \"3/4 × 5/2 = 15/8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3280, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 169, 6, 'Which list of rational numbers is in order from LEAST to GREATEST?', NULL, 6, 'High', '-3.5, -2, 1/2, 0, 2.7', '-3.5, -2, 0, 1/2, 2.7', '0, -2, -3.5, 1/2, 2.7', '-2, -3.5, 0, 1/2, 2.7', 'B', 'active', 'On a number line, values further left are smaller: -3.5 is the smallest, followed by -2, then 0, then 0.5 (1/2), and 2.7 is the largest.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student placed 1/2 before 0.\", \"remediation\": \"1/2 (0.5) is greater than 0.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student placed 0 before negative numbers.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative numbers are less than 0: -3.5 < -2 < 0.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student thought -2 is less than -3.5.\", \"remediation\": \"-3.5 is further left on the number line than -2, so -3.5 < -2.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3281, 6, 3, 11, 'Percentages', 174, 6, 'A jacket originally priced at $80 is on sale for 25% off. If sales tax is 5% on the discounted price, what is the final cost of the jacket?', NULL, 6, 'High', '$60.00', '$64.00', '$63.00', '$68.00', 'C', 'active', 'Discount = 25% of $80 = $20. Discounted price = $80 - $20 = $60. Tax = 5% of $60 = $3. Final cost = $60 + $3 = $63.00.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to add sales tax.\", \"remediation\": \"Add 5% tax on $60: $60 × 0.05 = $3; $60 + $3 = $63.00.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student calculated 5% tax on original $80 ($4) and added to $60.\", \"remediation\": \"Tax applies to the discounted price $60: 5% of $60 is $3: $63.00.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student miscalculated the discount.\", \"remediation\": \"$80 - 25% = $60; $60 + 5% = $63.00.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3282, 6, 3, 11, 'Integers', 171, 7, 'What is -8 + (-5)?', NULL, 7, 'Low', '-13', '3', '-3', '13', 'A', 'active', 'Adding two negative numbers: add their absolute values and keep the negative sign: 8 + 5 = 13 => -13.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student treated it as positive subtraction.\", \"remediation\": \"-8 + (-5) = -13.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted 8 - 5 = 3 and made it negative.\", \"remediation\": \"Adding two negatives makes a larger negative: -8 + (-5) = -13.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student dropped the negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Adding two negative numbers always yields a negative result: -13.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3283, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 175, 7, 'What is (-3/4) × (-8/9)?', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '-2/3', '24/36 (which simplifies to 2/3, but sign error?)', '2/3', '12/13', 'C', 'active', 'A negative times a negative is positive. (-3/4) × (-8/9) = +(3 × 8)/(4 × 9) = 24/36 = 2/3.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student kept a negative sign.\", \"remediation\": \"Negative × Negative = Positive: the answer must be positive 2/3.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student added numerators and denominators.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply straight across and simplify: 24/36 = 2/3.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student added fractions.\", \"remediation\": \"Multiply numerators and denominators: 2/3.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3284, 6, 3, 11, 'Converting', 184, 7, 'Convert the fraction 7/8 into a decimal.', NULL, 7, 'Medium', '0.75', '0.78', '0.87', '0.875', 'D', 'active', 'Divide 7 by 8: 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"0.75 is 6/8 (or 3/4).\", \"remediation\": \"7/8 = 0.875.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"Student just joined the digits 7 and 8 into 0.78.\", \"remediation\": \"Divide 7 by 8 using long division: 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student truncated without the last digit.\", \"remediation\": \"7 ÷ 8 = 0.875.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3285, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Numbers', 175, 7, 'A scuba diver is 18 meters below sea level (-18 m). She ascends 7.5 meters, then descends 4.2 meters. What is her current elevation relative to sea level?', NULL, 7, 'High', '-11.3 meters', '-14.7 meters', '-21.3 meters', '-6.3 meters', 'B', 'active', 'Start at -18. Ascend (+7.5): -18 + 7.5 = -10.5. Descend (-4.2): -10.5 - 4.2 = -14.7 meters.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student added 4.2 instead of subtracting.\", \"remediation\": \"Descending means moving deeper (subtracting): -10.5 - 4.2 = -14.7 m.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted both 7.5 and 4.2: -18 - 7.5 - 4.2 = -29.7 or made an arithmetic slip.\", \"remediation\": \"Ascending is positive (+7.5) and descending is negative (-4.2): -18 + 7.5 - 4.2 = -14.7 m.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student subtracted from 0.\", \"remediation\": \"Net elevation is -14.7 meters.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3286, 6, 3, 11, 'Decimal Expansions', 177, 7, 'Which fraction converts into a REPEATING decimal rather than a terminating decimal?', NULL, 7, 'High', '5/12', '9/25', '3/8', '7/20', 'A', 'active', 'A fraction in simplest form terminates if and only if the prime factorization of its denominator contains only 2s and/or 5s. 12 = 2² × 3 (contains a 3), so 5/12 = 0.41666... is a repeating decimal.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"9/25 = 0.36, which terminates (25 = 5²).\", \"remediation\": \"5/12 produces 0.41666..., which repeats.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"3/8 = 0.375, which terminates (denominator 8 = 2³).\", \"remediation\": \"Fractions with denominators having only 2 and 5 terminate; 12 has a factor of 3, so 5/12 repeats.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"7/20 = 0.35, which terminates (20 = 2² × 5).\", \"remediation\": \"20 only has prime factors 2 and 5, so it terminates.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3287, 6, 3, 11, 'Square Roots', 180, 8, 'What is √144?', NULL, 8, 'Low', '72', '12', '14', '24', 'B', 'active', 'Since 12 × 12 = 144, √144 = 12.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 144 by 2 (144 ÷ 2 = 72) instead of taking square root.\", \"remediation\": \"Square root asks what number multiplied by itself equals 144: 12 × 12 = 144.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"14 × 14 = 196, not 144.\", \"remediation\": \"12² = 144, so √144 = 12.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 144 by 6.\", \"remediation\": \"√144 = 12.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3288, 6, 3, 11, 'Scientific Notation', 183, 8, 'How is the number 4,500,000 written in scientific notation?', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '0.45 × 10⁷', '45 × 10⁵', '4.5 × 10⁵', '4.5 × 10⁶', 'D', 'active', 'In scientific notation a × 10ⁿ, 1 ≤ a < 10. Shifting the decimal 6 places left gives 4.5 × 10⁶.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"0.45 is less than 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Coefficient must be at least 1: 4.5 × 10⁶.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"45 is not between 1 and 10.\", \"remediation\": \"The coefficient a must satisfy 1 ≤ a < 10: shift one more place to get 4.5 × 10⁶.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student only shifted 5 decimal places.\", \"remediation\": \"Count from the end: 6 places to place decimal after 4: 4.5 × 10⁶.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3289, 6, 3, 11, 'Integer Exponents & Radicals', 179, 8, 'Simplify: (2³ × 2⁵) ÷ 2⁴.', NULL, 8, 'Medium', '2⁴ (or 16)', '2¹⁵', '2²', '2⁸', 'A', 'active', 'Using exponent rules: 2³ × 2⁵ = 2³⁺⁵ = 2⁸. Then 2⁸ ÷ 2⁴ = 2⁸⁻⁴ = 2⁴ = 16.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"Student multiplied exponents in the numerator: 3 × 5 = 15.\", \"remediation\": \"When multiplying powers with the same base, ADD the exponents: 3 + 5 = 8.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"Student divided exponents: 8 ÷ 4 = 2.\", \"remediation\": \"When dividing powers with the same base, SUBTRACT the exponents: 8 - 4 = 4: 2⁴ = 16.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"Student forgot to divide by 2⁴.\", \"remediation\": \"Subtract 4 from the exponent: 8 - 4 = 4: 2⁴.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3290, 6, 3, 11, 'Rational Approximations', 178, 8, 'Between which two consecutive whole numbers does √53 lie on the number line?', NULL, 8, 'High', 'Between 26 and 27', 'Between 6 and 7', 'Between 7 and 8', 'Between 8 and 9', 'C', 'active', '7² = 49 and 8² = 64. Since 49 < 53 < 64, √53 lies between √49 and √64, which is between 7 and 8.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"Student divided 53 by 2.\", \"remediation\": \"Square root is not division by 2: compare squares: 7² = 49, 8² = 64.\"}, \"B\": {\"error\": \"6² = 36 and 7² = 49, both of which are less than 53.\", \"remediation\": \"Compare to perfect squares: 49 < 53 < 64, so it is between 7 and 8.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"8² = 64, which is already greater than 53.\", \"remediation\": \"53 is between 49 (7²) and 64 (8²): between 7 and 8.\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-11 18:39:03', '2026-09-12 12:13:07'),
(3292, 6, 2, 8, 'Algebraic Expressions', 198, 3, 'x+5=-10', NULL, 3, 'Medium', '10', '-15', '12', '15', 'B', 'active', 'To solve for x in the equation x + 5 = -10, we need to isolate x by using the inverse operation of adding 5, which is subtracting 5 from both sides:\n\nx + 5 - 5 = -10 - 5\nx = -10 - 5\n\nStarting at -10 on a number line and moving 5 units to the left (subtracting 5) leads to -15. \n\nWe can check our answer by plugging -15 back into the original equation: -15 + 5 = -10, which is correct. Therefore, Option B (-15) is the correct answer.', '{\"A\": {\"error\": \"The student likely ignored the negative sign on -10 and solved x + 5 = 15 (getting 10), or simply picked the number 10 directly from the right side of the equation without performing the necessary inverse operation.\", \"remediation\": \"Encourage the student to circle negative signs before solving and to always check their solution by substituting their answer back into the original equation (e.g., 10 + 5 = 15, not -10).\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student made a counting or arithmetic error while attempting to combine -10 and -5, and also dropped the negative sign from the final answer.\", \"remediation\": \"Provide a printed visual number line so the student can physically trace moving 5 units to the left from -10 to avoid off-by-one or off-by-two counting errors.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student correctly calculated the combined distance of 10 and 5 to get 15 (performing |-10| + 5 or -10 - 5 = 15), but forgot that subtracting from a negative number yields a negative result.\", \"remediation\": \"Remind the student that when you start at a negative number and subtract (move left), the answer must stay negative and become even smaller (e.g., -10 - 5 = -15, not +15).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-12 21:22:57', '2026-09-13 05:16:38'),
(3293, 6, 3, 11, 'Addition Using Place Value', 151, 2, '1+4', NULL, 2, 'Low', '5', '6', '8', '10', 'A', NULL, 'To solve 1 + 4, you can start with the larger number, 4, and count on 1 more: 4... 5! You can also picture 1 object and add 4 more objects to have 5 objects in total. Therefore, Option A (5) is correct.', '{\"B\": {\"error\": \"The student likely made an off-by-one error by counting on 2 instead of 1 (counting \'4... 5, 6\') or confused 1 + 4 with 1 + 5.\", \"remediation\": \"Have the student practice counting on using a number line, physically hopping forward exactly 1 space from 4 to land on 5.\"}, \"C\": {\"error\": \"The student may have doubled the second number (4 + 4 = 8) instead of adding 1.\", \"remediation\": \"Prompt the student to touch and read each number in the equation out loud to ensure they are adding the given numbers rather than doubling.\"}, \"D\": {\"error\": \"The student likely confused the combination with a make-ten fact (such as 5 + 5 = 10) or chose 10 as a familiar benchmark number.\", \"remediation\": \"Use a ten-frame to show that 1 red counter and 4 yellow counters fill only half the frame (5), not the full frame (10).\"}}', NULL, 1, '2026-09-13 05:25:44', '2026-09-13 05:25:44');
